lt0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 | names |}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li328\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-205\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs12 \'c3\'e9\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  '3. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161 \'d0\'ba\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 Q \lang1032 5  \i\lang1033 *~ \i0\ul BQBiI3cBPffiufEEBlBuB\ul0  ri Q '\super i\nosupersub /\super 1\nosupersub ,\super 1\nosupersub '\lang1032 .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi317\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-205\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 self\expndtw0  \expndtw-9 a c c u rn u I a t e \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'c9\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161 \'e7\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 s t V a r \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'cd\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 a. rn e s\lang1032 : \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161 \'e7\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 a rn e s\line \expndtw-6 t r a v e r s e d CI a s s e s\lang1032 : \lang1033 S e t \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 e w\lang1032 ,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li328\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-205\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 t \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 a rn e s}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1164\margrsxn8650\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb601\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1164\margrsxn8650\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1116\colsr259\colno2\colw720\linebetcol \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb486\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Figure 10.13b}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-576\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex46\expndtw-13\dn11\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs96 ooo}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1118\margrsxn1894\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr1466\colno2\colw6710 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-48\shpright4723\shpbottom-48\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 7620}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Preface}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri36\sb406\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 implementors with whom we can discuss our ideas, and from whom we\line \expndtw4 can learn about successful and less successful implementation experi-\line \expndtw14 ences. Paul McCullough of Tektronix suggested that all the\line \expndtw4 implementors submit papers describing their experiences to a software\line \expndtw7 engineering journal or ^to collect papers from each group into book\line \expndtw3 form. This book, then, is the outcome of that suggestion.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li12\fi235\ri38\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The papers in this book should be of value to other Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw3 implementors. To implement the Smalltalk-80 system, one has to match\line \expndtw7 the specification with his or her own hardware environment. Each of\line \expndtw4 the groups represented in this book had different experiences with this\line problem. In addition, some of the groups tested (or speculated about)\line \expndtw5 various schemes for better Smalltalk-80 virtual machine implementa-\line \expndtw-1 tions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi240\ri24\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition to Smalltalk-80 implementors, software engineers should\line \expndtw7 be interested in the papers in this book. Although they are written in\line \expndtw4 the context of Smalltalk-80 implementations, the papers cover the gen-\line eral software engineering topics of managing large software projects,\line virtual memory design and implementation, software caching mecha-\line nisms, and mapping software needs onto hardware design.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi238\ri0\sb12\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The papers in this book raise more issues than they resolve.\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk is still quite young\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 the Smalltalk-80 system is just a snap-\line \expndtw7 shot of research in progress. There are many other issues that need to\line \expndtw3 be raised and many ideas that need to be tested before some of the reso-\line \expndtw2 lutions can be found. It is our hope that this collection of works will get\line \expndtw5 other implementors thinking about key issues in Smalltalk implemen-\line \expndtw3 tations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li17\fi245\ri24\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Part One of this book is a collection of papers that provide some\line background and history of the Smalltalk-80 implementation. The first\line \expndtw0 paper is by Adele Goldberg, manager of the Xerox PARC Software Con-\line \expndtw2 cepts Group (SCG); it describes the history of releasing the Smalltalk-80\line system to the non-Xerox world. Dan Ingalls, who has been the chief ar-\line \expndtw4 chitect of the many Smalltalk implementations, tells how the previous\line \expndtw3 systems led up to the Smalltalk-80 system. Glenn Krasner, also of SCG,\line \expndtw5 presents the design of the format of files that are used for sharing\line Smalltalk-80 code among implementations. The last paper in this sec-\line \expndtw3 tion is by Allen Wirfs-Brock of Tektronix, and explores the various de-\line \expndtw4 sign decisions that Smalltalk-80 implementors may face.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li12\fi242\ri31\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Part Two we present papers that describe the experiences four\line \expndtw5 implementors had in bringing their systems to life. Paul McCullough\line writes about the process they went through at Tektronix, including a\line \expndtw3 step-by-step description taken directly from their logs. His paper points\line \expndtw5 out how valuable the outside implementors were at discovering prob-\line \expndtw9 lems with the system and its documentation. Joe Falcone and Jim\line \expndtw7 Stinger describe the experience they had at Hewlett-Packard bringing\line \expndtw8 up a couple of implementations. Peter Deutsch, of Xerox SCG, gives}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3407\margrsxn1806\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1870\shptop-41\shpright6632\shpbottom-41\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6350}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Preface}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri5\sb204\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 some details of how he took advantage of hardware architecture to in-\line \expndtw6 crease the performance of his Smalltalk-80 implementation. Stoney\line Ballard and Steve Shirron describe an implementation they made at\line \expndtw2 Digital Equipment Corp., which differs radically from the suggested im-\line \expndtw3 plementation of the storage manager, in order to provide improved per-\line \expndtw0 formance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Descriptions of implementation experiences help others make their\line design choices; actual measurements and analyses provide additional\line concrete help. Part Three is a collection of measurements made by the\line \expndtw1 implementation groups. The first paper, by Kim McCall of Xerox SCG,\line \expndtw4 describes a set of benchmarks that is provided in the Smalltalk-80 sys-\line tem to help measure the performance of an implementation. All the im-\line \expndtw6 plementation groups were willing to run these benchmarks, and a\line \expndtw5 comparison of their results is included in the paper. This gives a num-\line ber of implementations against which new implementors can measure\line \expndtw2 their progress. Rick Meyers and Dave Casseres of Apple Computer pro-\line vide an interesting set of analyses of their MC68000-based implementa-\line tion. David Ungar and David Patterson of the University of California\line Berkeley give a before-and-after description of the process of measuring\line \expndtw5 an implementation, optimizing the time-consuming parts, and measur-\line \expndtw3 ing the effects of the optimizations. Joe Falcone made measurements of\line \expndtw9 the Hewlett-Packard implementation that compare static properties\line \expndtw2 with dynamic properties of the system. Finally, Tom Conroy and Ed\line \expndtw5 Pelegri-Llopart of UC Berkeley present an analytic model for measur-\line \expndtw3 ing the potential performance gains of a particular cache scheme for\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 implementations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Part Four we present papers that look toward the future of\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk systems and propose ideas for extending the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw3 system beyond its initial form. The first paper is a description by Ted\line \expndtw2 Kaehler and Glenn Krasner of Xerox SCG of an object-oriented virtual\line memory design. Steve Putz, also of SCG, presents a solution to the prob-\line \expndtw4 lem of coordinating changes made by many people to an evolving\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk-80 system. Jason Penney describes his implementation of a\line \expndtw3 file system at Tektronix, and discusses the use of the Smalltalk-80 sys-\line tem for programming. From the University of Washington, Guy Almes,\line \expndtw7 Alan Borning, and Eli Messinger, present an analysis of the potential\line \expndtw5 for implementing the Smalltalk-80 system on the Intel \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'da\'c1\'d1\'d7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 432\line \expndtw4\lang1033 microprocessor. Although they did not actually implement the system,\line \expndtw2 their paper provides a good analysis of how to match an object-oriented\line \expndtw1 system to object-oriented hardware. Applying compiler technology, Rob-\line \expndtw6 ert Hagmann of the University of California, Berkeley, proposes ways\line \expndtw4 to increase the performance of a Smalltalk-80 implementation. The last\line \expndtw5 paper, by Scott Baden of the University of California, Berkeley, pro-\line \expndtw7 poses hardware architecture support that would enhance the perfor-\line \expndtw3 mance of implementations.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1069\margrsxn1951\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2952\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8604 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex126\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 213}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2988\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex126\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 10.4 \lang1033 Which Methods Reference a Particular Variable or Literal?}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3870\margrsxn6131\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx