5\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6969 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx7545 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-451\slmult0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex50\expndtw-17\dn9\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs84 uoo}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell {\par \cell }\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-192 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx806 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2553 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6969 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx7343 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx7545 {\par \cell }{\par \cell }\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1883\margrsxn1098\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft22\shptop-25\shpright4774\shpbottom-25\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 System Release Process}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2282\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the applications designer \i (Smalltalk-80: Creating a User Interface and\line \expndtw0 Graphical Applications).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa1426\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For their participation in the review process, each manufacturer re-\line \expndtw6 ceived a right to use the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Image in their research\line \expndtw7 and in their developed hardware products. Thus the Virtual Machine\line \expndtw3 has been provided outside Xerox without obligation, while the Virtual\line Image for use in conjunction with the Machine has been licensed under\line \expndtw2 the auspices of copyright. That is, the reproduction and redistribution of\line \expndtw7 the Virtual Image or portions of the Virtual Image are permitted only\line \expndtw5 as incorporated into a product of these manufacturers/licensees.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1883\margrsxn1116\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1429\colsr810\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-313\shpright8914\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Review\line \expndtw1 Process}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first tape containing a Virtual Image was delivered February \lang1032 17,\line \expndtw1 1981. \lang1033 The image file contained \lang1032 328 \lang1033 records, \lang1032 512 \lang1033 bytes per record. The\line \expndtw3 purpose of this tape was to debug the image file format, and to get the\line \expndtw6 reviewers started loading in and running a version of Smalltalk. The\line \expndtw2 image had two deficiencies: the source code for the class hierarchy was\line \expndtw6 primarily a subset of the system, and the primitives called from each\line \expndtw4 class had only preliminary class/method and number assignments. The\line \expndtw3 reviewers were also provided a detailed memo instructing them how to\line \expndtw7 read the image file format and summarizing the information provided\line \expndtw2 in the book on formats for object pointers, object space, contexts, com-\line piled methods, and classes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As part of the agreement, telephone consultation was available to the\line \expndtw6 implementors. Any major bugs or discrepancies in the specifications\line \expndtw2 were reported via telephone and logged. It was possible to monitor each\line \expndtw3 implementor's progress with respect to their discovery of or compensa-\line tion for the bugs. The process of revising the system image itself was\line \expndtw6 carried out at Xerox with the aid of electronic mail: bug reports, bug\line \expndtw5 fixes, status reports, and new ideas were typically communicated\line \expndtw8 electronically. Eventually these communications evolved into a\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk-80 subsystem called the Version Manager which supported\line \expndtw2 (re-Configuration of new system releases.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The second tape was delivered on July \lang1032 24, 1981. \lang1033 In addition to the\line \expndtw3 image file (this time \lang1032 589 \lang1033 records long), the tape contained a file of the\line \expndtw7 source code, a file into which the system writes its "audit trail" of\line \expndtw16 changes, and three files containing traces generated by the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 simulator as it executes the first bytecodes in the Virtual\line \expndtw3 Image. The traces were made by running the formal specification of the\line \expndtw5 interpreter written in Smalltalk-80 code (the code is included in the\line \expndtw2 chapters of the book).}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3929\margrsxn1291\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Review Process}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi241\ri14\sb238\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The three traces, provided in all subsequent tape releases, show de-\line creasing levels of detail over increasing durations.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi-252\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx515 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi-252\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx515 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The    first    trace    shows    all    memory    references,    allocations,\line \expndtw6 bytecodes, message transmissions, returns, and primitive invoca-\line \expndtw2 tions for the first \lang1032 115 \lang1033 bytecodes executed.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi-252\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx515 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi-252\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx515 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The second trace shows only the bytecodes, message transmissions,\line \expndtw4 returns, and primitives for the first \lang1032 409 \lang1033 bytecodes.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx515 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx515 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The third trace shows message transmissions, primitives, and re-\line \expndtw3 turns for the first \lang1032 1981 \lang1033 bytecodes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri22\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The traces allow the implementors to compare their system's actual be-\line havior with the "expected" behavior.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This second tape contained a full system according to the specifica-\line \expndtw6 tion of the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine. All the source code had ei-\line \expndtw7 ther been rewritten according to the class hierarchy for Smalltalk-80,\line \expndtw12 or had been translated from the Smalltalk-76 classes into the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 syntax. However, this translated code was not the defini-\line tion for the final system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi227\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The third tape was delivered four months later on November \lang1032 18,\line \expndtw4 1981. \lang1033 It contained the same kinds of files as were provided on the sec-\line \expndtw3 ond tape. By this time, however, the system user interface had been\line \expndtw5 completely rewritten and a great deal of new functionality had been\line \expndtw3 provided in the program development environment. The image file was\line \expndtw0 now \lang1032 977 \lang1033 records long.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Once again, the Virtual Machine had been changed, in particular,\line \expndtw3 several primitives were added. This time the changes were mostly those\line \expndtw10 discussed and agreed upon by the implementors who attended the\line \expndtw5 "First Ever Smalltalk-80 Implementors' Conference" held September\line \expndtw1\lang1032 24-25, 1981, \lang1033 in Palo Alto. Much of the discussion at this conference cen-\line \expndtw5 tered around the uses of reference counting, garbage collecting, and\line \expndtw3 method caches. The Smalltalk-80 system design separates storage man-\line \expndtw11 agement from the Virtual Machine specification. The various\line \expndtw12 implementors were able to try out several storage management\line \expndtw3 schemes, as well as several different approaches to reference counting.\line \expndtw4 Source code management was also discussed, notably the solution of\line \expndtw5 making source code bona fide Smalltalk objects in a virtual memory\line system, rather than trying to use external text files. Benchmarks for\line \expndtw3 comparing system implementations were specified, and agreement was\line \expndtw7 reached on writing a book on implementation considerations (that is,\line \expndtw5 the book in which this chapter appears).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A fourth tape was later provided in order to distribute a Virtual Im-\line \expndtw9 age that had been used for some time and in which many bugs had\line \expndtw7 been fixed and some new features added. In particular, the fourth im-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1892\margrsxn1104\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3877 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 8{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 System Release Process}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri11\sb248\sa954\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 age added a model interface to a file system. The image file was now\line \expndtw4\lang1032 1011 \lang1033 records long. The implementors who had been successful in run-\line ning the third tape were able simply to load and run this fourth tape,\line \expndtw9 without any changes to their Virtual Machine implementation. The\line \expndtw3 goal of distributing system releases as Virtual Images was thus reached\line \expndtw4 and the review process terminated.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1896\margrsxn1104\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1756\colsr497\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-310\shpright8906\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Additional\line \expndtw-4 Collaborations}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Prior to the delivery of the third image, an additional research license\line \expndtw6 was given to the University of California at Berkeley in order to pro-\line \expndtw4 vide material for study by a graduate seminar on computer architecture\line (taught by Professors David Patterson, John Ousterhout, and Richard\line Fateman). The students in the seminar obtained an early version of the\line \expndtw6 Hewlett-Packard implementation of the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine\line \expndtw5 on which to run the Virtual Image provided by Xerox. After some ini-\line \expndtw7 tial use and study, the students wrote their own implementation in the\line \expndtw2 C language for a VAX/\lang1032 780. \lang1033 The purpose in executing this license was to\line \expndtw3 establish a close collaboration with a group of researchers experienced\line \expndtw5 with the application of state-of-the-art hardware architecture technolo-\line \expndtw3 gy to high-level programming languages.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Once the review process was completed, a special collaboration was\line \expndtw4 formulated with the Fairchild Laboratory for Artificial Intelligence Re-\line \expndtw3 search (FLAIR). Several implementations had been carried out on hard-\line \expndtw1 ware systems consisting of an \lang1032 MC68000 \lang1033 processor, from which several\line \expndtw3 clever ideas for improving the performance of the interpreter had been\line devised. The researchers at Xerox and FLAIR felt that by working to-\line \expndtw1 gether they could combine these clever ideas into a MC68000-based sys-\line \expndtw10 tem with better performance than so far demonstrated. At the time\line \expndtw5 that this chapter was written, this implementation project was still un-\line \expndtw1 der way.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1892\margrsxn1111\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb911\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1892\margrsxn1111\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1623\colsr630\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-263\shpright8910\shpbottom-263\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 A Final Word}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb54\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The book review process, as envisioned by the research team, satisfied\line \expndtw5 two needs: publication of research results, and setting a standard for a\line \expndtw2 new form of personal computing. Publication took the form of written\line \expndtw9 articles; the quantity of material and the integrated presentation of\line \expndtw8 that material required a full book and a special issue of a magazine.\line \expndtw6 The published system, however, was best appreciated in its dynamic\line \expndtw2 form; publication was best served by distribution of the actual system\line \expndtw4 software. Through this distribution, a shared system base has been cre-\line \expndtw0 ated.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1640\margrsxn1371\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4190\shptop-313\shpright8935\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4198\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-439\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 The Evolution\line \expndtw10 of the Smalltalk\line \expndtw7 Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4198\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Daniel H. H. Ingalls}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4198\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1645\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Software Concepts Group\line \expndtw2 Xerox Palo Alto Research Center\line Palo Alto, California}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1640\margrsxn1371\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1512\colsr738\colno2\colw6649 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-241\shpright8899\shpbottom-241\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb72\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this paper we record some history from which the current design of\line the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine springs. Our work over the past de-\line \expndtw3 cade follows a two- to four-year cycle that can be seen to parallel the\line \expndtw8 scientific method and is shown in Fig. \lang1032 2.1. \lang1033 The paper appears in two}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri54\sb86\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Use}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2534\fi0\ri0\sb47\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Observe whether the\line prediction worked}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2658\margrsxn8279\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1163\posnegy54\absw0\absh3369\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw11823\pich5943\picwgoal6703\pichgoal3369\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5627\posnegy479\absw0\absh-439\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs38 Applications}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1267\posnegy1696\absw1335\absh-724\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Implement}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1267\posnegy1696\absw1335\absh-724\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Make a prediction\line \expndtw3 based on theory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx6415\posnegy2059\absw1429\absh-544\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Formulate a theory\line based on\line \expndtw0 experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb3208\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 2.1}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1640\margrsxn1371\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2243\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Xerox Corporation \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1923\margrsxn1062\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4547 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-26\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 10{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri11\sb256\sa1159\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sections that are relatively independent of one another. The first sec-\line \expndtw5 tion traces the evolution of the current design from the perspective of\line \expndtw2 form following function. It follows the major implementation challenges\line and our solutions to them. The second section relates some of the \i meth-\line \expndtw3 odology \i0 which evolved in pursuing this cycle of reincarnation. Readers\line \expndtw6 who are less interested in the details of Smalltalk can skip to the sec-\line \expndtw4 ond section and interpret our experience relative to other programming\line \expndtw3 languages and systems.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1923\margrsxn1062\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1684\colsr569\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft25\shptop-306\shpright8845\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Form Follows\line \expndtw0 Function}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop3917\shpright1890\shpbottom3917\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4050\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Smalltalk-72}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw16\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 From the first Smalltalk interpreter to the definition of the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine, the Smalltalk language has been charac-\line \expndtw5 terized by three principal attributes:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx454 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx454 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent206\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Data stored as objects which are automatically deallocated,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx454 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx454 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent206\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Processing effected by sending messages to objects,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx454 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx454 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent206\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Behavior of objects described in classes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri7\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In spite of other opinions to the contrary, we consider these to be the\line \expndtw2 hallmarks of the "object-oriented" style of computing. In this section we\line \expndtw5 shall trace the evolution of the underlying machinery which has sup-\line \expndtw6 ported language systems in this style over the last ten years. Some of\line \expndtw4 the changes have augmented the power of the language, and some have\line \expndtw3 increased its efficiency. Each change can be seen as an attempt to bring\line \expndtw4 the underlying machinery more into harmony with the day-to-day de-\line \expndtw2 mands of object-oriented programming.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The very first Smalltalk evaluator was a thousand-line BASIC program\line \expndtw6 which first evaluated \lang1032 3 + 4 \lang1033 in October \lang1032 1972. \lang1033 It was followed in two\line \expndtw3 months by a Nova assembly code implementation which became known\line \expndtw2 as the Smalltalk-72 system\super 1\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Storage Management \i0 Objects were allocated from a linked list of\line \expndtw3 free storage using a first-fit strategy. Objects which were no longer ac-\line \expndtw5 cessible were detected by reference-counting. They were then returned\line to the free storage list, with adjacent entries being automatically co-\line \expndtw8 alesced. Since pointers were direct memory addresses, compaction\line \expndtw3 would have been complicated, and was not attempted. Contexts, the\line \expndtw7 suspended stack frames, were managed specially as a stack growing\line \expndtw4 down from high memory while normal allocation grew up from low\line \expndtw11 memory. This separation reduced the tendency to leave "sandbars"}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2637\margrsxn1334\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li5749\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7758 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'f0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Form Follows Function}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1238\fi0\ri40\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 when returning values from deep calls, a problem in the absence of\line \expndtw0 compaction.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1217\fi0\ri61\sb234\sa241\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Token Representation of Code \i0 All code was stored in a single tree\line \expndtw4 made up of instances of Array (it was called Vector then), a variable-\line \expndtw5 length array of pointers. The code in this tree represented a pattern de-\line scription, similar to Meta. Fig. \lang1032 2.2 \lang1033 presents the Smalltalk-\lang1032 72 \lang1033 definition\line \expndtw0 of a class of dotted-pair objects, followed by a few examples of its use.\line \expndtw5 Responses printed by the system are underlined.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2637\margrsxn1403\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw982\colsr756\colno2\colw2095\colsr637\colno3\colw3398 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb5764\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 2.2}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa61\sl-1\slmult0\column\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-187 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 to pair}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-230 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li137\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 |   head tail}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-252 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li137\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 (isnew \lang1032 =>}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-223 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li374\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs14 (" \lang1033 head \lang1032 <\dbch\af24\loch\af0\hich\f0\cchs161 \u9632\'3f}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 - :. \lang1033 "tail \lang1032 <}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-360 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li140\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\scaps\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 \'eb\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 . \i0\scaps0\lang1033 head \lang1032 =>}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-360 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ("head \lang1032 - :)}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li382\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 \'f4\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 head)}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-353 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li140\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 \'cb\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \i0\lang1033 tail \lang1032 =>}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-360 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li374\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 (\dbch\af2\loch\af0\hich\f0\cchs0 \u9632\'3f\hich\af0\dbch\af2\loch\f0\cchs0 ^c \lang1032 <\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161 \'97\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  =$>\line \b0\expndtw13\lang1033\fs18 ("tail}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw21\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 - :)}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li382\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 \'d4\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 tail)}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li137\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 \'cb\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \i0\lang1033 print \lang1032 =>}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li371\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw14\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ("[print.}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 head print}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 " . \lang1033 print.}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-252 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li360\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 tail print.}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 "] \lang1033 print))}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-209 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li126\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 pair}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-547 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li162\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 " \lang1033 a \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \lang1033 pair \lang1032 2}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 5}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-230 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li126\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 [2.5]}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-540 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li122\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 a tail \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \lang1033 pair}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 37}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-288 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li122\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 b tail head}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-194 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1253 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2095 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li119\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 3}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 to \i is the defining word, as in LOGO.\line \i0\expndtw0 |   \i declares instance variable names.\line \i0\expndtw-1 isnew \i is true if an instance is just created.\line \expndtw2\lang1032 " \lang1033 means literally the next token, here the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi79\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 names \i0 head \i and \i0 tail.\line \expndtw0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 - \i\lang1033 is a message like any other.\line \i0\expndtw-8\lang1032 : \i\lang1033 fetches the next value from the incoming.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 message stream.\line \expndtw1\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'cb\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 - \lang1033 matches the next literal token}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li198\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 like the Smalltalk-80 message}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li191\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 peekFor:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 false \i0\lang1032 =\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'df\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 > \i\lang1033 (body) does nothing, but\line true \i0\lang1032 => \i\lang1033 (body) evaluates the body, and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li198\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 then leaves the outer scope.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In this way several such constructs}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 work as a CASE statement.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-536\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Here a \i0 pair is \i created, called \i0 a.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-536\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 a \i gets its \i0 tail \i changed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-536\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 a\i 's \i0 tail \lang1032 (= \i [3.\i0 7]) \i\lang1033 gets the message \i0 head.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2637\margrsxn1334\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1217\fi0\ri61\sb432\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The code was viewed by the interpreter as simply a stream of tokens.\line \expndtw4 The first one encountered was looked up in the dynamic context, to de-\line \expndtw5 termine the receiver of the subsequent message. The name lookup be-\line gan with the class dictionary of the current activation. Failing there, it\line moved to the sender of that activation and so on up the sender chain.\line \expndtw3 When a binding was finally found for the token, its value became the\line \expndtw7 receiver of a new message, and the interpreter activated the code for\line \expndtw3 that object's class.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1485\margrsxn1493\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4522 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 12{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi241\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the new context, the interpreter would begin executing the receiv-\line \expndtw3 er's code, matching it with the token stream which followed the original\line \expndtw4 occurrence of the receiver. Various matching operators would select a\line route through the code which corresponded to the message pattern en-\line countered. The matching vocabulary included matching a literal token,\line \expndtw5 skipping a token, picking up a token literally, and picking up the value\line \expndtw3 of a token. The latter operation invoked the same dynamic lookup de-\line scribed above for the receiver.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri22\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Classes \i0 Most class properties were stored in a single dictionary. In-\line \expndtw4 stance variable names were denoted by a special code which included\line their offset in the instance. Class variables appeared in the same dictio-\line \expndtw6 nary as normal name/value pairs. Another entry gave the size of in-\line \expndtw3 stances, and another gave the "code" for the class. When a class was\line mentioned in code, it would automatically produce a new instance as a\line value. The unfortunate result of this special behavior was to make clas-\line \expndtw4 ses unable to be treated as ordinary objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri36\sb328\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Applications \i0 Smalltalk-72 was ported to the Alto\super 2\nosupersub  as Soon as the\line \expndtw3 first machines were built, and it provided a stable environment for ex-\line \expndtw10 perimentation over the next few years. The Alto provided a large\line \expndtw7 bitmap display and a pointing device, and thus made an ideal vehicle\line \expndtw4 for working with graphical user interfaces.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi241\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Development of the Smalltalk-72 system began with Textframes and\line Turtles. Textframes provided text display with independent composition\line \expndtw4 and clipping rectangles; Turtles gave us line drawing capability, mod-\line eled after Papert's experiments with turtle geometry\super 3\nosupersub . In both cases,\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk's ability to describe multiple instances added considerable le-\line verage to these primitive capabilities. Soon many interesting and useful\line applications were written, including a mouse-driven program editor, a\line \expndtw5 structured graphics editor, an animation system and a music system.\line \expndtw6 Finally, Smalltalk-72 served as the basis for an experimental curricu-\line \expndtw2 lum in object-oriented computing for high-school children\super 4\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi0\ri36\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'d0\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Shortcomings \i0 The Smalltalk-72 system was used heavily by a\line \expndtw5 dozen people for four years. The many practical applications gave us a\line \expndtw3 lot of experience with the power of classes and the message-sending\line \expndtw8 metaphor. In the course of this work, we also became increasingly\line \expndtw5 aware of many limitations in the Smalltalk-72 system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi238\ri43\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Dynamic lookup of tokens was both inefficient and unmodular. The\line \expndtw3 dynamic lookup tempted some programmers to write functions which\line \expndtw4 "knew" about their enclosing context. This code would then cause sub-\line \expndtw7 tle errors when apparently innocent changes were made in the outer\line \expndtw-4 level.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3265\margrsxn1952\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1940\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6520 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 13}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Form Follows Function}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi238\ri4\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The message stream model was complicated and inefficient. One\line \expndtw5 could not tell what a given piece of code meant in isolation. This be-\line \expndtw4 came a problem as we attempted to build larger systems in which mod-\line \expndtw3 ularity was more of an issue. Also, a considerable amount of time was\line wasted on execution-time parsing (determining whether the result of a\line \expndtw8 receiver expression should gobble the next token in the execution\line \expndtw1 stream).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi241\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As mentioned above, classes were not first-class objects. Also, as our\line \expndtw4 experience increased, we came to realize the need for inheritance. This\line \expndtw3 was felt first in the commonality of behavior of Arrays, Strings, and\line sub-Arrays. For the time being, we referred to common functions from\line \expndtw5 these similar classes so as to factor the behavior, but it was clear that\line \expndtw4 some sort of inheritance mechanism was needed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri25\sb284\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In \lang1032 1974 \lang1033 we produced a major redesign of the Smalltalk interpreter with\line \expndtw6 the aim of cleaning up its semantics and improving its performance.\line \expndtw4 While the redesign was a mixed success, Smalltalk-74 was the site of\line several advances which fed into the later systems.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri25\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Message Stream Formalism \i0 We succeeded in formalizing the oper-\line ation of the interpreter, a step in the direction of simplicity and gener-\line \expndtw5 ality. For instance, we were able to provide a programmer-accessible\line \expndtw3 object which represented the incoming message stream. Thus, not only\line \expndtw5 could all the message stream operations be examined in Smalltalk, but\line \expndtw3 the user could also define his own extensions to the message stream se-\line \expndtw7 mantics. While this was a local success, it did not solve either of the\line \expndtw3 real problems: token interpretation overhead, and non-modularity of re-\line ceiver-dependent message parsing.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri25\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Message Dictionaries \i0 Classes were given a message dictionary to\line \expndtw3 allow primary message matching to be done by hashing. This did not do\line \expndtw4 much for execution speed, since the previous interpreter had tight code\line for its linear scan, but it did help compilation a great deal since a single\line \expndtw5 message could be recompiled without having to recompile all the code\line \expndtw4 for the class. Unfortunately classes were still not able to be treated as\line \expndtw1 normal objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri32\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 BitBlt \i0 Smalltalk-74 was the first Smalltalk to use BitBIt as its\line \expndtw5 main operation for bitmap graphics. The specification for BitBIt arose\line out of earlier experience with Turtle graphics, text display, and other\line screen operations such as scrolling and menu overlays. Our specifica-\line \expndtw2 tion of BitBIt has been used by others under the name RasterOp\super 5\nosupersub . While\line \expndtw10 the general operation was available to the Smalltalk programmer,\line \expndtw5 much of the system graphics were still done in machine-coded primi-\line tives, owing to inadequate performance of the token interpreter.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1964\margrsxn1018\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4529 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 14{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa180 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1964\margrsxn1018\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1609\colsr652\colno2\colw6667 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb3290\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Smalltalk-76}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft14\shptop5072\shpright1915\shpbottom5072\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb5256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Experience with\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk-76}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'d0\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 OOZE \i0 Smalltalk-74 was the system in which the OOZE ("Object-\line \expndtw2 Oriented Zoned Environment")\super 6\nosupersub  virtual memory was first implemented.\line \expndtw6 OOZE provided uniform access to 65K objects, or roughly a million\line \expndtw3 words of data. Smalltalk-74 served as the development environment for\line \expndtw4 OOZE, so that when Smalltalk-76 was designed, OOZE was debugged\line and ready for use.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx317 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw3\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Applications    \i0 In addition to the previous applications which we had\line \expndtw5 developed, Smalltalk-74 served as host to an information retrieval sys-\line tem and complete window-oriented display interface. Owing to the vir-\line \expndtw7 tual memory support, it was possible to integrate many functions in a\line \expndtw5 convenient and uniform user interface.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In \lang1032 1976 \lang1033 we carried out a major redesign of the Smalltalk language and\line implementation\super 7\nosupersub . It addressed most of the problems encountered in the\line previous four years of experience with Smalltalk:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb180\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx450 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb180\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx450 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Classes and contexts became real objects;}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx450 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx450 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A class hierarchy provided inheritance;}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx450 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx450 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A simple yet flexible syntax for messages was introduced;}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb90\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx450 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb90\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx450 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The syntax eliminated message stream side-effects and could be\line \expndtw-3 compiled;}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-245\slmult0\tx450 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-245\slmult0\tx450 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A compact and efficient byte-encoded instruction set was intro-\line \expndtw-4 duced;}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx450 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx450 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A microcode emulator for this instruction set ran \lang1032 4 \lang1033 to \lang1032 100 \lang1033 times\line \expndtw5 faster than previous Smalltalks; and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx450 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li450\fi-205\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx450 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 OOZE provided storage for 65K objects\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 roughly the capacity of\line \expndtw5 the Alto hardware.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri4\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The design for this system was completed in November of \lang1032 1976 \lang1033 and sev-\line \expndtw4 en months later the system was working. This included a full rewrite of\line \expndtw3 all the system class definitions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-76 design stood the test of time well. It was used for four\line years by \lang1032 20 \lang1033 people daily and \lang1032 100 \lang1033 people occasionally. A large portion of\line \expndtw6 the design survives unchanged in the Smalltalk-80 system. However,\line the Smalltalk-76 design did have some snags which we encountered\line \expndtw4 during our four-year experience.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx317 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw3\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Block Contexts    \i0 Smalltalk-76 had to provide a mechanism for pass-\line \expndtw5 ing unevaluated code which was compatible with a compiled represen-\line \expndtw4 tation.  A  syntax  was  devised which  used  open-colon  keywords  for}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3825\margrsxn1366\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3323\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6548 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 15}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Form Follows Function}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 passing unevaluated expressions (the semantics were the same as the\line square bracket construct in the Smalltalk-80 language). This approach\line \expndtw4 was supported by block contexts which allowed executing code remote-\line \expndtw7 ly. Since the Smalltalk-76 design had no experience to draw from, it\line \expndtw5 was weak in several areas.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One problem which was discovered in the process of supporting error\line \expndtw8 recovery was that block contexts could not be restarted because they\line \expndtw4 did not include their initial PC as part of their state. This was not nor-\line mally needed for looping, since all such code fragments ended with a\line branch back to the beginning. Happily, we were able to fix this by de-\line \expndtw3 fining a new subclass.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Two other problems were discovered with remote contexts when\line \expndtw5 users began to store them as local procedures. For one thing, there was\line \expndtw2 no check in the interpreter to recover gracefully if such a piece of code\line \expndtw6 executed a return to sender after the originating context had already\line \expndtw4 returned. Also, the system could crash if remote contexts were made to\line call one another recursively, since they depended on their home context\line \expndtw6 for stack space, rather than having their own stack space.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There were two other weaknesses with remote code. There was an\line \expndtw1 assymmetry due to use of open-colon keywords. For example one would\line \expndtw4 write}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li256\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 newCursor showWhile:: [someExpression]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb313\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 to cause a different cursor to appear during execution of\line \expndtw2 someExpression. But if the code contained a variable, \i action, \i0 which was\line \expndtw3 already bound to remote code, one wanted that variable to be passed di-\line \expndtw6 rectly, as with a closed-colon keyword. The only way to handle this\line \expndtw3 without needing a pair of messages with and without evaluation was to\line \expndtw4 write}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li266\fi0\ri0\sb288\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 newCursor showWhile [action eval].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri25\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This would do the right thing, but caused an extra remote evaluation\line \expndtw4 for every level at which this strategy was exercised. Besides being cost-\line ly, it was just plain ugly.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another weakness of remote contexts was that, while they acted\line \expndtw4 much like nullary functions, there was no way to extend the family to\line functions which took arguments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi241\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Finally, there was a question about variable scoping within remote\line \expndtw2 code blocks. Smalltalk-76 had no scoping, whereas most other languages\line \expndtw1 with blocks did.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri32\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All of these problems with RemoteContexts were addressed one way\line \expndtw5 or another in the Smalltalk-80 design.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1823\margrsxn1121\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4540 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 16{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa180 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1823\margrsxn1121\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1602\colsr662\colno2\colw6699 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb7924\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Experience with\line \expndtw-4 OOZE}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Compilation Order \i0 The Smalltalk-76 interpreter assumed that the\line \expndtw1 receiver of a message would be on the top of the execution stack, with\line \expndtw3 arguments below it. The number of arguments was not specified in the\line \expndtw5 "send" instruction, but was determined from the method header after\line \expndtw6 message lookup. From the designer's perspective this seemed natural;\line \expndtw4 the only other reasonable choice would be for the receiver to lie under-\line \expndtw9 neath the arguments, as in the Smalltalk-80 system. In this case it\line \expndtw5 seemed necessary to determine the number of arguments from the se-\line lector in order to find the receiver in the stack, and this looked both\line complex and costly to do at run time. There were two problems with\line \expndtw7 having the receiver on the top of the stack. First the compiler had to\line \expndtw6 save the code for the receiver while it put out the code for the argu-\line \expndtw5 ments. This was no problem for three of the compilers which we built,\line \expndtw7 but one particularly simple compiler design foundered on this detail.\line \expndtw5 The second problem with post-evaluation of receivers was that the or-\line \expndtw2 der of evaluation differed from the order of appearance in the code. Al-\line \expndtw5 though one should not write Smalltalk code which depends on such\line \expndtw6 ordering, it did happen occasionally, and programmers were confused\line \expndtw4 by the Smalltalk-76 evaluation scheme.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Instruction Set Limitations \i0 The Smalltalk-76 instruction set was\line \expndtw4 originally limited to accessing \lang1032 16 \lang1033 instance variables, \lang1032 32 \lang1033 temps, and \lang1032 48\line \expndtw5\lang1033 literals. These limits were both a strain on applications and on the in-\line \expndtw7 struction set. A year later we added extended instructions which re-\line \expndtw3 lieved these limits. This was important for applications, and it also took\line pressure off the future of the instruction set. With extended codes avail-\line \expndtw7 able, we had the flexibility to change the instruction set to better re-\line \expndtw4 flect measured usage patterns. For example we found that we could get\line \expndtw2 rid of the (non-extended) instructions which accessed literals \lang1032 33-48, \lang1033 be-\line \expndtw5 cause their usage was so low. Such measurements led us eventually to\line the present Smalltalk-80 instruction set.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Address Encoding \i0 In OOZE, object pointers encoded class informa-\line \expndtw8 tion in the high \lang1032 9 \lang1033 bits of each pointer. This had the benefit of saving\line \expndtw4 one word per object which would have been needed to point to the class\line of the object. In fact, it actually saved two words on many objects be-\line \expndtw5 cause classes contained the length of their instances. Variable length\line \expndtw3 objects had separate class-parts for common lengths \lang1032 (0 \lang1033 through \lang1032 8). \lang1033 How-\line \expndtw4 ever, the address encoding had several weaknesses. It squandered \lang1032 128\line \expndtw5\lang1033 pointers on each class, even though some never had more than a couple\line of instances. It also set a limit on the number of classes in the system\line \expndtw3\lang1032 (512). \lang1033 This did not turn out to be a problem, although an earlier limit of\line \lang1032 128 \lang1033 did have to be changed. Finally, owing to the encoding, it was not\line possible to use object pointers as semantic indirection. For this reason,\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-76 could not support become: (mutation of objects through\line \expndtw5 pointer indirection) as in later Smalltalks.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1623\margrsxn1354\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4183\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8759 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 17}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Form Follows Function}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Capacity \i0 While the OOZE limitation of 65K objects is small by to-\line \expndtw3 day's standards, it served well on the Alto. The Alto has a \lang1032 2.5 \lang1033 megabyte\line disk, and with a mean object size of \lang1032 16 \lang1033 words, OOZE was well matched\line \expndtw1 to this device.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri4\sb234\sa508\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Interpreter Overhead \i0 OOZE had a couple of weaknesses in the area\line \expndtw3 of performance, which only became significant after our appetites had\line \expndtw4 increased from several years' experience. One was that the object table\line \expndtw6 required at least one hash probe for every object access, even just to\line \expndtw5 touch a reference count. Another was a design flaw in the management\line \expndtw3 of free storage which required going to the disk to create a new tempo-\line \expndtw2 rary object if its pointer had been previously placed on a free list. We\line \expndtw3 designed a solution to both of these problems. Temporary objects would\line \expndtw4 be treated specially with pointers which were direct indexes into their\line \expndtw6 object table. Freelists would only be consulted when an object "ma-\line \expndtw5 tured" and needed a permanent pointer assigned. Because temporary\line \expndtw1 objects account for many accesses, much of the overhead of probing the\line \expndtw4 permanent object table would be eliminated. Since Smalltalk-76's days\line seemed numbered, we did not take the time to implement this solution.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1623\margrsxn1365\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1458\colsr778\colno2\colw6685 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Efficiency and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Portability:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Smalltalk-78}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In \lang1032 1977 \lang1033 we began a project to build a portable computer capable of run-\line \expndtw5 ning the Smalltalk system. Known internally as NoteTaker, it began as\line \expndtw4 a hand-held device for taking notes, but ended up as a suitcase-sized\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk machine. Several factors converged to define this project. We\line \expndtw6 wanted to be able to bring Smalltalk to conferences and meetings to\line break through the abstractions of verbal presentations. With the Intel\line \expndtw1\lang1032 8086 \lang1033 and other 16-bit microprocessors (the Z8000 and \lang1032 MC68000 \lang1033 were\line \expndtw5 coming, but not available yet), we felt that enough computing power\line \expndtw2 would be available to support Smalltalk, even without microcode. Final-\line \expndtw8 ly, portability seemed to be an essential ingredient for exploring the\line \expndtw3 full potential of personal computing.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The design challenge was significant. We were moving to an environ-\line \expndtw3 ment with less processing power, and the whole system had to fit in \lang1032 1/4\line \expndtw4\lang1033 Mbyte, since there was no swapping medium. Also we faced transport-\line \expndtw2 ing 32K bytes of machine code which made up the Smalltalk-76 system,\line \expndtw7 and it seemed a shame not to learn something in the process. The re-\line \expndtw3 sult of these forces was the design of Smalltalk-78.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri32\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Cloned Implementation \i0 The Smalltalk-78 implementation was sig-\line \expndtw6 nificant in that it was not built from scratch. We were happy enough\line with the basic model that we transported the entire Smalltalk level of\line \expndtw5 the system from Smalltalk-76. In order to do this, we used the system\line \expndtw3 tracer (see p. \lang1032 24) \lang1033 which could write a clone of the entire system onto an\line \expndtw10 image file. This file could then be loaded and executed by the\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk-78 interpreter. The tracer had provisions in it for transmut-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1176\margrsxn1794\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx2070\tlul\tx4511 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-29\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 18{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 .{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \li2257\ri475\sb252\sa0{\pict\picw10947\pich7620\picwgoal6206\pichgoal4320\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1030\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 2.3}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri4\sb223\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ing object formats as necessary, and even for changing the instruction\line \expndtw5 set used in the methods.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2239\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2570 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \u9633\'3f\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2239\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2570 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent331\pnf25{\pntxtb \u9633\'3f}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Indexed OT   \i0 The Smalltalk-\lang1032 78 \lang1033 design centered around an indexed\line \expndtw8 object table, which is the same design as in the Smalltalk-80 system.\line \expndtw9 This greatly simplified object access and yet retained the indirection\line \expndtw8 which made for easy storage management in Smalltalk-76. Reference\line \expndtw7 counts were stored as one byte of the 4-byte table entry. Given an ob-\line \expndtw11 ject pointer in a register, a reference count could be incremented or\line \expndtw4 decremented with a single add-byte instruction with an overflow check.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2239\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2570 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \u9633\'3f\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2239\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2570 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent331\pnf25{\pntxtb \u9633\'3f}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Small Integers    \i0 Since there would not be room in core for more\line \expndtw4 than 10K objects or so, it was possible to encode small integers \lang1032 (-16384\line \expndtw5\lang1033 to \lang1032 16383) \lang1033 in part of the pointer space. Since object table indices would\line \expndtw2 all be even (on the \lang1032 8086, \lang1033 they were multiples of \lang1032 4), \lang1033 we encoded small in-\line \expndtw6 tegers as two's complement integers in the high-order \lang1032 15 \lang1033 bits, with the\line \expndtw7 low-order bit turned on. With this design, allocation of integer results\line \expndtw6 was trivial, integer literals could be stored efficiently, and integer val-\line \expndtw4 ues did not need to be reference counted.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi0\ri7\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i In-line Contexts \i0 In order to save time allocating new contexts, and\line \expndtw5 to take advantage of the stack-oriented instructions available in most\line \expndtw4 microprocessors, the representation of contexts was redesigned. Instead\line \expndtw3 of having a separate object for each context, a large object was allocated\line for each process, in which contexts could be represented as conventional}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3315\margrsxn1912\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1922\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6498 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-24\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 19}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Form Follows Function}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri4\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 stack frames. This special representation complicated the handling of\line blocks and the debugger, requiring an interface which referred to the\line \expndtw3 process and an offset within the process.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition to reduced allocation time, the time to transfer argu-\line \expndtw7 ments was eliminated by allowing the contexts to overlap; the top of\line \expndtw4 one context's stack (receiver and arguments) was the base of the next\line \expndtw3 context's frame.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri4\sb313\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Reduced Kernel\i0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 The Leverage of BitBlt \i0 We have always sought\line \expndtw4 to reduce the size of the Smalltalk kernel. This is not only an aesthetic\line \expndtw8 desideratum; kernel code is inaccessible to the normal user, and we\line \expndtw3 have always tried to minimize the parts of our system which can not be\line \expndtw7 examined and altered by the curious user. In this particular case, we\line \expndtw3 were also moving to a new machine. While writing a certain amount of\line machine code seemed inevitable, we did not relish the idea of transcrib-\line \expndtw2 ing all 32K bytes of code which comprised the Smalltalk-76 kernel. For-\line \expndtw7 tunately, much of that bulk consisted of various routines to compose\line \expndtw5 and display text, to draw lines and implement Turtle geometry, and to\line \expndtw6 provide various interfaces to bitmap graphics such as moving rectan-\line \expndtw3 gles, and copying bits to buffers as for restoring the background under\line \expndtw1 menus.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The definition of BitBlt grew out of our experience with text, lines\line \expndtw4 and other bitmap graphics. Now the constraints of the NoteTaker im-\line \expndtw5 plementation provided the motivation to implement all these capabili-\line \expndtw3 ties in Smalltalk, leaving only the one primitive BitBlt operation in the\line \expndtw4 kernel. This was a great success in reducing the size of the kernel. The\line \expndtw2 full NoteTaker kernel consisted of around 6K bytes of \lang1032 8086 \lang1033 code. This\line \expndtw3 figure did not include Ethernet support, real-time clock, nor any signifi-\line cant support for process scheduling.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb342\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Performance \i0 The performance of the NoteTaker was interesting to\line \expndtw5 compare with the Alto. The Smalltalk instruction rate improved by a\line \expndtw3 factor of two, and yet the display of text was much slower (being in\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk, rather than machine code). By adding a small primitive for\line \expndtw4 the inner loop of text display and line drawing, this decrease was large-\line \expndtw1 ly compensated. User response for such actions as compiling was signifi-\line \expndtw3 cantly improved, owing to the faster execution and to the freedom from\line \expndtw1 the swapping delays of OOZE.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Mutability \i0 Smalltalk-78 used no encoding of object pointers other\line \expndtw4 than for small integers. Class pointers and length fields (for variable-\line \expndtw3 length objects) were stored just as any other fields. It was therefore pos-\line \expndtw5 sible in this design to allow mutation of objects, and this was made\line \expndtw4 available as the primitive method for become:.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1942\margrsxn996\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4543 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa220 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1952\margrsxn996\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1310\colsr950\colno2\colw6699 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb1631\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 TinyTalk}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop2466\shpright1904\shpbottom2466\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2617\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Smalltalk-80}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx324 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw5\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Relevance    \i0 We learned a great deal from the NoteTaker challenge,\line \expndtw6 even though only \lang1032 10 \lang1033 prototypes were built. We made the system much\line \expndtw4 more   portable,   and   had  demonstrated  that  the   new  generation  of\line \expndtw8 microprocessors could indeed support Smalltalk. The decision not to\line \expndtw4 continue the project added motivation to release Smalltalk widely.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri4\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At the same time as the NoteTaker implementation, we performed an\line \expndtw2 experiment\super 8\nosupersub  to see if a very simple implementation could run on a con-\line \expndtw5 ventional microprocessor such as a Z80 or \lang1032 6502. \lang1033 This implementation\line \expndtw3 used marking garbage collection instead of reference-counting, and was\line \expndtw4 able to use simple push and pop operations on the stack as a result. A\line method cache largely eliminated the overhead in message lookup and,\line \expndtw6 since primitive codes were included in the cache, access to primitives\line \expndtw7 was fast. The system did actually fit in 64K bytes with a little bit of\line \expndtw3 room to spare. Another experiment which was done in conjunction with\line \expndtw4 this implementation was to demonstrate that a special case of BitBlt for\line \expndtw6 characters could run much faster than the general version.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With Smalltalk-78 behind us, few changes were made to the Virtual\line \expndtw10 Machine to produce the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine. The main\line \expndtw3 change was an increase in power from allowing blocks with arguments.\line \expndtw4 Beyond this, mostly we cleaned up many details, some of which sup-\line \expndtw5 ported more extensive cleanups in the Smalltalk level of the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Contexts Again \i0 We felt that the optimized contexts of\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk-78 did not justify the loss in clarity which they entailed. So\line \expndtw3 in the Smalltalk-80 language we reverted to Contexts as objects, leaving\line \expndtw9 such optimizations up to implementors clever enough to hide their\line \expndtw4 tricks entirely from Smalltalk. In order to simplify the management of\line \expndtw2 Contexts in the Virtual Machine, we decided to use two sizes of contexts\line \expndtw5 instead of making them truly variable-length. This meant that, if sepa-\line \expndtw4 rate free lists were managed for these two lengths, storage for contexts\line \expndtw3 could be allocated and freed with relatively little fragmentation and co-\line \expndtw1 alescence overhead.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Blocks with Arguments \i0 While the syntax changed little in the\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk-80 language (open colon and other non-ASCII selectors were\line \expndtw3 banished), our extended discussions of syntax led to the current descrip-\line \expndtw5 tion for blocks with arguments. In fact, this required no change to the\line \expndtw6 Virtual Machine, but it had the feel of such a change in the language.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx324 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw2\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 BlockContexts    \i0 We   re-engineered   BlockContexts   in   the   Small-\line \expndtw6 talk-80 language. Smalltalk-78 had already handled their recursive ap-\line \expndtw4 plication by providing independent stack space for each invocation. Be-\line \expndtw6 yond this, mechanisms were defined for checking and diagnosing such\line \expndtw8 anomalous conditions as returning to a context which has already re-\line \expndtw4 turned.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1642\margrsxn1300\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8773 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 21}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Form Follows Function}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Compilation Order \i0 Smalltalk-78 had perpetuated the post-evalua-\line \expndtw4 tion of receiver expressions so as to avoid delving into the stack to find\line the receiver. In the Smalltalk-80 language, however, we encoded the\line number of arguments in the send instruction. This enabled strictly left-\line \expndtw5 to-right evaluation, and no one has since complained about surprising\line order of evaluation. We suspect that this change will yield further fruit\line in the future when someone tries to build a very simple compiler.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Instruction Set \i0 In addition to revamping the send instructions, we\line \expndtw5 made several other improvements to the instruction set. We completed\line \expndtw9 the branch instructions by adding branch-if-true. We put in \lang1032 2- \lang1033 and\line \expndtw4 3-byte extensions to retain reasonable compactness without restricting\line \expndtw8 functionality. We also added a few compact codes for returning true\line \expndtw4 and false, and for pop-and-store into temps and fields of the receiver.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri4\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Methods \i0 The encoding of method headers followed the earlier\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-78 design. In order to simplify the allocation of contexts, a bit\line \expndtw8 was included to indicate whether a large frame was necessary to run\line \expndtw5 the method or not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 While the present Smalltalk design has evolved over a decade now, that}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Future Directions         \i0 does not mean it is finished. As when one climbs a large mountain, the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 higher reaches are gradually revealed and it seems there is as much to\line \expndtw4 do now as when we started.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri22\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Virtual Memory \i0 An obvious shortcoming of the Smalltalk-80 speci-\line \expndtw4 fication is that it does not include a virtual memory. There are several\line \expndtw3 reasons for this. Our experience with OOZE suggested that object-ori-\line \expndtw5 ented approaches might be significantly better than simple paging, and\line \expndtw3 we did not want to commit ourselves to one or the other. From our ex-\line \expndtw9 perience with porting the system from one interpreter to another, it\line \expndtw5 was clear to us that implementors could experiment with the virtual\line \expndtw4 memory issue fairly easily, while still working from the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw3 image specification. The current object formats allow a simple resident\line implementation, and yet lend themselves to extension in most of the ob-\line \expndtw4 vious directions for virtual memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri18\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Reducing Concepts \i0 It is always useful to reduce the number of\line \expndtw6 concepts in a language when possible. Smalltalk distinguishes many\line \expndtw3 levels of refinement: subclassing, instantiation, blocks and contexts, to\line name a few. It is likely that some of these distinctions can be dissolved,\line \expndtw6 and that a cleaner virtual machine design would result.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri11\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Typing and Protocols \i0 While the Smalltalk-80 language is not a\line \expndtw4 typed language in the normal sense, there is nonetheless an implicit no-\line \expndtw3 tion of variable type in the protocol (full set) of messages which must be}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1634\margrsxn1304\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx724\tlul\tx4536 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa216 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1645\margrsxn1304\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1314\colsr950\colno2\colw6696 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb1642\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 TinyTalk}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop2470\shpright1904\shpbottom2470\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2628\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Smalltalk-80}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx320 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw5\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Relevance    \i0 We learned a great deal from the NoteTaker challenge,\line \expndtw6 even though only \lang1032 10 \lang1033 prototypes were built. We made the system much\line \expndtw4 more   portable,   and  had  demonstrated  that  the   new  generation   of\line \expndtw8 microprocessors could indeed support Smalltalk. The decision not to\line \expndtw4 continue the project added motivation to release Smalltalk widely.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At the same time as the NoteTaker implementation, we performed an\line \expndtw2 experiment\super 8\nosupersub  to see if a very simple implementation could run on a con-\line \expndtw5 ventional microprocessor such as a Z80 or \lang1032 6502. \lang1033 This implementation\line \expndtw3 used marking garbage collection instead of reference-counting, and was\line \expndtw4 able to use simple push and pop operations on the stack as a result. A\line method cache largely eliminated the overhead in message lookup and,\line \expndtw6 since primitive codes were included in the cache, access to primitives\line \expndtw7 was fast. The system did actually fit in 64K bytes with a little bit of\line \expndtw3 room to spare. Another experiment which was done in conjunction with\line \expndtw4 this implementation was to demonstrate that a special case of BitBlt for\line \expndtw6 characters could run much faster than the general version.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With Smalltalk-78 behind us, few changes were made to the Virtual\line \expndtw10 Machine to produce the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine. The main\line \expndtw4 change was an increase in power from allowing blocks with arguments.\line Beyond this, mostly we cleaned up many details, some of which sup-\line \expndtw5 ported more extensive cleanups in the Smalltalk level of the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Contexts Again \i0 We felt that the optimized contexts of\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk-78 did not justify the loss in clarity which they entailed. So\line \expndtw3 in the Smalltalk-80 language we reverted to Contexts as objects, leaving\line \expndtw9 such optimizations up to implementors clever enough to hide their\line \expndtw5 tricks entirely from Smalltalk. In order to simplify the management of\line \expndtw2 Contexts in the Virtual Machine, we decided to use two sizes of contexts\line \expndtw4 instead of making them truly variable-length. This meant that, if sepa-\line rate free lists were managed for these two lengths, storage for contexts\line could be allocated and freed with relatively little fragmentation and co-\line \expndtw2 alescence overhead.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx320 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af25\hich\f1\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \u9633\'3f{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw12\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Blocks with Arguments    \i0 While the syntax changed little in the\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk-80 language (open colon and other non-ASCII selectors were\line \expndtw4 banished), our extended discussions of syntax led to the current descrip-\line \expndtw7 tion for blocks with arguments. In fact, this required no change to the\line \expndtw6 Virtual Machine, but it had the feel of such a change in the language.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb169\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i BlockContexts \i0 We re-engineered BlockContexts in the Small-\line \expndtw5 talk-80 language. Smalltalk-78 had already handled their recursive ap-\line \expndtw2 plication by providing independent stack space for each invocation. Be-\line \expndtw3 yond this, mechanisms were defined for checking and diagnosing such\line \expndtw5 anomalous conditions as returning to a context which has already re-\line \expndtw4 turned.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1357\margrsxn1584\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4190\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8773 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 21}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Form Follows Function}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri7\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Compilation Order \i0 Smalltalk-78 had perpetuated the post-evalua-\line \expndtw4 tion of receiver expressions so as to avoid delving into the stack to find\line the receiver. In the Smalltalk-80 language, however, we encoded the\line number of arguments in the send instruction. This enabled strictly left-\line \expndtw5 to-right evaluation, and no one has since complained about surprising\line order of evaluation. We suspect that this change will yield further fruit\line in the future when someone tries to build a very simple compiler.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Instruction Set \i0 In addition to revamping the send instructions, we\line \expndtw5 made several other improvements to the instruction set. We completed\line \expndtw9 the branch instructions by adding branch-if-true. We put in \lang1032 2- \lang1033 and\line \expndtw4 3-byte extensions to retain reasonable compactness without restricting\line \expndtw8 functionality. We also added a few compact codes for returning true\line \expndtw4 and false, and for pop-and-store into temps and fields of the receiver.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri4\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Methods \i0 The encoding of method headers followed the earlier\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-78 design. In order to simplify the allocation of contexts, a bit\line \expndtw8 was included to indicate whether a large frame was necessary to run\line \expndtw5 the method or not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 While the present Smalltalk design has evolved over a decade now, that}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Future Directions         \i0 does not mean it is finished. As when one climbs a large mountain, the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 higher reaches are gradually revealed and it seems there is as much to\line \expndtw4 do now as when we started.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri25\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Virtual Memory \i0 An obvious shortcoming of the Smalltalk-80 speci-\line \expndtw4 fication is that it does not include a virtual memory. There are several\line \expndtw3 reasons for this. Our experience with OOZE suggested that object-ori-\line \expndtw5 ented approaches might be significantly better than simple paging, and\line \expndtw3 we did not want to commit ourselves to one or the other. From our ex-\line \expndtw9 perience with porting the system from one interpreter to another, it\line \expndtw5 was clear to us that implementors could experiment with the virtual\line \expndtw4 memory issue fairly easily, while still working from the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw3 image specification. The current object formats allow a simple resident\line implementation, and yet lend themselves to extension in most of the ob-\line vious directions for virtual memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri25\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Reducing Concepts \i0 It is always useful to reduce the number of\line \expndtw6 concepts in a language when possible. Smalltalk distinguishes many\line \expndtw3 levels of refinement: subclassing, instantiation, blocks and contexts, to\line name a few. It is likely that some of these distinctions can be dissolved,\line \expndtw6 and that a cleaner virtual machine design would result.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri14\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Typing and Protocols \i0 While the Smalltalk-80 language is not a\line \expndtw4 typed language in the normal sense, there is nonetheless an implicit no-\line \expndtw3 tion of variable type in the protocol (full set) of messages which must be}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1656\margrsxn1310\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4554 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 22{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri25\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 understood by a given variable. We have described an experimental sys-\line \expndtw5 tem based on this notion of type\super 9\nosupersub , and a serious treatment of this ap-\line \expndtw4 proach would likely involve changes to the Virtual Machine.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri22\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Multiple Inheritance \i0 While the Smalltalk-80 system does not pro-\line \expndtw5 vide for multiple inheritance, we have described an experimental sys-\line tem which supports multiple superclasses using the standard Virtual\line \expndtw4 Machine\super 10\nosupersub . This is another area in which serious use of the new para-\line \expndtw5 digm might suggest useful changes to the Virtual Machine.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri18\sb241\sa1595\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Tiny Implementations \i0 While, on one end of the spectrum, we seek\line \expndtw4 to build vastly larger systems, we should not ignore the role of small\line \expndtw5 systems. To this end, there is a great deal of room for experimentation\line \expndtw13 with small systems that provide the essential behavior of the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 system. Threaded interpreters offer simplicity and speed,\line \expndtw6 and it shouldn't be difficult to capture the essence of message sending\line \expndtw5 in an efficient manner.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1667\margrsxn1310\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1483\colsr781\colno2\colw6667 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-313\shpright8943\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Maintaining\line \expndtw2 an Evolving\line \expndtw0 Integrated\line System}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have had considerable experience maintaining an evolving integrat-\line \expndtw3 ed system. In this section we cover several of the challenges and our so-\line \expndtw5 lutions which support the Smalltalk approach to software engineering.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1656\margrsxn1310\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb338\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1656\margrsxn1310\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1299\colsr965\colno2\colw6678 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Applying the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Smalltalk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Philosophy}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One way of stating the Smalltalk philosophy is to "choose a small num-\line \expndtw9 ber of general principles and apply them uniformly." This approach\line \expndtw4 has somewhat of a recursive thrust, for it implies that once you've built\line \expndtw2 something, you ought to be using it whenever possible. For instance, the\line \expndtw5 conventional approach to altering such kernel code as the text editor of\line \expndtw7 a programming system is to use off-line editing tools and then reload\line \expndtw5 the system with the new code and try it out. By contrast, Smalltalk's\line \expndtw2 incremental compilation and accessibility of kernel code encourages you\line \expndtw7 to make the change while the system is running, a bit like performing\line \expndtw3 an appendectomy on yourself.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The recursive approach offers significant advantages, but it also\line \expndtw2 poses its own special problems. One of the benefits is that system main-\line \expndtw6 tainers are always using the system, so they are highly motivated to\line \expndtw3 produce quality. Another benefit is high productivity, deriving from the\line \expndtw2 elimination of conventional loading and system generation cycles. Con-\line \expndtw8 sistent with the Smalltalk philosophy as articulated above, things are}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1434\margrsxn1543\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8716 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 23}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri32\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Maintaining an Evolving Integrated System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri32\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 also simpler; the tools and the task are one, so there are fewer versions\line \expndtw4 to worry about. The complementary side of this characteristic is that if\line the only version is compromised, you are "down the creek without a\line \expndtw1 paddle."}\par \pard \plain \li2228\ri0\sb65\sa0{\pict\picw11823\pich7124\picwgoal6703\pichgoal4039\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1030\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa634 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 2.4}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1434\margrsxn1590\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1368\colsr860\colno2\colw6656 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Snapshot\line \expndtw-6 Concept}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Alto had a particularly nice characteristic as a personal machine:\line \expndtw2 being based on a removable disk pack, once you had installed your per-\line \expndtw3 sonal pack, any machine you used behaved as your personal machine.\line \expndtw5 When we built the Smalltalk environment, based on an extensible pro-\line gramming language, we arranged the system so that when you termi-\line \expndtw6 nated a working session, or \i quit, \i0 the entire state of your system was\line saved as a \i snapshot \i0 on the disk. This meant that as Smalltalk came to\line \expndtw4 be a stand-alone environment, containing all the capabilities of most\line \expndtw5 operating systems as well as the personal extensions of its owner, any\line \expndtw6 Alto instantly took on that specialized power as soon as you inserted\line \expndtw5 your disk and \i resumed \i0 your working session. The snapshot also served\line \expndtw4 as a useful checkpoint in case of fatal errors.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the later virtual memory systems, OOZE automatically saved a\line \expndtw2 snapshot from time to time, which could subsequently be resumed fol-\line \expndtw3 lowing catastrophes such as loss of power or fatal programming errors.\line \expndtw5 The robustness of OOZE in this respect was remarkable, but owing to\line the finite latency period of the checkpointing process, it was necessary\line \expndtw3 to act quickly when fatal errors were recognized, lest they be enshrined\line \expndtw8 forever in the mausoleum of a snapshot. In such circumstances, the\line \expndtw5 alert user would quickly reach around to the rear of the keyboard and\line press the "boot" button of the Alto before the next automatic snapshot.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1810\margrsxn1170\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4540 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 24{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa245 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1810\margrsxn1170\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1857\colsr403\colno2\colw6667 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb1444\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Minimum Kernel\line \expndtw8 for Maximum\line \expndtw5 Flexibility}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft11\shptop2905\shpright1905\shpbottom2905\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb3089\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Fear of\line \expndtw0 Standing A lone}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop3427\shpright1897\shpbottom3427\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb3607\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Standing Alone\line \expndtw2 Without Fear: The\line \expndtw3 System Tracer}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Then he could resume his work from a previous state saved a few min-\line \expndtw5 utes before. This process was known as "booting and resuming." The\line \expndtw3 term came to be jokingly applied to other situations in life, such as un-\line successful research efforts and other less serious endeavors.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri4\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Most systems are built around a kernel of code which cannot easily be\line \expndtw3 changed. In our Smalltalk systems, the kernel consists of machine code\line \expndtw7 and microcode necessary to implement a virtual Smalltalk machine.\line \expndtw3 You clearly want the kernel to be as small as possible, so that you en-\line \expndtw4 counter barriers to change as infrequently as possible. For example, in\line Smalltalk-72 it was a great improvement when the primitive read rou-\line tine was supplanted by one written in Smalltalk, since it could then be\line easily changed to handle such extensions as floating-point constants.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Speed comes into play here, because if the kernel is not fast enough,\line \expndtw4 it will not support certain functions being implemented at a higher lev-\line \expndtw3 el. This was the case for text display in Smalltalk-76. Similarly, gener-\line \expndtw5 ality is important, for the more general the kernel is, the more kernel-\line \expndtw2 like functions can be built at a higher level. For example, the one BitBIt\line \expndtw9 primitive in Smalltalk-80 supports line drawing, text, menus and\line \expndtw3 freehand graphics.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 While Smalltalk-72 and \lang1032 -74 \lang1033 were used as long-lived evolving images,\line \expndtw4 the systems as released were always generated from scratch, by reading\line \expndtw14 a set of system definitions into a bootstrap kernel. With the\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk-76 system, we took a bold step and opted to ignore support\line \expndtw3 for system generation. The system was built in two parts: a Virtual Ma-\line \expndtw4 chine was written in microcode and machine code, and a virtual image\line was cross-compiled from a simulation done in Smalltalk-74. Although\line \expndtw7 this paralleled our previous strategy, we knew that we would soon\line \expndtw6 abandon support for Smalltalk-74, and thus the Smalltalk-76 system\line \expndtw5 would be truly stand-alone. In other words, if a bit were dropped from\line \expndtw3 the system image, or if a reference-count error occurred, there would be\line no way to recover the state of the system except to backtrack through\line whatever earlier versions of the system had been saved. As the system\line \expndtw5 became more reliable, we went for days and then weeks without start-\line \expndtw6 ing over, and finally we realized that Smalltalk-76 was on its own. If\line \expndtw3 this sounds risky to you, think of how we felt!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 While the foregoing approach may seem foolhardy, we actually had a\line plan: Ted Kaehler said that he would write a Smalltalk program, the\line \i\expndtw1 system tracer, \i0 which would run inside of Smalltalk and copy the whole\line \expndtw3 system out to a file while it was running. Considerable attention would\line \expndtw6 have to be paid to the parts of the system which were changing while\line \expndtw7 the process ran. Two months after the launch of Smalltalk-76, Ted's\line first system tracer ran and produced a clone without errors. While we}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2415\margrsxn1466\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3935\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7841 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-24\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 25}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Maintaining an Evolving Integrated System}\par \pard \plain \li940\ri515\sb205\sa0{\pict\picw11595\pich12255\picwgoal6573\pichgoal6948\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 2.5}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1372\fi0\ri4\sb277\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 all breathed a sigh of relief at this point, the full implications only\line dawned on us gradually. This truly marked the beginning of an era:\line \expndtw4 there are many bits in the Smalltalk-80 release of today which are cop-\line \expndtw2 ies of those bits first cloned in \lang1032 1977.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1368\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The system tracer solved our most immediate problem of ensuring\line \expndtw6 the integrity of the system. It caught and diagnosed inaccurate refer-\line \expndtw2 ence counts, of which there were several during the first few months of\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-76. Also, although it took four hours to run, it performed the\line \expndtw3 function of a garbage collector, reclaiming storage tied up in circular\line structures, and reclaiming pointers lost to OOZE's zoning by class. The\line essential contribution of the system tracer, however, was to validate our\line \expndtw5 test-pilot philosophy of living in the system we worked on. From this\line \expndtw3 point on, we never started from scratch again, but were able to use the\line \expndtw2 system we knew so well in order to design its follow-ons.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1722\margrsxn1241\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4561 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex109\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 26{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex109\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \li2329\ri14\sb317\sa0{\pict\picw11645\pich6032\picwgoal6602\pichgoal3420\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa346 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 2.6}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1722\margrsxn1241\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1461\colsr767\colno2\colw6717 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Spawning and\line \expndtw-3 Mutation}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex109\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 As time passed we found that the system tracer had even more poten-\line \expndtw5 tial than we had imagined. For one thing, it offered an answer to the\line \expndtw4 problem of using a fully integrated system for production applications.\line This problem manifests itself in several ways: a fully integrated system\line contains many components which are not needed in production, such as\line \expndtw3 compiler, debugger, and various editing and communications facilities.\line \expndtw5 Also, at a finer grain, much of the symbolic information which is re-\line \expndtw1 tained for ease of access may be wasteful, or even objectionable (for se-\line \expndtw5 curity reasons) in a production release of the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi234\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex109\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The system tracer could be instructed to \i spawn \i0 an application with\line all unnecessary information removed. This could be done \i post facto,\line \i0\expndtw5 thus freeing application programmers from the integrated/production\line dichotomy until the final release of a product. In actual fact, since the\line \expndtw7 goal of our research is integration, we never pursued the full potential\line \expndtw6 of the system tracer to drop out such "product" applications. The clos-\line \expndtw3 est we came was to eliminate unnecessary parts of the system when we\line \expndtw4 were short of space for certain large projects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri29\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex109\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The possibility of using the system tracer to produce mutations be-\line \expndtw3 came evident soon after its creation, and we took full advantage of this.\line \expndtw7 For instance, prior to the Smalltalk-80 release, we wanted to convert\line \expndtw6 from our own private form of floating-point numbers to the standard\line \expndtw4 IEEE format. In this case, we simply included an appropriate transfor-\line \expndtw7 mation in the system tracer and wrote out a cloned image which used\line the new format. Then we replaced the floating-point routines in the\line \expndtw8 Virtual Machine and started up the new image. Similar transforma-\line \expndtw6 tions have been used to change the instruction set of the Virtual Ma-\line \expndtw5 chine, to change the format of compiled methods, and to change the\line encoding of small integers. It was in this manner that Smalltalk-78 and\line \expndtw3\lang1032 -80 \lang1033 were built out of Smalltalk-76.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1420\margrsxn1543\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7906\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8762 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 27}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Conclusion}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri43\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is hard to say how far one should take this approach. Sometimes a\line \expndtw11 change is so fundamental that it requires starting again from the\line \expndtw7 ground up, as we did from Smalltalk-74 to \lang1032 -76. \lang1033 Even in such cases\line \expndtw8 though, it seems easiest to simulate the new environment in the old,\line \expndtw5 and then use the simulation to produce the actual new system.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2284\margrsxn5211\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4874\posnegy652\absw0\absh3824\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw5486\pich6743\picwgoal3110\pichgoal3823\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \li1577\ri0\sb292\sa0{\pict\picw5003\pich3340\picwgoal2836\pichgoal1893\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \li1879\ri115\sb410\sa0{\pict\picw4267\pich3289\picwgoal2419\pichgoal1864\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa234 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 2.7}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1434\margrsxn1583\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1864\colsr371\colno2\colw6656 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Virtual Image}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When we decided to release the Smalltalk-80 system, the question arose\line as to what form it should take. From the discussion above, it should be\line \expndtw4 clear why we chose the virtual image (a fancier term for snapshot) for-\line \expndtw5 mat. This was the one way in which we could be sure that the release\line \expndtw7 would set a standard. Any implementation, if it worked at all, would\line \expndtw5 look and behave identically, at least in its initial version. At the same\line time, we tried to decouple the image format as much as possible from\line such implementation-related details as reference counting versus gar-\line \expndtw2 bage collection, machine word size, and pointer size. At the time of this\line \expndtw4 writing, implementations exist which vary in all of these parameters. It\line \expndtw1 should be possible to decouple similarly our choice of bitmap display\line \expndtw5 representation, but this project was not of immediate interest to us.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1420\margrsxn1575\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb929\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1420\margrsxn1575\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1328\colsr929\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-245\shpright8921\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusion}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb68\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The evolution of the Smalltalk system is a story of good and bad de-\line \expndtw3 signs alike. We have learned much from our experiences. Probably the\line \expndtw5 greatest factor that keeps us moving forward is that we use the system\line all the time, and we keep trying to do new things with it. It is this "liv-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1884\margrsxn1111\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4522 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 28{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Evolution of the Smalltalk Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ing-with"    which    drives    us    to    root    out    failures,    to    clean    up\line \expndtw1 inconsistencies, and which inspires our occasional innovation.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop1069\shpright8899\shpbottom1069\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1390\sa0\tx2509 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 References{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\charscalex112\expndtw4\lang1032 1. \b0\lang1033 Goldberg, Adele, and Kay, Alan, Eds., "Smalltalk-72 Instruction}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Manual", Xerox PARC Technical Report SSL-76-6, \lang1032 1976.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Thacker, C. P., et al., "Alto: A Personal Computer", in \i Computer\line Structures: Readings and Examples, \i0\lang1032 2nd \lang1033 Edition, Eds. Sieworek,\line \expndtw-2 Bell,  and  Newell,   McGraw-Hill,   New  York,   \lang1032 1981;  \lang1033 (also  Xerox\line \expndtw-6 PARC CSL-79-11), Aug. \lang1032 1979.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent260\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Papert, Seymour, \i Mindstorms, \i0 Basic Books, New York, \lang1032 1980.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Goldberg,  Adele,  and  Kay, Alan,  "Teaching Smalltalk",  Xerox\line \expndtw-1 PARC Technical Report SSL-77-2, June \lang1032 1977.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\