dtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 McCall,   Kim,   "TinyTalk,   a   Subset   of   Smalltalk-76   for   \lang1032 64KB\line \expndtw0\lang1033 Microcomputers", \i Sigsmall Newsletter, \i0 Sept. \lang1032 1980.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  9.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Borning, Alan H., and Ingalls, Daniel \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 . \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 "A Type Declaration\line \expndtw2 and Inference System for Smalltalk", Ninth Symposium on Princi-\line \expndtw3 ples of Programming Languages, pp. \lang1032 133-141, \lang1033 Albuquerque, NM,\line \expndtw-12\lang1032 1982.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2743\fi-342\ri25\sb119\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tlul\tx6836 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 10. {\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw8 , \lang1033 "Multiple Inheri-\line \expndtw2 tance in Smalltalk-80", pp. \lang1032 234-237, \lang1033 Proceedings at the National\line \expndtw1 Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Pittsburgh, PA, \lang1032 1982.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn799\margrsxn1368\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr104\colno2\colw8917 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5015\shptop-302\shpright9745\shpbottom-302\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb10534\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn7\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs42 L}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-439\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 The Smalltalk-80 Code\line \expndtw11 File Format}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4205\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Glenn Krasner}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4187\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Software Concepts Group\line \expndtw0 Xerox Palo Alto Research Center\line Palo Alto, California}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop1890\shpright8907\shpbottom1890\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2200\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2254 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Introduction{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw5 In the Smalltalk-80 system, programmers define classes and methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 incrementally by editing in system browsers. At some point, program-\line mers may want to share their class descriptions with others using dif-\line \expndtw-1 ferent Smalltalk-80 systems. Some means of communication is therefore\line \expndtw2 required. We have chosen to use files as the means of communication,\line \expndtw0 and call such files \i code files.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Code files for the Smalltalk-80 system allow the programmer to com-\line municate source code (class descriptions or parts of class descriptions)\line \expndtw2 between one Smalltalk-80 system and another (possibly the same sys-\line \expndtw-1 tem, later in time). The format of such files was devised as a result of a\line \expndtw0 number of design considerations:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2470\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\tx2671 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2470\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\tx2671 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Restrictions on the allowable character set,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2671\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2671 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2671\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2671 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Whether to allow the code to be "readable" when printed on con-\line \expndtw3 ventional printers,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2671\fi-202\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2671 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2671\fi-202\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2671 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Whether to retain/allow more than one emphasis of the text (font\line \expndtw-1 and face changes),}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2671\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2671 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2671\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2671 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Whether to include source code in the system image (core) or to\line \expndtw1 place it on external files,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri169\sb97\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Xerox Corporation \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb40\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 29}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1747\margrsxn1197\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3337 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 30{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Code File Format}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-198\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-198\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whether to allow for executable expressions in addition to source\line \expndtw1 code of methods, and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2552\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2552\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whether to provide for system crash recovery.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2315\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa947\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 code file format is restricted to contain a common set\line of characters ("printing" ASCII characters plus a small number of con-\line \expndtw8 trol characters). It gives up the ability to have multiple emphases of\line \expndtw7 text in order to be as good (or as bad) on conventional printers as it\line would be on more capable printers (raster laser xerographic printers,\line \expndtw4 for example). The approach taken keeps source methods on file storage\line \expndtw3 to minimize the amount of core used, includes intermingling of execut-\line \expndtw2 able expressions with source code for methods, and provides for some\line amount of system recovery.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1805\margrsxn1197\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1476\colsr767\colno2\colw6663 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-252\shpright8917\shpbottom-252\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Background}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 system is powerful and comprehensible, in part be-\line \expndtw6 cause everything in the system is treated in a uniform way, as an ob-\line \expndtw4 ject. However, at the point where the Smalltalk-80 system meets the\line \expndtw8 external world, this uniformity cannot be maintained. The external\line \expndtw1 world does not consist of Smalltalk-80 objects, but rather of disk files,\line \expndtw4 network communication, hardware storage devices, and printers. These\line \expndtw3 define a much more limited structure of information. For example, disk\line \expndtw1 files typically consist of collections of 8-bit bytes; networks define a lim-\line \expndtw3 ited set of data that can be communicated; hardware storage devices de-\line \expndtw4 fine what kinds and, more importantly, how much information can be\line \expndtw6 handled; and printers typically are restricted to a small number of\line \expndtw5 characters as the only data they can handle. If the Smalltalk-80 pro-\line \expndtw7 grammer never had to meet this external world, there would be no\line \expndtw11 problem. Everything he or she dealt with would be within the\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 system. For some programmers, this is sufficient. Most\line \expndtw8 programmers, however, must meet the external world because they\line \expndtw6 want to share information with some other system. The Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw5 code file format is a format for representing code objects (i.e., source\line \expndtw1 code for methods or expressions), especially for communication via the\line \expndtw3 media of electronic secondary storage and paper.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1747\margrsxn1259\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1015\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1747\margrsxn1259\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1371\colsr886\colno2\colw6645 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-295\shpright8910\shpbottom-295\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Constraints\line \expndtw-2 and Goals}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The constraints and goals for the design of the format consisted of:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li439\fi-205\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'95\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161   \lang1033 Having a format that would serve as a communications protocol\line \expndtw6 among Smalltalk-80 systems and from a system to paper. We saw}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1429\margrsxn1544\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8744 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 31}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Code File Format}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2718\fi0\ri18\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 the paper version as being used by people not fortunate enough to\line \expndtw5 have display-based browsers and, perhaps more importantly, as\line \expndtw0 one in which algorithms or pieces of code could be published.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2714\fi0\ri7\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Having a format that could be printed without translation on con-\line \expndtw7 ventional printers. We are interested in first having the world\line \expndtw0 build Smalltalk engines, with bit-map displays and good pointing\line \expndtw-1 devices and lots of computing power. Later, we \i expect \i0 them to want\line \expndtw1 to use good printers; we are willing to wait before we require them\line \expndtw-5 to do so.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2714\fi0\ri14\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Having a format that would look satisfactory on paper. Often this\line \expndtw0 means allowing multiple font and type face information, as well as\line \expndtw4 formatting information. However, given the first goals, it means\line \expndtw2 not having extra characters that describe such information clutter-\line ing up the printed page.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2707\fi0\ri4\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Having the source code for the more than \lang1032 4000 \lang1033 methods in the sys-\line \expndtw2 tem take up as little main memory as possible. We expect that\line \expndtw5 reasonable Smalltalk-80 machines will have virtual memories,\line \expndtw2 where space restrictions would not be a problem. However, the\line \expndtw1 number of bytes taken up by the source code alone (more than a\line \expndtw0 million) would preclude most current machines from having \i any\line \i0\expndtw3 resident implementation. Therefore the file format was to serve as\line \expndtw1 a somewhat limited form of virtual memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2704\fi0\ri18\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Having a format that would include executable expressions. Typi-\line cally what people want to communicate between systems includes\line method definitions, class descriptions, and class initialization ex-\line \expndtw-1 pressions. By providing for executable expressions, all these desires\line \expndtw1 can be met.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2700\fi0\ri18\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Minimizing the amount of disk space taken up. For example, one\line would not want an entire disk system to become full of text when\line \expndtw2 only a small percentage of the text is actually needed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2693\fi0\ri18\sb115\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Allowing a certain amount of recovery from system disasters. The\line Smalltalk-80 system is one in which it is possible to change almost\line \expndtw3 anything. This provides great power and flexibility, but has the\line \expndtw1 danger that a system change could destroy the system itself. It was\line \expndtw4 desired that code files, since they are somewhat external to the\line \expndtw0 system itself, could provide recovery from such disasters.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop673\shpright8914\shpbottom673\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb983\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Code{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw6 Smalltalk-80 code files are defined as text files that contain only the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 File Format{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 "printing" ASCII characters, codes \lang1032 32 \lang1033 (space) through \lang1032 127 \lang1033 (DEL), plus}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 carriage return, line feed, tab and form feed (codes \lang1032 13, 10, 9 \lang1033 and \lang1032 12, \lang1033 re-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1854\margrsxn1142\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3326 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 32{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Code File Format}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri4\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 spectively). The file is broken into \i chunks, \i0 which are sequences of char-\line acters terminated by an exclamation point \lang1032 (!), \lang1033 with exclamation points\line \expndtw3 within a chunk doubled. The meanings of chunks will be described in\line \expndtw-1 the sections to follow.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Exclamation point was chosen for a number of reasons. It is not used\line \expndtw2 as a Smalltalk-80 selector. It is used in English at the end of sentences,\line \expndtw5 so it is a reasonable terminator. It tends not to appear in code, so it\line \expndtw-1 rarely has to be doubled. Also, it is thin, so we felt it would not be too\line \expndtw1 obtrusive on the page.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop630\shpright8914\shpbottom630\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb947\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 File Streams{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex112\expndtw1 The interface between Smalltalk-80 objects and disk files is encapsulat-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ed by the Smalltalk-80 class FileStream. File Streams support sequen-\line \expndtw1 tial and random access of disk files through the following protocol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri104\sb162\sa0\tx2988 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 class name{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6 FileStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa0\tx5483 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 superclass{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3 External Stream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 opening and closing}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 openOn: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Answer  an   instance  of  FileStream   representing  the  disk  \expndtw19 file\expndtw9   named\line \expndtw5 aString.\line \b\expndtw-4\fs20 close}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2977\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Close the file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi-475\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 sequential accessing\line \b\expndtw1 next}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Answer the character in the file after the current position, and update the\line \expndtw-3 position.\line \b\expndtw1\fs20 nextPut: aCharacter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Store aCharacter as the character of the file after the current position,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 and update the position.\line \b\expndtw-2\fs20 atEnd}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2722\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Answer true if the current position is at the end of the file, false if it is not.\line \b\expndtw-3\fs20 setToEnd}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2966\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Set the current position to be the end of the file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-479\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 random accessing\line \b\expndtw-5 position}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Answer the current position.\line \b\fs20 position: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2970\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Set the current position to be anlnteger.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2254\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Two messages were added to FileStream to deal with chunks, one to\line \expndtw4 read a chunk from the file, and another to write a chunk onto the file}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn799\margrsxn1444\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6307\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx9482 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex121\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 33}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex121\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Code Files Used for Source Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3218\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 fileln/Out}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3460\fi0\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 nextChunk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3463\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Answer  a   string   that   is  the   chunk   starting   at  the   current  position,\line \expndtw1 undoubling embedded exclamation points, and excluding the terminator.\line \b\expndtw-1\fs20 nextChunkPut: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3701\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1188\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Write aString as a chunk starting at the current position, doubling embed-\line \expndtw1 ded exclamation points, and terminating it with an exclamation point.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1526\margrsxn1487\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1224\colsr1008\colno2\colw6663 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-317\shpright8906\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Code Files\line \expndtw-3 Used for\line \expndtw-4 Source\line \expndtw-5 Methods}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 system relies on the code file format to read and\line \expndtw6 write the source methods of the system. In this way, the files are used\line \expndtw4 to communicate information from the system to itself at a later point in\line \expndtw8 time. Each CompiledMethod in the system keeps a short (3-byte)\line \expndtw3 encoding of the location of its source. This encoding includes which of\line \expndtw4 four file streams contains the chunk of source code for that method and\line \expndtw5 the position, within that file, of the first character of its chunk. A glob-\line \expndtw4 al array called SourceFiles points to the four file streams.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The code for getting the source method in class CompiledMethod\line \expndtw-4 could be*}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 getSource}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li716\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 | sourcelndex sourceFile sourcePosition |\line \expndtw2 sourcelndex \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \lang1033 self sourcelndex.\line \expndtw3 sourceFile \lang1032 - \lang1033 SourceFiles at: sourcelndex.\line \expndtw1 sourcePosition \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \lang1033 self sourcePosition.\line \expndtw0 sourceFile position: sourcePosition.\line \expndtw1 tsourceFile nextChunk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Of the four file streams, one, SourceFiles at: \lang1032 1, \lang1033 is a read-only file\line \expndtw5 stream of source methods. It is known as the \i sources file. \i0 Periodically\line \expndtw3 all the source code of the system may be condensed into this file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The second file stream, SourceFiles at: \lang1032 2, \lang1033 is a file stream to which ev-\line \expndtw4 ery change to the system sources is appended. It is known as the \i chang-\line \expndtw5 es file. \i0 When a method is changed and recompiled, its new source code\line \expndtw3 is appended to this file (and the encoding bytes of the CompiledMethod\line \expndtw5 are changed to point at the new source). Periodically, the changes file\line \expndtw2 may be condensed as well. Since this file grows while a programmer is\line \expndtw4 working, the changes file may want to be condensed much more often\line \expndtw6 than the sources file. The third and fourth file streams are currently\line \expndtw1 unused.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb241\sa0\sl-173\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 *For implementation efficiency, the code in the Smalltalk-80 system is not actually this,\line \expndtw1 but provides the same function.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn799\margrsxn1444\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb594\sa0\sl-454\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn11\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs62 L}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1667\margrsxn1329\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3337 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 34{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Code File Format}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi234\ri0\sb266\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The code in class CompiledMethodto store a new source code string\line \expndtw-1 could be}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2747\fi0\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 storeSourceString: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2992\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 | sourceFile sourcePosition |\line \expndtw1 sourceFile \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 SourceFiles at: \lang1032 2.\line \expndtw-1\lang1033 sourceFile setToEnd.\line sourcePosition \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 sourceFile position,\line \expndtw1 self setSourcelndex: \lang1032 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2992\fi349\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 sourcePosition: sourcePosition.\line \expndtw-1 sourceFile nextChunkPut: aString.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because the changes file is only altered by appending to it, previous\line \expndtw3 versions can always be found. Periodically, a Smalltalk-80 programmer\line \expndtw4 will make a \i snapshot \i0 of the state of the system. If the system crashes at\line a later time, then, when the system is resumed at the point of snapshot,\line \expndtw5 the compiled methods in the snapshot of the system will still point to\line \expndtw7 their place in the files at the time the snapshot was made. The end of\line \expndtw4 the file will contain changes added between the snapshot and the crash,\line \expndtw9 and these can be recovered. For example, suppose a programmer\line \expndtw1 changed the definition of getSource in class CompiledMethod once (ver-\line \expndtw7 sion A), then made a snapshot, then changed it twice (versions \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 and\line \expndtw6 C), and the system crashed because of an error in C. Then the system\line \expndtw4 can be restarted at the snapshot point, the source and compiled method\line \expndtw5 for getSource will be version A, but versions \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 and C will be near the\line \expndtw3 end of the changes file. The programmer could look at the end of the\line \expndtw4 changes file, copy version \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 into the browser for compilation, and ig-\line \expndtw5 nore C. Then the programmer has recovered to the equivalent of just\line \expndtw4 before the C change brought down the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To improve recovery, the system also records several other things on\line \expndtw5 the changes file. For example, whenever the user executes an expres-\line \expndtw7 sion in a code editor, the expression is appended as a chunk on the\line \expndtw5 changes file. Also, when the user performs a system snapshot, a chunk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tldot\tx5404\tldot\tx6876 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 consisting of the comment \lang1032 "{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1\lang1033 SNAPSHOT{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw13\lang1032 " \lang1033 is appended to the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1177\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 changes file, marking the occurrence of a snapshot.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1667\margrsxn1340\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1515\colsr742\colno2\colw6645 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-310\shpright8910\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Code Files\line \expndtw-1 Used for\line \expndtw-5 Shared Code}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Besides storing the system sources, the code file format serves to com-\line \expndtw6 municate code and expressions between one system and another. For\line \expndtw7 this we have added a level of syntax and semantics above the chunk\line \expndtw5 level. The syntax and semantics are defined and implemented by the\line \expndtw0 message fileln in class FileStream,}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3602\margrsxn1655\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1904\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6469 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 35}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Code Files Used for Shared Codes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li454\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li702\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 | aString sawExclamation |\line \expndtw0 self position: \lang1032 0.\line \lang1033 [self atEnd]\line \expndtw-2 whileFalse:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [sawExclamation \lang1032 <- \lang1033 self peekFor: \lang1032 $!.\line \expndtw5\lang1033 aString \lang1032 - \lang1033 self nextChunk.\line \expndtw-2 sawExclamation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ifFalse: [Compiler evaluate: aString]\line \expndtw1 ifTrue: [(Compiler evaluate: aString) filelnFrom: self]]\line \b\fs20 peekFor: aCharacter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li720\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 "Answer true and move past if next \lang1032 = \lang1033 aCharacter"\line self next \lang1032 = \lang1033 aCharacter\line \expndtw-3 ifTrue: [Ttrue]\line \expndtw1 ifFalse: [self position: self position \lang1032 - 1. \lang1033 Tfalse]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 That is, when sent the message fileln, a file stream looks for an excla-\line mation point. As long as it does not see one, it reads and has class Com-\line \expndtw4 piler evaluate the next chunk as a Smalltalk-80 expression. If it did see\line \expndtw7 an exclamation point, then after the expression is evaluated, it hands\line \expndtw4 the stream (itself) to the object that was returned as the value of the\line \expndtw0 expression (see example below).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In particular, the methodsFor: aString message sent to a class will re-\line \expndtw4 turn an instance of ClassCategoryReader that has its instance variable\line \expndtw2 myClass set to the class, and its instance variable myCategory set to the\line string. The class category reader will respond to the filelnFrom: message\line \expndtw5 by reading chunks from the file stream. Each chunk is presumed to be\line \expndtw4 the source for a method, and, for each chunk, the class category reader\line \expndtw8 has the compiler compile it and install the compiled method in the\line \expndtw9 proper class and category. This continues until an empty chunk is\line \expndtw-4 found.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li475\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 filelnFrom: aFileStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li727\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 | aString |}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li950\fi-227\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [aFileStream atEnd or: [(aString \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \lang1033 aFileStream nextChunk) isEmpty]]\line whileFalse:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li731\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [Compiler compile: aString\line \expndtw0 forClass: myClass\line \expndtw-1 inCategory: myCategory]\line \expndtw-8 Tself}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri25\sb310\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, the code for fileln, peekFor: aCharacter, and filelnFrom:\line \expndtw3 aFileStream would appear in a code file as}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1494\margrsxn407\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw8913\colsr374\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3326 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 36{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Code File Format}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2696\fi0\ri0\sb288\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 IFileStream methodsFor: \b0\lang1032 ' \b\lang1033 filel/O \b0\lang1032 '!\line \b\expndtw-2\lang1033 fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2941\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 | aString sawExclamation |\line \expndtw0 self position: \lang1032 0.\line \lang1033 [self atEnd]\line \expndtw-1 whileFalse:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3420\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [sawExclamation ^- self peekFor: \lang1032 $"\line \expndtw0\lang1033 aString \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 self nextChunk.\line \expndtw-2 sawExclamation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2704\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ifFalse: [Compiler evaluate: aString]\line \expndtw0 ifTrue: [(Compiler evaluate: aString) filelnFrom: self]]\super 1\line \nosupersub\b\expndtw5\fs16 peekFor: aCharacter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2945\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw14\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 "Answer true and move past if next \lang1032 = \lang1033 aCharacter"\line \expndtw10 self next \i\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \i0\lang1033 aCharacter\line \expndtw6 ifTrue: [Ttrue]\line \expndtw7 ifFalse: [self position: self position \lang1032 - 1. \expndtw21\lang1033 Tfalse]!!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2707\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 ICIassCategoryReader methodsFor: \lang1032 ' \lang1033 filel/O\lang1032 '!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2696\fi0\ri0\sb173\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 filelnFrom: aFileStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3056\fi0\ri0\sb36\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 aString |}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3175\fi-230\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [aFileStream atEnd or: [(aString \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \lang1033 aFileStream nextChunk) isEmpty]]\line \expndtw-1 whileFalse:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2959\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [Compiler compile: aString\line \expndtw0 forClass: myClass\line \expndtw-1 inCategory: myCategory]\line \expndtw-17 TselfM}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The class category reader created by the expression FileStream\line \expndtw2 methodsFor: 'filel/O' will read and compile the methods for fileln and\line \expndtw7 peekFor: aCharacter before returning control to the file stream; the\line \expndtw3 reader created by ClassCategoryReader methodsFor: \expndtw27 'filel/O'\expndtw3  will read\line \expndtw1 in only the method for filelnFrom: aFileStream.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi241\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The example shows a number of visual properties that make it easier\line \expndtw8 to read code format files when they are printed. Each category is\line \expndtw7 delimited by a short "methodsFor:" line; it is easy to locate the names\line \expndtw6 of the class and the category for each. Each method is a visual chunk\line \expndtw3 with all but its first line indented. This of course, depends on the pro-\line \expndtw7 grammer keeping the convention of including a tab before each line\line \expndtw5 other than the message pattern. This convention is supported by the\line \expndtw4 "format" command in the code editor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition to the source code for methods, the code file format and\line \expndtw4 its interpretation allow any expression to be saved on a file. In particu-\line \expndtw5 lar, one may save an entire class description from a Smalltalk-80 sys-\line \expndtw4 tem. That file will consist of an expression defining the class, followed\line \expndtw7 by an expression setting the global comment of the class, followed by}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb12150\sa0\sl-310\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn7\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs46 L}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1083\margrsxn1912\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3301\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8694 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 37}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3316\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Code File Format for Both Source Methods and Shared Code}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the source code for the methods in the class, in the format described\line above. When a file stream on such a file, in this or some other system,\line \expndtw3 is told to fileln, it will recreate the entire class description.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Users could also define their own "readers," objects created with ex-\line \expndtw22 pressions preceded by an exclamation point, just as\line \expndtw3 ClassCategoryReaders are created with the "methodsFor:" expressions.\line \expndtw4 These user-defined readers would be used for creating an object with\line \expndtw5 special external representation, just as class category readers create\line \expndtw4 methods whose external representations are strings of characters for-\line matted for readability.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi241\ri7\sb0\sa1192\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note: In addition to this, since many classes have initialization code\line \expndtw3 especially for class variables, if the message initialize is defined in the\line class, the expression chunk \lang1032 " < \lang1033 class \lang1032 > \lang1033 initialize!" will appear at the end\line \expndtw4 of the file containing the class definition. Then, when the file is told to\line file in, the initialization code will be executed after the methods are\line \expndtw-3 compiled.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1083\margrsxn1930\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1537\colsr698\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-310\shpright8907\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Code File\line Format for\line \expndtw-1 Both Source\line \expndtw-4 Methods and\line Shared Code}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The conventions for shared code given in the previous section are also\line \expndtw4 followed in the two source code files. That is, the sources file is orga-\line \expndtw2 nized as a sequence of class descriptions in the above format, arranged\line alphabetically by class name, with form feeds between them. The com-\line \expndtw5 piled methods in the system point to the beginning of their chunks. In\line \expndtw3 this way, printing out the sources file will give an alphabetical listing of\line the system sources with page breaks between each class.\line The changes file is a collection of chunks of the form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi0\ri0\sb90\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 ! < \lang1033 class name \lang1032 > \lang1033 methodsFor: \lang1032 ' < \lang1033 category Name \lang1032 > '!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\tx659 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 <{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2\lang1033 source method \lang1032 > !!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The compiled methods in the system that point to the changes file also\line \expndtw7 only point to the beginning of their chunks. The chunks appended to\line \expndtw4 the changes file when the user evaluates an expression are in the exe-\line \expndtw3 cutable form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx659 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 <{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-10\lang1033 expression \lang1032 >!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This means that the sources file could be filed in to redefine the system\line \expndtw6 classes to their state before changes were made, and the changes file\line \expndtw2 could be filed in to redefine the changes and re-execute the expressions\line made since changes were started. The Smalltalk-80 system provides two\line \expndtw5 ways of looking at the changes file for recovery, to create a view on a\line \expndtw4 file whose contents are the last few thousand characters of the changes\line \expndtw3 file and to create a ChangeList browser to view the changes.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1848\margrsxn1151\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3319 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 38{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa594 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Code File Format}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1848\margrsxn1151\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1602\colsr641\colno2\colw6667 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-317\shpright8914\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Matching the\line \expndtw-2 Goals and\line \expndtw-3 Constraints}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 code file format meets most of the goals and satisfies\line most of the constraints of its design. It serves as a communications for-\line \expndtw4 mat between systems. It is used extensively, not only for source code\line within a system, but also for exchanging source code between systems.\line \expndtw5 In addition, because it is restricted to the standard ASCII character set,\line \expndtw3 it can be printed on conventional printers as well as on more capable\line \expndtw4 printers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi230\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The code files have a reasonable appearance on paper. Of course, this\line \expndtw2 is a matter of taste. In a few years we will likely no longer consider its\line appearance reasonable because our opinions of "reasonable appearance"\line \expndtw5 will change. Methods are "chunked" together, and the "methodsFor:"\line \expndtw3 lines separate categories of methods. Except for the single exclamation\line \expndtw5 point at the end of each method, there are no characters to distract the\line \expndtw4 reader. The form feeds between class descriptions also help the read-\line \expndtw2 ability of the code.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The amount of main memory required to handle sources is quite\line \expndtw1 small, especially compared with the size of the source code itself. It re-\line \expndtw7 quires only \lang1032 3 \lang1033 bytes per compiled method, plus the space taken up by\line \expndtw3 the interpretation code (outlined in this paper). Compared with over a\line \expndtw7 million bytes of source code, this is a great savings and considered\line \expndtw3 worth the added complexity.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi234\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The format includes executable expressions. The system currently\line \expndtw7 uses this feature sparingly\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 for class descriptions and initialization,\line \expndtw4 and for recording expressions evaluated by the user. However, the for-\line \expndtw5 mat provides a generality that can be exploited in the future.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Disk space efficiency is a compromise in our design. Having one\line \expndtw5 read-only sources file does help meet this goal since multiple systems\line \expndtw3 can share this file. At Xerox, we often keep this shared file on a remote\line \expndtw4 server, where multiple systems can access it and where it does not take\line up any local disk space. On the other hand, constantly appending to the\line \expndtw3 changes file consumes disk space. The Smalltalk-80 development style\line often involves defining and redefining methods many times. Since each\line \expndtw4 definition is appended to the changes file, this causes the file to grow.\line \expndtw9 However, the user can invoke the system's ability to compact the\line \expndtw4 changes file at any time by sending the message compactChanges to\line \expndtw-1 Smalltalk, an instance of SystemDictionary.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi230\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Although using the changes file in this way is wasteful of disk stor-\line \expndtw3 age, there is the advantage that a considerable amount of recovery from\line \expndtw2 system crashes is possible. By recording all code changes and many oth-\line \expndtw5 er user events on the changes file, the programmer can recover should\line \expndtw7 the system go down. In particular, if a programmer is experimenting\line \expndtw5 with the user interface code and makes a mistake that renders the in-\line \expndtw4 terface unusable, there will be a trail of the experiments when the sys-\line \expndtw6 tem is restarted.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1400\margrsxn1613\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-166\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-15\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 \'97\'97\'97\'97\'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 39\line \expndtw9\lang1033 Other Paths}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop252\shpright8896\shpbottom252\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb569\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2243 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Other Paths{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw2 A couple of other directions in the design of the Smalltalk-80 code file}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 format that we did not take are worth noting. One direction would have\line \expndtw2 been to invent a standard representation for the Smalltalk-80 virtual\line \expndtw1 memory, and insist that everyone's system use it. The advantages with\line \expndtw2 this would have been that the source code would fit in the system and\line \expndtw0 that no special mechanism would be needed to retain system sources.\line \expndtw-1 However, it would not solve the communication problem, nor the recov-\line ery problem.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Another idea we discussed was to store only comments and tempo-\line \expndtw0 rary variable names on the files; the other pieces of text could be gener-\line \expndtw-1 ated by the system, as is currently done by the Decompiler. The benefits\line \expndtw2 here are that fewer characters of disk space are needed and that code\line \expndtw0 would appear in a consistent "pretty-printed" way. We decided that the\line \expndtw3 disadvantages of this approach outweigh its advantages. Although it\line \expndtw1 saves disk space in terms of the number of characters, there would be\line \expndtw2 extra space used (either on disk or in main memory) to describe the\line \expndtw1 mapping between comments and their place in the methods. This map-\line \expndtw-2 ping would also involve a more complex computation. Another major\line \expndtw1 disadvantage is that the code files would not be human readable. Also,\line we did not want to give up the flexibility of storing and displaying the\line \expndtw2 code in the way it was typed, rather than in the pretty-printer's format.\line \expndtw0 Keeping the sources "as is" seemed an easier approach than designing a\line \expndtw4 more flexible pretty-printer, and even a flexible pretty printer would\line \expndtw1 not provide that total flexibility.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Smalltalk-80 system uses only two of the four source file streams\line \expndtw1 provided; it does not exploit the flexibility that four can provide. One\line \expndtw-2 could imagine ways to use two more files. For example, one of the files\line \expndtw-3 could be used as a read-only version of the condensed changes file, pro-\line \expndtw0 viding a level of system backup between the sources and changes file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Another direction that we did not pursue was to allow the text stored\line \expndtw3 on the files to have multiple emphases (font and face changes) of the\line \expndtw1 text. The Smalltalk-76 system\super 1\nosupersub  had two formats that preserved empha-\line \expndtw0 ses, and produced listings that were easier to read and that allowed pro-\line \expndtw2 grammers to tailor the visual aspects of their code to suit their tastes.\line \expndtw1 Since the Smalltalk-80 principles allow such tailoring in other areas of\line the system, tailoring source text ought to be allowed as well. Unfortu-\line \expndtw2 nately, we were unable to design a file format that allowed both em-\line \expndtw6 phasis description and direct printing of the file on conventional\line \expndtw-1 printers. Solutions to keeping emphasis descriptions always involved us-\line \expndtw2 ing non-ASCII characters or had extra characters which would clutter\line \expndtw1 the page if directly printed. We assume that in the future more printers\line \expndtw2 will be available that print many fonts and faces. When this is the case\line \expndtw4 (or to help make this be the case), we hope that some other standard\line \expndtw1 that preserves emphases will emerge.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1706\margrsxn1296\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3319 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 40{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa554 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Code File Format}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1713\margrsxn1296\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1321\colsr925\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-256\shpright8907\shpbottom-256\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusion}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 code file format was developed to allow communica-\line tion between the flexible world of a Smalltalk-80 system and the less\line \expndtw2 flexible world of disks, networks, storage devices and printers. It is used\line \expndtw6 by the system to keep the source code for the methods on disk files,\line \expndtw3 rather than within the memory of a resident system. It is also used to\line \expndtw5 communicate changes between one system and another, and to provide\line \expndtw3 a level of recovery from errors. The format is flexible enough to allow\line both code (source methods) and executable Smalltalk-80 expressions to\line \expndtw4 be read in and/or evaluated; it also includes a general mechanism to al-\line \expndtw6 low objects in the Smalltalk-80 system to read and interpret the files.\line \expndtw3 The format satisfies its design constraints and leaves the door open for\line \expndtw2 several useful extensions.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1706\margrsxn1311\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1116\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1706\margrsxn1311\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1306\colsr1202\colno2\colw6382 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-248\shpright8899\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 References}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li241\fi-241\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 1. \lang1033 Ingalls, Daniel \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 . \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 "The Smalltalk-76 Programming System:\line \expndtw5 Design and Implementation", Conference Record, Fifth Annual\line \expndtw2 ACM Symposium on Principles of Programming Languages, \lang1032 1978.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1227\margrsxn1217\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4194\shptop-306\shpright8939\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4198\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Design Decisions for}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4201\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4198\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Implementors}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Allen Wirfs-Brock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1937\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tektronix, Inc.\line \expndtw0 Beaverton, Oregon}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1245\margrsxn1761\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1018\colsr1217\colno2\colw6667 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-245\shpright8914\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Abstract}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 virtual machine specification describes the required\line \expndtw6 behavior of any Smalltalk-80 interpreter. The specification takes the\line \expndtw4 form of a model implementation of a Smalltalk-80 interpreter. An\line \expndtw8 implementor of a Smalltalk-80 interpreter is not required to exactly\line \expndtw6 copy the data structures and algorithms of the model interpreter. The\line only requirement is that any Smalltalk-80 interpreter exhibit external\line \expndtw2 behavior which is identical to that described by the formal specification.\line \expndtw4 The implementor is free to make design tradeoffs that may increase the\line performance of the implementation while preserving the required ex-\line \expndtw8 ternal behavior. This paper identifies some of the design decisions\line \expndtw3 which face a Smalltalk-80 implementor and discusses several design\line \expndtw-1 trade-offs.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1227\margrsxn1768\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1130\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1227\margrsxn1768\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1515\colsr742\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-245\shpright8925\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb72\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 virtual machine specification as it appears in\line \i\expndtw3 Smalltalk-80: The Language and Its Implementation\super 1\nosupersub  \i0 describes the re-\line \expndtw8 quired low level  behavior of any Smalltalk-80 implementation.  The}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3473\margrsxn1217\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb209\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3473\margrsxn1217\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw3981\colsr2516\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Tektronix, Inc. \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 41}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1147\margrsxn1816\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4522 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 42{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri0\sb263\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 specification takes the form of a Smalltalk-80 "program" which exhibits\line this behavior. One approach to the implementation of a Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw3 interpreter is to literally translate this program into some appropriate\line \expndtw1 implementation language. While this approach will result in an inter-\line preter which exhibits the required behavior, the performance of the re-\line \expndtw2 sulting interpreter may be unsatisfactory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 An alternate implementation approach is to construct an interpreter\line \expndtw4 that uses algorithms and data structures which differ from those used\line \expndtw-1 in the formal specification. These would be chosen to optimize perfor-\line \expndtw3 mance for the host implementation environment. Such an interpreter\line \expndtw1 may achieve higher performance but requires greater implementation\line \expndtw-4 effort.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi241\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 This paper presents an overview of the design decision space which\line \expndtw0 confronts the implementors of Smalltalk-80 interpreters. Specifically, it\line \expndtw-1 examines \i some \i0 of the potential design trade-offs concerning the host\line \expndtw6 hardware and implementation language, the interpreter data struc-\line \expndtw3 tures, the actual execution of Smalltalk-80 instructions, and the cre-\line \expndtw2 ation and destruction of objects. Even though the design issues are\line \expndtw1 examined assuming an interpreter implementation utilizing a conven-\line \expndtw3 tional computer or microprocessor as a host, many of the trade-offs\line \expndtw0 should be applicable to a microcoded or hardware implementation.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop1336\shpright8928\shpbottom1336\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1649\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Formal{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw2 The first part of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine specification defines}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Specification{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw7 the virtual machine architecture. This includes the definition of the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 primitive data types, the instruction set, and the interface to the object\line \expndtw2 memory manager. The second part describes the internal operation of\line \expndtw1 the object memory manager. An implementation of the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw0 virtual machin\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  is commonly referred to as a Smalltalk-80 interpreter.\line \expndtw-2 The formal specification completely defines the required behavior of a\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk-80 interpreter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi238\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The formal specification takes the form of a collection of\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 methods which implement a Smalltalk-80 interpreter. It\line is, in effect, an implementation of a "model interpreter." Within this\line \expndtw6 model the "registers" of the virtual machine are represented as\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk-80 instance variables, the data structures are explicitly de-\line \expndtw1 fined via constant field offsets and bit masks, and the required seman-\line tics of the interpreter are implicit in the behavior of the methods. The\line \expndtw-1 model bytecode interpreter implementation can be viewed as the defini-\line \expndtw1 tion of the correct behavior of a Smalltalk-80 implementation.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1504\margrsxn2294\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3341\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7902 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 43}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Formal Specification}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1444\fi241\ri4\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The specification does not place any particular requirements upon\line \expndtw6 the internal implementation of the object memory manager. Of course,\line \expndtw5 it assumes that any implementation will correctly preserve stored data\line \expndtw6 and that this data will be available to the interpreter when requested.\line \expndtw4 The memory manager implementation chapter may also be viewed as a\line model for how an object memory manager may be implemented.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1440\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An implementor of a Smalltalk-80 interpreter must design and con-\line \expndtw6 struct an interpreter whose behavior conforms to that defined by the\line \expndtw3 formal specification. One method of accomplishing this is to directly\line \expndtw7 translate the Smalltalk-80 methods of the model implementation into\line \expndtw5 an appropriate implementation language. One might even consider us-\line \expndtw6 ing a program to perform this translation. Figure \lang1032 4.1 \lang1033 gives an example\line \expndtw2 of a method from the formal specification and Figure \lang1032 4.2 \lang1033 shows how it\line \expndtw6 might be translated into Pascal.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1436\fi234\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The principal advantage of the direct translation approach is that it is\line \expndtw6 a simple method of obtaining a semantically correct interpreter. It also\line \expndtw8 is a very good way for an implementor to learn how the interpreter\line \expndtw4 works internally. The principal disadvantage associated with this ap-\line \expndtw5 proach is that the resulting interpreter may exhibit disappointing per-\line \expndtw7 formance levels. The data structures and algorithms of the book's\line \expndtw3 interpreter were selected to provide a clear definition of the required be-\line \expndtw4 havior; they will probably not be optimal for any particular host com-\line \expndtw5 puter. The challenge for a Smalltalk-80 implementor is to design an\line \expndtw2 interpreter which will yield acceptable performance within some particu-\line \expndtw5 lar host environment. At Tektronix, we utilized the direct translation\line \expndtw3 approach (see Chapter \lang1032 5) \lang1033 and were able to very quickly build a working\line \expndtw4 (but slow) Smalltalk-80 implementation. Experience gained from this\line \expndtw6 initial implementation enabled us to later design a significantly im-\line \expndtw4 proved second generation interpreter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1908\fi0\ri0\sb450\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 initializeGuaranteedPointers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1897\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 " \lang1033 Undefined Object and Booleans \lang1032 "\line \expndtw0\lang1033 nilPointer \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - 2.\line \expndtw1\lang1033 falsePointer \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - 4.\line \expndtw6\lang1033 truePointer \lang1032 - 6.\line \expndtw8\lang1033 "and so on \lang1032 ... "\line \b\expndtw-1\lang1033\fs20 pushConstantBytecode}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2135 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 currentBytecode \lang1032 = 113 \lang1033 ifTrue:  [Tself push: truePointer].\line \expndtw1 currentBytecode \lang1032 = 114 \lang1033 ifTrue:   [Tself push: falsePointer].\line currentBytecode \lang1032 = 115 \lang1033 ifTrue:   [Tself push: nilPointer].\line currentBytecode \lang1032 = 116 \lang1033 ifTrue:   [Tself push: minusOnePointer],\line currentBytecode \lang1032 = 117 \lang1033 ifTrue:   [Tself push: zeroPointer].\line currentBytecode \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  118 \lang1033 ifTrue:  [Tself push: onePointer],\line \b\charscalex120\expndtw-6 Figure \lang1032 4.1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex100\expndtw1\lang1033 currentBytecode \lang1032 = 119 \lang1033 ifTrue:   [Tself push: twoPointer].}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1803\margrsxn1156\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li58\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4576 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 44{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li61\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa205 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2890\margrsxn4277\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw986\colsr724\colno2\colw3031 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4068\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 4.2}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 const}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li234\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 (Undefined Object and Booleansj\line \expndtw4 nilPointer \lang1032 = 2;\line \expndtw5\lang1033 falsePointer \lang1032 = 4;\line \expndtw4\lang1033 truePointer \lang1032 = 6;\line \expndtw0\lang1033 (and so on \expndtw14 ...j}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li238\fi-234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 procedure  \b0 pushConstantBytecode;\line \expndtw-4 begin}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li472\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 case  currentBytecode of}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li727\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 113: \lang1033 push(truePointer);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li727\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 114: \lang1033 push(falsePointer);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li727\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 115: \lang1033 push(nilPointer);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li724\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 116: \lang1033 push(minusOnePointer);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li724\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 117: \lang1033 push(zeroPointer);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li720\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 118: \lang1033 push(onePointer);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li223\fi256\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 119: \lang1033 push(twoPointer);\line \expndtw8 end jcasej\line \expndtw0 end \lang1032 (\lang1033 pushConstantBytecode j;}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1803\margrsxn1156\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1458\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1803\margrsxn1156\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1180\colsr1058\colno2\colw6710 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-317\shpright8953\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Host\line Processor}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first major design decision which will confront a Smalltalk-80\line implementor will be the choice of the hardware which will host the im-\line \expndtw5 plementation. In many situations the implementor will have little free-\line dom in this area. Where the implementor has the freedom to select the\line \expndtw3 host processor, there are a number of considerations which should enter\line \expndtw2 into the decision process.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A processor which is to host a Smalltalk-80 interpreter should be\line \expndtw2 fast. An interpreter which executes \lang1032 10,000 \lang1033 bytecodes per second may be\line \expndtw4 perceived by a Smalltalk-80 programmer to be quite slow. The original\line \expndtw2 Tektronix implementation, which could execute \lang1032 3500 \lang1033 bytecodes per sec-\line ond, was considered to be just barely usable. The Xerox Dolphin imple-\line \expndtw5 mentation executes \lang1032 20,000 \lang1033 bytecodes per second and is considered to\line \expndtw7 have "adequate" performance, while the Xerox Dorado at \lang1032 400,000\line \expndtw6\lang1033 bytecodes per second has excellent performance (see Chapter \lang1032 9). \lang1033 At\line \expndtw5\lang1032 10,000 \lang1033 bytecodes per second the interpreter will have, on the average,\line only \lang1032 100 \lang1033 microseconds in which to fetch, decode, and execute each\line \expndtw3 bytecode. At a more acceptable performance level of \lang1032 100,000 \lang1033 bytecodes\line \expndtw5 per second, the interpreter will have only \lang1032 10 \lang1033 microseconds for each\line \expndtw-2 bytecode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi227\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Smalltalk-80 host processor architecture must support a large\line \expndtw12 amount of main memory (either real or virtual). The standard\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk-80 virtual image consists of approximately \lang1032 500,000 \lang1033 bytes of}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1283\margrsxn1676\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4187\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8752 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 45}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Implementation Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri4\sb259\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Smalltalk-80 objects. To this must be added the space for interpreter,\line \expndtw6 the interpreter's data structures, the display bitmap, and additional\line \expndtw-1 space to contain objects created dynamically as the system runs. The to-\line tal requirements of the system will easily approach one million bytes of\line \expndtw-2 memory with even a modest application. Although it may be possible to\line \expndtw-1 configure a virtual image with fewer features and more modest memory\line \expndtw1 requirements, this can be most easily done utilizing an operational\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk-80 system. For this reason, the implementor will need a de-\line \expndtw1 velopment system with at least \lang1032 1 \lang1033 megabyte of main memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 By caching a number of variables which represent the execution\line \expndtw1 state of a Smalltalk-80 method in internal registers, an implementation\line \expndtw-1 will probably get dramatically improved performance. A good host pro-\line \expndtw2 cessor should have sufficient internal registers to allow these values to\line \expndtw0 be cached in its registers. The exact number of registers needed to con-\line tain cached values will depend upon the specifics of the interpreter de-\line \expndtw-1 sign. However, as a general rule, \lang1032 8 \lang1033 is probably not enough while \lang1032 32 \lang1033 is\line \expndtw2 probably more than enough. For example, one of our implementations\line \expndtw-2 for the Motorola \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 processor could have easily made use of several\line \expndtw2 more than the \lang1032 15 \lang1033 registers which were available.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2293\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Smalltalk-80 interpreters frequently look up values in tables and fol-\line \expndtw1 low indirect references. For this reason it is desirable that the host pro-\line \expndtw0 cessor provide good support for indexed addressing and indirection.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Hardware support for the Smalltalk-80 graphics model is another\line \expndtw1 major consideration. Smalltalk-80 graphics is entirely based upon the\line manipulation of bitmaps. Although some implementations have simu-\line \expndtw0 lated this model using other display technologies (for example, by using\line \expndtw2 a vector oriented raster terminal), the results have been less than satis-\line \expndtw0 factory (see Chapter \lang1032 5). \lang1033 Acceptable results will only be achieved if an\line \expndtw1 actual hardware bitmapped display is provided. A frequent concern of\line new implementors is the performance of BitBIt, the bitmap manipula-\line \expndtw3 tion operation. One concern is whether specific hardware support will\line \expndtw0 be required for this operation. Our experience with the \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 was that\line \expndtw-1 adequate BitBIt performance was easy to achieve with straightforward\line coding, while adequate bytecode interpreter performance was very diffi-\line cult to achieve. This leads us to believe that a host processor capable of\line achieving adequate performance when interpreting bytecodes will prob-\line ably perform adequately when BitBlt-ing. In particular, the processor's\line \expndtw1 ability to perform shifting and masking operations will affect the over-\line \expndtw-4 all performance of BitBIt.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop626\shpright8928\shpbottom626\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb943\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw25\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw1 The choice of an implementation language for a Smalltalk-80 interpret-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Implementation      \b0 er is typically a trade-off between the ease of implementation of the in-\line \b\charscalex140\expndtw-3 Language{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw1 terpreter and the final performance of the system. Implementors should}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1794\margrsxn1187\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4540 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 46{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 consider using a high-level programming language as the first imple-\line mentation tool. A high-level language based interpreter can be quickly\line implemented and should be relatively easy to debug. Unfortunately, the\line final performance of such implementations may be disappointing. This\line \expndtw-1 may be the case even if a very good optimizing compiler is used.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 It is generally accepted that the code generated for a large program\line \expndtw1 by an optimizing compiler will be "better" than that which a human\line \expndtw0 assembly language programmer would write for the same problem.\line \expndtw-1 Conversely, for short code sequences, a human programmer can usually\line \expndtw2 write better code than that generated by an optimizing compiler. Al-\line \expndtw0 though a Smalltalk-80 interpreter may appear to be a complex piece of\line \expndtw1 software, it is actually a relatively small program. For example, our as-\line \expndtw0 sembly language implementation for the Motorola \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 contains ap-\line \expndtw3 proximately \lang1032 5000 \lang1033 instructions. Furthermore, a large portion of the\line \expndtw-1 execution time tends to be spent executing only a few dozen of the in-\line \expndtw1 structions. These instruction sequences are short enough that carefully\line \expndtw5 written assembly code can achieve significantly better performance\line \expndtw-2 than optimized compiler generated code. Our \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 bytecode dispatch\line \expndtw3 routine consists of five instructions, while the bodies of many of the\line \expndtw0 push and pop bytecodes consist of only one or two instructions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A successful Smalltalk-80 interpreter design will consist of an effi-\line cient mapping of the virtual machine architecture onto the available\line \expndtw-2 resources of the host processor. Such a mapping will include the global\line allocation of processor resources (registers, preferred memory locations,\line \expndtw1 instruction sequences, etc.) for specific purposes within the interpreter.\line \expndtw5 An assembly language programmer will have complete freedom to\line \expndtw-1 make these allocations. Such freedom is typically unavailable to a high-\line \expndtw1 level language programmer who must work within a general purpose\line \expndtw0 resource allocation model chosen by the designers of the compiler.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop1123\shpright8924\shpbottom1123\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1429\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Object Pointer         \b0 The most common form of data manipulated by a Smalltalk-80 inter-\line \b\charscalex139\expndtw-4 Formats{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw0 preter are Object Pointers (commonly referred to as Oops). An Oop rep-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 resents either an atomic integer value in the range \lang1032 -16,384 \lang1033 to \lang1032 16,383 \lang1033 or\line \expndtw1 a reference to some particular Smalltalk-80 object. The formal specifi-\line cation uses a standard representation for Oops. This representation de-\line \expndtw2 fines an Oop to be a 16-bit quantity. The least significant of the \lang1032 16 \lang1033 bits\line \expndtw4 is used as a tag which indicates how the rest of the bits are to be\line \expndtw2 interpreted. If the tag bit is a \lang1032 0 \lang1033 then the most significant \lang1032 15 \lang1033 bits are\line \expndtw1 interpreted as an object reference. If the tag bit is a \lang1032 1 \lang1033 then the most\line \expndtw5 significant \lang1032 15 \lang1033 bits are interpreted as a 2's complement integer value.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3608\margrsxn1590\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1969\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlhyph\tx4673\tlul\tx6527 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 47}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Object Pointer Formats}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri25\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that the size of an Oop determines both the total number of ob-\line \expndtw3 jects which may exist at any time \lang1032 (32,768) \lang1033 and the range of integer val-\line \expndtw4 ues upon which arithmetic is primitively performed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because Oops are used so frequently by the interpreter, their format\line can have a large impact upon the overall performance of the interpret-\line \expndtw3 er. The most common operations performed upon Oops by the interpret-\line er are testing the tag bit, accessing the object referenced by an Oop,\line \expndtw9 extracting the integer value from an Oop, and constructing an Oop\line \expndtw4 from an integer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Even though the standard Oop format pervades the formal specifica-\line \expndtw5 tion, use of a different format will not violate the criteria for confor-\line mance to the specification. This is possible because the internal format\line \expndtw4 of an Oop is invisible to the Smalltalk-80 programmer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are several possible alternative Oop formats which may offer\line \expndtw4 varying performance advantages. One alternative is to change the posi-\line tion of the tag bit.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Placing the tag bit in the least significant bit position (the position in\line the standard Oop format) is most appropriate for a processor which re-\line \expndtw2 flects the value of this bit in its condition codes. This is the case for the\line \expndtw1 Xerox processors\super 2\nosupersub  upon which the Smalltalk-80 system was originally\line developed, and for some common microprocessors. Using such a proces-\line \expndtw6 sor, the tag bit is automatically "tested" each time an Oop is accessed.\line \expndtw4 A simple conditional branch instruction can then be used by the inter-\line \expndtw5 preter to choose between integer and object reference actions. Proces-\line \expndtw4 sors which lack this feature will require a more complex instruction\line \expndtw2 sequence, shifting the Oop, a masking operation, and comparison to per-\line \expndtw5 form the same test.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi241\ri25\sb11\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Placing the tag in the most significant bit position causes the tag to\line \expndtw1 occupy the sign-bit position for 16-bit 2's complement processors. For a\line \expndtw6 processor that has condition codes which reflect the value of the sign\line \expndtw5 bit, a test of the tag becomes a simple branch on positive or negative\line \expndtw0 value.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi238\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Other factors which will affect the tag bit position might include the\line \expndtw5 relative performance cost of setting the least significant bit as opposed\line \expndtw2 to the most significant bit (is adding or logical or-ing a \lang1032 1 \lang1033 less expensive\line \expndtw8 than the same operation involving \lang1032 32,768) \lang1033 for converting an integer\line \expndtw2 into an Oop, and the relative cost of shifts as opposed to adds for con-\line \expndtw3 verting Oops into table indices.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The standard format uses a tag bit value of \lang1032 1 \lang1033 to identify an integer\line value and a tag bit value of \lang1032 0 \lang1033 to identify an object identifier. Inverting\line \expndtw4 this interpretation has potentially useful properties, some of which are\line \expndtw2 also dependent upon the choice of tag bit position. For example, if a tag\line \expndtw5 value \lang1032 0 \lang1033 is used to indicate an integer valued Oop and the tag occupies\line \expndtw4 the least significant bit position, then Smalllnteger values are, in effect,\line \expndtw7 2's complement values which have been scaled by a factor of \lang1032 2. \lang1033 Such}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1218\margrsxn672\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw4503\colsr4795\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb400\sa0\tlul\tx4504 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex116\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 48{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex116\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-432\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex42\expndtw0\dn10\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs60 \'d4}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1172\margrsxn672\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri1130\sb259\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 values can be added and subtracted (the most common arithmetic oper-\line ations) without requiring a conversion from the Oop format and the re-\line \expndtw0 sult will also be a valid Small Integer Oop. Only one of the operands of a\line \expndtw3 multiplication operation will need to be converted from the Oop format\line \expndtw1 for the operation to yield a valid Small Integer Oop.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi241\ri1138\sb11\sa1048\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If a tag value of \lang1032 0 \lang1033 is used to indicate object identifier Oops and the\line \expndtw2 tag occupies the most significant bit position, then object identifier Oops\line \expndtw4 can serve as direct indices into a table of 8-bit values on byte address-\line \expndtw2 able processors. This would allow reference counting to be implemented\line using an independent table of 8-bit reference-count values which is di-\line rectly indexed using Oops. For a word addressed processor, the standard\line \expndtw4 format allows Oops to be used to directly index a \lang1032 2 \lang1033 word per entry ob-\line \expndtw3 ject table.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1172\margrsxn1821\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1335\colsr911\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-256\shpright8928\shpbottom-256\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Object}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Memory}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The object memory implementation described in the formal specifica-\line \expndtw2 tion views the object memory as being physically divided into \lang1032 16 \lang1033 physi-\line \expndtw6 cal segments, each containing 64K 16-bit words. Individual objects\line \expndtw3 occupy space within a single segment. Object reference Oops are trans-\line \expndtw6 lated into memory addresses using a data structure known as the Ob-\line \expndtw7 ject Table. The object table contains one 32-bit entry for each of the\line \expndtw2 32K possible object referencing Oops. Each object table entry has the\line \expndtw0 following format:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3645\margrsxn2274\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb187\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3645\margrsxn2274\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1584\colsr439\colno2\colw3967 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bits \lang1032 0-15 \lang1033 (lsb):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bits \lang1032 16-19:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bit \lang1032 20:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bit \lang1032 21:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bit \lang1032 22:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bit \lang1032 23:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bits \lang1032 24-31 \lang1033 (msb):}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The word offset of the object within its segment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The number of the segment which contains the object}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Reserved}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Set if the Oop associated with this entry is unused}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Set if the fields of this object contain Oops}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Set if object contains an odd number of \lang1032 8 \lang1033 bit fields}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This object's reference count}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1172\margrsxn672\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri1141\sb187\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For each segment there is a set of linked lists which locate all free\line \expndtw6 space within the segment. In addition there is a single list which links\line \expndtw5 all unassigned Oops and object table entries. Objects are linked using\line \expndtw0 Oop references.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri1148\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The above design includes several implicit assumptions about the\line \expndtw2 memory organization of the host processor. It assumes that the unit of\line \expndtw5 memory addressability is a 16-bit word. It assumes that the processor\line \expndtw9 uses a segmented address space and that each segment contains 64K}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3076\margrsxn2119\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1962\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6523 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 49}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Object Memory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri22\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 words. Finally, it assumes that at most 1024K words \lang1032 (16 \lang1033 segments) are\line \expndtw5 addressable. This organization may be considerably different from that\line \expndtw2 of an actual host processor. Many processors support a large, byte ad-\line \expndtw3 dressable, linear address space. Although the formal specification's de-\line \expndtw4 sign can be mapped onto such a memory organization, such a mapping\line will result in reduced interpreter performance if it is carried out dy-\line \expndtw1 namically.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An object memory design will consist of two inter-related elements,\line \expndtw3 the organization of the actual object space and the format of the object\line \expndtw5 table. The goal of the design will usually be to minimize the time re-\line \expndtw2 quired to access the fields of an object when given an Oop. However, if\line \expndtw5 main memory is limited, the goal of the design may be to limit the size\line \expndtw1 of the object table. A performance oriented object table will usually be\line \expndtw3 represented as an array which is directly indexed by Oops (or a simple\line function on Oops). A hash table might be used for a space efficient ob-\line \expndtw4 ject table representation\super 3\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The most important component of an object table entry is the field\line \expndtw6 which contains the actual address of the associated object within the\line \expndtw3 object space. Ideally this field should contain the physical memory ad-\line \expndtw8 dress of the object represented so that it may be used without any\line \expndtw6 masking or shifting operations. Such a format will permit the contents\line \expndtw7 to be used to directly address the associated object, either by loading\line \expndtw2 the field into a processor base register or by some type of indirect ad-\line \expndtw6 dressing mechanism. In this case, the size of the address field will be\line \expndtw3 the size of a physical processor address.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the host processor's physical address is larger than the 20-bits used\line in the formal specification, the size of an object table entry will have to\line \expndtw5 be increased beyond 32-bits or the size of the reference count and flag\line \expndtw2 bits will have to be decreased. Since Oops are typically used as scaled\line \expndtw4 indexes into the object table, it is desirable that the size of an object ta-\line \expndtw1 ble entry be a power-of-two multiple of the processor's addressable word\line size so that object table offsets may be computed by shifting instead of\line \expndtw2 multiplication. For most conventional processors, 64-bits \lang1032 (8 \lang1033 bytes, four\line \expndtw1 16-bit words, two 32-bit words) would be the next available size. Howev-\line \expndtw2 er, a 64-bit object table entry will require 256K bytes and will probably\line \expndtw7 contain many unused bits. An alternate approach is to use separate\line \expndtw6 parallel arrays to hold the address fields and the reference count/flag\line \expndtw7 fields of each entry. This results in an effective entry size which is\line \expndtw8 greater than 32-bits without requiring a full 64-bit entry. Decreasing\line \expndtw5 the size of the reference-count field is another valid alternative. Since\line most reference count values are either very small \lang1032 (8 \lang1033 or less) or have\line \expndtw6 reached the overflow value where they stick\super 4\nosupersub , a reference-count field\line \expndtw3 size of \lang1032 3 \lang1033 or \lang1032 4 \lang1033 bits should be adequate. The main consideration will be\line \expndtw4 whether the host processor can efficiently access such a field.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1682\margrsxn1284\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4525 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 50{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop295\shpright8939\shpbottom295\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb716\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Bytecode{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw5 The bytecode interpreter performs the task of fetching and executing}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2275 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Interpreter{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw0 individual Smalltalk-80 bytecodes (virtual machine instructions). Before}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 examining the actual functioning of the bytecode interpreter, we will\line \expndtw2 consider the general question of time/space trade-offs within Small-\line \expndtw0 talk-80 implementations. A complete, operational Smalltalk-80 system\line requires approximately one million bytes of storage to operate. The ac-\line \expndtw-1 tual interpreter will occupy only a small fraction of this. (Our first im-\line \expndtw5 plementation, which was very bulky, required approximately 128K\line \expndtw1 bytes for the interpreter. A later assembly language implementation for\line the same host needed less than 25K bytes.) Since Smalltalk-80 inter-\line \expndtw0 preters seem to strain the computational resources of conventional pro-\line \expndtw1 cessors, most interpreter designs will tend towards reducing execution\line \expndtw2 time at the expense of increasing the total size of the implementation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi230\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The model implementation in the formal specification takes an algo-\line \expndtw2 rithmic approach to interpretation. The interpreter fetches a bytecode,\line \expndtw3 shifts and masks it to extract the operation code and parameter fields,\line \expndtw1 and uses conditional statements to select the particular operation to be\line performed. While this approach is quite effective for illustrating the\line \expndtw0 encoding of the bytecodes it is often not suitable for a production inter-\line preter because of the computation required to decode each bytecode. A\line \expndtw-1 more efficient implementation technique for the bytecode dispatch oper-\line \expndtw2 ation may be to use the bytecode as an index into a 256-way dispatch\line \expndtw0 table which contains the addresses of the individual routines for each\line \expndtw3 bytecode. For example, rather than using one routine, as in the exam-\line \expndtw0 ple in Fig. \lang1032 3.1, \lang1033 there could be seven individual routines, each one opti-\line \expndtw2 mized for pushing a particular constant value.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The model implementation exhibits a high degree of modularity. This\line \expndtw1 is particularly true in the area of the interface between the bytecode in-\line \expndtw4 terpreter and the object memory manager. The bytecode interpreter\line \expndtw-1 makes explicit calls to object memory routines for each memory access.\line The performance of a production implementation can, however, be im-\line \expndtw-2 proved by incorporating intimate knowledge of the object memory im-\line \expndtw7 plementation into the bytecode interpreter. Many object memory\line \expndtw0 accesses may be performed directly by the interpreter without actually\line \expndtw2 invoking separate routines within the object memory manager.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 As mentioned earlier, the selection of which interpreter state values\line \expndtw0 to cache is a critical design decision for the bytecode interpreter. The\line designer must evaluate the cost of maintaining the cached values (load-\line \expndtw2 ing the values when a context is activated and storing some of the val-\line \expndtw1 ues back into the context when it is deactivated) relative to the actual\line \expndtw0 performance gains from using the cached values. The evaluation should\line \expndtw2 consider the average duration of an activation. Our observations indi-\line \expndtw3 cate that most activations span a small number of bytecodes (less than}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1368\margrsxn1613\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8726 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 51}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Memory Management}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri4\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 10). \lang1033 Caching too much of the active context can thus lead to situations\line \expndtw1 where considerable execution time is spent caching values that are not\line used over the span of the activation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The model implementation caches the actual Oop values of several\line context fields. This implies that these values must be decoded into real\line \expndtw4 memory addresses (performing an object table lookup or conversion\line \expndtw0 from Smalllnteger format) each time they are used. An alternative is to\line \expndtw3 decode these values when they are fetched from the active context and\line \expndtw4 to cache the addresses. This means that the cached program counter\line \expndtw3 would be the actual address of the next bytecode and that the cached\line \expndtw2 stack pointer would be the actual address of the top element of the ac-\line \expndtw4 tive context's stack. If this technique is used, care must be taken that\line \expndtw1 the cached values are correctly updated, e.g., when the memory manag-\line \expndtw-1 er changes the physical location of objects (performs a segment com-\line \expndtw2 pression). It is also essential that the values of the stack pointer and\line program counter field get updated when the active context changes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi241\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Smalltalk-80 system's required support for multiple processes,\line \expndtw6 when implemented in an obvious manner, can impose an overhead\line \expndtw2 upon each bytecode. The formal specification requires that a process\line \expndtw-2 switch may occur before each bytecode is fetched. An obvious way to\line \expndtw0 implement this requirement is to have a global boolean flag which indi-\line \expndtw1 cates that a process switch is pending, and to test this flag before fetch-\line \expndtw8 ing each bytecode. This technique has the disadvantage that the\line \expndtw2 overhead of testing this flag occurs for each bytecode executed even\line \expndtw0 though actual process switches are infrequent. Since the number of in-\line structions required to implement most bytecodes is relatively small, this\line \expndtw2 test can be a significant overhead. Alternative implementations tech-\line \expndtw8 niques can avoid this overhead. For example, the address of the\line \expndtw2 bytecode dispatcher might be stored in a processor register. Routines\line \expndtw1 which implement bytecodes would then terminate by branching to the\line address contained in the registers. A pending process switch could then\line \expndtw0 be signaled by changing the address in the register to the address of the\line routine which performs process switches. When the current bytecode\line \expndtw1 finishes, control would therefore be transferred to the process switcher.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop1354\shpright8928\shpbottom1354\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1670\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Memory{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 The routines of the formal specification's object memory manager may}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Management{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw1 be grouped into two categories. The first category consists of those rou-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 tines which support accesses to objects. The second category consists of\line \expndtw2 those routines which support the allocation and deallocation of objects.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1858\margrsxn1156\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4511 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 52{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi0\ri7\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The access routines (such as fetchPointer.ofObject: and\line \expndtw3 storeByte:ofObject:withValue:) are used by the bytecode interpreter to\line \expndtw4 store and retrieve the information contained in the fields of objects. In\line many implementations of the bytecode interpreter, these functions will\line \expndtw8 not be performed by independent routines, but will be implicitly\line \expndtw3 performed by inline code sequences within the routines of the interpret-\line \expndtw2 er. The object allocation and deallocation routines form the bulk of the\line \expndtw4 memory manager.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2228\fi241\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Collectively, the memory management routines will probably com-\line prise the most complex part of a Smalltalk-80 interpreter implementa-\line \expndtw11 tion. In addition, unless great care is taken in their design, the\line \expndtw5 percentage of execution time spent in these routines can easily domi-\line nate the time spent in all other parts of the interpreter. Our initial im-\line \expndtw2 plementation was found to be spending \lang1032 70% \lang1033 of its time within memory\line \expndtw4 management routines (see Chapter \lang1032 5).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri0\sb497\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The bytecode interpreter normally requests the allocation of an object}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2228\fi-2228\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object Allocation \i0 in two circumstances. The first circumstance is the execution of a prim-\line \expndtw2 itive method (most commonly the primitive new or new:) which explicit-\line ly calls for the creation of a new object. The second circumstance is the\line \expndtw4 activation of a new method. This implicitly requires the creation of a\line \expndtw3 context object to represent the state of the activation. The formal speci-\line fication provides a single generalized set of routines which handle both\line \expndtw4 types of allocation requests. These routines perform the following ac-\line \expndtw5 tions. First they must assign an Oop which will be used to refer to the\line \expndtw2 new object. Second they must find an area of free storage within the ob-\line \expndtw5 ject memory, large enough to contain the requested object. Next they\line must initialize any internal data structures (for example an object table\line \expndtw4 entry or object length field) used to represent the object. Finally, they\line must initialize the fields of the object with a null value.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Observation of actual Smalltalk-80 implementations indicates that\line \expndtw4 the vast majority of allocation requests are for the creation of context\line \expndtw2 objects (see Chapter \lang1032 11). \lang1033 In addition, most of these requests are for the\line \expndtw5 smaller of the two possible context sizes. A memory manager design\line \expndtw2 which optimizes the creation of a small context object should thus yield\line \expndtw4 better performance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are a number of possible approaches to achieving such an opti-\line \expndtw2 mization. A memory manager might have a dedicated list of available\line \expndtw3 contexts. These available contexts might be preinitialized and have pre-\line \expndtw6 assigned Oops associated with them. If the memory manager attempts\line \expndtw8 to ensure that this list will not be empty (perhaps by using a back-\line \expndtw3 ground process to maintain the list), then a context could usually be al-\line \expndtw4 located by simply removing the first element from the list.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1364\margrsxn1613\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8741 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-23\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 53}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Memory Management}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi238\ri25\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A memory manager might choose to dedicate a memory segment to\line \expndtw-1 the allocation of contexts. Since such a segment would only contain ob-\line \expndtw5 jects of a single size, the actual allocation and deallocation process\line \expndtw-2 should be simplified.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2225\fi241\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Any scheme to optimize context allocation must, of course, conform\line \expndtw3 to the formal specification's requirement that a context behaves as a\line \expndtw0 normal Smalltalk-80 object. The representation of activation records\line (contexts) as objects contributes much to the power of Smalltalk-80 (it\line allows programs such as the Smalltalk-80 debugger to be implemented)\line \expndtw2 but requires a large amount of system overhead to support. A major\line \expndtw0 challenge to Smalltalk-80 implementors is to develop techniques to re-\line \expndtw1 duce this overhead while preserving the inherent power of context ob-\line \expndtw-1 jects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb490\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2243 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 \'97\'97{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw5\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Storage reclamation is the second major function of the Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2239 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Storage{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw3 memory manager. While the Smalltalk-80 storage model allows a pro-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2236 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex122\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Reclamation{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw1 gram to explicitly request the creation of an object, it does not require a}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi0\ri36\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 program to explicitly request that an object be deallocated. Once an ob-\line \expndtw1 ject has been allocated it must remain in existence as long as it is ac-\line \expndtw-2 cessible from any other object. An object may only be deallocated if no\line \expndtw1 references to it exist. It is the memory manager's responsibility to auto-\line \expndtw-1 matically deallocate all inaccessible objects. This process is commonly\line \expndtw0 referred to as garbage collection\super 5\nosupersub . The classical method (called mark/\line sweep) of performing garbage collection is to periodically halt process-\line ing, identify all inaccessible objects, and then deallocate them. This is\line \expndtw-1 commonly done as a two-phase process. First all accessible objects are\line \expndtw2 marked. This requires starting at some root object and traversing all\line \expndtw1 accessible pointers in the system. Second, all unmarked objects are\line \expndtw-1 deallocated. With a large object memory, such a process may consume a\line \expndtw0 considerable period of time (ranging from several seconds to several\line \expndtw1 minutes). Because of the interactive nature of the Smalltalk-80 system,\line \expndtw2 such delays are unacceptable. Instead, a garbage collection technique\line which distributes the storage reclamation overhead over the entire\line computation is required. The most commonly known technique for\line achieving this is reference counting. This is the technique used by the\line \expndtw0 formal specification's model implementation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi241\ri40\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Reference counting requires that each object have associated with it\line \expndtw1 a count of the number of pointers to it which exist in the system. Each\line \expndtw-1 time an Oop is stored into a field the reference count of the object asso-\line \expndtw3 ciated with the Oop is incremented. Since storing an Oop into a field\line \expndtw0 must overwrite the previous contents of the field, the reference count\line \expndtw1 associated with the old value is decremented. When the reference count\line \expndtw2 of an object reaches zero, the object is deallocated. The deallocation of}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1379\margrsxn360\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw8964\colsr486\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li58\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4547 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 54{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li54\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2308\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 an object invalidates any object references contained in it and hence\line \expndtw0 will decrement their reference counts. This may recursively cause other\line \expndtw-1 objects to be deallocated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2297\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Although reference counting eliminates the long delays characteristic\line \expndtw0 of mark/sweep collection, it introduces considerable overhead into the\line normal operations of the system. We have found that for our host pro-\line \expndtw-1 cessor (a Motorola \lang1032 68000), \lang1033 the code sequences that implement simple\line \expndtw0 bytecodes such as the push and pop operations using reference counting\line \expndtw2 are several times longer than the equivalent routines without reference\line \expndtw1 counting. A Smalltalk-80 interpreter design that can decrease this over-\line head should have greatly improved performance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 There are several possible approaches to achieving this improved\line \expndtw3 performance. One technique which reduces the actual counting over-\line \expndtw-1 head is called deferred reference counting\super 6\nosupersub . It is based upon the obser-\line \expndtw6 vations that the most frequent and most dynamic object references\line \expndtw0 occur from context objects and that many of these references are quite\line \expndtw1 transitory. For example, assigning an object to a variable causes the ob-\line \expndtw4 ject's reference count to be first increased by one as it is pushed onto\line \expndtw7 the context's stack, then decreased by one as it is popped from the\line \expndtw1 stack, and finally increased by one as it is stored into the variable. Our\line measurements show that "store instance variable" bytecodes (the most\line common means of creating an object reference from a non-context ob-\line \expndtw3 ject) account for less than \lang1032 4% \lang1033 percent of the dynamically executed\line \expndtw-2 bytecodes. If the need to perform reference counting for references con-\line \expndtw2 tained within contexts is eliminated, then almost all of the reference\line \expndtw1 counting overhead will have been eliminated.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop1123\shpright8924\shpbottom1123\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1433\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A Second{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw4 The first Tektronix Smalltalk-80 interpreter was implemented in Pas-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Generation{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 cal on a Motorola \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 (see Chapter \lang1032 5). \lang1033 Even though the performance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Design{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw3 of this implementation was so poor that it was only marginally useful,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri43\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 the experience gained from this effort enabled us to design a new inter-\line \expndtw1 preter which exhibits much better performance. In developing this sec-\line \expndtw4 ond generation interpreter we encountered many of the design trade-\line \expndtw3 offs mentioned in the previous sections of this paper. The new inter-\line \expndtw-1 preter was designed and implemented by the author over a period of\line \expndtw1 approximately nine months.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi234\ri47\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We choose to continue using a \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 as the host for the new inter-\line \expndtw3 preter but component advances enabled us to use a \lang1032 10 \lang1033 Mhz processor\line \expndtw5 with one memory wait state instead of an \lang1032 8 \lang1033 Mhz processor with two\line wait states. We choose to implement the interpreter in assembly Ian-}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11556\sa0\sl-936\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn23\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs130 1}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1400\margrsxn1570\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4172\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8744 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 55}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Summary and Conclusions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri4\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 guage. In addition, great care was taken in choosing the code sequences\line \expndtw1 for all of the frequently executed portions of the interpreter. The com-\line \expndtw-1 mon byte codes are all open coded with separate routines for each possi-\line \expndtw3 ble instruction parameter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The active context's stack pointer, instruction pointer, and home con-\line text pointer are cached in \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 base registers as \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 addresses. The\line \expndtw1 stack pointer representation was chosen such that \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 stack-oriented\line addressing modes could be used to access the active context stack. Oth-\line \expndtw0 er registers are dedicated to global resources such as addressing the ob-\line ject table and accessing free context objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Oop format chosen requires only a simple add instruction to con-\line \expndtw2 vert an Oop into an object table index. Object table entries can be di-\line \expndtw0 rectly loaded into base registers for accessing objects. A separate\line \expndtw3 reference-count table is used. Deferred reference counting is used to\line \expndtw1 limit the reference-counting overhead and to streamline the code se-\line \expndtw-1 quences for push/pop bytecodes. Complete context objects are not creat-\line \expndtw2 ed for leaves of the message send tree. Context objects are only created\line \expndtw1 if a method references the active context or causes another new context\line \expndtw0 to be activated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The initial (before any tuning and without some optional primitives)\line \expndtw4 performance benchmarks of our second generation interpreter (see\line \expndtw2 Chapter \lang1032 9) \lang1033 show that it is between five and eight times faster than our\line \expndtw3 original implementation. We feel that these results demonstrate that it\line \expndtw-2 is feasible to build usable microprocessor based Smalltalk-80 implemen-\line \expndtw0 tations.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop1127\shpright8938\shpbottom1127\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1433\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Summary and{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw5 For any given host processor, its performance as a Smalltalk-80 host}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Conclusions{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw0 can potentially vary widely depending upon how the Smalltalk-80 inter-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 preter is implemented. The goal of a Smalltalk-80 implementor should\line \expndtw0 be to achieve the best possible mapping of the Smalltalk-80 virtual ma-\line \expndtw1 chine specification onto the chosen host computer. To accomplish this,\line \expndtw2 the implementor will need to intimately understand both the internal\line \expndtw1 dynamic behavior of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine and the idiosyn-\line \expndtw11 crasies of the host processor. We would recommend that an\line \expndtw1 implementor gain an understanding of the behavior of the virtual ma-\line \expndtw3 chine by first using a high-level language to implement the interpreter\line \expndtw1 as described by the formal specification. This implementation can then\line \expndtw0 be used to study the actual behavior of the Smalltalk-80 system and ex-\line \expndtw3 plore design alternatives. Finally, a new implementation should be\line \expndtw2 designed which takes maximum advantage of the characteristics of the}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1717\margrsxn1328\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4439 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 56{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw17\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Design Decisions for Smalltalk-80 Implementors}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2203\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 host processor. We have presented a few of the design alternatives\line \expndtw2 which should be considered by Smalltalk-80 implementors as they de-\line \expndtw4 velop their interpreters.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop875\shpright8852\shpbottom875\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1199\sa0\tx2444 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 References{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\sub\charscalex111\expndtw-1 L\nosupersub  \sub Goldberg>\nosupersub  \sub Adele)\nosupersub  \sub and\nosupersub  R\sub ODS\nosupersub on, David, \i Smalltalk-80: The Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2693\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 and Its Implementation, \i0 Addison-Wesley, Reading, MA, \lang1032 1983.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2668 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2668 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Lampson, Butler W., "The Dorado: A High Performance Personal\line \expndtw0 Computer," Xerox PARC Technical Report CSL-81-1, Jan. \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2668 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2668 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Kaehler,   Ted,   "Virtual   Memory   for   an   Object-Oriented   Lan-\line guage," \i Byte \i0 vol. \lang1032 6, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 8, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 378-387, \lang1033 Aug. \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2668 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2668 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Baden, Scott, "Architectural Enhancements for an Object-Based\line \expndtw2 Memory System," CS-292R Class Report, Computer Science Div.,\line \expndtw-1 Dept. of E.E.C.S., University of California, Berkeley, CA, Fall \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2668 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2668 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Cohen, Jacques; "Garbage Collection of Linked Data Structures",\line \i\expndtw0 ACM Computing Surveys \i0 vol. \lang1032 13, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 3, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 341-367, \lang1033 Sept. \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2668 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2668\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2668 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Deutsch, L. Peter, and Bobrow Daniel G., "An Efficient Incremen-\line \expndtw7 tal Automatic Garbage Collector," \i Communications of the ACM\line \i0\expndtw-1 vol. \lang1032 19, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 9, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 522-526, \lang1033 Sept. \lang1032 1976.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3776\margrsxn3453\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-425\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex117\expndtw-22\dn6\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs54 PART    TWO}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2189\fi0\ri0\sb2448\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i\scaps\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs14 ^\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ,}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn5936\margrsxn5253\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1188\posnegy619\absw0\absh2103\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw2019\pich3708\picwgoal1144\pichgoal2102\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3776\margrsxn3453\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li3427\ri108\sb425\sa0{\pict\picw2019\pich863\picwgoal1144\pichgoal489\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1930\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 LA"D  ComPlLER Toes NO}\par \pard \plain \li3470\ri36\sb1224\sa0{\pict\picw2070\pich901\picwgoal1173\pichgoal511\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1807\fi0\ri0\sb176\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs14 3+4-}\par \pard \plain \li3442\ri0\sb205\sa0{\pict\picw2184\pich1016\picwgoal1238\pichgoal576\jpegblip
}\par\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn10829\margrsxn360\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs36 \'e9}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1132\margrsxn1838\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4165\shptop-292\shpright8910\shpbottom-292\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4165\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Implementing the\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk-80 System:\line \expndtw8 The Tektronix\line \expndtw5 Experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4158\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Paul L. McCullough \i0\lang1032 *}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Tektronix, Inc.\line \expndtw-4 Beaverton, Oregon}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop1472\shpright8924\shpbottom1472\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb1786\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Introduction{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 The Tektronix Smalltalk-80 implementation went through a number of}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 hardware and software phases. Our experience will probably prove to\line \expndtw1 be similar to that of other research and prototype groups desiring to\line implement the Smalltalk-80 system. At best, we will point out some\line mistakes to avoid; at the very least we can provide an entertaining view\line \expndtw-2 of our successes and follies.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 This paper gives an overview of our initial hardware and software\line \expndtw1 environments and our initial implementation design. We then present a\line \expndtw0 fairly detailed account of debugging our first system. Next, we describe\line \expndtw1 the evolution of our hardware, software, and development tools. We\line \expndtw11 conclude with some observations and conclusions about the\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk-80 system and its implications for the future.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri40\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Readers should note that we were debugging both our implementa-\line \expndtw0 tion and the formal specification. Although we detected a number of er-\line \expndtw1 rors in the formal specification, these errors have since been corrected\line and are discussed herein to provide historical perspective.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb785\sa0\sl-202\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 *Mr. McCullough is currently employed by Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, Palo Alto,\line \expndtw1 California. Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Tektronix, Inc., \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb36\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 59}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1623\margrsxn1358\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6228 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 60{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa540 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1641\margrsxn1358\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1483\colsr770\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-248\shpright8910\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Initial Goals}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb43\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Initially we had four goals for our Smalltalk-80 work:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li439\fi-202\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx439 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li439\fi-202\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx439 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Learn about the Smalltalk-80 system, in particular the implemen-\line tation of the virtual machine,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li238\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx439 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li238\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx439 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Learn about programming in the Smalltalk-80 language,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li238\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx439 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li238\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx439 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Report on errors in the book draft, and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li439\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx439 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li439\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx439 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implement the virtual machine, realizing that it would not be our\line \expndtw4 final implementation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tektronix had no previous experience with object-oriented software, so\line \expndtw6 we were very interested in having a system with which we could\line \expndtw8 interactively program in the Smalltalk-80 language, and in studying\line \expndtw7 the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine. As part of our agreement with Xe-\line \expndtw6 rox, we were to use our implementation as a means to detect errors in\line \expndtw3 the book draft and to identify ways in which the book might be made\line clearer. We realized that our initial implementation would suffer from\line \expndtw4 performance problems, but felt that a timely implementation was more\line \expndtw5 desirable than a high performance one.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1623\margrsxn1365\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1174\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1623\margrsxn1365\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1209\colsr1037\colno2\colw6674 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-292\shpright8921\shpbottom-292\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Initial\line \expndtw-4 Hardware}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our initial hardware consisted of:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Motorola \lang1032 68000 \lang1033 processor \lang1032 (8 \lang1033 MHz)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 4 \lang1033 MHz proprietary bus}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 768 \lang1033 Kbytes of RAM}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tektronix \lang1032 4025 \lang1033 terminal}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx443 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A microprocessor development system, used as a file server}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The choice of hardware was based on the availability of a Tektronix\line designed 68000-based system, along with the need for a large, prefera-\line \expndtw5 bly linear, address space. We also wanted to use a processor amenable\line \expndtw3 to the construction of personal workstations. The Tektronix \lang1032 4025 \lang1033 termi-\line \expndtw6 nal is a raster graphics terminal, primarily oriented toward drawing\line vectors. While our bitmapped display was being designed, the \lang1032 4025\line \expndtw5\lang1033 served as an interim display device. Because the initial Smalltalk-80\line virtual image did not depend on the use of a file system, we only used\line \expndtw8 the microprocessor development system as a file server to load and\line \expndtw4 store virtual images.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1407\margrsxn1527\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7481\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8788 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 61}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7538\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa515 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Initial Software}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1443\margrsxn1559\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1566\colsr677\colno2\colw6663 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft950\shptop-313\shpright8906\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Software}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Development}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Environment}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our virtual machine was developed in a cross-compilation environment\line \expndtw5 using a DECSYSTEM-20. The bulk of the virtual machine was written\line \expndtw4 in a dialect of the proposed ISO Standard Pascal. This particular dialect\line \expndtw6 of Pascal supports the independent compilation of modules and pro-\line \expndtw3 duces assembly language files which are assembled and linked. The re-\line sulting executable file is downloaded to the 68000-based system over a\line \expndtw2\lang1032 1200 \lang1033 baud serial line. Though the \lang1032 1200 \lang1033 baud line was an obvious bottle-\line \expndtw5 neck, the Pascal software already existed on the DECSYSTEM-20 and\line we had no desire to port it.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1407\margrsxn1559\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1033\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1407\margrsxn1559\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1915\colsr349\colno2\colw6678 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft29\shptop-252\shpright8943\shpbottom-252\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Initial Software}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop3856\shpright1901\shpbottom3856\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4036\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object Memory\line \expndtw12 Manager}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 According to Dan Ingalls: "an operating system is a collection of things\line \expndtw5 that don't fit into a language. There shouldn't be one"\super 1\nosupersub . Taking those\line \expndtw4 words to heart, we chose to implement our virtual machine on a system\line \expndtw5 that had no operating system. This choice meant that we could not rely\line on runtime services normally provided by an operating system and had\line to write those portions that we needed, such as routines to handle\line \expndtw4 input/output to an RS-232 port and perform IEEE 32-bit floating point\line \expndtw5 arithmetic.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our software implementation team consisted of three software engi-\line \expndtw5 neers. We chose to partition the programming task into three distinct\line \expndtw3 parts:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object Memory Manager}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interpreter and Primitives}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf24{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 BitBIt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The initial object memory manager was, for the most part, a strict\line \expndtw4 translation from Smalltalk-80 to Pascal of the methods presented in the\line \expndtw3 formal specification. During the translation phase, we noted four minor\line \expndtw7 typographical errors in the draft of the book involving improper bit\line \expndtw3 masks, or incorrect variable, constant, or method names. We chose to\line \expndtw4 implement the reference-counting garbage collector. Later, because the\line \expndtw6 image creates circular garbage, we added a simple, recursive, mark-\line \expndtw2 sweep collector. The translation process took less than one week and re-\line \expndtw7 sulted in a working memory manager that maintained a very clear\line \expndtw6 boundary between itself and the rest of the virtual machine. As dis-\line \expndtw4 cussed below, this clear differentiation is both a blessing and a curse.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi245\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With minor changes due to different dialects of Pascal, we were able\line \expndtw7 to run programs that tested the object memory manager on the\line \expndtw6 DECSYSTEM-20 with its more sophisticated debugging and perform-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1719\margrsxn1258\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6217 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 62{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri4\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ance monitoring software. The test programs read the virtual image,\line then made calls to the various entry points in the memory manager.\line \expndtw1 Then, with Pascal write statements and the debugger, we were able to\line \expndtw2 examine the state of the object space and determine whether the mem-\line \expndtw1 ory manager was working correctly. These tests indicated several errors\line \expndtw2 in the book's methods: for example, the method that determined where\line \expndtw-1 to find the last pointer of an object was incorrect for CompiledMethods,\line \expndtw1 and the recursive freer needed an extra guard to prevent Small Integers\line \expndtw-2 from being passed off to the pointerBitOf: routine.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 At this point, we were able to run test programs that created in-\line \expndtw-1 stances of classes, stored object pointers in other objects, destroyed such\line links and thus invoked the deallocation of objects, and performed com-\line \expndtw6 pactions of the object space. Further testing demonstrated that the\line \expndtw-1 book's method for swapPointersOf:and: was also incorrect.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In order to speed up the performance of the deallocation portions of\line \expndtw-1 the memory manager, we modified the countDown: routine to call\line \expndtw-3 forAIIObjectsAccessibleFrom:suchThatDo: only when the object's refer-\line \expndtw-1 ence count was going to drop to zero, thus saving a procedure activation\line \expndtw2 that was usually unnecessary.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A few other minor changes provided us with a memory manager that\line \expndtw-1 was tested on the DECSYSTEM-20. Thus, we had a great deal of assur-\line \expndtw7 ance that the memory manager would perform correctly on the\line \expndtw-1 68000-based system. Also, we felt that when problems were encountered\line \expndtw0 in our implementation of the virtual machine, we could concentrate on\line \expndtw2 looking for the problem in the bytecode interpreter or primitives, and\line could ignore the memory manager. In actual practice we made many,\line \expndtw4 many runs of the virtual machine before any problems were found in\line \expndtw2 the memory manager. We heartily recommend having a trustworthy\line \expndtw0 memory manager.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In parallel with the development of the object memory manager, we}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex121\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Interpreter and{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw7 coded  the bytecode  interpreter and primitives.  The interpreter and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex125\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Primitives{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw6 many of the primitives were written in Pascal. The arithmetic primi-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 tives were coded in assembly language in order to simplify the mainte-\line \expndtw2 nance of the small integer tag bit.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi223\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The outer block of the interpreter consists of a call to an initializa-\line tion routine and a loop containing a very large case statement that acts\line \expndtw1 as the bytecode dispatcher. While the memory manager was a fairly lit-\line eral translation of the book's methods, much greater care was exercised\line in the construction of the interpreter. Code that in the book was several\line \expndtw0 message sends was often collapsed into a single Pascal statement. We\line included in our interpreter the capability of producing traces which du-\line \expndtw1 plicate those supplied with the virtual image by Xerox.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In order to give the reader a measure of the complexity of\line implementing an interpreter (in Pascal), we present the lengths (in}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1314\margrsxn1660\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4183\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8744 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 63}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Initial Software}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri11\sb259\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 printer pages at \lang1032 60 \lang1033 lines per page) of some of the major routines. These\line \expndtw1 figures include the length of tracing code:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Looking up a message, including the perform: primitive: two and\line \expndtw-1 one-half pages}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Sending a  message  (including cache  lookup):  one  and  one-half\line \expndtw-4 pages}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Executing the current method, including the primitives written in\line \expndtw0 Pascal: twelve pages}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Returning a value from the active context: one and one-half pages}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The scan characters primitive (used for text composition): three\line \expndtw1 and one-half pages}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Large integer primitives: four pages}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Process primitives: five pages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri22\sb292\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We strongly recommend that the first implementation of an interpreter\line be in a high-level language. By writing the virtual machine in a high-\line \expndtw4 level language, implementors gain a more thorough understanding of\line \expndtw1 the virtual machine as well as a much more quickly completed imple-\line mentation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The BitBIt primitive handles all graphics in the Smalltalk-80 system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2254 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 BitBlt{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw1 Due to its importance, we decided to have one person concentrate on its}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi0\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 implementation. The routines to implement BitBIt were written in as-\line \expndtw2 sembly language and closely reflect the structure of the BitBIt methods\line \expndtw-2 in the book. To assist in the debugging of BitBIt, there are many condi-\line \expndtw2 tionally assembled calls to the Pascal runtime print routines. The main\line \expndtw-2 BitBIt routine accepts one argument, the address of a Pascal record con-\line \expndtw4 taining the various BitBIt parameters. When called, the routines per-\line \expndtw-1 form the following actions:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Clip the source parameters to the physical size of the source form}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Clip the clipping rectangle to the true size of the destination form}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Clip and adjust the source origin}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Compute the masks necessary for the logical operations}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Check for possible overlap of the source and destination forms}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Calculate the offsets for the starting and ending words}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Copy the bits as appropriate}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1087\margrsxn1901\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9436\shptop6613\shpright9436\shpbottom10695\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6199 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 64{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Certain optimizations are performed for the special cases of clearing,\line setting, or complementing the destination forms. BitBIt is approximately\line \expndtw0\lang1032 2 \lang1033 Kbytes of assembly code.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We maintained a fairly detailed log of our attempts to get the virtual}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Summary of Runs        \i0 machine up and running. The comments we made for each of these}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 runs may be helpful to future implementors of the virtual machine.\line \expndtw5 This summary should provide a sense of the types of errors and prob-\line \expndtw4 lems one should expect when implementing the virtual machine.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reached the first send, then encountered an error in a debugging\line \expndtw5 routine we had written.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reached the first send again,  encountered another error in a\line \expndtw2 debugging routine.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Encountered a Pascal compiler bug.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reached first send of the \i @ \i0 selector, and discovered that we had\line \expndtw2 transcribed the constant for class Small Integer incorrectly.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method specified in the book for initializing the stack pointer\line \expndtw3 of a new context was incorrect.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We forgot to initialize the sender field when creating a context.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the book, the method returnValue:to: caused the reference count\line \expndtw10 of the sender context to go to zero (thereby making the sender\line \expndtw4 garbage) just before returning to that context. We had to explicitly\line increase the reference count of the sender context, perform the re-\line \expndtw5 turn, then explicitly decrement the reference count.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We had decided to implement the "common selector" bytecodes\line \expndtw6 using full message lookup. Unfortunately, the method header for\line \expndtw9 selector  \lang1032 = =  \lang1033 in class Object did not specify the execution of a\line \expndtw7 primitive. We patched the image to specify the correct primitive\line \expndtw3 number.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  9.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first conditional branch we encountered failed because we did\line \expndtw5 not advance the instruction pointer past the second byte of the in-\line \expndtw3 struction.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\par{\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-342\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  10.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-342\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We discovered that the source code for Small Integer   \lang1032 <   \lang1033 did not\line specify a primitive, resulting in an infinite recursion. We patched\line \expndtw4 the image again.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  11.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Discovered that other methods of class Smalllnteger did not have\line \expndtw4 primitives specified. We retrenched to executing the following se-\line \expndtw5 lectors without lookup: class, \lang1032 = =, \lang1033 arithmetics, relationals.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3162\margrsxn2220\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1775\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6332 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 65}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Initial Software}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  12.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Selector at: failed. Our fault, in the routine positive16BitValueOf: a\line \expndtw1\lang1032 " > " \lang1033 should have been a \lang1032 " < ".}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  13.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Multiply primitive failed due to an assembly language coding er-\line \expndtw0 ror.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  14.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All relational primitives written in assembly language had an in-\line \expndtw2 correct (and uninitialized) register specified.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  15.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Made it through the first trace. (Listings of four traces of the in-\line terpreter's internal operations were included with the first distri-\line \expndtw9 bution of the virtual image.  Subsequent distributions included\line \expndtw4 three traces.)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  16.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The book's method for the primitive value: caused the stack to be\line \expndtw-5 off-by-one.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  17.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Once again, we found an error initializing the stack pointer of new\line \expndtw0 contexts.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  18.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Again, the stack pointer is off. These three errors were caused by\line an incorrect constant in the book draft.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  19.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A message selector was not found. Another run is necessary to de-\line \expndtw5 termine what happened.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  20.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At the beginning of execution for a block, the cached stack pointer\line \expndtw13 is one too large. In the past, message sends and returns have\line \expndtw4 worked   because   the   routine   that   stored   the   stack   pointer\line \expndtw3 decremented it.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  21.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We had coded the at:put: primitive incorrectly: we forgot to have it\line \expndtw4 return anything, hence the stack was off-by-one.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  22.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We incorrectly coded the at: put: primitive with an uninitialized\line \expndtw2 variable.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  23.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The at:put: primitive had a \lang1032 > \lang1033 that should have been a \lang1032 > =.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  24.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalllnteger bitShift: primitive added in the Smalllnteger bit,\line \expndtw5 but should have Or'ed it in.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  25.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interpreting lots of bytecodes, unfortunately not the correct ones.\line \expndtw4 Apparently, we took a bad branch somewhere.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  26.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We found that the book's methods for the bytecode "push self" did\line \expndtw2 not necessarily work for block contexts.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  27.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart12\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Almost through the fourth trace when the Smalllnteger division\line \expndtw7 primitive failed to clear the high-order half of a register. The er-\line \expndtw5 ror was detected by a Pascal runtime check.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1087\margrsxn1912\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9436\shptop7931\shpright9436\shpbottom10710\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6196 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 66{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb263\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  28.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb263\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Through the fourth trace when Sensor primMousePoint dies be-\line \expndtw6 cause of a clash between the interpreter and the Pascal runtimes.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  29.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We are well beyond the fourth trace when we discover that the\line \expndtw2 method frame:window:para:style:printing: has a MethodHeader ex-\line \expndtw8 tension that specifies primitive number \lang1032 0. \lang1033 We had assumed that\line \expndtw7 an extension always specified a valid primitive number, but find\line that it may also be used to specify a method with more than four\line \expndtw4 arguments.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  30.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have changed all unimplemented primitives so that they fail,\line \expndtw3 and now correctly handle primitive \lang1032 0 \lang1033 in MethodHeader extensions.\line By now, we should have something up on the \lang1032 4025 \lang1033 display, but do\line \expndtw7 not. Investigating, we find that the book says that the bitmap for\line a Form is the first field, whereas the sources say it is the second\line \expndtw-4 field.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  31.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We are halftoning the display. We have to make a few adjust-\line \expndtw7 ments to prevent overrunning the display. Halftoning will take a\line \expndtw6 long time, approximately two hours. After a while, a runtime ex-\line \expndtw9 ception was raised by a Pascal support routine that contained a\line \expndtw-4 bug.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  32.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The "T" for the TopView window title tab is present on the dis-\line \expndtw3 play. Interpreter stopped after sending copyTo: to a Smalllnteger.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  33.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have disabled halftoning to the \lang1032 4025, \lang1033 continuing with the\line \expndtw2 study of the problem of sending copyTo: to a Smalllnteger.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  34.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The problem is that the BitBIt primitive, copyBits did not return\line \expndtw6 anything, thus forcing the stack off by one. Similarly, beDisplay,\line \expndtw8 and beCursor did not return anything. We have added more dis-\line \expndtw3 play memory to the \lang1032 4025.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  35.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Hurray! "Top View" window title tab is on the screen. Pascal\line \expndtw8 runtime checks detected an out-of-range scalar while setting up\line \expndtw2 arguments for copyBits. We have always assumed that BitBIt argu-\line \expndtw8 ments are unsigned, but that is not so. We were told that BitBIt\line \expndtw1 should do source clipping, so we will add that to BitBIt.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  36.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The entire "Top View" window is on the display, then erased. We\line eventually crashed because we are out of object space, but unsure\line \expndtw-5 why.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  37.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We are out of object space because the book's methods for super-\line \expndtw3 class send was incorrect: another level of indirection is necessary.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  38.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2714\fi-353\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2714 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart28\pnindent353\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We now have "Top View" window, Browser, and Transcript window\line \expndtw5 on the display. Interpreter stopped when the mouseButtons primi-\line \expndtw1 tive failed.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3139\margrsxn2197\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1825\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6386 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 67}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Initial Software}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  39.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We turned on halftoning to see what would happen. This was a\line \expndtw4 mistake because windows are half toned black and then white. We\line \expndtw5 decided to reload and try again without halftoning.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  40.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have reached the idle loop, checking if the mouse is in any\line \expndtw7 window. We changed the position of the mouse (by altering two\line \expndtw5 memory locations) and placed it within the browser. The browser\line awoke and refreshed four of its panes. The fifth pane (code pane)\line \expndtw8 caused an interpreter crash with a Pascal out-of-range error due\line \expndtw5 to a minor bug in the mod primitive.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  41.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Great excitement! We have refreshed the window containing a\line \expndtw6 "Congratulations!!" message. Eventually we crashed because the\line \expndtw7 Float  \lang1032 < \lang1033 primitive fails. The system tried to put up a Notify win-\line \expndtw3 dow, but had difficulty because of other primitive failures. Howev-\line \expndtw5 er, it was able to put up messages in the Transcript window. For a\line \expndtw7 system that is not yet fully implemented, it is amazingly robust.\line \expndtw4 We noticed that certain BitBIt operations seem to put up incorrect\line \expndtw11 information, then erase  it.  For example, putting up the  "Top\line \expndtw9 View" title tab, the text reads "Top Vijkl" for a short time, and\line \expndtw10 the incorrect part is then  repainted.  Investigation showed the\line \expndtw5 method computeMasks to have a \lang1032 < \lang1033 selector that should have been\line \expndtw7 a \lang1032 < =, \lang1033 an error carried over from the book.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  42.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Generally poking around with the system. We have found that we\line \expndtw6 need floating point primitives in order for scroll bars to work, so\line we have implemented all but the fractional Part primitive. Rather\line \expndtw9 than develop an IEEE Floating Point package, we acquired one\line \expndtw5 from another group at Tektronix. We have also speeded up BitBIt\line \expndtw3 by using 4010-style graphics commands with the \lang1032 4025.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  43.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have implemented object memory statistics to report the num-\line \expndtw5 ber of fetch Pointers, storePointers, etc. performed. We have also\line \expndtw4 added a lookup cache for faster message send processing. A cleri-\line \expndtw6 cal error in the caching routines crashes the virtual machine.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  44.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An uninitialized variable causes the cache to misbehave.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  45.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The cache is functioning well. Our initial algorithm is to exclu-\line \expndtw7 sive-or the Oops of the receiver's class and the method, then ex-\line \expndtw5 tract bits \lang1032 3-7 \lang1033 and index a \lang1032 256 \lang1033 element array of \lang1032 8 \lang1033 byte entries. The\line \expndtw8 interpreter definitely runs faster with the cache. The cache con-\line \expndtw4 sists of the Oop of the selector, Oop of the receiver's class, Oop of\line \expndtw8 the method, the most significant byte of the method header, and\line \expndtw5 one byte indicating either the primitive index or \lang1032 0.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  46.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li356\fi-356\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx356 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart39\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tried a new hash function, shifting two bits to the left before the\line \expndtw4 exclusive-or because we observed that the Oops of different selec-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1659\margrsxn1336\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6217 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 68{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi0\ri0\sb259\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 tors in the same class are very similar to one another. Some\line \expndtw-1 speedup was noted.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  47.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart47\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Another hash function, this time adding the Oops rather than ex-\line \expndtw0 clusive-oring them. No noticeable change. We did move the mouse\line \expndtw3 to the first pane of the Browser and crashed the system when the\line \expndtw0 interpreter passed a Small Integer to the memory manager.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  48.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart47\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Further examination of the previous problem shows that we did\line \expndtw2 not cut the stack back far enough after a value: message. This bug\line \expndtw6 was carried over into our code from the book, but only appears\line \expndtw1 when sending value: within an iterative loop.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2383\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  49.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2383\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart47\pnindent357\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We have fixed value:, now we need to write the perform: primitive.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  50.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart47\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We have installed perform:, but get an infinite recursion because\line the floating point package is \i not \i0 IEEE format. We will write one\line \expndtw0 in Pascal.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  51.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-356\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart47\pnindent356\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 With the new floating point code, we can now cut text, pop up\line \expndtw0 menus, and so on. This is great!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri4\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 At this point, we added some simple performance monitoring code. We\line \expndtw2 counted the number and type of object memory references, the number\line \expndtw1 of bytecodes executed, and information concerning the performance of\line the lookup cache. For each bytecode executed, an average of just under\line \expndtw0\lang1032 10 \lang1033 object memory references were made. The majority were calls to\line \expndtw-2 fetchPointer:, then storePointer:, fetchByte:, and fetchClass:. The various\line \expndtw0 lookup cache algorithms were found to perform either fairly well \lang1032 (50 \lang1033 to\line \expndtw-1\lang1032 70% \lang1033 hit rate) or very poorly \lang1032 (20% \lang1033 or worse hit rate). Evidently, caching\line \expndtw2 algorithms either perform quite well or miserably.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We feel that we were able to implement a relatively complex piece of}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex126\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Summary of{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw2 software in less than six weeks (that is, from nothing to a working sys-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex126\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Initial Software{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw1 tern) in less than \lang1032 60 \lang1033 runs for several reasons:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We were fortunate to have very good software engineers.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We had a well-defined task.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-198\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-198\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Because it took so long to load the virtual image (about \lang1032 10 \lang1033 min-\line \expndtw3 utes)  from  the  file  server and  so  long (again,   \lang1032 10  \lang1033 minutes)  to\line \expndtw2 download our virtual machine from the host, we were very careful\line \expndtw1 in coding and in analyzing crashes. We were also sharing the hard-\line \expndtw3 ware with another group, so we made good use of our time on the\line \expndtw-2 machine.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2488\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The specification, though not without error, was well written.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1370\margrsxn1622\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6437\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8719 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex124\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 69}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex124\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Second Virtual Image}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7\shptop259\shpright8921\shpbottom259\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb558\sa0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex127\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Second{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex100\expndtw4 About this time, we received the second virtual image from Xerox Palo}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx2254 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex127\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Virtual Image{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex100\expndtw5 Alto Research Center (PARC). With this image, the handling of primi-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tive methods was much cleaner, access to BitBIt was improved, the ker-\line \expndtw3 nel classes were rewritten, and a source code management system was\line \expndtw4 added. Several significant changes to the virtual machine specification\line \expndtw5 were made, with the intention that these would be the final modifica-\line \expndtw4 tions. The second image also made use of the process primitives, while\line the first image did not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because a general cleanup of our interpreter seemed a good idea, and\line \expndtw6 because a fair amount of the interpreter needed to be changed to sup-\line \expndtw4 port processes and new primitive numbers, we rewrote much of it. A\line \expndtw3 history of our runs for the second virtual image follows:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb400\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb400\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We got our "Initializing \expndtw29\lang1032 ..."\expndtw4  \lang1033 message, and the system crashed be-\line \expndtw7 cause we were trying to initialize the cursor frame buffer. Since\line \expndtw4 our bitmap display was not yet available, the presence of this code\line \expndtw5 was premature.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We are through one-third of the first trace, but a conditional\line \expndtw4 branch bytecode branched the wrong way.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Several problems noted:}\par \pard\plain\fs2\par{\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2718\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\tx2880 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2718\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\tx2880 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent162\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Metaclass names no longer print properly on our traces.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2880\fi-162\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2880 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2880\fi-162\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2880 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent162\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We encountered off-by-one errors in stack operations while han-\line \expndtw3 dling bytecode \lang1032 187 \lang1033 because we forgot to adjust the stack index.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2880\fi-162\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2880 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2880\fi-162\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2880 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent162\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We   encountered   off-by-one   errors   in   stack   operation   for\line \expndtw3 Smalllnteger \lang1032 //.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2718\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\tx2880 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2718\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\tx2880 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent162\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our trace does not print operands for Smalllnteger \lang1032 * \lang1033 properly.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2718\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2880 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2718\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2880 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent162\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We need to carefully check the code for all stack operations.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\par{\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 M68000 stack overflow causes parity errors.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We are through trace \lang1032 1, \lang1033 and three-quarters through trace \lang1032 2 \lang1033 when\line \expndtw5 Pascal   detects   an   out-of-range   scalar   because   the   routine\line \expndtw8 returnValue:to: returned to a deallocated block context. We had\line \expndtw4 failed to increase a reference count.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We   are   almost   halfway   through   trace   \lang1032 3   \lang1033 when   we   hit   an\line \expndtw7 unimplemented process primitive. We also noticed the primitive\line \expndtw6 return of an instance variable did not pop the receiver, thus caus-\line \expndtw2 ing the stack to be off-by-one.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1505\margrsxn609\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw8888\colsr40\colno2\colw867 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb752\sa0\tlul\tx6185 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 70{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart7\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We are about \lang1032 60% \lang1033 through trace \lang1032 3 \lang1033 when we try to add nil to an\line \expndtw2 instance of class Rectangle. Caused by our coding error: when a di-\line \expndtw4 rect execution send fails, we fail to tidy up the stack pointer.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart7\pnindent260\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We find that we need to implement the process primitives.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  9.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-259\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart7\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BitBIt fails to clear the high-order bits of a register causing a crash\line on the 21380th message sent.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\par{\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  10.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Sending the selector \lang1032 + \lang1033 to an Array fails. Stack is off-by-one be-\line \expndtw4 cause the copyBits primitive failed to return self.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  11.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We find that the  resume:  primitive does  not work due to an\line \expndtw3 uninitialized variable.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2387\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  12.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2387\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 More problems with resume:, it fails to set a boolean.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  13.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 More problems with the resume: primitive: the process to be re-\line \expndtw5 sumed has nil as its instruction pointer because the initial instruc-\line \expndtw0 tion pointer is not set in primitiveBlockCopy.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  14.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The resume: primitive works finally! Unfortunately, the wait prim-\line itive does not because of an incorrectly coded branch.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  15.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The wait primitive works, and we are through the third trace cor-\line \expndtw9 rectly. We forgot to code the setting of the success boolean for\line \expndtw3 primitive become:, so a notify window is created.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  16.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-342\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent342\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Fired up the system. We have executed more than \lang1032 15,000,000\line \expndtw3\lang1033 bytecodes and it is still alive!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2228\fi0\ri7\sb342\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In order to improve performance, we made many changes to the inter-\line \expndtw7 preter and the memory manager. Changes to the interpreter included\line \expndtw5 the caching of absolute addresses in the interpreter, thus employing\line \expndtw4 considerably fewer calls to the memory manager. For example, to ex-\line \expndtw3 tract the fields of a source form, rather than a fetchPointer call to the\line \expndtw5 memory manager for every field, the interpreter merely cached an ab-\line \expndtw3 solute address and stepped through a range of offsets. Within the mem-\line \expndtw4 ory manager, many procedure calls were replaced with macro calls that\line \expndtw5 were expanded by a macro preprocessor. Not only did this save the\line \expndtw10 overhead of procedure calls, but quite often allowed common\line \expndtw11 subexpression elimination to occur, thus actually decreasing the\line \expndtw2 amount of compiler-generated code.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi245\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We also sped up certain parts of the interpreter based on where we\line \expndtw7 believed the interpreter was spending its time. With these optimiza-\line \expndtw2 tions, performance is approximately \lang1032 470 \lang1033 bytecodes a second.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2218\fi234\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An observation: Utilizing a raw computer (that is, one without an\line \expndtw5 underlying operating system) to implement a Smalltalk-80 system is a\line \expndtw4 double-edged sword: on the one hand, you can place data structures and}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-734\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex214\expndtw0\dn14\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs80 \'f4}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1121\margrsxn1864\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8726 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b\i\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw-19\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs14 \'f4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 \\_}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Performance Modeling Tool}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa1123\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 code anywhere in the system, and you have complete control of the\line hardware. On the other hand, the lack of performance monitoring tools\line \expndtw3 and underlying file systems can be a problem because it takes time to\line \expndtw7 implement them, rather than just interfacing to them.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1150\margrsxn1864\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1962\colsr274\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-310\shpright8906\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-252\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Second Version\line \expndtw-3 of the Hardware}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At about this time, we added floppy disks to the system, as well as a\line utility program that could save and restore arbitrary memory locations\line \expndtw3 on the disks, thus freeing us from the microprocessor development sys-\line \expndtw5 tem file server. The \lang1032 10 \lang1033 minute delay for the loading of a virtual image\line \expndtw3 was reduced to about \lang1032 45 \lang1033 seconds. A more dramatic change to the hard-\line \expndtw5 ware was the addition of our bitmap display. No longer would we have\line \expndtw9 to translate bitmap operations to vector drawing commands on the\line \expndtw3\lang1032 4025, \lang1033 nor wait for a window to be halftoned. We also added a standard\line \expndtw5 Tektronix keyboard and a mouse. In order for the mouse and keyboard\line \expndtw2 (as well as portions of the Smalltalk-80 software) to work, we also added\line \expndtw5 a one millisecond timer interrupt.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As part of another project, a new M68000 processor board was made\line \expndtw5 available to us. Recall that the bus that we were using ran at \lang1032 4 \lang1033 MHz,\line \expndtw2 which introduced wait states into the M68000. The new processor board\line \expndtw6 used a one longword data cache and a one longword instruction cache\line \expndtw3 to reduce bus requests. This resulted in a \lang1032 70% \lang1033 speedup in system per-\line \expndtw2 formance, to approximately \lang1032 800 \lang1033 bytecodes per second.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1135\margrsxn1878\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1112\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1135\margrsxn1878\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1666\colsr576\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-306\shpright8899\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Third\line \expndtw-4 Virtual Image}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At this point, our goal became to build a virtual machine that was\line \expndtw3 clearly faster (approximately \lang1032 4000 \lang1033 bytecodes per second), but to do it\line quickly and at relatively low expense. The method we chose was to de-\line velop a performance analysis tool and, using the results of the measure-\line \expndtw4 ments, to rewrite time consuming portions of the virtual machine in\line \expndtw3 assembly language. The following sections summarize our findings and\line \expndtw5 our techniques for speeding up the virtual machine.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1121\margrsxn1885\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1094\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1121\margrsxn1885\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1731\colsr522\colno2\colw6649 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-317\shpright8906\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Performance\line \expndtw-3 Modeling Tool}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To monitor the execution of the virtual machine, we developed a simple\line analysis tool that was called by the one millisecond timer interrupt rou-\line \expndtw12 tine. Each time it was called, it stored the value of the interrupted}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn959\margrsxn779\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6210 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 72{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri1231\sb270\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 M68000 program counter. By changing a memory location, a routine\line could be activated to print a histogram showing ranges of program\line addresses, the number of times the program counter was found to be\line \expndtw8 within the range, and the percentage of time spent within the range.\line \expndtw9 The size of the address range for each line of the histogram was\line \expndtw3 selectable by the user. We mapped routine addresses to these ranges so\line \expndtw5 that the histogram showed time spent in each routine. This tool proved\line to be invaluable in speeding up the virtual machine.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi245\ri1235\sb0\sa1404\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Prior to utilizing this tool, we decided to measure how much time\line \expndtw6 was spent in the interrupt service routine. The Smalltalk-80 virtual\line machine expects a timer interrupt every millisecond and the routine\line \expndtw4 checks the mouse and keyboard motion registers. If a change has oc-\line curred, the routine makes note of the change so that the bytecode dis-\line \expndtw3 patch loop can create a Smalltalk-80 event. Like much of our virtual\line \expndtw6 machine, our timer interrupt routine was initially written in Pascal.\line \expndtw5 Because the interrupt routine has many basic blocks, and the optimizer\line \expndtw2 of the Pascal compiler operates only upon one basic block at a time, the\line \expndtw5 interrupt service routine spent a great deal of time reloading registers\line with previously loaded values. We discovered that an amazing \lang1032 30% \lang1033 of\line the M68000 cycles were going to the interrupt service routine! One of\line \expndtw3 the first optimizations that we performed was to take the Pascal com-\line piler-generated code and to perform flow analysis on it. The new inter-\line \expndtw5 rupt service routine consumed \lang1032 9% \lang1033 of the M68000 cycles. Future plans\line \expndtw3 call for hardware to track mouse and keyboard events, and for timers to\line \expndtw4 interrupt the M68000 only when necessary (for example, when an in-\line \expndtw2 stance of class Delay has finished its wait period).}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn959\margrsxn2036\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1731\colsr518\colno2\colw6663 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-317\shpright8925\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Results of}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Performance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Monitoring}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The performance monitoring tool showed us some statistics that were\line surprising to us (the percentage figures presented below do not include\line \expndtw5 time spent in the interrupt service routine nor the performance moni-\line \expndtw2 toring tool). Approximately \lang1032 70% \lang1033 of the M68000 cycles were being spent\line \expndtw9 in the memory manager, \lang1032 20% \lang1033 in the interpreter and primitives, and\line \expndtw5\lang1032 10% \lang1033 in BitBlt. The bulk of the time in the memory manager was spent\line \expndtw2 in only a few routines: fetchPointerofObject:, storePointenofObject:-\line \expndtw-2 withValue:, fetchClassOf:, countUp:, countDown:, and two sets of routines\line \expndtw5 generally referred to as the recursive freer and the niller. Previous sta-\line \expndtw3 tistics we gathered had indicated that fetchPointenofObject: and store-\line \expndtw2 Pointer:ofObject:withValue: were popular routines, but they were rela-\line tively short and (so it seemed) should consume relatively little processor\line \expndtw0 time.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn959\margrsxn779\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li9450\fi0\ri0\sb756\sa0\sl-382\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex198\expndtw0\dn6\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs56 i}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3081\margrsxn2135\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1933\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6502 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 73}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Results of Performance Monitoring}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri22\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Looking at the Pascal-generated code, we felt that we could do far\line \expndtw4 better with assembly language, and we recoded all memory manager\line \expndtw13 routines that the interpreter and primitives could call directly.\line \expndtw2 Recoding fetchPointenofObject: resulted in a \lang1032 4.5% \lang1033 speedup. Next, we\line \expndtw0 recoded storePointer;ofObject:withValue: and achieved an additional \lang1032 13%\line \expndtw5\lang1033 speedup. The major difference between these two routines is in refer-\line \expndtw3 ence counting: when storing pointers, reference counts must be updated;\line \expndtw4 when fetching pointers they do not. Although we had previously con-\line \expndtw3 cluded that reference counting was an expensive operation, we now had\line \expndtw4 measurements of just how expensive. After recoding in assembly lan-\line \expndtw6 guage all the routines callable by the interpreter and primitives, the\line \expndtw4 system was an aggregate \lang1032 19% \lang1033 faster.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next, we considered routines that were private to the memory man-\line agement module. From the histograms, it was obvious that we spent a\line \expndtw3 great deal of time initializing just-instantiated objects to nil pointers (or\line zeroes for non-pointer objects). This inefficiency again arose from the\line \expndtw1 strict basic block analysis of the Pascal compiler. For the price of a pro-\line \expndtw3 cedure call to an assembly language routine, we were rewarded with a\line \expndtw2 speedup of nearly \lang1032 10%.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi241\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another major change to the memory manager came in the area of\line \expndtw3 the so-called recursive freer. When an object's reference count drops to\line \expndtw5 zero, this set of routines is activated to decrement the reference counts\line \expndtw3 of the object's referents and, should their counts drop to zero, recursive-\line \expndtw4 ly free them. The first attempt at speeding up this process was done in\line Pascal and resulted in nearly a \lang1032 10% \lang1033 speedup. Later on, we rewrote the\line \expndtw6 recursive freer again in assembly language achieving an additional\line \expndtw-1 speedup.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi241\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instantiation of objects was also expensive because several proce-\line \expndtw2 dure calls were made. We rewrote this code (still in Pascal), collapsing\line \expndtw4 several procedures into one. Later, the instantiation routines were re-\line written in assembly language.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri36\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Changes to the interpreter and primitives were done in an interest-\line \expndtw4 ing manner. Recall that we had a functioning, albeit slow, interpreter.\line \expndtw7 With the belief that it is far better to change one thing at a time, rath-\line \expndtw6 er than everything at once, we modified a small portion of the inter-\line \expndtw9 preter and tested the change. Once the change was shown to be\line \expndtw5 satisfactory, we changed another part of the interpreter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Initially, we rewrote the bytecode dispatch routine, but, in keeping\line \expndtw4 with our philosophy of small changes, none of the bytecode interpreta-\line \expndtw7 tion routines. Thus, the assembly language bytecode dispatch routine\line \expndtw4 set a boolean indicating that the assembly language dispatch had failed\line \expndtw5 and that the Pascal routine would have to take over. Then we added\line \expndtw3 bytecode interpretation routines, more or less one at a time. Eventually,\line \expndtw5 we were able to discard the Pascal dispatch loop and bytecode inter-\line \expndtw2 preters completely.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1620\margrsxn1372\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6196 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 74{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi241\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Once all the bytecode interpretation routines were completed, we\line \expndtw1 turned our attention to the primitive routines. These changes were ac-\line \expndtw0 complished in a similar manner: initially, all assembly language primi-\line \expndtw-1 tives failed, forcing the Pascal-coded primitives to run. We would then\line \expndtw0 select a primitive, code it in assembly language, and test it. Once it was\line \expndtw1 found to be acceptable, we selected another primitive to re-code. Final-\line ly, the Pascal primitives were discarded. Rather than call high-frequen-\line cy primitive routines, we included many of them in-line.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In order to save some procedure calls to the memory manager when\line \expndtw4 instantiating objects, the interpreter first tries to directly acquire the\line \expndtw5 new object off the free lists. If the attempt fails, the interpreter calls\line \expndtw1 the memory manager. Such "fuzzing" of the line between the pieces of\line \expndtw2 the virtual machine seem necessary to achieve acceptable performance\line \expndtw0 on current microprocessors. This demonstrates how a clear boundary\line \expndtw5 between the memory manager and the rest of the virtual machine is\line \expndtw1 both a blessing and a curse.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi238\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The changes to the memory manager and interpreter eventually re-\line \expndtw-1 sulted in a \lang1032 3500 \lang1033 bytecode per second system.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop1062\shpright8910\shpbottom1062\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1372\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Third and{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw5 Our technique of making incremental changes to the virtual machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Fourth Images         \b0 enabled us to use a working system and to bring up new virtual images}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 as they were received from Xerox. A log of the attempts to run the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2257\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 third image follows:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb389\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb389\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 At Xerox, the display bitmap is simply an object in the object\line \expndtw1 space. In our implementation, the display bitmap lives at a specif-\line \expndtw9 ic address, and we encountered a problem because this image\line \expndtw7 sends the become:  primitive to the current display object. We\line \expndtw0 modified our code in the become: routine.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We encountered a Pascal subscript-out-of-range error. The routine\line \expndtw6 that returns instance variables was coded incorrectly, due to an\line \expndtw0 error in the book's specification.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 There are some new primitives related to the Xerox implementa-\line \expndtw8 tion  in the image.  We modified our interpreter to understand\line \expndtw-1 them.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A bit of Smalltalk folklore: "If \lang1032 3 + 4 \lang1033 works, everything works."\line \expndtw0 We typed \lang1032 3 + 4 \lang1033 into a window and executed it. .It did not work be-\line \expndtw1 cause the Smalllnteger size message returned the wrong result.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1384\margrsxn1579\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4201\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8766 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 75}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Some Observations}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart5\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Executing "Circle exampleOne" causes infinite recursion because\line \expndtw4 the graphics classes were coded incorrectly by Xerox. They had\line \expndtw7 not noticed this problem because the Xerox implementation of\line \expndtw-4 primitive   new:   did   not   comply  with   the   formal   specification,\line \expndtw-1 allowing their code to execute.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart5\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The system is up and working.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The fourth image was brought up on the first attempt.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop886\shpright8921\shpbottom886\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1199\sa0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw27\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Some{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 If we analyze the coding errors that we encountered in our various im-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Observations{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw1 plementations, we find that most fall into the following categories:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Off-by-one errors}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2686\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Failing to return the correct object, or failing to return any object\line \expndtw-2 (leading to off-by-one errors)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Conditional branch reversals}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2686 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2686 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Errors in the specification}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi0\ri40\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Perhaps the most painful part of debugging a virtual machine is finding\line the off-by-one errors. These errors typically arise in primitive handling\line \expndtw1 and in the stack activation records. Certain primitives may fail, and\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk-80 methods are expected to take over. During the develop-\line \expndtw0 ment of the virtual machine, it is quite common to damage the object\line \expndtw1 references on the stack or to misadjust the stack pointer resulting in\line \expndtw0 off-by-one errors. When returning from a procedure call in many stack\line \expndtw2 machines (the M68000 is an example), if the processor's stack has an\line extra argument or does not have a return value, the correct return ad-\line \expndtw0 dress will not be found, and the processor will return to an erroneous\line \expndtw1 location. The typical result is a system crash. In the Smalltalk-80 virtu-\line al machine, the return address (actually the sender field) of the activa-\line \expndtw5 tion record (an instance of either class MethodContext or class\line \expndtw0 BlockContext) is always in a known place, and a correct return can al-\line \expndtw2 ways be made and the machine will definitely not crash. Nonetheless,\line the interpreter (or primitives) may have pushed an incorrect result val-\line ue or left garbage on the stack. Only later will this type of error mani-\line \expndtw3 fest itself. These errors can be time-consuming and relatively difficult\line \expndtw-3 to find.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2239\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Errors resulting from conditional branch reversals are common, and\line \expndtw2 are not further discussed here.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1764\margrsxn1210\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li68\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6250 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 76{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li65\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi241\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We certainly found our share of errors in the specification of the\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk-80 virtual machine. This statement should not be taken as an\line \expndtw7 affront to the Software Concepts Group at Xerox PARC. They were\line \expndtw3 both developing and documenting two complex software products (the\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk-80 system itself and the underlying virtual machine), and it\line \expndtw8 was our job to point out discrepancies. Indeed, they produced an\line \expndtw4 amazingly well constructed software system, and future implementors\line should have fewer problems with their own implementations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have programmed very few application programs in the\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk-80 language. However, we do have one very definite data\line \expndtw5 point in this area. Our file system (see Chapter \lang1032 16) \lang1033 was totally devel-\line \expndtw6 oped in the Smalltalk-80 system and in a relatively short time period.\line \expndtw4 All debugging was done using the Smalltalk-80 system: we never used\line \expndtw3 the Pascal or assembly language debugging tools.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa1130\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A final observation: the routines collectively known as primitives are\line \expndtw5 about one-third to one-half of the implementation effort. Bear this in\line \expndtw4 mind when scheduling an implementation.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1771\margrsxn1239\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1440\colsr814\colno2\colw6645 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-256\shpright8921\shpbottom-256\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusions}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb68\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our work with the Smalltalk-80 system has shown it to be a robust,\line \expndtw2 well-engineered piece of software. The initial, albeit incomplete, virtual\line \expndtw3 machine required six weeks of effort by three software engineers, pri-\line marily using a high-level language. This resulted in a slow but useable\line \expndtw7 system. By monitoring where the virtual machine spent its time, we\line \expndtw5 were able to construct a system with adequate performance. For first-\line \expndtw4 time implementors, we heartily recommend a similar approach.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Without question, the Smalltalk-80 system will have a strong impact\line \expndtw3 on many areas of computer science, including language design, system\line \expndtw4 architecture, and user interfaces. Perhaps most importantly, the system\line \expndtw5 and language cause the user to think about problems in new ways.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1764\margrsxn1242\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1177\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1764\margrsxn1242\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1555\colsr698\colno2\colw6649 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-313\shpright8907\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Acknowledg-\line \expndtw-1 ments}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Many people contributed to our Smalltalk-80 effort. Allen Wirfs-Brock\line \expndtw4 designed and implemented the Pascal-based interpreters and primitives\line \expndtw12 and the initial assembly language enhancements. Jason Penney\line \expndtw1 designed and implemented BitBIt, the floating point package, the floppy\line \expndtw9 disk driver, and the assembly-enhanced interpreters. Joe Eckardt\line \expndtw3 designed our excellent bitmap display and has made interesting modifi-\line \expndtw5 cations to the Smalltalk-80 code. Tom Kloos and John Theus designed}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn771\margrsxn1480\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr11\colno2\colw8928 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4896\shptop-29\shpright9648\shpbottom-29\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11822\sa0\sl-475\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn9\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs66 L}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 References}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri14\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 and maintained our M68000 system, as well as the interface to the\line \expndtw0 mouse, keyboard, and floppy disks. Allen Otis graciously shared his\line \expndtw1 hardware with us in the early days of the project and made some of the\line first measurements of the virtual machine. Larry Katz made many sug-\line \expndtw0 gestions for the improvement of the book and served as our unofficial\line \expndtw9 kibitzer during the implementation and provided much food for\line \expndtw3 thought. We would like to acknowledge the various managers (Jack\line Grimes, Don Williams, Dave Heinen, George Rhine, and Sue Grady)\line \expndtw1 who had the foresight and wisdom to allow us to work on the project.\line \expndtw-1 Glenn Krasner, of Xerox PARC, provided answers to our questions and\line \expndtw2 provided us with ideas for speeding up our implementation. And, we\line \expndtw3 would like to thank Adele Goldberg and the Software Concepts Group\line \expndtw-2 of Xerox PARC for including us in the book review and implementation\line \expndtw1 process. Without them, we would have naught.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop875\shpright8924\shpbottom875\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1195\sa0\tx2513 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 References{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\charscalex113\expndtw3\lang1032 1. \b0\lang1033 Ingalls, Daniel \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 . \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 "Design Principles Behind Smalltalk", \i Byte}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri108\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 vol. \lang1032 6, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 8, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 286-298, \lang1033 Aug. \lang1032 1981.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1496\margrsxn1417\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 78}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa245 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex127\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Implementing the Smalltalk-80 System: The Tektronix Experience}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1496\margrsxn1417\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx112\posnegy0\absw0\absh7596\nowrap\dfrmtxtx10080\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw15671\pich13398\picwgoal8884\pichgoal7596\jpegblip
}\par{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1257\margrsxn1735\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4194\shptop-306\shpright8939\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 The Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw9 Implementation at\line Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4172\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0\sl-277\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Joseph R. Falcone\line \expndtw6 James R. Stinger*}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4165\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1400\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Computer Research Center\line \expndtw3 Hewlett-Packard Laboratories\line \expndtw2 Palo Alto, California}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1267\margrsxn1739\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1512\colsr731\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-248\shpright8907\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This report describes one of the four test sites for the Smalltalk-80 re-\line lease process: a personal computing research group at the Computer\line \expndtw3 Research Center of Hewlett-Packard Laboratories in Palo Alto. The fol-\line lowing sections present a history of the work at Hewlett-Packard, an\line \expndtw2 overview of our implementation, a description of the development envi-\line ronment, and some conclusions. A comprehensive analysis of the\line \expndtw4 Hewlett-Packard implementation is in the companion paper (see Chap-\line \expndtw0 ter \lang1032 12).}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1257\margrsxn1735\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1213\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1257\margrsxn1735\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1476\colsr770\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-317\shpright8903\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw1 Project at\line \expndtw-3 Hewlett-\line \expndtw-1 Packard}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk project at Hewlett-Packard Laboratories received author-\line ization on December \lang1032 8, 1980. \lang1033 Beginning in November of \lang1032 1981 \lang1033 the proj-\line ect slowed considerably, and the new year found nearly all development\line \expndtw7 at a halt. The project officially closed on February \lang1032 22, 1982, \lang1033 though\line \expndtw3 some independent and academic work on the system continues. The im-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb252\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 *The views expressed herein are those of the authors, and do not necessarily represent\line \expndtw0 the position of Hewlett-Packard or any commitment to products or services. Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\line \expndtw1\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Joseph R. Falcone and James R. Stinger, \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb36\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 79}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1075\margrsxn1917\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9490\shptop6631\shpright9490\shpbottom11772\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5033 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 80{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri4\sb263\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 plementation portion of the project produced nine releases on five dis-\line \expndtw2 tinct host architectures. Documentation review and background work\line \expndtw1 took one person-year of our group's time. We produced the first release\line \expndtw0 of HP Smalltalk in two months, and subsequent releases followed coin-\line \expndtw-1 ciding with the availability of new Smalltalk images and new host com-\line \expndtw1 puters. The analysis of these systems consumed another person-year.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 This section describes our experience implementing a Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw1 system according to the specifications distributed during the test pro-\line gram. When the project began, neither the documentation nor the soft-\line \expndtw2 ware was complete, a fact which profoundly influenced the duration,\line \expndtw0 scope, and direction of the project.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb464\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The first three months of the project involved reviewing chapters of the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex118\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Project History{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113 Smalltalk-80 implementation guide and selecting the first host machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 for the implementation. The first few chapters covered the underlying\line \expndtw3 philosophy of Smalltalk and set the ground rules for an implementa-\line \expndtw-1 tion. As time passed, the chapters concerned with the specifics of imple-\line \expndtw2 mentation began to arrive.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi241\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Smalltalk-80 language itself was the specification language used\line to describe the implementation. We felt that this hindered our efforts in\line \expndtw0 two ways. First, it forced us to learn Smalltalk before we had our own\line system, and at a time when Xerox possessed the only Smalltalk-80 en-\line \expndtw-1 gines (and even those were incomplete). Second, it introduced some\line \expndtw0 unwelcome ambiguity into the specification.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Initially we considered the HP \lang1032 3000 \lang1033 Series \lang1032 44 \lang1033 for the implementation\line \expndtw2 host because of its stack architecture and position as the fastest HP\line \expndtw6 processor then available (approximately \lang1032 0.5 \lang1033 MIPS). This strategy\line \expndtw4 seemed appropriate for the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine architecture\line \expndtw1 as we understood it. However, after studying the matter further, we be-\line came aware of several implementation obstacles. We determined that\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk would perform significantly better on the Series \lang1032 44 \lang1033 if we put\line it directly on the bare machine, instead of layering it on MPE, the HP\line \lang1032 3000 \lang1033 operating system. As there is a tight coupling between MPE and\line the microcode on the Series \lang1032 44, \lang1033 the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine code\line \expndtw0 on the bare Series \lang1032 44 \lang1033 would have to satisfy the expectations of the mi-\line \expndtw6 crocode for MPE structures. We also were not sure how Smalltalk\line \expndtw1 would behave in an environment with separate code and data segments\line \expndtw0 (enforced by hardware), as is the case on the Series \lang1032 44. \lang1033 We explored\line changing the microcode of the Series \lang1032 44, \lang1033 but we felt that the task would\line take too much effort, especially since none of the members of the group\line \expndtw4 had microcoding experience. We also considered modifying the\line \expndtw-2 Smalltalk-80 compiler to produce Series \lang1032 44 \lang1033 machine code, but Xerox ad-\line \expndtw4 vised that this would be difficult without a working Smalltalk system\line \expndtw-1 on which to do it. Because of these problems, plus time restrictions, we\line \expndtw1 decided to postpone putting Smalltalk on the HP \lang1032 3000 \lang1033 Series \lang1032 44.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3083\margrsxn2141\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1933\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6494 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 81}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Smalltalk-80 Project at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instead, we decided to implement our first Smalltalk-80 system in\line \expndtw3 Pascal under TOPS-20 on a DECSYSTEM-20 mainframe. Our selection\line \expndtw5 of Pascal as the implementation language reflected the investigative\line \expndtw3 nature of the project. We were not sure that a strongly-typed high-level\line \expndtw5 language could implement the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine as speci-\line \expndtw4 fied, and it was an interesting exercise to try to do it and find out for\line \expndtw0 ourselves.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Around the middle of March we felt we had enough information\line \expndtw6 about the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine to begin writing the interpret-\line \expndtw4 er. The first test release Smalltalk-80 image also arrived in March. The\line \expndtw9 DEC-20 implementation proved sufficient and useful for the early\line \expndtw5 stages when many subtle implementation points were unclear. In par-\line \expndtw3 ticular, the type and range checking in Pascal exposed many implemen-\line \expndtw9 tation problems as we progressed. Such experimentation with the\line \expndtw5 manner of the implementation continued throughout the project.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By the middle of April, the first version of the object memory manag-\line \expndtw4 er was operating. This version included dynamic memory management\line \expndtw7 via reference counts. A month later, the interpreter managed to exe-\line \expndtw3 cute up to the first invocation of a primitive method. We had included a\line \expndtw2 monitor which allowed us to observe the operation of the system via a\line \expndtw3 motion picture display (see p. \lang1032 103 \lang1033 for more details on the development\line environment). Two weeks later, on June \lang1032 5, 1981, \lang1033 we reached a project\line \expndtw4 milestone: we ran the first Smalltalk-80 test image successfully. This\line system on the DEC-20 became the first HP Smalltalk release.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Although the system ran, we did not have graphics capability until\line \expndtw4 the middle of the summer. This used an HP \lang1032 2647 \lang1033 terminal connected to\line the DEC-20 system, and unfortunately, it took \lang1032 50 \lang1033 minutes to display a\line \expndtw6 single Smalltalk screen. We knew from the start that our DEC-20 did\line \expndtw3 not have a suitable graphics device, so as early as April we began to ex-\line plore different approaches. We had already ordered a Lexidata \lang1032 3400 \lang1033 bit-\line mapped graphics system with an HP-IB (IEEE-488) interface for the\line \expndtw4 proposed HP \lang1032 3000 \lang1033 Series \lang1032 44 \lang1033 implementation. Using a National Instru-\line ments GPIB11-2 IEEE-488 interface, we could connect the Lexidata to\line \expndtw3 the VAX-1\lang1032 1/780 \lang1033 UNIX system owned by our department. After much\line discussion, we adopted this plan. We gained several advantages by\line \expndtw4 transferring our efforts to VAX UNIX. First, it allowed us to use our\line \expndtw2 Lexidata system as the graphics device. Second, it took advantage of the\line \expndtw5 UNIX and C expertise of a team member. Third, and most importantly,\line \expndtw3 it would give us a version of Smalltalk which could be portable across\line \expndtw1 UNIX engines.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the process of moving from the DEC-20 to the VAX, we converted\line \expndtw8 the entire system from Pascal to C. We developed editor command\line \expndtw3 scripts for the code conversion to automate the process as much as pos-\line \expndtw4 sible. Over the course of the next month, as we completed various parts\line \expndtw5 of the system on the DEC-20, we moved them to the VAX, so that by\line \expndtw6 June \lang1032 19 \lang1033 we had transferred the entire svstem.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1274\margrsxn1740\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9425\shptop10303\shpright9425\shpbottom12449\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4997 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 82{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi234\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The object memory was the first part of the system transferred to\line \expndtw7 UNIX. Because of its dependence on the memory architecture of the\line host machine, the memory manager was almost completely rewritten.\line \expndtw4 A version of it was running during the first week of May. We eventual-\line \expndtw2 ly rewrote about half of the original Pascal code as part of the move to\line \expndtw1 UNIX. The recoding was necessary partly for reasons of efficiency and\line \expndtw4 partly to take advantage of certain VAX features. In addition to rewrit-\line ing the object memory manager, we redesigned the execution monitor,\line \expndtw11 the memory inspector, and the input/output routines. Most of the\line \expndtw3 small, frequently-called Pascal procedures became macros. The inter-\line \expndtw4 preter and primitive routines remained relatively unchanged. We also\line \expndtw5 integrated the object memory inspector into the execution monitor, so\line \expndtw11 that one could switch between them to examine objects during\line \expndtw2 debugging. By the end of the conversion process, the HP Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw6 system consisted of \lang1032 7500 \lang1033 lines of C code. Remarkably, it executed a\line \expndtw3 dozen or so instructions after its first successful compilation on UNIX.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Although some work continued on the DEC-20 Smalltalk-80 system,\line \expndtw3 particularly with the graphics interface, most of our effort shifted to the\line \expndtw8 UNIX version. By the end of June we had fast and slow versions of\line \expndtw4 both the object memory manager and the bytecode interpreter. The fast\line \expndtw1 versions coded many of the C procedures of the slow versions as macros.\line \expndtw3 Although the fast versions were indeed faster, they were less useful for\line debugging since C debugger breakpoints could only be set on the initia-\line \expndtw2 tion or completion of a macro, leaving the intermediate steps of the ex-\line pansion inaccessible. In addition, the slow version of object memory\line performed much error checking which efficiency considerations had\line \expndtw5 eliminated from the fast version. Once the interpreter stabilized, we\line \expndtw2 discarded the slow version of it, primarily to simplify version manage-\line \expndtw3 ment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2228\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A prime reason for moving the Smalltalk system to UNIX was to\line \expndtw3 take advantage of certain tools, such as the C profiling facility \i prof. \i0 It\line \expndtw4 showed not only where the system was spending its time, but also how\line fast it was running. Using the information gained from the profiles we\line \expndtw8 were able to improve the performance of the system considerably\line \expndtw3 through the following techniques:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-245\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-245\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent245\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We used structure casts overlaying object memory for contexts\line \expndtw3 and other fixed-size objects to reduce access time. After setting up\line \expndtw5 the base address, these casts allow direct access of all elements of\line \expndtw7 the structure.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent245\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We expanded small procedures with few invocations in-line.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent245\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We recoded \i very \i0 small procedures as macros.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-245\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-245\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent245\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We cached frequently accessed information in special variables,\line \expndtw4 including the stack and instruction pointers as absolute addresses,\line \expndtw5 and the current active context and method as base addresses.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3242\margrsxn1972\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1940\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6505 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-25\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 83}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Smalltalk-80 Project at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When the second image release arrived in June, instead of abandoning\line the first image and focusing our attention on converting the system to\line \expndtw8 run the new release, we decided to work with the first image until it\line \expndtw2 was fully functional. We felt the effort would pay off when getting the\line \expndtw5 system to work on subsequent image releases. Unfortunately, the pur-\line \expndtw2 pose of the first image, as stated by Xerox, was merely to provide a ve-\line \expndtw4 hicle for debugging the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine interpreter. Thus,\line \expndtw8 there was, no documentation available on the user interface for this\line \expndtw6 first image; neither did we have a complete Smalltalk source listing.\line \expndtw5 Also, the Lexidata graphics system was not available until the end of\line \expndtw6 June. To help ease the problem of not having any graphics output be-\line fore that time, we modified the scanword primitive to display any text\line \expndtw4 on the session terminal that would normally appear on the graphics de-\line \expndtw2 vice. Thus, we saw text output a full two weeks before we saw our first\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi241\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By the middle of August, keyboard input was working, including an\line \expndtw5 appropriate mapping of the keys into the Smalltalk character set and a\line \expndtw4 keyboard polling scheme that did not cause the system to wait for a\line \expndtw5 character to be typed if none was in the buffer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In early September we started to convert the system for the second\line \expndtw7 image while finishing the implementation for the first image. After\line \expndtw4 solving a number of very elusive problems, everything except floating\line \expndtw0 point worked. We were able to do \lang1032 32 \lang1033 factorial in \lang1032 14 \lang1033 seconds, \lang1032 100 \lang1033 factorial\line \expndtw1 in \lang1032 90 \lang1033 seconds, and \lang1032 200 \lang1033 factorial in \lang1032 360 \lang1033 seconds. At this point our chief\line \expndtw6 concern was simply getting Smalltalk to work, and the slow operation\line \expndtw4 of the system was the least of our worries.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By the end of September we had recompiled our first method in the\line \expndtw6 browser, run the \i Turtle demo \i0 successfully, and managed to get the\line \expndtw4 snapshot facility working. Early in October we discovered how to de-\line \expndtw3 clare variables which permitted more sophisticated top-level program-\line \expndtw6 ming. By the middle of October, the floating point primitives were\line working. One annoying problem with testing the system was that the\line \expndtw7 code which executed after loading a Smalltalk snapshot refreshed all\line \expndtw4 the windows and required some time-consuming reorganization on en-\line try to the first browser pane. To get around this, we constructed \i work\line images \i0 which were snapshots of the system taken after these tasks had\line \expndtw2 concluded. We also generated a version of the system which did not in-\line \expndtw6 corporate the object memory inspector or the execution monitor. We\line \expndtw4 used this new sleek version for demonstrations and software develop-\line \expndtw7 ment, while we continued to debug with the original system. By this\line \expndtw2 time the original system contained over \lang1032 10,000 \lang1033 lines of C code. These\line \expndtw4 systems constituted the second release of HP Smalltalk and the first on\line \expndtw-2 VAX UNIX.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri40\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We began to make the changes necessary to run the second image in\line \expndtw3 the beginning of September. By the first week in October the system\line \expndtw9 executed up to the first invocation of the inputSemaphore primitive.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1669\margrsxn1323\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4993 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 84{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This was a major accomplishment since the second image had required\line \expndtw12 a significant restructuring of the system. There were changes in\line \expndtw5 input/output handling, primitive invocation, and process management\line \expndtw2 (for multiple processes). We also discovered that some of the class Oops\line \expndtw6 in the second image differed from those in the previous version. This\line \expndtw3 was a recurring problem with each new release and the system would\line \expndtw7 behave somewhat strangely until we remembered to check the class\line \expndtw1 Oops. By the end of the first week in November the system was \lang1032 95%\line \expndtw3\lang1033 functional with the second image, and only a few unimplemented prim-\line itives remained.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At about this time, Dave Patterson, representing the University of\line \expndtw3 California at Berkeley, obtained a research license from Xerox enabling\line \expndtw5 him to study the Smalltalk-80 system in his graduate seminar on com-\line puter architecture. Dave requested a copy of our UNIX Smalltalk sys-\line tem for his studies, and upon receiving clearance from Xerox to do so,\line \expndtw4 we delivered the second release HP system to him. We expended very\line little effort in porting the system to their VAX; it took them considera-\line bly longer to interface their graphics terminal to it. The experiences of\line the Berkeley Smalltalk group are discussed in Chapter \lang1032 11.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By early December the system was fully operational with the second\line \expndtw5 image. This system featured an early version of the new Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw6 user interface, and it became the third release of HP Smalltalk. We\line \expndtw5 provided this release to Berkeley for use in their studies, since it dif-\line \expndtw3 fered significantly from the previous one.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Also in December, Xerox delivered the third Smalltalk-80 image. The\line system required only minor modifications for this new image, so it was\line \expndtw8 running in a few days. However, there were some minor bugs that\line \expndtw5 needed attention before it was fully functional. More serious were a\line \expndtw2 number of problems with the Smalltalk code itself which made it neces-\line \expndtw3 sary to revise this image release (see p. \lang1032 96).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At this point we were ready to consider enhancements to the system.\line \expndtw3 We added a more flexible image handler, a garbage collector, an opti-\line \expndtw4 mized object memory system, and a method lookup cache. In addition,\line \expndtw2 we implemented the replaceFrom:to:with:startingAt: method as a primi-\line \expndtw4 tive to speed up string processing. The third image system with these\line modifications constituted the fourth release of a HP Smalltalk-80 sys-\line \expndtw6 tem. The project closed shortly after this release, and there have been\line \expndtw4 no significant structural changes to the system since. All project mem-\line \expndtw5 bers went on to new assignments in the research center.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Cancellation was a major, but not fatal, setback as we undauntedly\line reached another milestone on March \lang1032 13, 1982 \lang1033 when the system execut-\line \expndtw7 ed the testStandardTests benchmark without error. Nagging object\line \expndtw5 memory allocator problems had thwarted previous attempts to run the\line complete benchmark for some time. Unfortunately, as the project had\line \expndtw9 been closed for nearly a month, we could only spare a few moments}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1413\margrsxn1572\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4165\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8726 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 85}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 here and there to work on the system. Debugging was usually done in\line pairs, and we found it particularly difficult to coordinate our schedules\line \expndtw3 now that we were all working on different projects. But we were deter-\line \expndtw8 mined to attain full functionality, and, given our limitations, it was\line \expndtw5 quite an achievement.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Two weeks later, the fourth Smalltalk-80 image arrived from Xerox.\line \expndtw3 Again, the modifications for this image were minor and took only a few\line \expndtw6 days to make. Enhancements to this version of the HP Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw7 system include an improved object memory inspector, an increase in\line \expndtw0 the speed of drawing lines by implementing the drawLoopX:Y: primitive,\line \expndtw6 and a new hard-copy facility for the Printronix line printer and the\line \expndtw7 Versatec electrostatic plotter. The system also includes a mechanism\line for displaying Smalltalk screens on HP graphics terminals, at about\line \expndtw6 three minutes per image\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 an improvement over the previous \lang1032 50 \lang1033 min-\line utes in the first release.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 On April \lang1032 28, 1982 \lang1033 we released our fifth version of the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw4 system, which we call \i HP Labs Smalltalk-84 \i0 for historical reasons re-\line \expndtw10 lated to our internal release numbering. This version was the first\line \expndtw3 made available for use by staff at Hewlett-Packard Laboratories Com-\line \expndtw5 puter Research Center.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In parallel with the documentation and analysis of the system, we\line \expndtw3 made a number of modifications to the fifth release. Many of these\line modifications were a direct result of having to explain our implementa-\line \expndtw4 tion in this and other technical reports. Documenting the system in de-\line tail exposed many aspects that we had overlooked in our rush for\line implementation. In addition, the battery of tests used to generate the\line \expndtw5 statistics in the companion report suggested many subtle ways to im-\line \expndtw2 prove performance. In tests this revised version of HP Smalltalk-84 exe-\line \expndtw3 cutes from \lang1032 33% \lang1033 to \lang1032 500% \lang1033 faster than our previous systems. We released\line this sixth version on September \lang1032 13, 1982.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop1127\shpright8921\shpbottom1127\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1440\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex125\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Overview of{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex100\expndtw7 Smalltalk is similar to other language implementations at Xerox Palo}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the System{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex100\expndtw15 Alto Research Center in that it has its own virtual machine. The}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk-80 virtual machine consists of a byte-oriented instruction set\line or \i bytecodes \i0 together with an associated support environment. Porting\line the Smalltalk-80 system to a new machine involves implementing a\line Smalltalk-80 virtual machine emulator to execute bytecodes and man-\line age resources such as memory, time, files, and graphics. The distribu-\line \expndtw8 tion format for the Smalltalk-80 system is an \i image \i0 or \i snapshot,\line \i0\expndtw3 somewhat analogous to an APL workspace.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1632\margrsxn1370\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5015 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 86{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi234\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The virtual machine is in three parts: the bytecode interpreter, the\line \expndtw4 primitives, and the object memory. The interpreter dispatches and exe-\line \expndtw5 cutes the bytecodes. The primitives are the gateway through which the\line \expndtw4 system makes requests to the underlying resource managers. The mem-\line \expndtw5 ory manager maintains a dynamic object store for the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The interpreter is the core of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine emula-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interpreter{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw5 tor.   The  Smalltalk-80  virtual   machine  is  an  abstract  architecture}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 designed to execute Smalltalk and is similar to the P-machine of Pas-\line \expndtw4 cal. It is a stack machine with a byte-oriented instruction set encoded\line \expndtw3 chiefly to conserve space. Its most unusual aspect is the message send\line facility, roughly analogous to procedure call in conventional architec-\line \expndtw4 tures. There are no computational instructions as such because message\line sends perform their function, with special cases to expedite the more\line \expndtw3 popular ones (such as arithmetic and logical operations). The implemen-\line \expndtw4 tations of most bytecodes are only a few lines of code, but certain types\line of message sends require many lines of code for selector lookup and\line \expndtw3 context activation, since the worst case amounts to a late-binding proce-\line dure call.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our first implementation of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine inter-\line \expndtw4 preter was a literal translation of the specification we were reviewing:\line each Smalltalk-80 method in the specification became a Pascal proce-\line \expndtw2 dure in the DEC-20 version. When we moved the system to UNIX, we\line \expndtw3 converted many of these procedures into C parameterized macros and\line \expndtw7 consolidated several sets of interrelated procedures. These changes\line \expndtw6 helped to avoid the substantial overhead of procedure invocation for\line \expndtw5 most simple operations. In fact, often the consolidated code took less\line time than the original with the embedded procedure call. The parame-\line \expndtw1 terized macro facility gave us the best of both worlds\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 we moved many\line \expndtw3 short procedures into in-line code without sacrificing the documentation\line \expndtw5 value of the calling structure.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We structured the interpreter as a large switch statement encased in\line \expndtw4 an instruction fetch loop. Early on we made several significant perfor-\line mance enhancements:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-252\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-252\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We moved most of the simple bytecode emulation into the switch\line cases to eliminate procedure call overhead (which is considerable\line \expndtw0 on some hosts).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We cached Oops and addresses of the active context and method to\line \expndtw8 speed up the frequent bytecode fetch and stack push/pop opera-\line \expndtw-1 tions.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We  also  cached  the  Smalltalk-80  virtual  machine  instruction\line \expndtw4 pointer (IP) and stack pointer (SP) in the form of C address point-\line ers, instead of representing them as integer field offsets.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3607\margrsxn1613\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1944\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6505 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 87}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li40\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the cached IP and SP values change during execution, occasional-\line ly it is necessary to synchronize them with their values in object memo-\line \expndtw2 ry. Some of these occasions are:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0\tx511 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0\tx511 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change of current active context.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li511\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx511 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li511\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx511 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Access   of   the   current   active   context   (e.g.,    instVarAt:    and\line \expndtw-1 instVarAtput: primitives).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx511 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx511 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Certain object memory management operations (e.g., compaction).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The most complex operation in the interpreter is message selector look-\line \expndtw4 up. A message send enters the interpreter along with the class of its re-\line \expndtw2 ceiver and a selector. The \i current class \i0 below is initially the class of the\line \expndtw3 receiver of the message. The algorithm implementing class behavior in-\line \expndtw2 heritance is as follows:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi-259\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx518 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi-259\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx518 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Search for the selector in the message dictionary of the current\line \expndtw-3 class.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx518 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx518 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the selector is not found and the superclass of the current class\line \expndtw5 is not nil, then set the current class to its superclass and go to \lang1032 (1).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx518 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx518 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the selector is not found or the search reaches the end of super-\line \expndtw3 class chain, then give a \i message not understood \i0 error.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri14\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Unfortunately, even though message dictionary access is through a\line \expndtw3 hash function, the inevitability of selector collisions and the attractive-\line \expndtw4 ness of behavior inheritance cause a fair amount of \i both \i0 linear diction-\line \expndtw5 ary searching and superclass chain traversal. We added a simple meth-\line \expndtw4 od cache which eliminated much of the overhead, especially during\line repetitive tasks. The clumping of both selector and class values required\line \expndtw7 a hash scheme with unusual characteristics. The cache has \lang1032 509 \lang1033 ele-\line \expndtw6 ments and the hash function is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ((selector bitShift: \lang1032 -1) \lang1033 bitXor: (class bitShift: \lang1032 -1) \lang1033 \\\\ \lang1032 509) \lang1033 bitShift: \lang1032 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb173\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Using a large prime for the cache size distributes hashes more evenly\line \expndtw3 and gives performance comparable to caches four times the size. Unfor-\line tunately, any time the system changes the object pointer of a compiled\line \expndtw8 method, selector, or class, Smalltalk invalidates the entire cache. A\line \expndtw3 more sophisticated approach to cache management would be a welcome\line \expndtw1 addition.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri25\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have recently modified the interpreter to transfer the receiver\line \expndtw4 and arguments using the fast block move instruction on the VAX. This\line has significantly reduced the overhead of context initialization.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1699\margrsxn1297\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5011 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 88{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The system primitives are the roots of the Smalltalk-80 virtual ma-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex124\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Primitives{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw2 chine. One can view the primitives as a collection of system calls, a li-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 brary of procedures, or a set of very important methods. Primitives\line \expndtw1 exist for two reasons: Smalltalk cannot implement all system functions\line \expndtw0 (e.g., I/O and basic arithmetic), and more important, Smalltalk code of-\line \expndtw3 ten cannot perform well enough for certain special functions (e.g.,\line \expndtw-3 graphics).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We implemented most of the non-arithmetic primitives with UNIX\line \expndtw2 system subroutines, and added additional software layers to handle er-\line \expndtw1 ror conditions, to interface with other software and hardware compo-\line \expndtw8 nents, and/or to simulate particularly unusual operations. In all, a\line \expndtw-1 mass of over \lang1032 100 \lang1033 C procedures implements the more than \lang1032 80 \lang1033 primitives\line \expndtw0 included in our system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 While it is not possible to code some primitive operations in\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk, not all of them \i have \i0 to be implemented underneath the sys-\line \expndtw0 tem. Indeed, some primitives begin life as Smalltalk methods and enter\line \expndtw5 native code after careful evaluation of cost and benefit. As a result,\line \expndtw2 some primitives remain as optional native implementation candidates,\line \expndtw1 with backup Smalltalk code in place to execute if the associated primi-\line \expndtw0 tive routine is absent or fails. An example is the set of primitives which\line \expndtw2 handle arbitrary precision arithmetic, all of which have Smalltalk code\line \expndtw1 to execute if the primitive invocation balks.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The term \i primitive \i0 does not refer to the complexity of the routine,\line \expndtw0 though many are simple, straightforward functions. Rather, it describes\line \expndtw-1 the level of implementation\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 the lowest level possible. Our decision to\line \expndtw1 develop the system in portable high-level languages was in direct con-\line \expndtw-2 flict with this notion. However, since we could not modify the microcode\line \expndtw1 on our VAX, we had no alternative for the prototype system and the\line \expndtw4 performance of the primitives suffered accordingly. We feel that\line \expndtw-1 microcoded Smalltalk-80 virtual machine support could have improved\line \expndtw1 performance by an order of magnitude over our existing system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 One of the more unwieldy concepts in the implementation was that\line \expndtw1 of primitive failure: how and where to look for it, and what to do about\line \expndtw5 it. We implemented most primitives with relatively strict runtime\line \expndtw3 checking of the classes and value ranges of receivers, arguments and\line \expndtw0 their fields. The current system probably has too many checks for valid\line receivers and arguments, but in some cases these were vitally necessary\line \expndtw2 and helped us considerably during the debugging phase. In particular,\line \expndtw-1 the copyBits primitive contains a number of such checks. This checking\line \expndtw11 may be stricter than absolutely necessary given the way the\line \expndtw-1 Smalltalk-80 code actually uses the primitives. However, we did not feel\line \expndtw6 the specification of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine was precise\line \expndtw2 enough to rule out the need for these checks.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The addition of primitives for string processing\line \expndtw-4 (replaceFrom:to:with:startingAt:) and for line drawing (drawLoopX:Y:) re-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3576\margrsxn1640\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1951\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6509 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 89}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri4\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 suited in impressive performance gains for work in those domains.\line \expndtw5 However, we did not implement the arbitrary precision arithmetic and\line \expndtw6 the file system primitives. Currently the arbitrary precision arithmetic\line primitives default to backup Smalltalk code which is relatively slow.\line This is not a serious problem since most Smalltalk system arithmetic\line \expndtw3 stays within the signed 15-bit range of immediate small integer objects.\line Unfortunately, we will have to redesign most of the Smalltalk file sup-\line \expndtw4 port code to incorporate a reasonable interface for sophisticated hierar-\line \expndtw10 chical file systems (like UNIX). The file interface supported by\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk is very low level, using too much information about physical\line disc operation. We do not consider this approach appropriate even for\line \expndtw4 implementations without an underlying file system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi241\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Currently, we use a Lexidata \lang1032 3400 \lang1033 graphics system connected to a\line \expndtw14 VAX UNIBUS via HP-IB. The physical pointing device, a\line \expndtw5 Summagraphics Bitpad One with a four-button \i puck, \i0 operates through\line \expndtw3 the Lexidata graphics processor. The routines for graphics device inter-\line \expndtw5 action shield the Smalltalk-80 system from the details of these lower\line \expndtw9 levels. The Lexidata display memory is maintained by tracking the\line \expndtw8 class Bitmap object associated with the current display form. Every\line \expndtw4 time the bit map of the current display form changes, the graphics driv-\line er sends a copy of the altered area to the Lexidata. Since the at:put:\line primitive can potentially change the bit map, it recognizes when the\line \expndtw5 destination is the display bit map and invokes the graphics driver.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The graphics system required much work to get to its present condi-\line \expndtw1 tion. We began with a \lang1032 1280 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  1024 \lang1033 display optimized for white on black\line \expndtw5 graphics and a track ball with illuminated throw switches. An HP-IB\line \expndtw3 interface was designed especially for the Lexidata under a special con-\line \expndtw5 tract and this was our pathway into the host machine. As time passed,\line \expndtw3 pieces of the system fell into place. We developed microcode to use the\line \expndtw5 bit pad in place of the track ball. The bit plane logic was reversed to\line \expndtw3 display a white dot when the corresponding bit is \i off \i0 for black on white\line \expndtw7 graphics. Other parameters and microcode in the graphics processor\line \expndtw5 were modified to enhance black on white graphics, but there were lim-\line \expndtw2 its to this. Regardless of mode, we could not use the system at its maxi-\line mum resolution. We compromised for improved readability by using the\line \expndtw4 graphics processor to double the pixels in both the X and \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d5\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 dimensions\line \expndtw6 so that a \lang1032 640 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  480 \lang1033 Smalltalk \i screen \i0 neatly occupies the display. We\line \expndtw5 even have room at the bottom of the display for system messages. In\line \expndtw4 short, this experience was a baptism of fire in computer graphics.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri40\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We did try to optimize copyBits by implementing the bit copying op-\line \expndtw4 erations in VAX assembly language. In this experimental version of\line \expndtw1 copyBits we could copy an entire \lang1032 640 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  480 \lang1033 pixel bit map in \lang1032 0.08 \lang1033 second\line \expndtw5 (about \lang1032 10 \lang1033 times faster than the C routine). Unfortunately, the VAX bit\line \expndtw3 operations work in the opposite direction of that needed for bit map\line \expndtw9 manipulation. Our priorities prohibited modifying all of the bit map}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1093\margrsxn1895\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9389\shptop6793\shpright9389\shpbottom8312\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4997 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex116\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 90{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex116\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 management code for reverse bit ordering, which now included micro-\line \expndtw3 code in the Lexidata graphics processor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The current implementation of copyBits makes little use of the ex-\line \expndtw3 tended functionality of the graphics processor, such as area fill or poly-\line \expndtw5 gon moves. The Lexidata system does manage and update the cursor as\line \expndtw6 it tracks the mouse. In addition, having a screen bit map that is sepa-\line \expndtw4 rate from CPU memory permits us to continuously display its contents\line \expndtw3 without regard to processor operation. This is not the case with some\line \expndtw4 integrated graphics configurations where display refresh consumes a\line \expndtw3 significant percentage of CPU bandwidth.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi234\ri4\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The buttons on our mouse conform to the Smalltalk-80 color scheme:\line \expndtw5 red on left, yellow in middle, blue on right. Because we had \i four \i0 but-\line \expndtw4 tons, we could attach more functions to our mouse. The fourth one, the\line \expndtw5 green or \i HP \i0 button, acts as a shift key for the other three buttons. The\line \expndtw3 following extended functions are available on our system:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2758\tx4529 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2758\tx4529 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent249\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 green \lang1032 + \lang1033 red{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw4 Take a snapshot of the syste}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2758 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2758 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent249\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 green \lang1032 + \lang1033 yellow       \i0 Stop the system.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2758\tx4529 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2758\tx4529 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent249\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 green \lang1032 + \lang1033 blue{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw6 Print the screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa220\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The HP system offers two flavors of snapshot. Besides the standard\line \expndtw8 Smalltalk snapshot, we implemented a \i flash freeze \i0 snapshot which\line \expndtw4 saves the image without executing any Smalltalk code. This avoids the\line \expndtw6 rather tedious motions of the standard mechanism, including restora-\line \expndtw4 tion of the display screen. The user may select this alternative snapshot\line \expndtw5 through a mouse function or a monitor command. Since this facility\line \expndtw4 permits snapshots at arbitrary bytecode boundaries, we must preserve\line \expndtw7 more state information than is strictly necessary for saving an image\line \expndtw4 via the Smalltalk mechanism. For example, the keyboard input buffer\line \expndtw7 would not need to be kept if we had only the Smalltalk facility. The\line \expndtw4 state information falls into three categories:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3350\margrsxn1910\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1548\colsr709\colno2\colw4392 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7132\shptop738\shpright7132\shpbottom2250\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Monitor State}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb752\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Cached Objects}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Bytecode count and current bytecode. Although this infor-\line \expndtw0 mation is not needed to resume execution, it is nonetheless\line \expndtw3 useful, especially during debugging. When a system re-\line \expndtw0 sumes we always know the exact bytecode where it left off.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb54\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Oops of the current display form, the current cursor bit\line \expndtw4 map, the current input semaphore, the clock semaphore,\line \expndtw5 and any new process waiting to be run. In addition, we\line \expndtw4 save the Oops of all semaphores that have been signaled\line \expndtw2 due to the occurrence of an input event. The Oop of the\line current active context is saved by storing it in the active\line \expndtw1 context field of the current active process prior to taking a\line snapshot; similarly the cached stack pointer and cached in-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2902\margrsxn2329\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1926\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6491 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 91}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 struction pointer are saved by storing their current values\line \expndtw3 in the current active context.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2272 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex151\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 I/O State{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw-3 Cursor   position,    mouse    position,    last    mouse   buttons}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 pressed, current sample interval, input polling interval, the\line time at which to signal the clock semaphore, the keyboard\line \expndtw2 input buffer, and various indices and flags including the\line state of the link between the mouse and the cursor (linked\line \expndtw-1 or unlinked).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The system can print screen images on Printronix line printers,\line \expndtw5 Versatec electrostatic plotters, and Hewlett-Packard graphics terminals\line \expndtw3 in a variety of resolutions and magnifications. Anytime during a session\line \expndtw7 the user may request a copy of the screen via the mouse buttons or\line \expndtw4 monitor. The screen image is sent to a general bit map spooler.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We process external events and manage the input word buffer on a\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 virtual machine bytecode-synchronous basis. The system\line \expndtw2 recognizes external events by (just before executing the next bytecode)\line \expndtw4 explicitly polling the devices to determine whether they have changed\line state. One UNIX process implements all Smalltalk processes as well as\line the switching mechanism. The Smalltalk-80 system does not explicitly\line use the UNIX interrupt mechanism to support multiple processes. The\line system checks certain I/O queues after executing a fixed number of\line \expndtw5 bytecodes since the last inspection. The user sets this inspection inter-\line \expndtw4 val at system initialization.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The object memory itself is a simulator underneath the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw6 virtual machine emulator, implementing a small-object dynamic stor-\line \expndtw4 age manager within the large virtual memory of our host machines. As\line \expndtw6 mentioned before, there are two versions of our memory system. The\line \expndtw3 slow or protected version checks field index bounds and operand classes\line \expndtw5 at the interpreter/object memory interface. The fast version does no\line checking and consists mainly of C macros. The protected version was\line \expndtw3 very useful for detecting memory problems close to their source.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi241\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Access to objects in Smalltalk is via object pointers (Oops) which can\line \expndtw6 be either signed 15-bit immediate integers (small integer objects) or\line \expndtw4 15-bit indexes into tables of state entries and field addresses (indirect\line \expndtw2 object pointers). The 15-bit range of the object pointer (the other bit de-\line \expndtw7 cides between small integer and indirect pointer objects) limits the\line \expndtw3 number of indirect objects in the system to \lang1032 32767. \lang1033 This limit was not a\line \expndtw5 factor in our research.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The final Smalltalk-80 image is by no means \i small \i0 as it contains\line \expndtw4 over \lang1032 450 \lang1033 kilobytes of objects and requires at least half a megabyte with\line its object table. The suggested Xerox implementation uses a \lang1032 20 \lang1033 bit ad-\line dress into the object space, but our implementation uses 32-bit virtual\line \expndtw6 addresses. This required a reorganization of the object table into sepa-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1204\margrsxn1816\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9360\shptop6649\shpright9360\shpbottom12445\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4982 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 92{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex130\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2232\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 rate tables for the state entries and the field addresses, but the results\line \expndtw1 were favorable.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2225\shptop414\shpright8878\shpbottom414\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2225\fi0\ri0\sb443\sa0\tlul\tx3100\tlul\tx8874 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Table \lang1032 6.1 \lang1033 Hewlett-Packard SmaIltalk-84 Image Format{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4176 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Word{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 Entry}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2228\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4172 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0-1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1\lang1033 number of entries in the object table}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2232\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4172 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 2-3{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3\lang1033 the length of the object space in 16-bit words}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2225\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2225\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent1937\pnf25}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 system state object Oop}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2225\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2225\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent1937\pnf25}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 offset to the first entry in the table (the first offset)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi-1937\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  6\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi-1937\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent1937\pnf25}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 object state header of the first active entry in the object\line \expndtw-2 table}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi-1937\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  7\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi-1937\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent1937\pnf25}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 length in 16-bit words of the first active entry in the ob-\line \expndtw-1 ject table}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi-1937\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  8\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi-1937\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4162 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent1937\pnf25}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Oop of the Class of the first active entry in the object ta-\line \expndtw-8 ble}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2236\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4180 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 9{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4\lang1033 offset to the second entry in the table (the second offset)\line \expndtw-16\lang1032 10-12{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2\lang1033 the second active entry in the object table}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2250\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4180 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-23\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 13{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1\lang1033 offset to the next entry in the table (the third offset)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2250\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4180 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 14-end of entries{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 the remainder of the object table entries}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2243\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4180 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 followed by{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 the fields of the objects in the table}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2239\shptop32\shpright8877\shpbottom32\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi0\ri4\sb335\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Because of host machine differences and efficiency considerations, the\line \expndtw7 HP Smalltalk-80 system uses the image format in Table \lang1032 6.1 \lang1033 which dif-\line \expndtw8 fers from the standard interchange format. The HP format uses a spe-\line \expndtw7 cial coding scheme to indicate free entries in the object table, whereas\line \expndtw8 the interchange format does not eliminate the space wasted by these\line \expndtw9 entries. This format is also space efficient and easy to load and save.\line \expndtw7 The first \lang1032 8 \lang1033 bytes contain the image size parameters as 32-bit unsigned\line \expndtw8 integers. The number of object table entries is followed by the number\line \expndtw4 of 16-bit words in the object fields space. Next, is the Oop of the system\line \expndtw7 state object, used to restore Smalltalk and the support environment to\line \expndtw6 their exact condition before a snapshot.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The object table follows. It is \i not \i0 stored as an exact image of the sys-\line \expndtw5 tem object table (that would waste space). Each object table entry in the\line \expndtw6 image is preceded by a 16-bit offset from which the next table entry lo-\line \expndtw9 cation is derived. For example: if the first active Oops in the system\line \expndtw8 were \lang1032 12, 16, 18, 26, \lang1033 and \lang1032 56, \lang1033 then the offset values (in brackets) would\line \expndtw-7 be:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 [121 > 12 + [4] > 16 + [2] > 18 + [8] > 26 + [30] > 56}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi0\ri4\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The first of these offsets determines the location of the first used object\line \expndtw10 table entry. Thereafter the offsets are used to skip the unused entries\line \expndtw6 in between the active ones.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3017\margrsxn2225\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1915\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6476 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 93}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi241\ri0\sb245\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The field pointer portion of the object table entry in memory is ini-\line tialized by cumulatively adding the object lengths to the base address of\line the memory allocated for the object space. Therefore, each object table\line \expndtw4 entry in the snapshot consumes \lang1032 8 \lang1033 bytes: \lang1032 2 \lang1033 for the offset, \lang1032 2 \lang1033 for the head-\line er, \lang1032 2 \lang1033 for the length, and \lang1032 2 \lang1033 for the class. After the object table entries are\line \expndtw3 read in, a single mass read brings the object field space into memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Saving an image is similar, except that the object field space must be\line \expndtw2 written on an object-by-object basis, since we cannot assume contiguous\line \expndtw3 allocation on a used system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We conVert the image from the standard interchange format into the\line HP format by the following transformations:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Convert the interchange format state entries into the HP layout.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Move the class and length from the fields of each object into the\line \expndtw3 object table entry.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Swap   the   bytes   of   all   non-byte   objects   (our   hosts   use   low-\line \expndtw6 byte/high-byte ordering rather than the high/low of the image in-\line \expndtw3 terchange format).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Convert objects of class Float in the image from IEEE format to\line VAX format. The ranges are slightly different so the conversion\line \expndtw13 program indicates when it encounters an IEEE floating point\line \expndtw4 number which has no VAX format correspondent.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li504\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Create the special HP system state objects in which the snapshot\line \expndtw4 process saves relevant details so that the system may continue lat-\line \expndtw6 er from the same state.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri14\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The conversion process takes less than a minute and includes a consis-\line \expndtw5 tency check on the interchange format image.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The implementation of our object memory system differs from that\line in the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine specification. The system main-\line \expndtw3 tains two tables of object information: the first table contains the 16-bit\line \expndtw5 state entries while the second table has the length and class Oops plus\line \expndtw1 the pointer to the object fields for a size of 64-bits. Since the size of the\line \expndtw4 entries in each table is a power of two, access is fairly efficient. Previ-\line \expndtw7 ous single table versions used either \lang1032 96- \lang1033 or 80-bit entries which re-\line \expndtw4 quired multiplies by three or five for indexing.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each state entry contains an 8-bit reference count (used fully), and \lang1032 6\line \expndtw2\lang1033 flag bits for the following conditions:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li227\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx486 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li227\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx486 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference-count overflow and exemption.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li227\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx486 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li227\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx486 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object is permanent.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li227\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx486 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li227\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx486 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object table entry is available.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1400\margrsxn1581\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9353\shptop7880\shpright9353\shpbottom12380\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4997 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 94{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2516\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2776 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2516\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2776 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent260\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object contains pointers.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2516\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2776 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2516\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2776 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent260\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Byte object is odd length.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2516\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2776 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2516\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2776 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent260\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object has a mark (from garbage collector).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The two remaining bits are for future enhancements, such as a code bit\line \expndtw2 for compiled method objects. We moved the length and class Oops from\line \expndtw5 the fields of the object into this table for more efficient access. This\line \expndtw2 change significantly improved performance for the protected version of\line \expndtw3 object memory, and also reduced the load on the dynamic memory allo-\line \expndtw4 cator by two words per request, but in doing so increased static alloca-\line \expndtw6 tion by \lang1032 128 \lang1033 kilobytes. With an object table that is three-quarters full,\line \expndtw4 the actual waste from this is \lang1032 32 \lang1033 kilobytes, which is negligible in a large\line virtual memory system. The length entry contains the actual number of\line \expndtw7 words allocated to the object fields (not the same plus two for length\line \expndtw3 and class, as suggested). The pointer to the fields is a 32-bit virtual ad-\line \expndtw2 dress instead of a 20-bit quantity divided into a 16-bit offset and a 4-bit\line \expndtw4 segment number.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi245\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Early in the implementation, we defined the interface through which\line \expndtw7 the interpreter and primitives communicate with the object memory.\line \expndtw5 The strict adherence to this interface permitted an easy transition from\line \expndtw3 the DEC-20 to the VAX even though their memory systems differed\line \expndtw2 considerably. The interface consists of:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instantiate pointer, word, and byte objects.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Load and store pointer, word, byte and float values.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Get object length and class.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Test and convert small integer and pointer objects.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Decrement and increment reference counts.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Find first and next instance of a class.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Swap object pointers.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2768 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Query memory allocator state.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri14\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The memory system initializes all objects upon instantiation. Word and\line \expndtw2 byte objects need to have their fields set to zero, while pointer objects\line \expndtw3 require nil values or 2's, which most architectures cannot do efficiently.\line \expndtw9 The VAX has a block move instruction which, combined with a\line \expndtw5 predefined array of nil values, is an efficient solution to this problem.\line \expndtw2 The cost of using standard memory accesses to store nil in every field is\line \expndtw1 excessive (since the average size is about \lang1032 18 \lang1033 words).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference-count activity dominates the time spent in the memory\line system. We implemented a straightforward strategy and attempted to\line \expndtw6 reduce the amount of checking necessary to determine whether an ob-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3191\margrsxn2025\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1940\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6509 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 95}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri4\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ject actually must be reference counted. For example, the shortest path\line for the count decrement operation is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 check if small integer bit of Oop is set (is object a small integer?).\line \expndtw2 ifFalse:[ fetch the header of the object pointed to by the Oop.\line \expndtw1 check if exempt flag is set (is object free, permanent or overflow?),\line \expndtw-2 if False: \lang1032 [ \lang1033 decrement reference count,\line \expndtw4 check if reference count \lang1032 > \lang1033 zero.\line \expndtw1 ifFalse: enter deallocation routine. \lang1032 ] ]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb108\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The sequence for the count increment operation is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li256\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 check if small integer bit of Oop is set (is object a small integer?).\line \expndtw2 ifFalse:\lang1032 [ \lang1033 fetch the header of the object pointed to by the Oop.\line \expndtw1 check if exempt flag is set (is object free, permanent or overflow?).\line ifFalse:[ increment reference count,\line \expndtw4 check if reference count \lang1032 > \lang1033 zero.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li673\fi202\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 (note: the byte arithmetic wraps around to zero on overflow.)\line \expndtw2 ifFalse: set exempt flag in header to overflow \lang1032 ] ]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb270\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Measurements of our Smalltalk-80 object memory and similar dynamic\line \expndtw6 memory systems indicate that count maintenance consumes from \lang1032 20%\line \expndtw2\lang1033 to \lang1032 40% \lang1033 of execution time. The implementors of the M3L machine\super 1\nosupersub  dem-\line \expndtw3 onstrated the performance advantages of special reference-count hard-\line \expndtw6 ware. Reference-count maintenance is expensive because it congests\line \expndtw9 data paths with extra traffic and requires ALU cycles during every\line \expndtw4 count function. For example, a \i store pointer \i0 operation includes both a\line count down on the Oop at the destination and a count up on the source\line \expndtw9 Oop. The cost of \i store pointer \i0 was the motivation behind its\line \expndtw4 reimplementation in assembly language.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri32\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The availability of virtual memory on our host machine was an ad-\line vantage in the early phases of design. Makeshift memory systems could\line \expndtw5 run even while wasting a lot of space or thrashing over lists. Thus a\line gradual evolution of object memory implementations progressed until\line the better ones emerged. The current version implements:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A set of special allocation pools.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A mechanism for coalescing blocks on the general free list.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A marking garbage collector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri40\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The special allocation pools were the most significant addition to the\line \expndtw3 system, and they differ considerably from the free lists described in the\line \expndtw6 specification. There are four pools in the system: one for each of the\line three most popular sizes of pointer objects and a general pool for the\line \expndtw4 rest. The three special pools take advantage of the allocation frequency\line \expndtw10 of small contexts, points, and large contexts. Each special pool is a}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1089\margrsxn1882\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9446\shptop8950\shpright9446\shpbottom12334\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5000 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 96{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri0\sb274\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 linked-list of fixed-length pieces of memory allocated at system initial-\line \expndtw1 ization and permanently residing in that pool. Only transactions requir-\line \expndtw2 ing pointer fields of the three sizes can use the special pools so there is\line \expndtw9 no fragmentation in the pool. In our standard configuration, \lang1032 16\line \expndtw-2\lang1033 kilobytes of memory in the special pools satisfies over \lang1032 97% \lang1033 of all instan-\line \expndtw2 tiation requests.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri7\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The transaction traffic of the memory system is very different from\line \expndtw9 that of most programming environments. The system allocates,\line \expndtw5 accesses, and just as quickly deallocates megawords of memory in\line \expndtw4 chunks typically less than \lang1032 20 \lang1033 words. Since the special pools handle\line \expndtw2 nearly all memory traffic, we have fragmentation effects from only \lang1032 3%\line \expndtw1\lang1033 of allocations. Our memory allocator continuously coalesces and recy-\line cles this space on the general free list, thus preventing most fragmenta-\line \expndtw0 tion problems. In view of these facts, we feel that the actual long term\line \expndtw1 fragmentation is not serious enough to warrant \i dynamic \i0 compaction.\line \expndtw0 Compaction does happen whenever we save and reload an image. This\line scheme suits our view of personal computer usage patterns well\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 load-\line \expndtw1 ing an image, working for a while, then saving the results\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 and since it\line takes less than \lang1032 15 \lang1033 seconds to save and reload an image, we see little ad-\line \expndtw2 vantage to having a separate compactor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Cycles of inaccessible objects occur with reference-counting memory\line \expndtw-1 management schemes. Sadly, some of the occasions for cycle creation\line \expndtw1 involve fundamental concepts in the language, such as sending a mes-\line \expndtw2 sage with a block as an argument (where the pointer to the block con-\line \expndtw1 text is in the temporary variable area of the home context). Periodically\line we run an auxiliary garbage collector to sweep the object table of all\line \expndtw3 derelict objects caught in cycles. The cost of such a collector in CPU\line \expndtw0 time is high, so it is advantageous to avoid running it. Because contexts\line \expndtw-1 are most frequently involved in these cycles, the system invokes the col-\line \expndtw2 lector when either of the two special allocation pools devoted to con-\line \expndtw1 texts becomes empty. By setting the sizes of these pools at system boot\line \expndtw-1 we can vary garbage collection frequency. The collector also runs when-\line \expndtw0 ever the object table becomes full, but this is a far less frequent occur-\line rence. We found it necessary to lock out the garbage collector whenever\line \expndtw1 the reference counts do not accurately reflect the condition of object\line memory. The current system requires collector lock-out in only a hand-\line \expndtw0 ful of situations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Smalltalk-80 distribution process proceeded through five test re-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex125\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Implementation{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw2 leases. Each new release posed a variety of problems which we usually}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex119\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Issues{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw9 resolved after a month or so of changes and corrections. Smalltalk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 proved to be an incredibly robust system\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 so robust that it could con-\line \expndtw5 tinue to run after bugs had corrupted major sections of data or code.\line \expndtw0 For example, in implementing the primitive methods sometimes we did\line \expndtw2 not push a result on the stack before returning. In spite of this, the sys-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3072\margrsxn2137\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1930\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6498 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 97}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li32\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tern was able to execute many bytecodes before giving any indication\line \expndtw4 that something had gone wrong. Problems such as this were often diffi-\line \expndtw3 cult to diagnose, even with the sophisticated debugging tools at our dis-\line \expndtw-3 posal.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We list here some of the problems and issues faced in implementing\line \expndtw5 the Smalltalk-80 system in the hope that future implementors can ben-\line \expndtw3 efit from our experience. These problems are in six categories: arithme-\line \expndtw4 tic, primitive methods, input/output, object memory, programming, and\line \expndtw3 general problems.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Arithmetic Problems \i0 Many of the problems in the arithmetic area\line \expndtw4 were with floating point numbers. One problem was that we were not\line \expndtw7 certain which floating point representation was used in the first test\line \expndtw3 image. In particular, floating point objects in the first image were \i three\line \i0\expndtw9 words or 48-bits, which seemed to contradict the claim that the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 system used IEEE standard 32-bit single precision format.\line \expndtw5 As it turned out, it was IEEE format and the system simply ignored the\line \expndtw4 third word. But this was bad news\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 the VAX did not have IEEE stan-\line \expndtw7 dard floating point arithmetic at that time. We had to convert all ob-\line \expndtw6 jects of class Float to VAX floating point format and hope that none\line \expndtw7 would be outside the VAX range. Fortunately, no Smalltalk floating\line \expndtw5 point numbers have exceeded this limit. And after we went through all\line \expndtw3 of this, DEC introduced IEEE floating point support for the VAX.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi234\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In another situation, the routine that extracted the fields of floating\line point numbers treated the fields as small integers \lang1032 (-16384 \lang1033 to \lang1032 16383),\line \expndtw4\lang1033 when in fact they have a range of zero to \lang1032 65535. \lang1033 This problem occurred\line \expndtw7 in several places throughout the system and was the source of many\line \expndtw5 bugs. As a result of these complications, we were well into converting\line \expndtw7 the system for the second image before the floating point primitives\line \expndtw4 went into operation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi223\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another problem involved the initialization of the fields of\line \expndtw2 LargePositivelnteger objects. When we first installed the special alloca-\line \expndtw4 tion pools in the memory system, we set the fields of all pool objects to\line \expndtw3 nil upon deallocation. The only requirement to request memory from a\line \expndtw2 special pool was to be the appropriate size\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 there was no pointer object\line \expndtw3 check. Occasionally a large integer happened to be one of the sizes han-\line \expndtw12 dled by the special pools, and thus had nils in its fields. If one\line \expndtw3 performed bit operations such as bitOr: on a large integer, these nil val-\line \expndtw4 ues could affect the result. Indeed, we found that when one added two\line \expndtw7 large positive integers of certain values there were extraneous 2's in\line \expndtw2 some of the lower digits of the result. The solution was to correct the al-\line \expndtw5 locator to ensure that it only initialized pointer objects with nils.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A third arithmetic problem had to do with the \lang1032 // \lang1033 operation. It was\line \expndtw5 not clear from the original documentation for the first image just what\line \expndtw4 the definition of \lang1032 // \lang1033 was. As we began to use the system, occasionally we}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1720\margrsxn1257\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5000 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 98{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 would get a mysterious error 'Subscript out of bounds:' which did not\line seem to have any relation to what we were doing. We had spent quite\line \expndtw4 some time searching for the cause when we discovered that the system\line \expndtw8 was using the \lang1032 // \lang1033 operation to compute the index for a table, and be-\line \expndtw4 cause it truncated in the wrong direction, it often produced a negative\line \expndtw3 index. As the corresponding C operator differed from the Smalltalk def-\line \expndtw4 inition, we had some difficulty implementing the \lang1032 // \lang1033 operation correctly.\line \expndtw5 Because of this confusion, the specification now includes both variants\line \expndtw3 of the operation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri4\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'d0\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Problems with Primitive Methods \i0 There was confusion over the\line \expndtw4 extent of error checking to include in the implementation of primitive\line methods. Because there were no specific guidelines in the documenta-\line \expndtw2 tion, we decided to implement comprehensive checking. We checked the\line \expndtw7 class and range of the receiver and the arguments and their fields.\line \expndtw3 However, we soon encountered problems with class checking. In some\line \expndtw4 cases the receiver or argument need only belong to a specified class or\line \expndtw3 any subclass thereof. The class check in this situation could be time\line \expndtw4 consuming since it involves a traversal of the superclass chain. Rather\line \expndtw6 than endure this overhead, we removed the class check in these cases.\line \expndtw4 In general, we feel that this checking gave us a useful safety net to pro-\line \expndtw6 tect the system from corrupted code and other problems, but it is not\line \expndtw4 clear that such checking would be desirable in a production system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi241\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 During testing of the system with the slow version of object memory,\line \expndtw6 we encountered many \i load word \i0 and \i store word \i0 range errors in the\line \expndtw1 copyBits primitive. Some of these were caused by insufficient clipping of\line \expndtw5 the source and destination forms. We eliminated them by adding more\line \expndtw8 checks for clipping based on the sizes and relative positions of the\line \expndtw9 source and destination forms. Other range errors stemmed from the\line \expndtw4 way the BitBlt algorithm handled the transition from one row of the bit\line \expndtw7 map to the next. When doing a preload on the last word of a bit map\line row, the next word loaded in computing the destination word comes\line \expndtw4 from the next bit map row instead of the current row. This did no harm\line except when the last word of the last bit map row was at the end of the\line \expndtw5 bit map. If it was, the next \i load word \i0 generated an error. We fixed this\line \expndtw4 by checking whether the second \i load word \i0 goes past the end of the bit\line map. If it reaches the end of the bit map, we substitute zero for the sec-\line \expndtw2 ond \i load word \i0 value. This is not the most efficient solution to the prob-\line \expndtw7 lem, but it preserved our investment in the copyBits primitive (which\line \expndtw3 no one wanted to rewrite).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi241\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another problem with copyBits on the third test image involved the\line \expndtw4 coordinate arguments. In some instances, these coordinates contained\line \expndtw3 floating point numbers instead of small integers. We had to check for\line \expndtw8 floating point arguments in the makePoint routine. When the argu-\line \expndtw3 ments to makePoint were instances of class Float, the primitive convert-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3609\margrsxn1626\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1926\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6480 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 99}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ed them to integers before making them into a coordinate point. The\line \expndtw3 final image does not suffer from this problem.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There was a rather simple problem with the swapPointersOf primi-\line \expndtw4 tive. An early implementation swapped the reference counts along with\line \expndtw3 everything else. Since the reference counts must follow the Oop and not\line \expndtw8 the table entry, the system behaved strangely until we realized what\line \expndtw6 had happened and repaired the primitive.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb493\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'d0\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Input/Output Problems \i0 We had many problems with keyboard in-\line put and character mapping. With each new Smalltalk-80 test release,\line \expndtw2 we modified our keyboard input routine to accept numeric ASCII codes\line \expndtw7 instead of characters in order to determine the appropriate mapping.\line \expndtw3 This process became easier with each new release and particularly after\line \expndtw4 the second release which was the first to use the ASCII character set.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With the second release came more problems as we had to find a way\line to input control key codes for those not in the ASCII character set (e.g.,\line \expndtw2 control-0 to control-9). Since the Smalltalk \lang1032 I/O \lang1033 code could handle either\line \expndtw3 ASCII-encoded or unencoded keyboards, we designated an escape se-\line \expndtw1 quence to input unencoded control characters (control-\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 followed by the\line \expndtw5 character). In the final release, the Smalltalk methods for keyboard in-\line \expndtw4 put interfered with this scheme so we rewrote most of them.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We found it difficult to implement the polling scheme for keyboard\line \expndtw10 input on the first test image. Often the last character in the buffer\line \expndtw6 would not appear on the screen until the user typed another character\line \expndtw5 and it was interminably slow. Finally, after rewriting the keyboard in-\line \expndtw4 put primitives and maintaining better control of the buffer, the system\line \expndtw3 improved, although it was still slow. With the second test image, key-\line \expndtw5 board input was to be interrupt-driven and synchronized with sema-\line \expndtw10 phores. Initially we simulated this behavior with another polling\line \expndtw2 scheme. The system checks for keystrokes every \i\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i0\lang1033 bytecodes, and if any\line \expndtw5 are waiting, it places the key codes in an input buffer and signals the\line \expndtw3 input semaphore. Recently we developed a way to eliminate polling for\line \expndtw8 text input using an interrupt scheme based on UNIX intrinsics. This\line \expndtw4 has improved overall system performance by eliminating the terminal\line \expndtw3 buffer count check which was a part of polling.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our Smalltalk system manages the mouse in a similar manner.\line \expndtw3 Whenever the system polls the keyboard, it also checks the mouse posi-\line \expndtw4 tion and mouse buttons for activity since the last poll. If there are any\line changes, the system generates appropriate event words, places them in\line \expndtw11 the input buffer, and then signals the input semaphore. Various\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk routines also query mouse position directly, posing some\line \expndtw2 question about the need for our system code to do it as well. A series of\line \expndtw3 experiments suggested that the system was more responsive with mouse\line \expndtw2 position polling in both places.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1150\margrsxn668\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw8895\colsr475\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb518\sa0\tlul\tx5033 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 100{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2228\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Object Memory Problems \i0 The problems with the object memory\line \expndtw4 manager centered around allocation, reference count management, and\line \expndtw5 garbage collection. At one point there was infrequent trashing of some\line \expndtw4 of the fields of method and block contexts. Somehow the memory sys-\line \expndtw5 tem was allocating objects that were overlapping contexts. While we\line \expndtw1 never discovered the source of the problem, we solved it for a while by\line \expndtw2 isolating contexts into special allocation pools. The problem reappeared\line \expndtw4 however, when running the interpreter on the final image. Again we\line \expndtw7 redesigned the memory allocator, this time using linked lists for the\line \expndtw3 pools instead of a table of pointers. We have had no problems with ob-\line ject memory since.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 During testing of the interpreter on the second image we noticed\line \expndtw4 that the memory system was allocating many small contexts while\line \expndtw3 deallocating only a few through the reference counting mechanism. We\line later discovered that when performing sends we neglected to nil out the\line \expndtw6 argument fields of the sender after transferring the arguments. This\line \expndtw2 enabled cycles to develop involving block arguments and contexts. The\line \expndtw4 correct transfer mechanism eliminated over \lang1032 90% \lang1033 of these cycles.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Shortly after investigating these cycles in object memory, we decided\line \expndtw4 to add a marking garbage collector to our system. At the time, our pri-\line \expndtw3 mary motivation was to reduce the size of the special pools from which\line \expndtw4 the system allocated contexts. In the process of implementing the gar-\line \expndtw2 bage collector we had to determine the root objects from which marking\line \expndtw5 should proceed. We start from all cached object pointers, plus the Oop\line \expndtw0 of the Smalltalk object. We were then faced with the problem of deciding\line \expndtw7 when to activate the collector. Activation would certainly be tied to\line \expndtw4 some sort of low watermark in the allocator, but should the collector\line \expndtw9 proceed directly from there or be postponed to a safe period or a\line \expndtw5 bytecode boundary? There are times when the garbage collector could\line do much damage, so it is vital to ensure that it cannot run during these\line \expndtw4 periods. For example, sometimes the system temporarily increases the\line reference count of an object to ensure that it remains through a critical\line operation. During the interval between the artificial increment and dec-\line \expndtw5 rement operations the reference count is inconsistent, and intervention\line \expndtw3 by the collector would discard such an artificially protected object. This\line \expndtw2 is because the collector chases pointers to determine the number of ref-\line \expndtw8 erences to an object instead of relying on the reference count in the\line \expndtw4 state header. To prevent intrusion during such operations, we devised\line \expndtw3 critical section locks which disable and re-enable the collector. In addi-\line \expndtw4 tion, we make permanent (via a \i permanent \i0 bit in our object state head-\line er) those objects that are not to go away under any circumstances (e.g.,\line \expndtw-6 true).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Once our garbage collector was working, we reduced the small con-\line \expndtw10 text pool size from \lang1032 8192 \lang1033 to \lang1032 256 \lang1033 contexts, a saving of nearly \lang1032 300\line \expndtw2\lang1033 kilobytes of memory. Our investigations have shown that garbage col-\line \expndtw9 lections are relatively infrequent when browsing or text editing, but}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-367\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex76\expndtw0\dn6\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs40 \'f4}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3092\margrsxn2099\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1969\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6440 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 101}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Overview of the System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li40\fi0\ri36\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 that at least one occurs when compiling or decompiling a method,\line \expndtw3 reclaiming as many as \lang1032 500 \lang1033 objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri29\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Programming Problems \i0 As with any programming project involv-\line \expndtw3 ing more than one person, we found ourselves confronted with problems\line \expndtw7 of version management, communication among the members of the\line \expndtw2 team, and implications of changes made to the system. We found UNIX\line \expndtw4 tools to be very useful for managing these situations. In most respects,\line the project was a model of modern software management. All of us had\line \expndtw2 a background of software projects and we placed a heavy emphasis on\line software tools and techniques. In fact, we considered managing the soft-\line \expndtw4 ware effectively to be almost as important as implementing Smalltalk\line \expndtw3 itself. Some of the management tools are described on p. \lang1032 103.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri47\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We had a resource problem in having to share our host machines\line \expndtw5 with other projects, some of which involved signal processing. There\line \expndtw4 were times when the load on the system was so heavy that it was hope-\line \expndtw3 less to attempt debugging the system. For demonstrations we would get\line \expndtw4 the machine all to ourselves for acceptable performance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri54\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Global variables, of which there were many, gave us the usual prob-\line \expndtw5 lems with proper initialization and usage. We tried to localize state\line \expndtw3 variables as much as possible to only those modules that used them. In\line \expndtw5 spite of these precautions, global variables were the source of several\line \expndtw4 bugs in the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri58\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Macros were also a source of problems in coding the system. Since we\line \expndtw3 tried to optimize code as much as possible by using macros, we some-\line \expndtw5 times nested them several levels deep. Upon expansion, these macros\line \expndtw3 could get quite large\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 nearly \lang1032 900 \lang1033 characters for the \i push \i0 macro, for ex-\line \expndtw6 ample. Increasing the size of these macros could produce complaints\line \expndtw2 from the C preprocessor in some situations. We also had to parenthesize\line \expndtw3 macro arguments to ensure the proper order of expansion. A significant\line \expndtw4 difference between macros and procedures is that macro arguments are\line \expndtw3 evaluated at each appearance in the definition instead of only once on\line \expndtw4 procedure entry. For example, a macro may use an argument in a num-\line \expndtw3 ber of different places in the definition. If we pass it a function which\line \expndtw4 always returns the same value, the macro will operate properly. How-\line \expndtw7 ever, if we pass a function like \i popStack, \i0 then at each appearance of\line \expndtw5 the function in the definition it will return a different result (plus there\line \expndtw3 will be too many stack pops). We used temporary variables within mac-\line \expndtw5 ro definitions to ensure that arguments are evaluated only once.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri65\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We encountered an optimization problem with printing hard copy of\line \expndtw5 the Smalltalk screen. The routine for dumping the screen to the printer\line worked fine until we ran the system through the C code optimizer. Af-\line \expndtw4 ter that, the hard copy routine would print only garbage. Later we dis-\line \expndtw2 covered that the c2 code optimizer for the VAX produced incorrect code\line \expndtw7 for certain bit operations, and unless it has been changed, it still has\line \expndtw2 this bug.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1683\margrsxn1316\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5033 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 102{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i General Problems \i0 The biggest general problem was with the in-\line \expndtw4 complete and sometimes erroneous details of the implementation given\line in the book. The agreement with Xerox made it our task to debug the\line \expndtw6 documentation and image test releases. As we read through the chap-\line \expndtw3 ters, we found we had many questions and comments. For example, we\line \expndtw4 had difficulty getting nested blocks to work. Our system was not prop-\line erly initializing the instruction pointer for a nested block. The initial\line \expndtw5 instruction pointer was coming from the home context instead of the\line \expndtw4 active context as it should have been. The documentation did not make\line \expndtw5 this detail clear, and we wasted some time tracking it down.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another problem was the result of a similar oversight. In early Sep-\line \expndtw6 tember, many of the methods in the first image were operational, but\line \expndtw3 some still were not working and others gave 'message not understood'\line \expndtw7 errors. There was no pattern to the failures and the cause seemed al-\line \expndtw3 most impossible to isolate. We had just about given up when, in a mar-\line \expndtw2 athon debugging session, we discovered that the Smalltalk compiler was\line \expndtw1 using \i byte \i0 stores to set the 16-bit header \i word \i0 of new compiled method\line \expndtw4 objects. Because byte ordering on our host machines is the opposite of\line that on Xerox systems, a method header constructed in this fashion had\line \expndtw6 the bytes in reverse order. As this header encodes the number of liter-\line \expndtw4 als and temporaries as well as a special method flag, mere chance dic-\line \expndtw7 tated whether the byte-reversed header would affect the execution of\line \expndtw5 the method. The final system avoids this problem by having primitives\line \expndtw4 for compiled method creation and manipulation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2225\fi241\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A fascinating problem cropped up when we ran the Arc example pro-\line \expndtw6 vided with the third release image. The system mysteriously went into\line \expndtw8 an infinite loop and eventually ran out of object table space through\line \expndtw2 the activation of thousands of contexts! By tracing the execution of the\line \expndtw5 interpreter and looking at the Smalltalk source code, we were able to\line \expndtw4 determine the cause of the infinite loop. The method for the Arc exam-\line \expndtw2 ple included the following}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2434\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 anArc \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161 - \lang1033 Arc new.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2203\fi0\ri47\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since Arc class did not understand the message new, it defaulted to the\line \expndtw1 new message understood by Path class, the superclass of Arc class. The\line \expndtw6 method for new in Path class contained the statement}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2452\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 \'d4\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  \lang1033 super new initializeCollectionOf Points.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2214\fi0\ri22\sb169\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Following the superclass chain, we made the transition from the\line \expndtw6 metaclass to the class hierarchy by passing through Object class into\line \expndtw3 Class and eventually ended up in Behavior. Here the method new had a\line \expndtw9 primitive associated with it. However, since the receiver was an\line \expndtw3 indexable object, the primitive failed and invoked the backup Smalltalk\line \expndtw1 code which included}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2473\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 self isVariable ifTrue: [tself new: \lang1032 0].}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1441\margrsxn1532\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8658 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 103}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Development Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri11\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Since self was Arc class, we now followed a similar path starting at Arc\line \expndtw2 class looking for a new: message. Again we found it in Path class, and\line the associated method contained}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2527\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Tsuper new initializeCollectionOfPoints: anlnteger.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The infinite loop now begins, since we were again looking for the new\line \expndtw3 method in the superclass of Path class and so on. This switching back\line \expndtw2 and forth between new and new: continues until memory is exhausted.\line \expndtw0 The final image release avoids this problem by having the new and new:\line \expndtw1 methods in Path class use basicNew instead of new. The method for\line \expndtw-2 basicNew in Behavior has backup Smalltalk code which uses basicNew:\line \expndtw2 instead of new:. Since Path class does not understand the basicNew:\line \expndtw0 message, the basicNew: message in class Behavior is executed and it\line succeeds, avoiding the infinite loop.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We had problems in making the transition of our system from one\line \expndtw0 release of a Xerox Smalltalk image to the next. These problems were\line \expndtw2 generally minor, although annoying. The most extensive changes oc-\line \expndtw5 curred in going from the first image to the second. In general, there\line \expndtw3 were differences in some class Oops, and sometimes the structures of\line \expndtw0 some objects changed. Unfortunately, in most cases we had to discover\line these differences ourselves. These should not be problems for future\line \expndtw6 implementors since the documentation has been rewritten and there\line \expndtw0 will be only one Smalltalk image to deal with. This image is the fifth or\line \expndtw3 final one referred to herein.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop1163\shpright8924\shpbottom1163\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1472\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Development{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw-3 An    extensive    collection    of    development    tools    complements   the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Environment{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw1 Smalltalk system at Hewlett-Packard. These tools compose our software}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2257\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 development environment, which consists of the following layers:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent244\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Smalltalk-80 system with its debugger.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-245\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-245\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent245\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Smalltalk-80 virtual machine execution monitor and object\line \expndtw-1 memory inspector.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-245\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-245\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent245\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The UNIX operating system\super 2\nosupersub , SCCS version controller, \i make \i0 sys-\line \expndtw0 tem builder, \i sdb \i0 symbolic debugger, and \i prof \i0 execution profiler.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri11\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 These levels offer access to different aspects of execution. For example,\line \expndtw2 we have the capability of setting breakpoints at the Smalltalk-80 state-\line \expndtw4 ment level (Smalltalk debugger), the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine\line \expndtw1 bytecode level (Smalltalk-80 virtual machine monitor), or the C source\line level \i (sdb). \i0 Often we use all three mechanisms to attack a problem from}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1668\margrsxn1313\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5036 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 104{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri11\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 each particular level of detail. The same goes for performance evalua-\line \expndtw3 tion as we have the capability to tap in at any level to spy on the activi-\line ties of the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We structured the first level of our development environment using\line \expndtw3 the hierarchical file system of UNIX. A read-only copy of the latest sta-\line \expndtw5 ble version of the system is in a source directory, and each of the proj-\line ect members owns a subdirectory under it. Each member can modify,\line \expndtw2 test, and debug sections of code in his subdirectory without affecting the\line other members who may also be debugging. This feature was made pos-\line \expndtw5 sible by the UNIX \i make \i0 facility for creating, maintaining, and install-\line \expndtw6 ing software. Someone working in a subdirectory who wants to put\line \expndtw3 together a system using some changed source files simply asks \i make \i0 to\line \expndtw5 build it. \i Make \i0 determines whether any source needs to be recompiled,\line \expndtw8 and then loads the new code from the subdirectory and the rest from\line \expndtw2 the source directory. Duplication is minimal as there is only one copy of\line \expndtw4 the master source plus those pieces that people are working on in their\line \expndtw1 subdirectories.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Source Code Control System (SCCS) was our primary tool for\line \expndtw5 dealing with version management issues. Whenever one wants to make\line \expndtw4 a change to a piece of the system, one asks SCCS for a modifiable copy\line \expndtw1 of the latest version. If no one else has a copy out, SCCS acquiesces and\line \expndtw2 places the copy in the subdirectory. After successful testing of the modi-\line \expndtw3 fications, one sends the modified copy back to SCCS and informs the\line system version manager of the change. The version manager asks SCCS\line \expndtw2 to place a read-only copy of the new files into the source directory, and\line \expndtw7 then gets \i make \i0 to build a new system. It is important that only well\line \expndtw8 tested modifications make it to the source directory since all of the\line \expndtw3 work in the subdirectories depends on the stability of that code. Fig. \lang1032 6.1\line \expndtw2\lang1033 depicts an execution monitor command menu, and Fig. \lang1032 6.2 \lang1033 shows an ex-\line \expndtw3 ecution monitor chain of context display.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2498\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 type \lang1032 ?? \lang1033 for the list of commands}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb475\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 System multiple step ms, single step ss, continue cs, run rs}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3085\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 display state ds, update freq us, breakpoint bs, trace ts}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2491\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Image load li, inspect ii, save si}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Graphics reset rg, print pg}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Other monitor me, statistics sc, context chain cc, receiver update ru}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3085\fi-590\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Window Help wh, Banner wb, Inspector wi, Smalltalk ws\line Context we, Receiver wr, Method wm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3092\fi-612\ri436\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Keys TC interrupt, TV quote, TX break, \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c6\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 suspend, i\\core dump\line \expndtw-7\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 upArrow, \lang1032 <- \lang1033 left Arrow, TT ifTrue:, TF if False:, \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 control\line \expndtw-4 ESC select, DEL delete, TW delete Word, \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  [0-9] \lang1033 fonts}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1033\fi1480\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 * * \lang1033 default radix is Decimal \lang1032 - \lang1033 prefix with \lang1032 0 \lang1033 for Octal, Ox for Hex\line \b\expndtw2 Figure \lang1032 6.1         \b0 ** \lang1033 Shell sh \lang1032 * \lang1033 Help \lang1032 ?? * \lang1033 Exit qq}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3578\margrsxn1631\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1940\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6419 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 105}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Development Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li238\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 context   class   method receiver    class}\par \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-180 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb