\intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 #40}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex105\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 6aa3de70da87701276}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3563\margrsxn1664\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1926\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6401 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 107}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Development Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb270\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 crepancies as well as those with permanent or overflow bits set. Usually\line all objects, except for a handful, have valid reference counts. The few\line \expndtw5 exceptions are the objects cached by the system (e.g., the active con-\line \expndtw1 text), and the Smalltalk object (SystemDictionary) which has an artificial-\line \expndtw4 ly increased reference count to prevent accidental deallocation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our system actually uses two displays: a graphics display for\line \expndtw8 Smalltalk and a CRT terminal for the monitor and UNIX. Smalltalk,\line \expndtw6 the monitor, and UNIX share the terminal keyboard. The presentation\line \expndtw4 of information from the monitor facilities posed an interesting problem.\line \expndtw5 It was impossible to squeeze everything into the \lang1032 24 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  80 \lang1033 terminal for-\line \expndtw3 mat so we took a lesson from Smalltalk itself and implemented seven\line overlapping windows for the user interface. The user can select which\line \expndtw6 windows to view and the system preserves the contents across selec-\line \expndtw2 tions. General command interaction uses a window consisting of the top\line \expndtw5 four lines of the screen and the other windows do not overlap this re-\line gion. Windows for diagnostic, inspector, and Smalltalk interaction fit\line into the other twenty lines. Fig. \lang1032 6.4 \lang1033 shows the three vertically-over-\line \expndtw3 lapping \lang1032 20 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  40 \lang1033 windows which display the active context, receiver, and\line compiled method components of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine state.\line \expndtw4 Most of the context and receiver information is in the leftmost \lang1032 20 \lang1033 col-\line \expndtw5 umns of their windows, so having those windows overlap was not a\line \expndtw2 problem. The user can also close the receiver window to get full view of\line \expndtw5 the context and the method.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft14\shptop410\shpright6674\shpbottom410\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri40\sb464\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 6.2 \lang1033 Notation for Displaying Objects}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft22\shptop4\shpright6668\shpbottom4\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb184\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 class{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 example{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }description}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Small Integer{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-5\lang1032 0205 \lang1033 258D{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3 small integer value in decimal}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Special Object{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0\lang1032 0006 \lang1033 True{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }name of object}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Symbol{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3\lang1032 0132 \lang1033 U \lang1032 " \lang1033 Array \lang1032 "{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1\lang1033 (unique strings) \i U \i0 followed by string}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 String{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6\lang1032 0664 \lang1033 S \lang1032 " \lang1033 a string\lang1032 "{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw1\lang1033 S \i0 followed by string}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Association{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3\lang1032 0008 \lang1033 A 6f20 \lang1032 < \lang1033 Arc \lang1032 >{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw1\lang1033 A \i0 followed by value field}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Metaclass{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 73ea C \lang1032 < \lang1033 File \lang1032 >{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw-1\lang1033 C \i0 followed by class name}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1386\tx3442 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Other{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 72cc \lang1032 < \lang1033 Turtle \lang1032 >{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3\lang1033 class name within angle-brackets}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7\shptop-11\shpright6667\shpbottom-11\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb317\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The user can have the context, receiver, and method windows of the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 virtual machine state display dynamically updated with\line \expndtw2 execution. The frequency of update is selectable, and performance is ac-\line \expndtw5 ceptable even for small granularity updates. One can watch the stack\line \expndtw4 values, temporaries, and instance variables change, and display en-\line \expndtw5 hancements indicate the current positions of the stack pointer and pro-\line \expndtw2 gram counter. We follow a convention for single-line object descriptions\line \expndtw3 in the window system. In all cases, we display the object pointer in\line \expndtw7 hexadecimal format, and the additional object-specific information is}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1811\margrsxn1167\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5036 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 108{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri18\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 described in Table \lang1032 6.2. \lang1033 The motion-picture Smalltalk-80 virtual ma-\line \expndtw0 chine state display proved invaluable to the debugging effort. The per-\line formance of the system under full display is quite good.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Both the monitor and the inspector can escape to the UNIX shell to\line \expndtw2 execute commands there. The monitor intercepts certain control char-\line acters and will halt execution, kill the system, or dump the core image\line to a file on request. As a special feature, the monitor traps all fatal er-\line \expndtw0 rors and recursively invokes a new copy of itself to hunt for the cause.\line Then we can use the inspector to fix wayward values and continue exe-\line \expndtw-3 cution.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7\shptop868\shpright8928\shpbottom868\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb1184\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2272 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Future{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw4 As Smalltalk enters the real world there is much speculation over fu-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2272 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Directions{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw4 ture directions for the system. Although the Smalltalk-80 distribution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 test process was thorough in eliminating problems in the system, many\line issues remain unresolved such as network interfacing and multiple in-\line \expndtw2 heritance, and there has been little time to implement the more ingen-\line \expndtw0 ious optimizations. Hence there is much room for improvement in the\line \expndtw-5 design.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 All of the test site implementations used high-level or assembly lan-\line \expndtw5 guage to implement an interpreter for the Smalltalk-80 virtual ma-\line \expndtw0 chine. Unfortunately, this approach produced significantly degraded\line \expndtw2 performance compared to native mode execution of the processor (a\line \expndtw-2 penalty of \lang1032 40 \lang1033 to \lang1032 200 \lang1033 times slower). The elimination of the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw0 virtual machine as an intermediate stage could boost performance con-\line siderably, but this would necessitate the implementation of a\line \expndtw-2 Smalltalk-80-to-native-code compiler.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Early in our project we had investigated a native compiler strategy.\line \expndtw3 Our bytecode execution frequency figures indicate that such an ap-\line \expndtw0 proach would be worthwhile. Over \lang1032 70% \lang1033 of the Smalltalk-80 virtual ma-\line \expndtw4 chine bytecodes executed in normal usage (push, pop, store, jump,\line \expndtw2 return) map to at most a few instructions in an ordinary computer. In\line \expndtw1 addition, nearly \lang1032 85% \lang1033 of all bytecodes executed (those above plus the\line \expndtw0 special sends) translate to in-line sequences of instructions, including a\line \expndtw1 procedure call escape for the exceptional cases. Only about \lang1032 15% \lang1033 of\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk execution requires the message lookup process where proce-\line \expndtw2 dures perform the late binding. Because the Smalltalk-80 virtual ma-\line \expndtw12 chine compiler is written in the Smalltalk-80 language, the\line \expndtw-1 development of a native code version requires a stable Smalltalk-80 sys-\line \expndtw0 tem to begin with.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In spite of the myriad optimizations proposed and implemented, a\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk-80 system seems doomed to lower performance than more}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3578\margrsxn1642\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1919\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6397 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 109}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Future Directions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 traditional programming systems. There are two areas where Smalltalk\line \expndtw3 loses to other languages. One is the \lang1032 15% \lang1033 of execution which requires\line \expndtw2 message lookup. Message cache schemes lessen the penalty, but speed is\line \expndtw5 still impaired by the symbol matching and indirection. The other area\line involves the automatic memory management provided by reference\line counting and/or garbage collection mechanisms. The memory manager\line is very expensive to operate and it is not clear that performance prob-\line \expndtw7 lems in this area can be solved. This may impact the ultimate success\line \expndtw8 or failure of Smalltalk, as users of traditional programming systems\line \expndtw5 may be reluctant to give up performance in exchange for Smalltalk.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi245\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Alternative implementation hosts also merit consideration. Although\line \expndtw3 we have yet to implement the system on an HP \lang1032 3000, \lang1033 we have derived\line \expndtw5 performance estimates for the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine on the HP\line \expndtw3\lang1032 3000 \lang1033 and the HP \lang1032 1000 \lang1033 A Series minicomputer systems. With its stack-\line \expndtw1 oriented architecture, an HP \lang1032 3000 \lang1033 could be capable of \lang1032 200,000 \lang1033 bytecodes\line \expndtw6 per second. The HP \lang1032 1000 \lang1033 A Series is a more attractive candidate be-\line \expndtw3 cause of its excellent price/performance ratio and compact packaging.\line Performance in excess of \lang1032 100,000 \lang1033 bytecodes per second might be possi-\line \expndtw4 ble for an A Series processor with writable control store.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi245\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implementations can benefit from special hardware for functions\line \expndtw2 which do not perform well on general-purpose processors. Xerox solved\line this problem by adding special-purpose microcode for such functions to\line \expndtw6 their general-purpose hardware. We have already commented on how\line \expndtw1 the performance of our primitives suffered from lack of access to micro-\line \expndtw4 code or hardware support. This situation is most serious for graphics\line \expndtw8 operations where microcode or instruction cache support for block\line \expndtw4 moves would expedite matters by at least a factor of \lang1032 10.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the Smalltalk-80 system is geared toward the personal comput-\line \expndtw4 ing community, the microprocessor is a natural area for investigation.\line \expndtw5 However, most microprocessors do not have modifiable control stores\line and so special-purpose hardware may be the only efficient option to\line \expndtw2 achieve acceptable performance. In fact, advances in VLSI design and\line \expndtw4 fabrication have promoted the development of many such special-pur-\line pose chips for graphics control, memory management, and I/O support.\line \expndtw6 A native code compilation system integrating these chips with one of\line \expndtw14 the new \lang1032 16- \lang1033 or 32-bit microprocessors could be an excellent\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 host.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi230\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There is also the issue of having an operating system alongside or\line underneath an implementation of the Smalltalk-80 system. The HP im-\line \expndtw7 plementation runs on top of UNIX, which has significant advantages\line \expndtw5 and disadvantages over the strategies of other implementors in the test\line \expndtw3 program. UNIX has many features which assist the development of pro-\line \expndtw5 totype software. For example, the debugging utilities are key compo-\line \expndtw3 nents of our development environment. However, there are a few points\line \expndtw9 where the integration of UNIX into Smalltalk could have helped us,}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1808\margrsxn1179\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5040 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 110{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri4\sb248\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 such as in multiprocess control. As it stands we simulate this feature on\line UNIX, thus incurring additional overhead. The file system was another\line area where UNIX could have helped. By integrating the UNIX hierar-\line chical file system into Smalltalk, we could have structured the system\line \expndtw-1 source into a hierarchical collection of files reflecting class relationships\line \expndtw1 instead of the single large file currently used.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Smalltalk-80 language itself is another direction for research.\line \expndtw-1 We find the language to be excellent for systems programming of appli-\line \expndtw0 cations with superior user interface characteristics. Contrary to popular\line \expndtw3 belief, we do not feel that it is a programming language for the naive\line \expndtw-1 user community. Instead, it has proven itself to be a very good language\line \expndtw2 for developing application kits to enable naive users to solve problems\line \expndtw-1 in limited domains, as in \i ThingLab\super 3\nosupersub . \i0 The object orientation lends itself\line especially well to the programming of graphics problems. As other lan-\line \expndtw0 guages incorporate similar graphics capabilities, it will be interesting to\line \expndtw2 note how their user interfaces compare with Smalltalk's.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 There have been proposals to include types in Smalltalk, either\line \expndtw1 through inference or declaration\super 4\nosupersub\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'b7\super\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 5\nosupersub . Each approach has its own merits,\line but more work needs to be done to determine the best way to integrate\line \expndtw4 type structure into the system. In particular, we look forward to ways\line \expndtw1 of using type information to optimize certain operations in the system.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop691\shpright8921\shpbottom691\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1008\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Conclusions{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw7 The Hewlett-Packard team was curious about Smalltalk and we ap-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 proached the task of implementing the Smalltalk-80 system with both\line \expndtw5 reservations and enthusiasm. And indeed, some of our reservations\line \expndtw0 were warranted: the Smalltalk-80 language did not prove to be a work-\line ingman's programming language and did not perform well enough on\line \expndtw6 conventional hardware. The mystery surrounding Smalltalk for so\line \expndtw0 many years had inspired grand expectations in us. In the end we were\line \expndtw2 disappointed, but our curiosity was sated and we are perhaps better for\line \expndtw-1 it. The concepts embodied in Smalltalk have a heritage which goes back\line \expndtw3 over \lang1032 20 \lang1033 years, yet many have not achieved widespread acceptance by\line the research or marketing communities. There is little doubt now that\line \expndtw-2 the new economics of VLSI will make many of these concepts standard\line \expndtw0 features of computing products.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 It remains to be seen however, whether Smalltalk itself will succeed\line \expndtw13 in the marketplace. All during the project we dreamed of a\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 product. These visions gradually became a nightmare as\line \expndtw2 we discovered the pitfalls of an environment with the flexibility of\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk. A Smalltalk-80 user can break the system by changing a sin-\line \expndtw1 gle method. More important, one can make it incompatible with the\line \expndtw6 distribution just as easily. From a product support standpoint, this\line \expndtw1 would be chaos. Software distribution would be difficult to impossible.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1292\margrsxn1689\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4158\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8644 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 111}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 References}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa1188\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Application software could assume very little about the runtime envi-\line \expndtw3 ronment, since it might have been modified extensively by the user. In\line \expndtw4 tracing down system problems, it would be tricky to determine whether\line the problem was in the distribution or in the changes made by the user.\line \expndtw5 One solution would be to restrict what a user can change, but this de-\line \expndtw3 feats the whole purpose of Smalltalk. We can not see how an organiza-\line \expndtw4 tion could hope to provide any form of comprehensive support for a\line Smalltalk product. The only way we can envision Smalltalk in the mar-\line \expndtw5 ketplace is as an unsupported package.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1324\margrsxn1689\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1540\colsr673\colno2\colw6681 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-22\shptop-317\shpright8899\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Acknowledg-\line \expndtw-6 ments}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We would like to acknowledge the tenacious efforts of our fellow mem-\line \expndtw4 bers of the HPL Smalltalk team in coping with the vagaries of a distri-\line \expndtw6 bution test program. In particular, Alec Dara-Abrams for stalking the\line \expndtw4 wild interpreter, Bob Shaw for graphics support beyond the call of duty\line \expndtw5 on the Lexidata, and Bob Ballance for his work on the interpreter and\line \expndtw3 primitives, especially in floating point and process control. Our project\line leader, Jim Stinger, provided technical arbitration and primitive design.\line \expndtw5 Joe Falcone contributed in the object memory, development environ-\line ment, and performance evaluation areas.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are others who contributed to the project in advisory and man-\line \expndtw6 agerial capacities. Ching-Fa Hwang, Dan Conway, and Sam Gebala\line \expndtw8 helped review the chapters of the Smalltalk book and participated in\line the early discussions about target machines. We would also like to\line \expndtw6 thank our department manager, Ted Laliotis, for his support through-\line out and beyond the life cycle of the project. None of this would have\line \expndtw2 been possible without the work of Jim Duley and Paul Stoft, who were\line \expndtw4 responsible for bringing the Smalltalk-80 system to Hewlett-Packard.\line Finally, we express our appreciation to David Casseres, Ann Falcone,\line \expndtw8 Karri Kaiser, Glenn Krasner, Rick Meyers, and Steve Muchnick for\line \expndtw5 their editorial assistance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi234\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All of us involved with the Smalltalk-80 system at Hewlett-Packard\line appreciate the efforts of the Xerox Software Concepts Group. We grate-\line \expndtw5 fully acknowledge their support and tolerance during the test program.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1292\margrsxn1696\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1181\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1292\margrsxn1696\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1299\colsr1206\colno2\colw6415 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-259\shpright8917\shpbottom-259\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 References}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li245\fi-245\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 1. \lang1033 Sansonnet, J. P., et. al., "Direct Execution of LISP on a List-Di-\line \expndtw3 rected Architecture", Proceedings at the Symposium on Architec-\line \expndtw14 tural Support for Programming Languages and Operating\line \expndtw1 Systems, Palo Alto, CA, pp. \lang1032 132-139, \lang1033 March \lang1032 1982.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1901\margrsxn1066\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5033 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-23\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 112{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Implementation at Hewlett-Packard}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-230\slmult0\tx2794 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-230\slmult0\tx2794 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Ritchie, Dennis M., and Thompson, Ken, "The UNIX Time-Shar-\line \expndtw2 ing System" \i Comm. ACM \i0 vol. \lang1032 17, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 2, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 365-375, \lang1033 July \lang1032 1974.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2794 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2794 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Borning, Alan H., "The Programming Language Aspects of Thing\line \expndtw5 Lab, A Constraint-Oriented Simulation Laboratory", \i ACM Trans-\line \expndtw4 actions of Programming Languages and Systems \i0 vol. \lang1032 3, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 4, \lang1033 pp.\line \expndtw-2\lang1032 353-387, \lang1033 Oct. \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2794 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2794 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Borning Alan H., and Ingalls, Daniel \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 . \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 "A Type Declaration\line \expndtw3 and Inference System for Smalltalk", Ninth Symposium on Princi-\line \expndtw4 ples of Programming Languages, pp. \lang1032 133-141, \lang1033 Albuquerque, NM,\line \expndtw-10\lang1032 1982.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2794 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2794\fi-263\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2794 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Suzuki, Nori, "Inferring Types in Smalltalk", Eighth Symposium\line \expndtw4 on Principles of Programming Languages, Williamsburg, VA, pp.\line \expndtw-4\lang1032 187-199, 1981.}\par \pard \plain \li2902\ri367\sb101\sa0{\pict\picw10007\pich12484\picwgoal5673\pichgoal7077\jpegblip
}\par\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1296\margrsxn1268\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4140\shptop-302\shpright8892\shpbottom-302\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4136\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 The Dorado Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw6 Implementation:\line \expndtw10 Hardware Architecture's\line \expndtw7 Impact on Software\line \expndtw10 Architecture}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4129\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 L. Peter Deutsch}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4133\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa770\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Software Concepts Group\line \expndtw2 Xerox Palo Alto Research Center\line Palo Alto, California}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1306\margrsxn1703\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1011\colsr1224\colno2\colw6663 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-245\shpright8902\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Abstract}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The implementation of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine on the Dora-\line \expndtw2 do, an experimental high-performance microprogrammed personal com-\line \expndtw9 puter, was strongly influenced by a few attributes of the Dorado\line \expndtw5 hardware architecture: a large microprogram memory, a hardware in-\line \expndtw6 struction prefetch and decoding unit, an effective memory cache, and\line \expndtw3 the use of base registers for memory addressing. Each of these features\line \expndtw5 substantially reduced the complexity and/or improved the performance\line of the Smalltalk implementation.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1296\margrsxn1718\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1120\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1296\margrsxn1718\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1501\colsr742\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-14\shptop-252\shpright8900\shpbottom-252\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Dorado is an experimental high-performance microprogrammed\line personal computer, the latest and most powerful descendent of the Xe-\line \expndtw3 rox Alto. Because the Dorado is relatively easy to microprogram, and\line \expndtw10 because it incorporates many architectural features for aiding\line \expndtw3 implementors of higher-level languages, the Software Concepts Group\line \expndtw5 chose the Dorado as the first target machine for implementing the\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk-80 virtual machine. The first version of the implementation}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3524\margrsxn1268\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb270\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3524\margrsxn1268\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw4251\colsr2146\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Xerox Corporation \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 113}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1686\margrsxn1295\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3902 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-23\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 114{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Dorado Smalltalk-80 Implementation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ran successfully in early \lang1032 1981, \lang1033 after approximately six months' work by\line \expndtw-1 a single person.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Three excellent papers on the Dorado hardware architecture have\line \expndtw-1 appeared elsewhere\super 1\nosupersub , so we will only mention the machine's most im-\line \expndtw1 portant attributes. The Dorado is microprogrammed, with a \lang1032 70 \lang1033 ns mi-\line \expndtw0 croinstruction time and a 36-bit microinstruction. The microprogram\line memory holds 4K microinstructions, all in RAM. The internal registers\line \expndtw-1 and data paths are \lang1032 16 \lang1033 bits wide. The processor accesses main memory\line through a 4K-word cache, which can accept a reference every microin-\line \expndtw1 struction and deliver data \lang1032 1 \lang1033 or \lang1032 2 \lang1033 microinstructions after a read request.\line \expndtw-1 The processor includes pipelined hardware for prefetching and decoding\line macroinstructions. Later we will describe other details of the Dorado\line \expndtw2 hardware in connection with their impact on the Smalltalk-80 virtual\line \expndtw1 machine implementation.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop878\shpright8921\shpbottom878\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1188\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Emulator{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw4 The Dorado microinstruction memory (microstore) holds 4K instruc-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Architecture{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw2 tions. In the standard Dorado microcode, approximately \lang1032 1300 \lang1033 microin-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 structions implement I/O controllers and \lang1032 700 \lang1033 microinstructions\line \expndtw0 implement a simple macroinstruction set inherited from the Alto\super 2\nosupersub . (The\line \expndtw2 I/O control microcode is a consequence of the Dorado's I/O architec-\line \expndtw-1 ture, which emphasizes simple controller hardware and uses microcode\line multiprocessing to provide most of the control function.) The remaining\line \expndtw0\lang1032 2000 \lang1033 microinstructions are available for implementing other macroin-\line \expndtw2 struction sets and/or extending the Alto instruction set: normally they\line \expndtw-1 implement an instruction set specialized for Mesa\super 3\nosupersub . The hardware in-\line struction fetch unit (IFU, described in more detail below) allows switch-\line \expndtw3 ing between instruction sets in less than a microsecond, simply by\line \expndtw1 reloading an IFU register.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2491\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In our Smalltalk-80 implementation, we had a number of choices:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2722\fi-259\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2722 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2722\fi-259\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2722 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Keep the Alto and/or the Mesa macroinstruction set, and imple-\line \expndtw3 ment the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine entirely in it/them.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2722\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2722 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2722\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2722 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Keep the Alto macroinstruction set; implement the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw5 virtual machine mostly in microcode, and the remainder in Alto\line \expndtw-11 code.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2722\fi-259\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2722 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2722\fi-259\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2722 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Discard the Alto microcode; implement the Smalltalk-80 virtual\line \expndtw5 machine mostly in microcode, and partly in Smalltalk extended\line with special primitives for manipulating memory or the I/O de-\line \expndtw-3 vices directly.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3568\margrsxn1660\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1922\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6401 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 115}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex145\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Emulator Architecture}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li522\fi-263\ri4\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 4. \lang1033 Discard the Alto microcode; implement the Smalltalk-80 virtual\line \expndtw3 machine entirely in microcode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri4\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We rejected approach \lang1032 1 \lang1033 for performance reasons: although somewhat\line harder to write, microcode is approximately five times as fast as either\line \expndtw3 Alto code or Mesa for straightforward instructions, and also offered the\line \expndtw7 opportunity to fully exploit the available processor registers and the\line \expndtw-2 IFU.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It was likely that the microstore was not large enough for \lang1032 4; 4 \lang1033 would\line also have deprived us of the ability to write debugging aids in Alto code\line \expndtw2 or Mesa, and to use the standard debugger (as opposed to a microcode\line \expndtw3 debugger) during Smalltalk development.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In finally choosing \lang1032 2 \lang1033 over \lang1032 3, \lang1033 we were motivated by several principal\line \expndtw4 arguments:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li446\fi-198\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li446\fi-198\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We   wanted   the   parts   of  the   Smalltalk-80  system   written   in\line Smalltalk to be completely portable, i.e. not contain code specific to\line \expndtw7 any particular storage or I/O system, and were willing to tolerate\line \expndtw5 a larger non-Smalltalk kernel to achieve this.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li446\fi-198\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li446\fi-198\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Both the Dorado and the Dolphin, a less powerful machine for\line \expndtw10 which we also wanted a Smalltalk implementation, already had\line Alto emulation microcode. Those parts of the Smalltalk system\line \expndtw4 written in Alto code could be shared between the two implementa-\line \expndtw9 tions. We were also considering an implementation on the Alto,\line \expndtw2 which has less powerful microcode and a smaller microstore. A sys-\line \expndtw6 tem architecture which allowed us to move small functional units\line \expndtw9 of code between microcode and Alto code implementations was\line \expndtw5 likely to be more transportable between the three machines.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li446\fi-198\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li446\fi-198\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Keeping the Alto emulator, which occupies less than \lang1032 20% \lang1033 of the\line \expndtw10 microstore, would relieve us of the 4K limit (by allowing us to\line \expndtw4 write arbitrarily large fractions of the system in Alto code) without\line \expndtw3 depriving us of a lot of microstore space.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is interesting to note that Interlisp-D\super 4\nosupersub , where these desires were not\line \expndtw3 as important, made the opposite choice. Interlisp-D originally adopted\line approach \lang1032 2, \lang1033 later replacing it by \lang1032 3. \lang1033 The Interlisp-D system now contains\line \expndtw2 literally thousands of lines of machine-dependent source code written in\line \expndtw4 Lisp: it has no clear division between a portable virtual machine and an\line implementation level, but the instruction set is much closer to the ma-\line chine level and is much easier to implement efficiently.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi230\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Two features of the hardware architecture helped reduce the cost of\line \expndtw2 approach \lang1032 2 \lang1033 compared to \lang1032 3, \lang1033 specifically by reducing the cost of passing\line \expndtw5 control and data between the two implementation levels: quick switch-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1766\margrsxn1245\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3899 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 116{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Dorado Smalltalk-80 Implementation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ing between instruction sets in the IFU, and the fact that the Alto in-\line \expndtw4 struction set deliberately included a large number of undefined opcodes\line \expndtw2 to which we could assign our own interpretation. We used these opcodes\line to optimize the transfer of information between the microcode and Alto\line \expndtw1 code implementation levels. The appendix (p. \lang1032 123) \lang1033 lists these opcodes in\line \expndtw5 detail; we believe that any Smalltalk-80 implementation that uses a\line \expndtw2 combination of microcode and an existing macroinstruction set will find\line \expndtw4 this list a useful guide.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In retrospect, this decision to split the implementation between two\line \expndtw1 levels worked out extremely well. The decision gave us a great deal of\line \expndtw2 flexibility to move parts of the Smalltalk kernel between Bcpl (a system\line programming language which compiles into Alto code), Alto assembly\line \expndtw3 language, and microcode as we investigated its performance character-\line \expndtw7 istics. As it turned out, we only used Bcpl for initialization, since it\line \expndtw4 could not generate our extended Alto instructions and since its subrou-\line tine calling sequence is less efficient than a hand-coded one by a factor\line \expndtw7 of about \lang1032 3. \lang1033 Typical Smalltalk-80 benchmarks show between \lang1032 5% \lang1033 and\line \expndtw3\lang1032 15% \lang1033 of the time being spent executing Alto code, and the remainder in\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk microcode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the final implementation, the main part of the system is\line \expndtw3 implemented entirely in microcode:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb284\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb284\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instruction set interpreter (with assistance from the IFU)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Process switching}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference counting}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object allocation and deallocation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2232\fi0\ri0\sb385\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition, the following primitive methods are implemented in micro-\line \expndtw-10 code:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb400\sa0\tx2639 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb400\sa0\tx2639 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Small integer arithmetic}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2639\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2639 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2639\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2639 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Subscripting   and   instance   variable   access:    at:,    at: put:,    size,\line \expndtw-2 characterAt:,   characterAt:put:,   objectAt:,   objectAtput:,   instVarAt:,\line \expndtw-8 instVarAt:put:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2639 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2639 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object creation (new, new:)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2639\fi-202\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2639 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2639\fi-202\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2639 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Block  context creation  and  execution:   blockCopy:,  value/value:,\line \expndtw-5 valueWithArguments:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2639 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2639 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Process scheduling (signal, wait, resume, suspend)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2639 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2437\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2639 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BitBlt (copyBits)}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1286\margrsxn1673\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8672 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 117}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Instruction Sequencing}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2707\fi-202\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2707 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 \'95{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3\hich\af0\dbch\af2\loch\f0\cchs0\lang1033 Miscellaneous: asOop,   \lang1032 ==,  \lang1033 class, flushMessageCache,  perform:,\line \expndtw-2 performWithArguments:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri29\sb259\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The following primitive methods for storage management were original-\line \expndtw3 ly implemented in microcode, but were later translated into Alto code\line \expndtw-1 for reasons explained below: refct, become:, asObject, somelnstance,\line \expndtw-2 nextlnstance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The following primitive methods are implemented in Alto code:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0\tx2707 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0\tx2707 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 16-bit large integer arithmetic}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2707 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2707 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Floating point arithmetic}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2707\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2707 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2707\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2707 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 All I/O primitives (disk, Ethernet, display, keyboard, mouse) except\line \expndtw-19 BitBIt}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2707\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2707 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2707\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2707 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The remaining storage management primitives: newMethod:header:,\line \expndtw-11 core Left}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2707 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2707 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf24{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Snapshot}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri36\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The following optional primitive methods are not implemented on ei-\line \expndtw0 ther the Dorado or the Dolphin: next, nextPut:, atEnd.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 When the interpreter microcode encounters a Smalltalk method that\line includes a call on a primitive, it consults a table stored in main memo-\line \expndtw0 ry to determine whether the primitive is implemented in microcode (the\line \expndtw-1 table contains a microcode dispatch address) or Alto code (the table con-\line \expndtw2 tains the address of an Alto code routine). In the latter case, the inter-\line \expndtw4 preter switches the IFU to Alto emulation, and the machine starts\line \expndtw0 executing the Alto code that implements the primitive. The Alto code\line \expndtw-1 typically contains some special Smalltalk-specific instructions (described\line \expndtw9 in the appendix on pg. \lang1032 123) \lang1033 which allow it to interact with the\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk world: LDSS instructions to access the receiver of the mes-\line \expndtw0 sage and its arguments, SETDS and LDFD/STFD instructions to access\line the instance variables of the receiver, and finally a PRET instruction to\line \expndtw2 return the result of the primitive and resume Smalltalk execution. All\line \expndtw-1 these trapped opcodes are of course implemented in microcode. PRET\line \expndtw1 switches the IFU back to the Smalltalk instruction set and resumes ex-\line ecution with the next Smalltalk bytecode.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop691\shpright8921\shpbottom691\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1001\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Instruction{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 The Dorado's Instruction Fetch Unit (IFU)\super 5\nosupersub  prefetches and decodes in-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Sequencing{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 structions in a manner almost ideally matched to the Smalltalk instruc-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri40\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 tion set. In fact the three language-oriented instruction sets designed at\line \expndtw1 the Palo Alto Research Center (Lisp, Mesa, and Smalltalk) and the Do-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1712\margrsxn1281\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3906 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 118{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Dorado Smalltalk-80 Implementation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 rado design influenced each other considerably. The IFU prefetches up\line \expndtw3 to \lang1032 6 \lang1033 bytes, accessing the memory through the same cache the processor\line \expndtw7 uses, and only when the processor is not accessing it. The first 8-bit\line \expndtw3 byte of an instruction indexes a 256-entry RAM. The entry in the RAM\line gives the address of the microcode that implements the instruction, says\line \expndtw4 how many additional bytes the instruction uses \lang1032 (0, 1, \lang1033 or \lang1032 2), \lang1033 and supplies\line a 4-bit parameter for any purpose the microcode wants. (For example,\line \expndtw6 each group of Smalltalk "load" instructions uses the same microcode,\line \expndtw3 but different parameter values corresponding to the offset of the desired\line \expndtw5 variable within the context, instance, or literals.) The RAM also con-\line \expndtw4 tains information which allows the IFU to execute certain uncondition-\line \expndtw5 al jumps itself. The microcode that implements a given macroinstruc-\line \expndtw6 tion can read the parameter and the additional bytes from the IFU onto\line \expndtw3 a convenient bus. The last microinstruction of each macroinstruction\line \expndtw4 contains a field that informs the processor's control logic that the pro-\line \expndtw3 cessor should ask the IFU for a new dispatch address for the next mi-\line \expndtw8 croinstruction; under this arrangement, there is normally no delay at\line \expndtw2 all between macroinstructions. Thus the overhead of fetching, decoding,\line \expndtw5 and dispatching on macroinstructions, and returning to a central loop\line \expndtw3 after a macroinstruction is finished, is reduced essentially to zero. This\line \expndtw5 overhead may take up as much as \lang1032 20% \lang1033 of the time in an interpreter\line \expndtw3 implemented without hardware assistance\super 6\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa943\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The entire job of instruction sequencing and decoding is handled by\line \expndtw10 the IFU. The microprogram only intervenes to restart the IFU for\line \expndtw4 jumps, message sends, and returns, and when switching between the\line Smalltalk and Alto instruction sets. The IFU RAM actually holds up to\line \expndtw5 four instruction sets at once, and the overhead of switching is less than\line \expndtw4 a microsecond if the first instruction to be executed is in the memory\line \expndtw3 cache. Only \lang1032 2.4% \lang1033 of execution time is spent waiting for the IFU to sup-\line \expndtw6 ply a dispatch address; we cannot determine what fraction of this time\line \expndtw3 is due to cache misses and what fraction to competition with the proces-\line \expndtw1 sor for cache cycles. For a deeper discussion of IFU performance, see the\line \expndtw-3 Dorado papers.\super 7}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1712\margrsxn1291\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1551\colsr702\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-317\shpright8910\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Memory\line \expndtw-4 Management}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Dorado provides hardware support for a paged virtual address\line space, but the Smalltalk-80 implementation makes no use of it\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\line \expndtw6\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Smalltalk initializes the page tables to map virtual addresses one-for-\line \expndtw3 one into real addresses. Recovering from a page fault at the microcode\line \expndtw8 level is potentially complicated. A single Smalltalk instruction can\line \expndtw3 make many memory references, so we would have had to adopt some\line \expndtw4 combination of resuming execution of a Smalltalk instruction in mid-\line \expndtw10 flight, arranging all instructions to be restartable, and/or letting the}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1321\margrsxn1667\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4169\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8654 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Memory Cache}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 page fault handler complete the faulting reference. For this reason, and\line because past experience with a paged Smalltalk suggested that it would\line \expndtw5 perform very badly if the working set exceeded real memory signifi-\line \expndtw3 cantly, we did not go to the trouble of observing the microcoding re-\line strictions which would make recovery from faults possible.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Recursive freeing of objects with zero reference count is implemented\line \expndtw3 in microcode; the main loops of the compactor, but not the overall con-\line trol, are also in microcode. This arrangement has worked out extremely\line \expndtw5 well. In fact so little time is spent in recursive freeing \lang1032 (1.9%) \lang1033 that we\line \expndtw4 might be able to move at least some cases of the rather complex recur-\line sive freeing algorithm into Alto code as well with relatively little per-\line \expndtw5 formance penalty. This is partly a result of a minor interpreter change:\line \expndtw7 when control returns from a context which has no other references to\line \expndtw4 it, the interpreter essentially "unrolls" the top loop of the recursive fre-\line er, i.e., there is a special loop which iterates through the fields of the\line \expndtw6 context (but only up to the current position of the stack pointer),\line \expndtw5 decrementing the reference counts and calling the actual recursive fre-\line \expndtw2 er if needed.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop871\shpright8917\shpbottom871\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1188\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Memory Cache        \b0 The Dorado's memory cache is nearly as fast as the processor registers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A memory fetch in microinstruction \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'cd\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 produces data in a register in\line microinstruction N + l which can be used for computation in microin-\line \expndtw5 struction \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'cd\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 +2. \lang1033 Memory stores are even faster, taking a single microin-\line struction. The cache is pipelined, so a new fetch or store can be started\line \expndtw6 in every microinstruction. As a result, we decided to implement even\line \expndtw3 the very highest-bandwidth operations of the Smalltalk-80 implementa-\line \expndtw7 tion\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 pushing and popping quantities (particularly variables) on the\line \expndtw3 stack\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 using memory references at the microcode level without signifi-\line \expndtw8 cant time penalty. Thus for example, the microcode that implements\line \expndtw4 the "load local variable" instruction reads the local variable using the\line \expndtw5 current context as a base and the parameter supplied by the IFU as the\line \expndtw4 offset, increments a stack pointer held in a register, and then writes the\line \expndtw7 value onto the stack (in main memory) using the stack pointer as an\line \expndtw4 offset from another base register.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi234\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In retrospect this decision was almost certainly correct. The memory\line \expndtw5 references to the stack and the local variables in a context never cause\line \expndtw3 cache misses. The bookkeeping required to manage a finite-size register\line \expndtw6 stack (the Dorado has four 64-register stacks in the processor) would\line \expndtw5 have been relatively complex, especially since Smalltalk does not al-\line \expndtw3 ways transfer control between contexts in LIFO order. However, this\line \expndtw6 decision is critically dependent on the speed of the cache. If the cache}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1744\margrsxn1229\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li58\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3942 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 120{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Dorado Smalltalk-80 Implementation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 had been only half as fast\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 taking \lang1032 3 \lang1033 to \lang1032 4 \lang1033 microinstructions to fulfill a\line \expndtw2 request\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 it would probably have been better to store some of the cur-\line rent context in registers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi238\ri4\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Despite its small size, the Dorado cache works effectively for\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk. Our measurements show that cache misses account for only\line \lang1032 11% \lang1033 of execution time. References to the stack and the current context\line \expndtw-2 never cause misses; most misses are caused by accesses to method dic-\line \expndtw1 tionaries during message lookup, and to method headers when starting\line \expndtw-1 execution of a method.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop691\shpright8917\shpbottom691\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1001\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Registers{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw6 The Dorado includes \lang1032 256  \lang1033 16-bit working registers, organized as  \lang1032 16}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 groups of \lang1032 16 \lang1033 registers each. One of the \lang1032 16 \lang1033 groups is current at any time,\line \expndtw2 selected by a processor register called RBase. Microinstructions supply\line \expndtw1 a 4-bit register address within the current group, which is concatenated\line \expndtw2 with RBase to provide the full register address. The Smalltalk micro-\line \expndtw-1 code uses three groups:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2696 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2696 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The "main" group, described in more detail just below}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2696 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2696 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A group used only by the compactor and recursive freer}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2696 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2696 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A group used almost exclusively by the process scheduler}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri32\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Because changing the RBase register ties up a microinstruction field\line \expndtw1 that is also involved in many other operations (such as memory refer-\line \expndtw0 ences and constant generation), it is highly valuable to divide the regis-\line \expndtw-1 ters into blocks according to major sections of the microcode control, to\line minimize the frequency of group switching. The convention we chose in\line \expndtw0 the Smalltalk microcode was to define a single "main" group to be cur-\line \expndtw1 rent throughout the entire microcode, except for local switching to ac-\line \expndtw4 cess individual registers in other groups, and self-contained routines\line \expndtw2 like the recursive freer which would restore the "main" group to cur-\line \expndtw0 rency when they were done.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Since the contents of the "main" group are a valuable indication to\line \expndtw1 implementors as to what information should be held in machine regis-\line \expndtw2 ters, we list them here, in approximate order of decreasing utility:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2257\fi0\ri0\sb259\sa0\tx4514 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex103\expndtw26\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 SPR{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw0 The stack pointer, relative to the base of the current con-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4514\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex103\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 text}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2254\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx4514 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-4\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 9 \lang1033 temporary regis-{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex103\expndtw2 Also used for passing arguments and results to and from}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2412\fi0\ri0\sb32\sa0\tx4514 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ters{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex103 microcode subroutines}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2254\fi0\ri0\sb108\sa0\tx4511 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 NargS{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex103\expndtw1 The number of arguments to the current method}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1256\margrsxn1729\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4169\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8647 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 121}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Base Registers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2268\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\tx4529 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Self{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw0 The Oop (object identifier) of the current receiver}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4529 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw22\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 SI{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 A flag indicating whether the last (or current) primitive is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4532\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 "fast" (entered directly in response to a special selector) or\line \expndtw0 "slow" (entered through method lookup)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\tx4525 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 MyPC{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw1 The byte PC (relative within the method), saved whenever}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4525\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 control might switch to another context or to Alto code}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li151\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\tx2412 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Context{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw1 The Oop of the current context}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li148\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0\tx2412 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Method{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw1 The Oop of the current method}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri11\sb245\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that direct pointers to the bodies of some of these objects (the re-\line \expndtw3 ceiver, context, and method) are also held in base registers, as described\line \expndtw4 in the next section.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The limitation of \lang1032 16 \lang1033 readable registers was much less of a nuisance\line \expndtw3 than might be expected. The original Dorado prototype had \lang1032 32 \lang1033 groups of\line \lang1032 8 \lang1033 registers, which was a tremendous nuisance: the microcode shrank by\line about \lang1032 15% \lang1033 from the prototype version simply through elimination of\line microinstructions whose main purpose was to switch register groups.\line \expndtw2 Expansion to direct addressing of all \lang1032 256 \lang1033 registers would probably elim-\line \expndtw5 inate only another \lang1032 20 \lang1033 microinstructions: the figure is small because of\line \expndtw3 our careful division of registers into meaningful groups. In other words,\line \expndtw5 our experience indicates that for Smalltalk-80 implementation, there is\line \expndtw4 a "knee" in the utility curve somewhere between \lang1032 8 \lang1033 and \lang1032 16 \lang1033 directly ac-\line \expndtw2 cessible registers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Dorado also includes \lang1032 4 \lang1033 stacks of \lang1032 64 \lang1033 registers which can only be\line \expndtw4 accessed through a pointer register, the processor stack pointer (StkP).\line \expndtw7 The microinstruction format supports pushing and popping quantities\line \expndtw4 in the current stack as easily as addressing the current block of \lang1032 16 \lang1033 ad-\line \expndtw6 dressable registers. Unfortunately, StkP is awkward to load and read,\line \expndtw2 and recovering from over- or underflow is even more difficult. For these\line \expndtw4 reasons, we chose not to use these stacks to model the Smalltalk stack,\line \expndtw13 but only for temporary storage within the microcode. When a\line \expndtw2 microcoded primitive fails, or other exceptional condition arises, the mi-\line \expndtw5 crocode resets StkP to a standard state before continuing with the re-\line \expndtw6 covery code. As indicated in the earlier section on the memory cache,\line \expndtw4 we believe this choice is better than using the processor stack for cach-\line ing parts of Smalltalk contexts.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop760\shpright8921\shpbottom760\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1069\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Base Registers         \b0 The Dorado memory system provides \lang1032 32 \lang1033 base registers which hold full}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 28-bit virtual addresses; the microinstructions that reference memory\line \expndtw6 normally provide 16-bit displacements relative to a current base regis-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1798\margrsxn1215\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3902 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 122{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Dorado Smalltalk-80 Implementation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ter, switched under microprogram control. Consequently, the Smalltalk\line \expndtw2 microcode dynamically maintains the following addresses in base regis-\line \expndtw6 ters at all times:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0\tx4504 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 TS{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw3 The current context  for accessing local  variables (home}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 context when executing a block, current context otherwise)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\tx4500 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 SS{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-1 The context used for execution and stack \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 always the cur-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4500\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-338\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 rent context, even when executing in a block}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2239\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-338\slmult0\tx4496 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw29\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 RS{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 The receiver in the current context (self)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2236\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-338\slmult0\tx4493 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 CS{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 The currently executing method (code)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2236\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition, it loads the following addresses at system initialization:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-335\slmult0\tx4500 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 OTBase{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw-1 The base of the object table (OT)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-335\slmult0\tx4500 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 FreeLists{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw1 The base of the table of free list heads}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-335\slmult0\tx4500 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 MCacheBase{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw-1 The base of the message lookup cache}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-335\slmult0\tx4500 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 SpecialBase{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw0 The SpecialSelectors array, used to handle sends of special}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 selectors (must also be reloaded after memory compactions)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi0\ri14\sb349\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is interesting to observe that the memory system is so fast, and the\line \expndtw3 indirect or indexed addressing of registers so awkward, that the system\line \expndtw5 actually runs (slightly) faster by putting the free list heads in memory\line \expndtw6 than by putting them in addressable registers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi241\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This choice of base registers is fairly obvious, except for the issue of\line \expndtw7 whether or not to maintain a pointer to the receiver. Maintaining such\line \expndtw10 a pointer requires reloading it at each activation or return; not\line \expndtw5 maintaining such a pointer requires indirect access through the OT at\line \expndtw4 each reference to an instance variable. (More complicated schemes are\line \expndtw6 also possible, such as a flag to indicate whether the base register has\line \expndtw3 been reloaded since the last change of context.) Measurements indicate\line \expndtw5 that references to instance variables are slightly more common than\line \expndtw3 context switches, so we come out slightly ahead by always loading the\line \expndtw5 base register at a context switch.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2228\fi234\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 While the base register model works well for memory references,\line \expndtw4 loading a base register is awkward for two relatively minor reasons:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2657\fi0\ri32\sb432\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Base registers are \lang1032 28 \lang1033 bits wide, but the Dorado's data paths are\line \expndtw4 only \lang1032 16 \lang1033 bits. Thus loading a base register takes at least \lang1032 3 \lang1033 microin-\line structions (one to select the register, and one to load each half).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2657\fi0\ri32\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because the Dorado implementation packs flag bits into the OT\line along with the data address, exactly as described in the Smalltalk}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1210\margrsxn1768\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8658 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-24\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 123}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Appendix: Extended Alto Instructions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2711\fi0\ri4\sb227\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 virtual machine documentation, a masking operation is required.\line \expndtw3 Also, again because of the 16-bit architecture, two memory refer-\line \expndtw5 ences are required to fetch the OT entry.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa1224\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As a result of these problems, the standard subroutine which loads a\line \expndtw2 base register with the address of an object whose Oop is known (essen-\line \expndtw3 tially the SETDS instruction described in the appendix below) takes \lang1032 6\line \expndtw5\lang1033 microinstructions; over \lang1032 10% \lang1033 of execution time is spent in this subrou-\line \expndtw2 tine (and various open-coded copies of it). By using \lang1032 3 \lang1033 words per OT en-\line \expndtw9 try instead of \lang1032 2, \lang1033 we could have eliminated the masking operation,\line \expndtw2 saving \lang1032 1 \lang1033 of the \lang1032 6 \lang1033 microinstructions. However, besides adding 32K to the\line \expndtw5 system's memory requirements, this design would have increased the\line \expndtw2 cache working set size (and hence the wait time due to misses) signifi-\line \expndtw4 cantly, so there might well have been no net speed improvement.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1221\margrsxn1782\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1926\colsr328\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-313\shpright8910\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Microcode\line \expndtw-2 Multiprocessing}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Dorado micro-architecture includes multiple processes at the mi-\line \expndtw2 crocode level. The Smalltalk-80 emulator itself makes no use of this ca-\line \expndtw6 pability, which is primarily designed to allow a single processor to\line \expndtw2 function effectively as a controller for multiple high-speed I/O devices.\line However, one of these processes can be awakened periodically, at inter-\line \expndtw5 vals as short as a few microseconds, to collect a histogram of the ad-\line \expndtw3 dress of the next microinstruction to be executed by the instruction set\line emulator. This facility proved invaluable in analyzing performance bot-\line \expndtw4 tlenecks in the Smalltalk microcode; essentially all the measurements\line reported in this paper were obtained from the micro-address histogram.\line \expndtw8 One particularly interesting number was the amount of time spent\line \expndtw2 waiting for cache misses; we computed this by looking at the number of\line \expndtw4 samples at addresses of microinstructions that had to wait for memory\line data to arrive, compared to the instructions immediately preceding and\line \expndtw-1 following these.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1210\margrsxn1789\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1192\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1210\margrsxn1789\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1710\colsr547\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-317\shpright8914\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Appendix:\line \expndtw-3 Extended Alto\line \expndtw0 Instructions}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the following descriptions of the extensions to the Alto instruction\line \expndtw4 set, AC0-3 refer to four 16-bit registers which the Alto instruction set\line \expndtw5 views as central registers, but which are addressable registers at the\line \expndtw-1 microcode level.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Many of these instructions are designed for use only from Altocode\line \expndtw7 primitives, and consequently can cause "primitive failure" under cer-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1744\margrsxn1251\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3906 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex116\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 124{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex116\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Dorado Smalltalk-80 Implementation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 tain circumstances. Primitive failure means that the execution of the\line primitive code is abandoned, and the Smalltalk method is executed in-\line \expndtw-8 stead.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2268\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa536\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The instructions are arranged below in groups, with the most heavily\line \expndtw-5 used groups listed first.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1744\margrsxn1251\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1576\colsr684\colno2\colw1904\colsr349\colno3\colw4399 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Access to\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk Data}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex99\expndtw5\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 LDSSn}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb774\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex99\expndtw-7\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 SETDS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb770\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex99\expndtw-26\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 LDFD \loch\af34\hich\af34\dbch\f46\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af34\dbch\af46\loch\f34\cchs161  / \lang1033 LDF1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb1253\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex99\expndtw-31\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 STFD \loch\af34\hich\af34\dbch\f46\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af34\dbch\af46\loch\f34\cchs161  / \lang1033 STF1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1008\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex99\expndtw-19\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 LDNFD \loch\af34\hich\af34\dbch\f46\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af34\dbch\af46\loch\f34\cchs161  / \lang1033 LDNF1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb774\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex99\expndtw-23\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 STNFD \loch\af34\hich\af34\dbch\f46\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af34\dbch\af46\loch\f34\cchs161  / \lang1033 STNF1}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \lang1032 - \lang1033 word \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 relative to SS+SPR-Nargs, the location of\line \expndtw10 receiver and arguments on the stack in the current\line \expndtw3 context. \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'cd\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  = 0 \lang1033 is the receiver, \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  = 1 \lang1033 is the first argument,\line \expndtw-4 etc.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb83\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO  \lang1032 =   \lang1033 an Oop. Sets the DS base register to the real\line \expndtw3 memory address of the object, taken from the OT. Causes\line primitive failure if ACO is a small integer rather than an\line \expndtw-10 Oop.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb90\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 word \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 relative to DS; for LDF1, \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 is taken from\line \expndtw12 AC1 rather than from the instruction itself. The\line \expndtw5 parameters .LENGTH, .CLASS, and .FLDS define the\line \expndtw0 offsets of the length word, class word, and first field of an\line \expndtw1 object, e.g. the assembly-language instruction to load the\line \expndtw-2 second field of an object would be LDFD .FLDS + 1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb86\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 a new Oop to be stored at word \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 relative to DS.\line \expndtw2 This instruction does reference counting (increases the\line \expndtw1 reference count of the object identified by ACO, decreases\line \expndtw-1 the reference count of the old contents): use STNFD (below)\line \expndtw1 to store a non-Oop without reference counting.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb97\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 word \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 relative to DS. Meant for use when the data\line \expndtw7 being loaded are bits rather than Oops. Equivalent to\line \expndtw2 LDFD in the current system, only provided for symmetry\line \expndtw-1 with STNFD.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb97\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 a new value to store into word \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 relative to DS.\line \expndtw-1 Does not do reference counting (see STFD above).}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1762\margrsxn1262\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb270\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1762\margrsxn1262\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1076\colsr1170\colno2\colw986\colsr1267\colno3\colw4384 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb482\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Conversion\line \expndtw0 of Integers}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-529\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 IVAL\line \expndtw-14 WRDVAL}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb684\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 MKINT}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 a small integer; ACO \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 - \lang1033 the value of the integer.\line Causes primitive failure if ACO is not a small integer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb54\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 an Oop of a Smalltalk LargePositivelnteger, or a\line \expndtw2 Smalllnteger; ACO \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 - \lang1033 the value of the number. Causes\line \expndtw1 primitive failure if the Oop is not a Largelnteger, or if the\line value does not fit in one word.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3028\posnegy11\absw0\absh-209\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 the corresponding}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO  \lang1032 =  \lang1033 an unsigned integer; ACO\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk integer (small or large).}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1256\margrsxn1707\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1767\colsr504\colno2\colw932\colsr1321\colno3\colw4420 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb893\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Primitive Control}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft11\shptop619\shpright1912\shpbottom619\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb792\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Access to OT}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb418\sa0\sl-529\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PRET\line \expndtw3 PFAIL\line \expndtw-3 READOT\line \expndtw-4 SETOT}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li886\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4144 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 125}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li940\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex126\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Appendix: Extended Alto Instructions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri18\sb238\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 A primitive has completed successfully. ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 the value\line to return from the primitive.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb86\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Cause primitive failure.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri50\sb284\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Read the OT entry for a given Oop. ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 an Oop. ACO \lang1032 -\line \expndtw0\lang1033 address word from OT, AC1 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 - \lang1033 flag word from OT.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri36\sb61\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Set the OT entry for a given Oop. ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 an Oop, AC1 \lang1032 =\line \expndtw3\lang1033 address word to store in OT, AC3 \lang1032 = \lang1033 flag word to store in\line \expndtw-12 OT.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1245\margrsxn1743\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb310\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1245\margrsxn1743\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1144\colsr1138\colno2\colw849\colsr1397\colno3\colw4392 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb274\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Access to}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Microcoded}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Primitives}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 SIGNAL}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb518\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CINST}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 a Semaphore. Does a "signal" operation on the\line \expndtw2 semaphore. Used only by I/O interrupts and the low space\line \expndtw0 notification mechanism.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 the Oop of a class, ACl \lang1032 = \lang1033 the number of extra\line \expndtw1 fields (for variable-length classes); ACO \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 the Oop of a\line newly created instance. DS is also set as for SETDS.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1281\margrsxn1732\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb302\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1281\margrsxn1732\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1155\colsr1091\colno2\colw1486\colsr770\colno3\colw4392 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Compaction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CLEANSTATE}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-302\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ACOMPACT\line \expndtw-8 CSEG}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb508\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CLOOP}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb760\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CRESUME}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Store all of Smalltalk's state into memory in preparation\line \expndtw1 for a compaction or a snapshot.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb83\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Start a compaction. ACO \lang1032 = \lang1033 the Oop of the display bitmap.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb68\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Prepare to compact the area of memory starting at ACO/1\line \expndtw7 and going up to an end marker which is the maximum\line \expndtw2 legal Oop \lang1032 +3.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Do the main compactor lOop. ACO/1 \lang1032 <- \lang1033 the end of the\line \expndtw0 occupied area after compaction. AC2/3 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 - \lang1033 the end of the\line occupied area just below the display bitmap, if the display\line \expndtw2 bitmap was in the area being compacted.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb79\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Resume Smalltalk execution after a compaction.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1267\margrsxn1732\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb302\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1267\margrsxn1732\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1389\colsr864\colno2\colw1083\colsr1184\colno3\colw4388 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Miscellaneous}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 RESUME}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb749\sa0\sl-299\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 SETCNT\line READCNT}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Resume Smalltalk execution with the next bytecode. Used\line \expndtw6 only during initialization, and for resuming after an\line interrupt. (All hardware interrupts cause control to go\line \expndtw2 from the Smalltalk interpreter to Alto code.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb79\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Set the low-space trap parameter to ACO.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb65\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ACO \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 - \lang1033 the number of Oops left before a low-space trap\line \expndtw-2 will occur \lang1032 -1.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1260\margrsxn1743\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb317\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1260\margrsxn1743\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw3564\colsr958\colno2\colw4384 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CONFIG}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Initialization and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0