\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Thacker, C. P., et. al., "Alto: A Personal Computer", in \i Computer\line \expndtw4 Structures: Readings and Examples, \i0\lang1032 2nd \lang1033 Edition, Eds. Sieworek,\line \expndtw0 Bell,  and  Newell,  McGraw-Hill,  New York,   \lang1032 1981;  \lang1033 (also  Xerox\line \expndtw-4 PARC Report CSL-79-11 Aug. \lang1032 1979).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Mitchell,  James  G.,  et.  al.,  "Mesa Language  Manual,"  Xerox\line \expndtw-3 PARC Report CSL-79-3, Apr. \lang1032 1979.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Burton, Richard R., et. al., (The Interlisp-D Group), "Papers on\line \expndtw2 Interlisp-D", Xerox PARC CIS-5, July \lang1032 1981; \lang1033 (a revised version of\line \expndtw-5 Xerox PARC SSL-80-4).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2491\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2491\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 1.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Markoff, John, "Smalltalk: A Language for the \lang1032 80s", \i\lang1033 InfoWorld,\line \i0\expndtw0 cover story, Jan. \lang1032 24, 1983.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2491\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2491\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 1.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1240\margrsxn1284\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4169\shptop-302\shpright8914\shpbottom-302\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4183\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 The Design and\line \expndtw7 Implementation of\line \expndtw0 VAX/Smalltalk-80*}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs32 Stoney Ballard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Three Rivers Computer Corporation\line \expndtw6 Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs32 Stephen Shirron}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa972\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Digital Equipment Corporation\line \expndtw3 Marlboro, Massachusetts}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1248\margrsxn1755\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1515\colsr734\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-245\shpright8914\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb68\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 VAX/Smalltalk-80 is an implementation of the Smalltalk-80 system\line \expndtw3 written for the VAX family of computers running with the VMS oper-\line \expndtw4 ating system. This version differs from standard Smalltalk-80 imple-\line \expndtw3 mentations by using a basic word size of \lang1032 32 \lang1033 bits and an incremental\line \expndtw6 compacting garbage collector. This paper describes the rationale for\line \expndtw4 these changes, their implementation, and various tricks that were de-\line \expndtw2 veloped to enhance the performance of this version of the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw3 virtual machine. We also discuss some ideas for future work aimed at\line improving the performance of paged virtual object systems.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1240\margrsxn1762\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb994\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1240\margrsxn1762\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1612\colsr644\colno2\colw6649 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7\shptop-238\shpright8935\shpbottom-238\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Some History}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our group at DEC (the Corporate Research Group) was involved in\line \expndtw3 studying issues relating to personal workstations and graphics. We had\line \expndtw4 been interested in the Smalltalk-72 and Smalltalk-76 systems for sever-\line al years because they were the best examples of powerful personal\line \expndtw5 workstation environments that we had seen. Consequently, we jumped\line at the chance to participate in the Smalltalk-80 review.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb173\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The work described in this paper was done at Digital Equipment Corp. Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\line \expndtw2\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Stoney Ballard and Stephen Shirron \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1240\margrsxn1284\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li8665\fi0\ri0\sb407\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 127}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1719\margrsxn1284\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4986 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 128{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi234\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The VAX Smalltalk-80 implementation was our second, the first hav-\line ing been done for the PDP-\lang1032 11/23. \lang1033 The \lang1032 PDP-11 \lang1033 version was implemented\line as closely as possible to the virtual machine specification published in\line \i\expndtw3 Smalltalk-80: The Language and Its Implementation. \i0 This version was\line \expndtw2 notable primarily for its execution speed, which resembled molasses in\line \expndtw3 December. By the time we were out of ideas to speed it up, the \lang1032 PDP-11\line \expndtw2\lang1033 was executing about 5K bytecodes/second.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \lang1032 PDP-11 \lang1033 version exhibited three major performance bottlenecks:\line \expndtw2 the \lang1032 PDP-11 \lang1033 memory manager, the lack of writable microcode hardware,\line \expndtw5 and the reference counter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The memory management hardware on the PDP-\lang1032 11/23 \lang1033 was designed\line \expndtw3 to map a 64Kbyte virtual space into physical space by using eight sepa-\line \expndtw6 rate 8Kbyte segments. Our problem however, was not to map virtual\line \expndtw4 space to physical, but rather to directly access the full 256Kbyte physi-\line \expndtw3 cal space of the PDP-\lang1032 11/23. \lang1033 This involved adjusting the map for every\line \expndtw4 object access (as well as every object table access) to map it into the\line \expndtw6 64Kbyte directly addressable range. Since some Smalltalk-80 objects\line \expndtw5 are larger than 8Kbytes, we had to adjust the map to point at the par-\line \expndtw9 ticular field we wanted. This was particularly painful since it took\line \expndtw8 about \lang1032 30 \lang1033 instructions to adjust the map. By setting up map segments\line \expndtw7 for the objects commonly used by the interpreter (e.g. the context,\line \expndtw4 method, self) at context switch and process switch, we eliminated most\line \expndtw7 of this overhead. Nevertheless, the interpreter spent about half of its\line \expndtw5 time with map box adjustment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi234\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The PDP-\lang1032 11/23 \lang1033 had no writable microstore, so we wrote the bytecode\line \expndtw4 interpreter and all the primitives in assembly language. If we had been\line \expndtw1 able to write microcode, we could have achieved about a factor of two to\line \expndtw4 four improvement in speed. The \lang1032 PDP-11 \lang1033 is not suitable for microcoding\line \expndtw3 a Smalltalk-80 interpreter because the hardware is designed specifically\line \expndtw4 for fetching and decoding its native instruction set.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi234\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reference-counting scheme caused serious performance problems.\line \expndtw7 Updating the reference counts of objects and tracing objects whose\line \expndtw6 counts had gone to zero was the primary source of memory manage-\line \expndtw5 ment overhead. Mapping problems aside, we felt that reference count-\line \expndtw7 ing was inherently inefficient anyway because of the many places in\line the code that were required to update reference counts. Reference\line \expndtw3 counting is probably the best scheme to use for small machines since it\line reclaims space immediately as it becomes garbage. All other garbage\line \expndtw6 collection schemes leave a relatively large amount of garbage lying\line \expndtw2 around between collections.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2210\fi238\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The space compactor was annoying since it caused the system to\line \expndtw7 pause for several seconds whenever it ran. Our compactor was slow\line \expndtw2 bqth because we compacted the whole memory at once, and because the\line \expndtw5 memory map had to be constantly adjusted as it ran.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2214\fi241\ri54\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After playing with the \lang1032 PDP-11 \lang1033 version for a while we came to sever-\line \expndtw3 al conclusions about the Smalltalk-80 system:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3603\margrsxn1638\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1908\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6383 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 129}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Some History}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A maximum of 32K objects is insufficient for a system that sup-\line posedly integrates the whole environment into one object space.\line \expndtw12 We felt that \lang1032 10 \lang1033 to \lang1032 100 \lang1033 times that number of objects would be\line \expndtw11 needed to properly support a rich environment. Even the base\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk-80 system suffers from the small object name space as\line \expndtw0 seen   in   the   non-uniform   handling  of   methods.   These   objects\line \expndtw8 should be broken into a literal vector and a bytecode vector, but\line \expndtw12 weren't for performance reasons and because that would have\line \expndtw7 added about 6K objects to the base system. This non-uniformity\line \expndtw4 requires a great deal of the code to handle methods specially.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The  performance we achieved was clearly inadequate for real\line \expndtw4 work. Compilation and browsing were far too slow to support rap-\line \expndtw3 id development work. Touch typing was virtually impossible since\line \expndtw7 we could type faster than the input process could accept charac-\line \expndtw5 ters. We felt that a tenfold increase in speed would be needed be-\line \expndtw3 fore we would feel comfortable with the system.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Fifteen-bit Smalllntegers are too small. Although this is somewhat\line \expndtw10 of a minor problem, it would clean up the array indexing code\line \expndtw6 substantially if a Small Integer was sufficient to index the largest\line \expndtw3 possible array.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reference-counting scheme should be discarded in favor of an\line \expndtw9 incremental compacting garbage collector. Not only would this\line \expndtw8 eliminate the problem of reclaiming circular garbage (which re-\line \expndtw3 quired us to implement a "separate mark/sweep garbage collector),\line \expndtw13 but would also reduce the work required when storing object\line \expndtw2 pointers into other objects, especially contexts. Additionally, an in-\line \expndtw4 cremental compacting garbage collector eliminates the pauses due\line \expndtw1 to compacting space.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-252\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx479 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Some sort of virtual object storage is necessary to support large\line \expndtw2 applications or sets of applications where millions of objects would\line \expndtw9 be needed. Paging systems are commonly available and easy to\line \expndtw4 use. We were interested in determining the performance of paging\line \expndtw8 for a system like the Smalltalk-80 system, where the locality of\line \expndtw6 reference between adjacent objects is much smaller than in tradi-\line \expndtw4 tional language environments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These considerations led to our decision to implement a modified ver-\line \expndtw5 sion of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine on a VAX-1\lang1032 1/780. \lang1033 The VAX\line \expndtw2 was an obvious choice for us since most groups at DEC had one, and it\line \expndtw7 would be much easier for people to use the Smalltalk-80 system if it\line \expndtw3 ran on computers accessible to them. To this end we decided to imple-\line \expndtw5 ment a portable system that could be used on any VAX running VMS.\line \expndtw1 We realized, of course, that we would be giving up a fair amount of per-\line \expndtw7 formance by using a timesharing system. Our plan was to build a ver-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1662\margrsxn1327\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5040 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 130{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 sion for VMS, written in assembly code, and then convert it to run\line \expndtw1 stand-alone with microcode assist. This latter phase has not yet been\line \expndtw-2 performed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The following sections give details of the characteristics of our\line Smalltalk-80 implementation (which we call VAX/Smalltalk-80) and\line \expndtw0 some suggestions for future work.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop878\shpright8921\shpbottom878\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1188\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Word Size{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw3 We reformatted the Smalltalk-80 image to use \lang1032 31 \lang1033 bit object names and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2272 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Changes{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw7 Smalllntegers, using the 32nd bit as a tag bit to distinguish between}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 them. Object headers increased from \lang1032 4 \lang1033 to \lang1032 8 \lang1033 bytes. Non-Oop fields, such\line \expndtw-1 as those of BitMaps and Strings, were not changed. This reformatting in-\line \expndtw4 creased the size of the base image by about \lang1032 50%. \lang1033 After reformatting,\line \expndtw0 the average object size in the base image was \lang1032 40 \lang1033 bytes. Fig. \lang1032 8.1 \lang1033 shows\line typical object formats.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A \lang1032 4 \lang1033 byte size field allows objects up to 2\super 24\nosupersub  bytes long, leaving a byte\line \expndtw-1 for various flags and an age field (described below). Our size field was a\line \expndtw0 byte count of the object, eliminating the "odd" bit from the object table\line \expndtw1 entry. Objects were stored on longword \lang1032 (4 \lang1033 byte) boundaries because\line \expndtw2 longword fetches are faster on the VAX if naturally aligned. Fig. \lang1032 8.2\line \expndtw0\lang1033 shows the layout of the size field.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The object name space is vastly larger than the 32K objects support-\line \expndtw1 ed by the standard Smalltalk-80 virtual machine. This not only allows\line \expndtw3 keeping such things as the source code for the methods in the object\line \expndtw0 space, but also allows us to use an incremental, compacting garbage col-\line \expndtw1 lector. Unlike reference-counting schemes, an incremental compacting\line \expndtw3 garbage collector does not reclaim either the names of objects or the\line \expndtw1 space they take as soon as they become inaccessible. This requires that\line \expndtw3 the name space be substantially larger than the maximum number of\line \expndtw-1 names otherwise needed. Although we could support two billion objects\line \expndtw5 with a 31-bit name, we are limited to a much smaller number by the\line \expndtw1 size of the virtual space allocated by VMS to the Smalltalk-80 process.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Although our version allocates \lang1032 31 \lang1033 bits in an object reference for the\line \expndtw4 name space, it is certainly feasible to allocate fewer (say \lang1032 24) \lang1033 for the\line \expndtw2 name reference and use the rest for tagging. Having tag bits in a refer-\line \expndtw-1 ence can be useful when one object needs to appear in different guises\line depending on who references it. This would allow a "monitor" object to\line \expndtw0 trap all messages from objects who refer to it as a monitor, but allow its\line \expndtw6 "owner" to send messages to it directly, bypassing the monitoring.\line \expndtw0 These tag bits could also be used to mark objects as being, for example,\line \expndtw9 usable by the interpreter as CompiledMethods even if they are\line \expndtw-1 subclasses of CompiledMethod. Currently, the interpreter does not check}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn898\margrsxn1150\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft10033\shptop-648\shpright10033\shpbottom2174\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-166 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1519 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2549 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4810 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5638 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6048 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 131}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-677 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2549 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4810 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6048 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1397\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 MSB}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1624\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Word}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Size Changes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LSB}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-418 \clvertalt\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1519 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2549 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4810 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw