sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3377\fi-209\ri0\sb544\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Age \lang1032 - \lang1033 Age of the object in Flips\line \expndtw3 P- Set if object has pointer fields"\line \expndtw4 X \lang1032 - \lang1033 Unused}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3168\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Size \lang1032 - \lang1033 Size in bytes of the object}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1012\fi1246\ri0\sb518\sa0\sl-205\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 field is duplicated here so that the scanner need not refer to the OTE (Object Ta-\line \b\expndtw5 Figure \lang1032 8.2     \b0\lang1033 ble Entry) to see if the object has pointer fields.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi0\ri43\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the class of objects such as CompiledMethods both for reasons of effi-\line \expndtw4 ciency, and to allow using subclasses of these classes. Having tag bits\line \expndtw7 that assert the compatibility of such objects with the ones expected\line \expndtw6 would lead to greater safety in the interpreter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi238\ri40\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the standard virtual machine, only \lang1032 15 \lang1033 bits are allocated for literal\line \expndtw3 Small Integers. This is inadequate to index large arrays, so the indexing\line \expndtw1 code must be able to use LargePositivelntegers as indices. Since a 31-bit\line \expndtw4 Smalllnteger can index any array, our array primitives are simpler and\line \expndtw1 faster.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi234\ri36\sb0\sa767\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Converting Smalltalk-80 to use 32-bit words was surprisingly pain-\line \expndtw6 less. We found few places in the Smalltalk-80 code that depended on\line \expndtw4 the word size. The worst one was that the decompiler found the first\line \expndtw3 bytecode of a method by taking twice the number of literals and adding\line \expndtw4 three (for the header and index origin). This prevented us from editing\line any of the methods to fix them, especially this one. We fixed this by\line \expndtw5 creating a primitive that determined the initial PC of a method, and\line \expndtw2 patching the initialPC method by hand to call this primitive. We recom-\line \expndtw6 mend that these dependencies be removed from Smalltalk-80 systems\line \expndtw5 by adding a primitive that returns information about the field size.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1698\margrsxn1305\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1202\colsr1055\colno2\colw6649 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-313\shpright8907\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Garbage\line \expndtw-3 Collection}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This is perhaps the most interesting aspect of our version, it is certainly\line \expndtw4 where most of our effort went. The \lang1032 PDP-11 \lang1033 version (and prior experi-\line \expndtw6 ence with Lisp implementations) showed us that it is vitally important}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1314\margrsxn1660\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4158\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8636 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 133}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Baker Garbage Collector}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri4\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 to have a reliable garbage collector early in the game. A buggy garbage\line \expndtw6 collector has a tendency to randomly smash parts of objects in such a\line \expndtw9 way that the damage does not come to light until much later. This\line \expndtw7 makes the whole virtual machine rather difficult to debug, as you are\line \expndtw2 not sure just which piece of code was responsible for the error. Since\line \expndtw3 mark/sweep collectors can be simple enough to debug easily, we started\line out by using one.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa734\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After debugging the virtual machine with the simple mark/sweep\line collector, we turned our attention to the implementation of an incre-\line \expndtw6 mental garbage collector. There were two candidates to choose from,\line \expndtw4 the Baker\super 1\nosupersub  and the Lieberman-Hewitt\super 2\nosupersub . Since the Lieberman-Hewitt\line \expndtw7 garbage collector was an elaboration of the Baker, and significantly\line \expndtw4 more complicated, we decided to implement the Baker first.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1314\margrsxn1663\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1263\colsr994\colno2\colw6674 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-310\shpright8924\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Baker}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Garbage}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Collector}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To gain a full understanding of this garbage collector, you should read\line \expndtw5 Baker's work on the subject\super 3\nosupersub . For those readers unfamiliar with this\line \expndtw4 garbage collector, we present a brief description of it here.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri4\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Baker garbage collector is an incremental, compacting garbage\line \expndtw5 collector. By incremental, we mean that the process of collecting gar-\line bage is interleaved with the process of allocating space in such a way\line \expndtw6 that the time required for collecting the garbage and compacting space\line \expndtw4 is distributed fairly smoothly over time. The individual incremental ex-\line \expndtw5 ecutions of the collector have an upper bound on their time consump-\line \expndtw6 tion proportional to the size of the largest object. This corresponds to\line \expndtw5 the time it takes to move an object from one place to another. Under\line \expndtw3 normal circumstances, no pauses in execution due to the garbage collec-\line \expndtw5 tor operating are perceptible.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Baker garbage collector divides the virtual space into two equal-\line \expndtw2 sized regions, or semispaces, called Fromspace and Tospace. Objects are\line \expndtw4 copied from Fromspace to Tospace (hence the names) as they are found\line \expndtw2 to be accessible. These copied objects are compacted sequentially at one\line end of Tospace. A scanning pointer starts at the base of this region, and\line moves field by field through the objects that were copied previously to\line \expndtw4 Tospace, copying objects in Fromspace that are referenced by them. In\line \expndtw2 this way, the objects in Tospace act like the stack commonly used by a\line \expndtw4 mark/sweep collector. This scanning and copying continues until the\line \expndtw6 scanning pointer runs out of objects. Newly created objects are placed\line \expndtw5 at the far end of Tospace, so that they are not scanned. Fig. \lang1032 8.3 \lang1033 shows\line \expndtw2 the layout of Tospace.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the original Baker scheme, whenever an object is copied, its old lo-\line \expndtw3 cation (in Fromspace) is marked as a "forwarding pointer," and the new\line address is placed there. An access to a copied object is redirected to the\line \expndtw9 new copy, and the reference is changed to point to the new location.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1672\margrsxn1320\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5029 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 134{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb266\sa868\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Roots of the object space\line \expndtw6 are placed here\line \expndtw3 when flipping}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4627\margrsxn1403\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1695\colsr2693\colno2\colw1490 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-666\shptop-832\shpright-666\shpbottom990\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next copied object\line \expndtw3 goes here}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next new object\line \expndtw3 goes here}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1672\margrsxn1320\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2228\fi0\ri0\sb500\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 \\}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri2848\sb421\sa1228 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Free Space}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2683\margrsxn7123\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw986\colsr241\colno2\colw874 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1228\shptop-896\shpright7881\shpbottom-896\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb623\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 8.3}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Scanning\line \expndtw6 Pointer\line \expndtw1 (scans to\line \expndtw5 the right)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1672\margrsxn1320\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This is necessary for systems (such as Lisp) where objects are referenced\line \expndtw4 by virtual address.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The whole process is started by an operation called a "flip", which\line \expndtw4 switches the identity of Fromspace and Tospace, and immediately cop-\line ies all the objects known to the interpreter (the "roots" of the world) to\line \expndtw6 the bottom of the new Tospace. Flips may occur any time after the\line \expndtw5 scanning pointer has reached the end.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We took the simplistic approach of flipping when there was\line \expndtw4 insufficent space to satisfy an allocation request. This minimizes the\line copying activity at the expense of maximizing page faulting. Flipping\line \expndtw3 when the scanning pointer runs out of objects to scan will minimize the\line \expndtw2 number of pages needed for both semispaces by keeping the accessible\line \expndtw3 objects as compacted as possible. It appears that the optimal time to flip\line \expndtw2 should be dependent on the average lifetime of newly created objects, so\line \expndtw6 that a flip will occur when the expected density of accessible objects\line \expndtw2 falls below a reasonable value. The object of this tradeoff is to minimize\line \expndtw7 the sum of the page faults due to accessing and creating objects and\line \expndtw1 those due to copying objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi241\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Objects that must be copied as roots are those objects that may not\line \expndtw5 be transitively accessible from any other object copied as a root. It is a}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3545\margrsxn1674\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1922\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6408 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 135}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Baker Garbage Collector}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 good idea to copy all the objects that are "wired into" the virtual ma-\line \expndtw8 chine to insure that they never get deleted under any circumstances.\line \expndtw11 The registers of the virtual machine are defined to always be in\line \expndtw3 Tospace, so any objects referred to by them must also be copied at flip\line \expndtw7 time. The objects that must be copied include the current context, all\line \expndtw5 the objects it refers to that are cached by the interpreter (e.g. the meth-\line \expndtw3 od, receiver, self), any semaphores waiting for external events, the\line ProcessorScheduler object, the current DisplayBitMap (if it is not in a\line \expndtw4 special location), and all the objects used by the interpreter such as nil\line \expndtw2 and the selector doesNotUnderstand. If a list of these objects other than\line \expndtw9 those relating to the current context is maintained, the list can be\line \expndtw6 scanned incrementally to reduce the time required to perform the flip.\line \expndtw2 It is not necessary to copy the object Smalltalk (the symbol table) at this\line \expndtw7 time, although it is known to the interpreter, because the interpreter\line \expndtw4 never uses it. If it is live, then it must be reachable from at least one of\line \expndtw5 the objects which are actually used by the interpreter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi256\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whenever a reference to an object in Fromspace is stored into an ob-\line \expndtw2 ject in Tospace, the Fromspace object must be copied to Tospace. This is\line \expndtw3 necessary because the scanner may have already passed the Tospace ob-\line \expndtw6 ject, or it is new. The interpreter registers are considered to always be\line \expndtw4 in Tospace, since they are the first things scanned, so that object refer-\line ences stored in them must be checked and the objects copied if neces-\line sary. Although at first glance this seems to require more overhead than\line reference counting, it is actually much faster to test a flag than to in-\line \expndtw7 crement a reference count and handle overflows. In practice, only a\line \expndtw6 small number of objects need to be copied when their references are\line \expndtw4 stored, and this copying would have been done by the scanner anyway.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The garbage collector is run (incrementally) every time an object is\line allocated. The amount of space to copy whenever a new object is creat-\line \expndtw7 ed should be adjusted so that the scanning pointer finishes at the time\line \expndtw4 a flip is desired (as per the above discussion). This will tend to distrib-\line \expndtw6 ute the copying activity as smoothly as possible. The semispaces must\line be large enough so that the probability of running out of room before\line \expndtw7 the scanning pointer finishes is negligible. In practice, the largest\line \expndtw4 semispaces possible should be allocated, as the middle pages of the\line \expndtw3 semispaces will never be used, and will only contribute to page table\line \expndtw5 overhead. Our implementation was not tuned in this fashion. Our sys-\line \expndtw7 tem was set to copy four times as much space as we allocated so that\line \expndtw4 we did not run out of room.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The net effect of this garbage collector is to copy all the objects\line \expndtw3 which are live by virtue of being accessible (transitively) by the inter-\line \expndtw5 preter, from one space to another, leaving behind all the garbage. This\line \expndtw6 old space can then be reused starting at the next flip.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With the Baker garbage collector objects are copied in a breadth-first\line \expndtw8 order. This ordering has been shown to be substantially better than a}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1204\margrsxn1777\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9461\shptop7070\shpright9461\shpbottom10656\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5033 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 136{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri22\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 random ordering for reducing page faults, though not as good as depth-\line \expndtw7 first\super 4\nosupersub . This ordering is largely invariant from one flip to the next, so\line \expndtw3 that the copying will tend to proceed linearly through Fromspace, mini-\line \expndtw4 mizing the page faulting due to the copying.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is difficult to compare the performance of this garbage collector\line \expndtw5 with reference counting schemes. There is no suitable analytical ap-\line \expndtw3 proach to this problem. We have not directly compared the two schemes\line \expndtw4 in the same environment, but we can offer some observations based on\line \expndtw2 our experience:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference counting uses less space (virtual or physical) than any\line \expndtw9 other incremental scheme, as long as circular garbage is rarely\line \expndtw1 created.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk-80 systems would generate a fair amount of circular\line \expndtw8 garbage if the programs did not explicitly unlink circular struc-\line \expndtw11 tures before deleting them.  This manual intervention in what\line \expndtw5 should be a completely automatic process of garbage collection is\line "unclean" because it prohibits easy sharing of circular data struc-\line \expndtw3 tures.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Baker garbage collector is closer to a "real time" process be-\line \expndtw12 cause the time an activation takes is no more than the time to\line \expndtw5 copy the largest object. A reference counter may take time that is\line \expndtw3 proportional to the size of the largest network of objects.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference counting does not compact space incrementally. If ob-\line jects are allocated from segments of limited size, the compaction\line time of a segment may be small enough to be unnoticeable. Seg-\line \expndtw11 mentation may make it substantially more difficult to allocate\line \expndtw4 large objects, such as bitmaps for high-resolution printers.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference   counting   is   not   sufficient   by   itself.   An   auxiliary\line \expndtw7 mark/sweep garbage collector is needed to reclaim both circular\line \expndtw3 garbage and objects whose reference counts have overflowed (if an\line \expndtw1 overflow table is not used).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Baker garbage collector traces accessible objects, while refer-\line \expndtw7 ence counters trace the inaccessible objects. Which is more effi-\line \expndtw13 cient depends on the rate of garbage generation and of space\line \expndtw-1 flipping.   The   Lieberman-Hewitt   garbage   collector   (see   below)\line \expndtw4 promises to radically reduce the amount of accessible storage that\line \expndtw10 needs to be traced and copied, making it a clear winner in this\line \expndtw-1 category.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2740\fi-256\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2740 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A detailed look at the implementation of both schemes shows that\line \expndtw5 the virtual machine code has fewer places where the space check-\line \expndtw7 ing for the Baker scheme must be performed, compared with the\line \expndtw5 places where reference counts must be updated. In both schemes,}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3181\margrsxn2032\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1930\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6422 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 137}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Baker Garbage Collector}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li504\fi0\ri7\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 storing an object pointer usually requires that the stored pointer\line \expndtw6 be checked, but reference counters require that the overwritten\line \expndtw4 pointer be dereferenced, while the Baker does not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For another comparison, it will be instructive to compare the processing\line \expndtw4 necessary to perform a "store" with both reference counting and Baker.\line \expndtw6 This is presented as a pseudo-Pascal code fragment in which the value\line \expndtw2 of the ith field of object "objA" is stored into the jth field of the object\line \expndtw5 "objB". The object table is expressed as an array of records containing\line \expndtw4 the appropriate fields.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li230\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 First, the reference-counting version.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 store: objA intoField: j of: objB}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li734\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3236 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 temp \lang1032 := \lang1033 objBfj];{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 get the old value of objB[j]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li742\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 if not isSmalllnteger(temp) then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li983\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 if ot[temp].refCount \lang1032 < > \lang1033 overflow then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1224\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3244 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 begin{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3 decrement refCount}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1458\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ot[temp].refCount \lang1032 := \lang1033 ot[temp].refCount \lang1032 - 1;\line \expndtw3\lang1033 if ot[temp].refCount \lang1032 = 0 \lang1033 then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1699\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 deallocate(temp);   if \lang1032 0 \lang1033 then delete it}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1224\fi2020\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 recursive deallocation\line \expndtw-5 end;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li738\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3240 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 temp2 \lang1032 := \lang1033 objA[i];{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw4 get the object pointer to store}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li745\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 if not isSmalllnteger(temp2) then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li986\fi2257\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Smalllntegers have no reference count\line \expndtw-1 if ot[temp2].refCount \lang1032 < > \lang1033 overflow then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1220\fi2023\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 if ref Count hasn't overflowed\line \expndtw1 ot[temp2].refCount \lang1032 := \lang1033 ot[temp2].refCount \lang1032 + 1;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li738\fi2506\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3240 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 increment refCount\line \expndtw0 objB[j] \lang1032 := \lang1033 temp2;{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6 then store it}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Next, the Baker version.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li486\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 store: objA intoField: j off: objB}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li731\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3236 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 temp \i\lang1032 :\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i0\lang1033 objA[i];{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw4 get the object pointer to store}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li742\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 if otfobjB].space \lang1032 = \lang1033 Tospace then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li976\fi2261\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 if the dest is in Tospace\line \expndtw-1 if not isSmalllnteger(temp) then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1213\fi2023\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 and the source is not a Smalllnteger\line \expndtw2 if ot[te.mp],space \lang1032 = \lang1033 FromSpace then}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3236\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 and the source is in Fromspace}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li731\fi724\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3233 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 moveObject(temp)      move source from Fromspace to Tospace\line objB[j] \lang1032 := \lang1033 temp;{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6 then store it}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that it is unnecessary to check if the destination object is in\line \expndtw4 Tospace if it is the current context, since it would have either been cre-\line \expndtw6 ated there or moved there when it became current.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1758\margrsxn1241\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx1429\tlul\tx5054 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 138{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 _{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa655 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1758\margrsxn1241\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1627\colsr612\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-425\shpright8918\shpbottom-425\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Modifications\line \expndtw10 to the Baker\line \expndtw6 Garbage\line \expndtw5 Collector}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One difference between Smalltalk-80 object spaces and those of Lisp-\line \expndtw9 like systems is that Smalltalk-80 objects are referenced by name,\line \expndtw7 whereas in Lisp, objects are typically referenced by virtual address.\line \expndtw2 This allows us to eliminate the forwarding pointers needed in Lisp sys-\line \expndtw5 tems using the Baker garbage collector, since in Smalltalk-80 systems\line \expndtw3 the object table is the only place that the actual address of an object is\line stored. We also use a flag in the object table entries to note in which\line \expndtw2 semispace an object is stored. This could be done by comparing the vir-\line \expndtw7 tual address of the object with the base addresses of the spaces, but\line \expndtw-1 would be slower.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The use of object names rather than virtual addresses is both a bless-\line \expndtw4 ing and a curse. The elimination of forwarding pointers simplifies the\line \expndtw3 object accessing code, since no check need be made for a forwarding\line \expndtw4 pointer, but adds an extra indirection for object accesses (the other be-\line \expndtw7 ing the virtual to physical mapping), and the difficult problem of\line \expndtw3 reclaiming the names of objects which have become garbage.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The indirect addressing caused by using object names rather than\line \expndtw5 virtual addresses is not nearly as expensive as having to handle for-\line \expndtw7 warding pointers. The use of forwarding pointers requires that the\line \expndtw12 accessing code test the virtual address pointer to see if it is in\line \expndtw2 Fromspace, and if so, read the object header to determine if it is a for-\line warding pointer. If it is, the source of the reference must be changed to\line \expndtw1 point to the new location. This is more expensive (without special hard-\line \expndtw4 ware) than accessing objects indirectly through the object table. Most\line \expndtw2 object references in Smalltalk-80 systems are to objects whose virtual\line \expndtw5 address is cached by the interpreter (the context, method, and receiver)\line \expndtw3 so that indirection through the object table is minimized. "Pure" objects\line \expndtw0 need never be changed if they reference other objects by name, allowing\line \expndtw5 them to be stored on read-only media like videodisks. An object table\line will take up about \lang1032 10-20% \lang1033 of the space of the whole world of objects,\line \expndtw4 but we don't feel that that is excessive given the benefits of naming.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Garbage objects are reclaimed by virtue of not being seen by the gar-\line \expndtw7 bage collector, so there is no direct way to reclaim their names. One\line \expndtw5 way to deal with this is to defer name reclamation until some conven-\line \expndtw9 ient time, such as overnight. Although a 31-bit name space is big\line \expndtw2 enough to do this, the object table would become quite large and sparse.\line This would be difficult to manage, so we looked for a way of incremen-\line \expndtw4 tally reclaiming names.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At first, we implemented a mechanism whereby a flag was set in the\line \expndtw6 object table entry for objects that were either newly created or copied\line \expndtw1 to Tospace. At flip time, we scanned the object table, freed those names\line \expndtw3 without the flag set, and cleared the flags in those that were set. This\line \expndtw5 resulted in a pause of several seconds at each flip, due to page faulting}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1327\margrsxn1669\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4169\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8636 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 139}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Lieberman-Hewitt Garbage Collector}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 in the object table. We could have just checked the pointers in the ob-\line \expndtw4 ject table to find the ones that were still pointing at Fromspace, but we\line \expndtw6 felt that the flag test was marginally faster.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Eventually, Steve Shirron developed a properly incremental method\line \expndtw4 of reclaiming names that works in the following way.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each object table entry has two flags, an "even" flag, and an "odd"\line \expndtw7 flag. Each flip of the garbage collector alternates between being an\line \expndtw2 "even" or an "odd" flip. As objects are created or copied to Tospace, the\line \expndtw6 appropriate flag in the object table entry is set, without touching the\line other flag. A name reclaiming process is run incrementally every time\line \expndtw4 a name is allocated. The name reclaimer scans the object table sequen-\line \expndtw3 tially, starting over at each flip. For each object table entry, it checks to\line \expndtw6 see if both flags are clear. If so, the object is dead and the name is\line \expndtw2 reclaimed. If not, the flag corresponding to the previous cycle is cleared.\line \expndtw3 In this way, names of objects that died in cycle \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'cd\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 are reclaimed in cycle\line \expndtw-1 N + l.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa1174\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The name reclaimer scans some number of entries each time it is ac-\line \expndtw5 tivated. Since it is not fatal for the name reclaimer to not be done when\line \expndtw7 a flip occurs (it merely finishes scanning the object table first), the\line \expndtw9 number of entries to scan each time is not critical. The number of\line \expndtw10 entries scanned per activation should dynamically depend on the\line \expndtw5 amount of free space left, the average object allocation size, and the\line \expndtw8 amount of object table left to scan, so that it will finish just before a\line \expndtw-1 flip occurs.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1327\margrsxn1672\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1353\colsr896\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-310\shpright8914\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Lieberman-\line \expndtw-1 Hewitt\line \expndtw-5 Garbage\line \expndtw-3 Collector}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Lieberman and Hewitt observe that with the Baker garbage collector\line \expndtw3 the cost of creating objects and garbage collection is not related to the\line objects' lifetimes\super 5\nosupersub . Traditional environments commonly use stack stor-\line \expndtw2 age for temporary objects, especially activation records, because stack\line \expndtw7 management is very inexpensive. Stacks have the nice property that\line \expndtw6 they are always compacted, so that allocating and deallocating objects\line \expndtw2 of varying sizes does not cause fragmentation of storage. Language en-\line \expndtw5 vironments such as C and Pascal follow a strict control hierarchy, and\line \expndtw3 the lifetimes of their temporary objects (local variables and activation\line \expndtw6 records) are directly related to this hierarchy. In these environments it\line \expndtw3 is either illegal or impossible to pass a reference to a stacked object to a\line previous level. These restrictions allow traditional languages to manage\line \expndtw1 space very efficiently.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Most Lisp systems allow only hierarchical control flow so that they\line \expndtw3 can allocate activation records on a stack. Temporary objects can be de-\line \expndtw5 clared to the compiler as "local" to a function activation, which allows}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1670\margrsxn1275\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5072 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 140{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2300\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 them to be deallocated when the function returns. Lisps which allow\line \expndtw4 multiprocessing, such as Interlisp, use either multiple stacks or a struc-\line \expndtw7 ture known as a "spaghetti stack"\super 6\nosupersub . Multiple stacks are difficult to\line \expndtw4 manage because they may each grow to a potentially unbounded size.\line \expndtw5 With spaghetti stacks, activation records are allocated from a heap and\line \expndtw3 linked together. This facilitates the use of multiprocessing and environ-\line \expndtw5 ment closures. Even here, the cost of allocating activation records from\line \expndtw4 a heap is not inordinately expensive because the virtual machine can\line \expndtw5 deallocate them when they are deactivated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2290\fi241\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk-80 activation records (contexts) are objects just like any\line \expndtw6 other object in the environment. Since a method may hand a reference\line \expndtw3 to its context to any other object in the system, contexts may not be au-\line \expndtw4 tomatically deallocated when returning from a message send. Contexts\line \expndtw6 are created at a very high rate, which makes the Smalltalk-80 system\line \expndtw5 have pretty much the worst space management problems.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Baker garbage collector is quite inefficient for Smalltalk-80 sys-\line \expndtw4 tems because the large number of contexts fill up free space rapidly,\line \expndtw2 causing frequent flips. Since each flip causes all the accessible objects to\line \expndtw3 be copied to the other semispace, the Baker garbage collector consumes\line \expndtw4 too much time to be practical.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Lieberman-Hewitt garbage collector eliminates this problem by\line \expndtw4 dividing the world into a lot of little Baker spaces. These spaces are set\line up so that objects of about the same age are in the same space. Newer\line \expndtw3 objects are more likely to die than older objects, so newer spaces are\line collected more often than older spaces. Garbage collecting a small space\line \expndtw4 is quite a bit cheaper than garbage collecting the whole world at once.\line \expndtw2 The individual spaces are collected separately, allowing the use of small\line \expndtw6 Tospaces. Unfortunately, dealing with references that cross space\line \expndtw2 boundaries is complex.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi234\ri32\sb0\sa1188\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This "generation" scheme is ideal for Smalltalk-80 systems. The\line \expndtw2 newest region will be filled with contexts rapidly, but when flipping the\line \expndtw6 region, very few of the contexts will survive. This drastically reduces\line the amount of copying needed to maintain a compacted region. The\line scheme can be arranged so that a new generation is created whenever\line \expndtw4 the newest region starts filling up with objects that have survived sev-\line \expndtw2 eral flips.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1670\margrsxn1322\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1720\colsr540\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-310\shpright8928\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Static\line \expndtw-2 Object Region}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have not yet implemented the Lieberman-Hewitt garbage collector.\line \expndtw6 There are several open questions about the details of this scheme that\line \expndtw5 we want to answer before we try it. Instead, we implemented a scheme}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3560\margrsxn1645\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1930\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6188 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-28\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 141}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Static Object Region}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi0\ri4\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 that is somewhat halfway between the Baker and the Lieberman-\line \expndtw6 Hewitt. This scheme involved the use of a separate region called the\line \expndtw2 static object region.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We noticed that of the approximately 700Kbytes of objects in the\line \expndtw3 base Smalltalk-80 system, about 600Kbytes of them are relatively per-\line \expndtw4 manent, and need not be subject to the continual scrutiny of the Baker\line \expndtw2 garbage collector. By dividing the objects into two groups, static and dy-\line namic, we can remove most of the objects from the Baker regions and\line \expndtw3 compact them together into the static object region. This region is not\line garbage collected dynamically because the objects in it are expected to\line have very long lifetimes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Static Object Region and the Baker region are equivalent to the\line oldest and the newest generation in the Lieberman-Hewitt garbage col-\line \expndtw9 lector. By leaving out all the middle generations, we simplify the\line \expndtw3 scheme at the expense of causing inefficiencies when running applica-\line \expndtw4 tions that generate lots of medium lifetime objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Static objects are differentiated from dynamic objects by their age.\line Each object has a \lang1032 6 \lang1033 bit (arbitrarily chosen) age field. This field is used\line \expndtw2 to keep track of how many flips the object had survived. An object older\line \expndtw4 than \lang1032 63 \lang1033 flips was placed in the Static Object Region the next time the\line \expndtw3 region was regenerated. Fig. \lang1032 8.4 \lang1033 shows the resulting layout of each ob-\line \expndtw6 ject table entry.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whenever a snapshot is requested (which already causes a long pause)\line we scan through all objects looking for "old" ones. A space is created\line \expndtw3 that is just big enough to hold them, and then they are copied to it. The\line \expndtw10 object table entries for these objects are marked to indicate to the\line \expndtw7 Baker collector that they should be ignored. This space is dumped to\line \expndtw8 the snapshot file separately from the "dynamic" objects, so that it is\line \expndtw5 easy to recreate when rolling in a snapshot.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Dynamic objects referred to only by static objects must be saved by\line \expndtw7 the Baker garbage collector. Instead of scanning all the static objects\line \expndtw8 for references to dynamics, we use an "exit table." This table is a list\line \expndtw3 of those static objects which may contain references to dynamic objects.\line \expndtw6 The static objects referred to by this table are scanned by the Baker\line \expndtw3 scanner and all dynamic object references found in them are handled\line \expndtw6 appropriately. Each static object has a flag, which when set indicates\line \expndtw5 that the static object has an entry in the exit table. Whenever an object\line \expndtw3 reference is stored in another object, the flags are checked. If they indi-\line \expndtw6 cate that the destination is a static object without an entry in the exit\line table, and that the source is a dynamic object, an entry for that static\line \expndtw3 object is placed in the table. If later, that static object ceases to have\line \expndtw7 references to dynamic objects, it is not removed from the exit table.\line \expndtw4 This does not appear to be the source of any inefficiency. Surprisingly,\line \expndtw15 the exit table had fewer than \lang1032 50 \lang1033 entries after running for several}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1687\margrsxn1334\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft8874\shptop1498\shpright8874\shpbottom1916\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5033 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 142{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3596\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An Object Table Entry is a \lang1032 32 \lang1033 bit word}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa450\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-202 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx418 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx843 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1261 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1679 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2097 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2515 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2940 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3358 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3840 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4178 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5100 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5460 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5863 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6281 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6663 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 31}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 30}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 29}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 28}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 27}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 26}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 25}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 24}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 23}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 22}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex76\expndtw23\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 21 20}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 19}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 18}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 17}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 16}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-418 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx418 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx843 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1261 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1679 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2097 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2515 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2940 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3358 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3840 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4178 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5100 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5460 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5863 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6281 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6663 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 ST}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 NE}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expn