i0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 ]}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex76\expndtw-26\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 -.ocation \lang1032 (}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex76\expndtw-21\dn0\f46\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 15:2)}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 X}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f35\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 F}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2677\margrsxn2392\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw990\colsr785\colno2\colw5065 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2146\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 8.4}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li644\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw17\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 F-Set if OTEisfree}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li508\fi122\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'d1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  - \lang1033 Set if object has pointer fields\line SE \lang1032 - \lang1033 Even flag for OTE sweeper\line \expndtw2 SO \lang1032 - \lang1033 Odd flag for OTE sweeper}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li472\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'cc\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  - \lang1033 Mark-used by Mark/Sweep GC\line \expndtw3 W \lang1032 - \lang1033 Set if object has word fields\line \expndtw5 NE \lang1032 - \lang1033 Set if object is Static and is not in exit table\line \expndtw3 ST \lang1032 - \lang1033 Set if object is static}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 X \lang1032 - \lang1033 Unused}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Location \lang1032 - \lang1033 Longword virtual address of object.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1687\margrsxn1334\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri11\sb428\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 weeks, indicating that all but about \lang1032 50 \lang1033 of the static objects only re-\line \expndtw4 ferred to other static objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi230\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The become: primitive causes problems because it switches the object\line \expndtw1 pointers of two objects which may be in different spaces. A static object\line \expndtw8 converted to a dynamic is the most difficult to manage. Rather than\line \expndtw5 scan all the statics looking for those which refer to this new dynamic\line \expndtw6 object (which would take a while), we add the new dynamic to a list\line \expndtw4 called the "becomelist". This list is scanned by the garbage collector at\line \expndtw3 flip time so that all the objects referred to by it are copied into Tospace.\line This insures that the new dynamic object is not deleted in case it is re-\line \expndtw4 ferred to only by static objects. It is fairly rare to convert a static object\line \expndtw5 to a dynamic object, so we are not concerned by the possibility that it\line \expndtw3 dies but is not seen as garbage. The exhaustive garbage collector that is\line \expndtw5 run at snapshot time will get rid of it if it has died.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Converting a dynamic object to a static merely requires that the new\line \expndtw5 static object be scanned for references to dynamic objects. If any are\line \expndtw4 found, the new static is installed in the exit table. Only one object is\line \expndtw3 scanned in this case, so no perceptible pause occurs.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1299\margrsxn1689\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4151\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlhyph\tx7157\tlul\tx8618 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 143}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Virtual Object Space}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Eventually, we have to collect the garbage that has accumulated in\line \expndtw1 the static region. This is accomplished by an exhaustive mark/sweep\line \expndtw2 garbage collector that is run at snapshot time. This same garbage col-\line \expndtw5 lector also compacts the dynamic objects so that there is no wasted\line \expndtw1 space in the snapshot image.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi241\ri7\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We found that the use of the static region resulted in a significant\line improvement in performance, more than doubling the apparent speed.\line \expndtw2 Removing most of the objects from the Baker regions reduced page\line \expndtw0 faulting substantially, because there were fewer objects to copy when a\line \expndtw-1 flip happened.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri11\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The implementation of this scheme was relatively simple compared\line \expndtw-1 to the full Lieberman-Hewitt. We acquired a lot of useful statistics from\line \expndtw3 this that should allow us to do a good job of implementing the\line \expndtw0 Lieberman-Hewitt garbage collector.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop882\shpright8914\shpbottom882\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1199\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Virtual{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw5 In order to support a very large number of objects, some "virtual ob-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2236 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Object Space{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 ject" mechanism must be used. The Software Concepts Group has used}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 variants of "object swapping" to achieve this with their OOZE and\line \expndtw-1 LOOM virtual object systems\super 7\nosupersub . Since the VAX/VMS system supports a\line large paged virtual address space, we decided to use it directly to imple-\line \expndtw0 ment a virtual object scheme. This approach allowed us to defer issues\line relating to virtual memory performance until after we got the basic sys-\line \expndtw3 tem running.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The virtual space is divided into several regions: two IMbyte "Baker"\line \expndtw1 semispaces, 512Kbytes for the object table (ot), and approximately\line \expndtw0 600Kbytes for the static object region. The object table is set at 128K\line \expndtw-1 objects. This number was derived by doubling the size until we stopped\line \expndtw0 running out of room. Although we do not know how much page faulting\line \expndtw1 is due to accessing the object table, we suspect that it is substantial.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Ted Kaehler's paper (see Chapter \lang1032 14) \lang1033 discusses the problems with\line \expndtw1 straightforward paging systems (such as we used) and compares them\line with object swapping schemes. The gist of the argument against paging\line \expndtw0 is that since there is a low locality of reference between adjacent objects\line \expndtw2 in a page, and objects tend to be much smaller than pages, the majority\line \expndtw3 of space in physical memory is taken up by useless objects that just\line happen to be on the same pages as useful objects. Object swapping\line \expndtw-2 schemes such as OOZE and LOOM compact the active objects into phys-\line \expndtw0 ical memory so that more active objects can fit at one time. This allows\line \expndtw2 larger applications to run without faulting than with a paging system.\line \expndtw1 Object swapping has two problems dealing with disks that like to trans-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1685\margrsxn1307\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5040 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 144{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 fer whole pages at once. First, a faulted-in object must be copied from\line \expndtw1 the disk page buffer to the object region in core. This is probably not se-\line \expndtw6 rious compared to the time it takes to access the object on disk in the\line \expndtw3 first place. The second problem is that when faulting-out an old object\line (when it has been modified), the disk page(s) it belongs in must first be\line \expndtw4 read so that the adjacent objects are not trashed. Although it is possible\line \expndtw3 to write all the objects you need to fault out compacted together and\line \expndtw4 appended to the end of the file, this will not only cause problems deal-\line \expndtw5 ing with the fragmentation of the disk image, but makes for extra work\line \expndtw4 in updating the directory of the objects on disk.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi230\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The VAX/Smalltalk-80 system is more properly thought of as a\line "mapped real memory" than a true virtual object memory because of\line \expndtw9 the excessive paging we encounter when we have less than about\line \expndtw3 3Mbytes of real core for our use. A proper system should provide ade-\line quate performance whenever the set of active objects fits into the avail-\line \expndtw4 able real memory. There may be more than a \lang1032 100 \lang1033 to \lang1032 1 \lang1033 ratio between all\line objects and the active objects in a production Smalltalk-80 system. A\line \expndtw3 virtual object system should be able to support such a system with ease.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi245\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our goal is to design a hybrid of object swapping and paging that\line \expndtw4 gives us a low faulting rate, yet runs on paged systems. This would al-\line \expndtw6 low us to use commonly available computers that have hardware to\line support paging, as well as allowing Smalltalk-80 systems to co-exist\line \expndtw5 with other language environments on the same machine.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our approach is based on the observation that you can create a com-\line \expndtw7 pact set of active objects in a paged system merely by copying them\line \expndtw3 from wherever they happen to be in virtual space into a region which is\line \expndtw4 locked into core. Faulting out objects is accomplished by just copying\line \expndtw10 them back. The disk activity of the paging system caused by this\line \expndtw3 scheme is virtually identical to that of the object swappers. In addition,\line \expndtw8 objects larger than a page need not be copied at all, allowing much\line \expndtw4 larger objects than would be possible with an object swapper. A large\line \expndtw3 linear virtual address space is helpful when implementing garbage col-\line \expndtw7 lectors such as the Baker. With a paged system, when Fromspace is\line \expndtw4 empty the pages underlying it can be deleted completely. Pages needed\line \expndtw8 for Tospace can be quickly created by using those released from\line \expndtw4 Fromspace. If a large amount of garbage is collected in each pass, then\line \expndtw3 the amount of physical memory required is much less than what both\line \expndtw4 semispaces would ordinarily require. If you need more space than is\line available physically, paging will happen automatically.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2210\fi230\ri40\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Lieberman-Hewitt garbage collector would be a natural candi-\line \expndtw8 date for use here. Newer objects are accessed more frequently than\line \expndtw2 older objects, so by keeping the newest regions in core and copying ob-\line \expndtw5 jects from older regions into a cache, we would obtain the benefits of\line \expndtw3 both object swapping and incremental garbage collection.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1366\margrsxn1604\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4158\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8633 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 145}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Paging Performance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri7\sb245\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The cache could be managed by combining the "clock" procedure\super 8\line \nosupersub\expndtw-1 with a Baker-like semispace set. The clock procedure would repetitively\line \expndtw1 scan a list of the cached objects. Those that had not been touched since\line \expndtw-1 the last scan would be copied back to their home (if they had been mod-\line \expndtw3 ified), those which had been touched would be copied to the other\line \expndtw-1 semispace. This would compact the cache incrementally. It is also possi-\line \expndtw1 ble to fault-out objects until a big enough hole is created to satisfy a\line faulted-in object, but this scheme may cause noticeable pauses whenev-\line \expndtw-1 er a flurry of objects are faulted out, as each will probably cause a page\line \expndtw5 fault when written back. Fortunately, most of the objects would not\line \expndtw-2 have been modified and therefore need not be copied back. Some experi-\line \expndtw1 mentation is called for here.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The object table should be implemented as a hash table in order to\line \expndtw2 eliminate page faulting when accessing active objects. Objects in the\line \expndtw0 cache, and those in the newer regions would have their names placed in\line \expndtw3 the hash table.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop882\shpright8935\shpbottom882\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1192\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex145\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Paging{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw4 The straightforward approach we have taken, although easy to imple-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Performance{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw4 ment, spends most of its time waiting in a page fault state when there}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 are other users on the system. VMS allocates most unused core for\line \expndtw1 faulted out pages, so when the Smalltalk-80 system is running by itself\line \expndtw0 (on Sunday mornings) the performance improves several fold as disk\line \expndtw1 accesses are usually unnecessary for page faults. A lot of the paging\line \expndtw2 overhead is due to the Baker garbage collector and the object name\line \expndtw1 reclaimer scanning their spaces. Since the pages touched by the name\line reclaimer, and many of the pages touched by the Baker scanner are\line completely predictable, a mechanism whereby the pager could be re-\line \expndtw3 quested to fault-in a page without suspending the program would have\line \expndtw1 a substantial impact on the performance. Unfortunately, VMS does not\line \expndtw0 have such a hook. We are looking into using a separate VMS process,\line \expndtw4 mapped into the same space as the Smalltalk-80 process, to fault-in\line \expndtw-1 pages so that it would be suspended instead of the Smalltalk-80 process.\line \expndtw3 Anything that reduces the rate at which garbage is created will have\line \expndtw-1 a favorable impact on paging rates because of the reduced garbage col-\line lection activity. Smalltalk-80 systems seem to be much worse than mod-\line \expndtw4 ern compiled Lisp systems in the rate at which garbage is generated.\line \expndtw1 This is attributable primarily to contexts, which must be allocated from\line \expndtw2 the heap, and secondarily to the fact that Smalltalk-80 methods tend to\line \expndtw3 require arguments packed into objects such as points and rectangles,\line \expndtw7 which are almost immediately discarded. Editing text also creates a}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1729\margrsxn1259\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5047 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 146{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa839\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 substantial amount of garbage as new strings are created for each mod-\line ification. Some object-based systems allow variable-length objects to\line \expndtw6 "grow" by allocating space for them that is larger than that actually\line needed. When an object such as a string needs more room, it can ex-\line \expndtw3 pand until it fills the contiguous chunk allocated for it by the memory\line \expndtw6 system. If it needs more, it is automatically copied to a larger area. It\line \expndtw3 may be worthwhile to use this technique in a Smalltalk-80 implementa-\line \expndtw-2 tion.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1729\margrsxn1262\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1504\colsr738\colno2\colw6674 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-310\shpright8928\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Context\line \expndtw7 Reclamation}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We noticed that we were generating about a megabyte per second of ob-\line \expndtw5 jects that did not survive even one space flip. We found that these ob-\line \expndtw8 jects were almost entirely contexts. Some method of reducing this\line \expndtw7 garbage without compromising the flexibility of the context linkages\line \expndtw1 had to be found. We could not just reclaim contexts when they were re-\line \expndtw3 turned from, since a reference to a context could be handed to another\line \expndtw-4 object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We found that there are only two occasions when a reference to the\line \expndtw5 active context was handed out: the PushActiveContext bytecode, and\line \expndtw2 when the context was placed on a process or semaphore queue. We as-\line \expndtw6 sumed that if either of these events happened, the current context and\line \expndtw4 all its ancestors could be accessible by any other object, so must be\line \expndtw5 reclaimed only by the garbage collector. Fortunately, these cases hap-\line pen infrequently. If neither of these events occurred, the context could\line \expndtw8 be reclaimed immediately upon returning. By maintaining a counter\line \expndtw11 that is incremented every time a non-primitive send happens and\line \expndtw3 zeroed when either of the special events happens, we reclaim contexts\line \expndtw12 (small only) whenever we return and the counter is non-zero\line \expndtw5 (decrementing the counter as we return).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These reclaimed contexts are linked together on a free list, and are\line \expndtw5 used instead of heap space if there is anything on the list. When the\line \expndtw3 garbage collector does a space flip, this list is cleared because the con-\line \expndtw5 texts are then in the wrong space and it would be silly to copy them to\line \expndtw1 the new Tospace.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This little trick reclaims about \lang1032 85-90% \lang1033 of the contexts. Occasionally,\line \expndtw3 it is much worse \lang1032 (40-50%), \lang1033 apparently due to executing tight loops that\line \expndtw1 perform a lot of blockCopys.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1729\margrsxn1262\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb821\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1729\margrsxn1262\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1778\colsr486\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-313\shpright8921\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Dealing with a}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Timesharing}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 System}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 system as distributed is not designed to either run\line \expndtw6 background processes or co-exist on a timesharing system. This is due\line \expndtw4 to the large number of places where the code loops waiting for a mouse}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1364\margrsxn1595\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8654 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 147}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Message Cache}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 button. The system can be converted to one which is entirely event\line driven by inserting wait messages to an "any event" semaphore into\line \expndtw4 the loops. We found these loops by noticing whenever the idle process\line \expndtw5 was not running, yet nothing else seemed to be happening. We would\line \expndtw6 then type control-C to interrupt the Smalltalk-80 system and find out\line \expndtw2 who was responsible. The debugger was then used to edit and recompile\line \expndtw5 the offending methods. Converting all the interaction to an event-driv-\line \expndtw6 en style allowed background Smalltalk-80 processes to run whenever\line \expndtw5 the user was'not actively interacting with the Smalltalk-80 system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is generally considered uncivil to run programs that are not doing\line \expndtw5 anything worthwhile on a timesharing system. To fix this, we replaced\line \expndtw6 the Smalltalk-80 idle process with one that called two special primi-\line \expndtw2 tives. The Smalltalk-80 code for this is as follows.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2765\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 idleLoop}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3002\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [true] whileTrue:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3244\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 [[Smalltalk collectGarbage] whileTrue.\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk hibernate]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri7\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The collectGarbage primitive performed an incremental activation of\line \expndtw8 the garbage collector, returning false if there was nothing left to do.\line \expndtw4 The hibernate primitive suspended the Smalltalk-80 VMS process, let-\line ting other users run. The hibernate primitive returned whenever an ex-\line ternal event happened. Since this loop runs at the lowest priority, it is\line preempted by any Smalltalk-80 process with something to do.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This made us more popular with the other users of the VAX, and\line \expndtw8 also reduced the overhead of the garbage collector when interacting\line \expndtw4 with the Smalltalk-80 system in a bursty manner (which is usually the\line \expndtw5 case). The Smalltalk-80 process itself also benefited from this because\line \expndtw2 the VMS scheduler assigns a lower priority to compute-bound processes.\line \expndtw3 By hibernating often enough, the Smalltalk-80 process would preempt\line \expndtw6 other users running compilers and the like, leading to a snappier re-\line \expndtw2 sponse when browsing or editing.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop864\shpright8935\shpbottom864\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1181\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Message{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex111\expndtw3 We  implemented a  message  cache  to  speed  up  message  lookup  as}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Cache{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex111\expndtw6 recommended by the book. We found, however, that using a two-way}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex111\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 set associative scheme was significantly better than the one-probe hash\line \expndtw5 table described in the book. Our cache used a hash table where every\line \expndtw1 key (constructed from the receiver's class and the selector Oop) had two\line \expndtw2 possible locations in the cache, determined by the hash value and its\line \expndtw1 complement. The location to store a message was determined by a ping-\line \expndtw4 pong scheme in which a flag was toggled whenever a message was in-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1636\margrsxn1330\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5058 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 148{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri7\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 serted into the cache. If the flag was set, the direct hash value was\line \expndtw1 used, if cleared, the complement was used. This allowed two messages\line \expndtw7 with the same hash value to co-exist in the cache. More elaborate\line \expndtw2 caches using \lang1032 4- \lang1033 and 8-way sets are feasible, but the payoff is less since\line \expndtw1 all possible locations may have to be checked when looking up a mes-\line \expndtw5 sage. In this cache we stored the full key for matching, the method's\line \expndtw0 Oop, the fields of the method header and extension unpacked into bytes,\line \expndtw7 and the address of the interpreter routine that handled that method.\line \expndtw2 This latter information proved very helpful in speeding up the process-\line \expndtw1 ing of message sends. Our hit rate was typically better than \lang1032 90% \lang1033 with a\line \expndtw0\lang1032 256 \lang1033 entry cache (two sets of \lang1032 128 \lang1033 entries).}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop1030\shpright8921\shpbottom1030\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1346\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Image{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex112\expndtw2 The differences in word formats between the Xerox and DEC hardware}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Preprocessor{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex112\expndtw4 forced us to write a program (in C on the VAX) that read the standard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Smalltalk-80 image, massaged it, and wrote out an image in our format.\line \expndtw0 This was done originally for the \lang1032 PDP-11 \lang1033 version, and was extensively\line \expndtw-2 modified for the VAX version. This preprocessor did the following con-\line \expndtw-3 versions:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Converted object headers to 32-bit word format.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Converted the size fields to be bytecounts.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Initialized the flags in the object header and object table entry.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb90\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb90\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Converted all  pointer fields to 32-bit words by sign extending\line \expndtw-1 Smalllntegers and zero extending Oops.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Byte-reversed     all      the     objects     with      byte      fields     (e.g.\line \expndtw1 CompiledMethods) so that we could use the byte-addressing capa-\line \expndtw0 bility of the VAX directly.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Bit-reversed all the words in bitmaps (our lsb is on the left of a\line \expndtw-3 scanline).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Converted the IEEE format floating point numbers to DEC format.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Converted LargePositivelntegers to Smalllntegers where possible.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  9.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Patched CompiledMethod initialPC to call a primitive.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-342\ri0\sb86\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw2\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 10. \lang1033 Patched the PCs in all the contexts to reflect the increased storage\line \expndtw4 taken by the literals.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1380\margrsxn1597\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4187\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8654 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 149}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Conclusions}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2776\fi-346\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2776 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  11.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2776\fi-346\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2776 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart11\pnindent346\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reformatted the object table because of the different flags and\line \expndtw0 addresses.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2776\fi-346\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2776 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  12.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2776\fi-346\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2776 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart11\pnindent346\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Formatted the image header to have the information about the\line \expndtw4 static region that we needed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa954\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This preprocessor also gathered information about the number and\line \expndtw2 sizes of the various types of objects.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1387\margrsxn1601\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1738\colsr508\colno2\colw6674 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-302\shpright8924\shpbottom-302\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Performance\line \expndtw-4 Measurements}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is difficult to come up with accurate performance figures for a system\line \expndtw5 like this. Small changes in such things as the working set size have a\line \expndtw6 major impact on the performance. Our performance monitoring soft-\line \expndtw8 ware could not distinguish between time due to page faults and time\line due to other users preempting the Smalltalk-80 system (it left them\line \expndtw2 both out). The page fault rate depended on the long-term history of the\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk-80 session, and the time it took to handle faults varied ac-\line \expndtw3 cording to the load on the VAX.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With that in mind, when using a working set of \lang1032 512, 512 \lang1033 byte pages\line (the largest our system let us have), we observed that we were getting\line \expndtw3 about \lang1032 250 \lang1033 page faults/second during compiling or browsing. We seemed\line \expndtw4 to get between 15K and 25K bytecodes/second (on Sunday mornings)\line \expndtw2 when compiling or browsing.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We found that CPU time spent in the garbage collector code\line \expndtw10 amounted to about \lang1032 7% \lang1033 of the total when executing long tasks. Of\line \expndtw4 course, the real time was substantially larger (except on Sunday morn-\line \expndtw3 ings) due to page faulting in the garbage collector.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1380\margrsxn1601\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb882\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1380\margrsxn1601\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1436\colsr824\colno2\colw6667 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-248\shpright8931\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusions}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After using this version of Smalltalk-80 implementation for a while, we\line \expndtw3 can make some useful observations:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\tx493 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\tx493 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 32 \lang1033 bit fields are the way to go.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-248\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx493 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-248\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx493 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Paging systems are very easy to use, but a lot of work needs to be\line \expndtw3 done to achieve adequate performance.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx493 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx493 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Incremental garbage collection is generally superior to reference\line \expndtw4 counting, both in ease of implementation and performance.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx493 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx493 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 system is marginally usable on a timesharing\line \expndtw3 system with \lang1032 40 \lang1033 users.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1688\margrsxn1307\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5058 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 150{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Design and Implementation of VAX/Smalltalk-80}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop277\shpright8917\shpbottom277\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb594\sa0\tx2513 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 References{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\charscalex112\expndtw7\lang1032 1. \b0\lang1033 Baker, Henry G., "Actor Systems for Real-Time Computation,"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 MIT Laboratory for Computer Science, MIT/LCS/TR-197,  \lang1032 1978.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Lieberman, Henry, and Hewitt, Carl, "A Real Time Garbage Col-\line \expndtw2 lector Based on the Lifetimes of Objects," MIT AI Memo no. \lang1032 569,\line \expndtw-12 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 1.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Stamos, James W., "A Large Object-Oriented Virtual Memory:\line \expndtw-1 Grouping   Strategies,   Measurements   and   Performance,"   Xerox\line \expndtw-2 PARC Technical Report SCG-82-2, May, \lang1032 1982.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 1.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Bobrow, Daniel G., and Wegbreit, Ben, "A Model and Stack Imple-\line \expndtw1 mentation  of  Multiple  Environments,"   \i Communications  \i0 of   the\line \i\expndtw-2 ACM, \i0 vol. \lang1032 10, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 591-602, 1973.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-248\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Kaehler,   Ted,   "Virtual   Memory   for   an   Object-Oriented   Lan-\line \expndtw-1 guage," \i Byte \i0 vol. \lang1032 6, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 8, \lang1033 Aug. \lang1032 1981.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3942\margrsxn2330\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3247\shptop-1145\shpright5220\shpbottom-1145\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3146\shptop-1073\shpright5472\shpbottom-1073\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-389\shptop554\shpright-389\shpbottom3096\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0{\pict\picw9944\pich8445\picwgoal5637\pichgoal4788\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi1670\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn10829\margrsxn360\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60\linebetcol {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-263\shpright428\shpbottom-263\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Inti}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1276\margrsxn2148\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3744\shptop-299\shpright8496\shpbottom-299\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3751\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-443\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 The Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw8 Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3744\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Kim McCall}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3748\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Software Concepts Group\line Xerox Palo Alto Research Cento\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'b7\line \expndtw-1\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Palo Alto, California}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop1735\shpright8489\shpbottom1735\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2048\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1832 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Oduction{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw2 At the first Smalltalk-80 "implementors meeting" on September \lang1032 24-25,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1825\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 1981, \lang1033 several of the groups pursuing implementations expressed a de-\line sire for some system facilities to help them measure the efficiency of\line \expndtw1 their interpreters. Such facilities, which were unavoidably labeled\line \expndtw-2 "benchmarks," would provide an objective means of comparing imple-\line \expndtw1 mentations, gauging the value of intended performance enhancements,\line \expndtw2 and tracking the progress of individual implementations over time.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1829\fi234\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 This author was given the responsibility of adding such facilities to\line \expndtw4 the system, and thus was born the class Benchmark found in the\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk-80 virtual image.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2063\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In this paper I:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2304\fi-259\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2304 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2304\fi-259\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2304 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Describe the general framework of the timing and reporting facili-\line \expndtw-2 ties in class Benchmark;}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2304\fi-259\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2304 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2304\fi-259\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2304 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Describe various individual tests (hereafter referred to as "bench-\line \expndtw-2 marks");}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2045\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2304 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2045\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2304 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Explain how to write a new benchmark;}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2304\fi-259\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2304 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2304\fi-259\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2304 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Suggest ways benchmarks can be used, i.e., how to gain useful in-\line formation from them; and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1825\fi0\ri0\sb180\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Xerox Corporation, \lang1032 1983. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 153}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1497\margrsxn1488\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li54\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx2898 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 154{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2776\fi-259\ri0\sb230\sa1166\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 5. \lang1033 Present the results of running the standard system benchmarks in\line \expndtw4 several different implementations.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1505\margrsxn1491\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1407\colsr846\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-317\shpright8918\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Benchmark}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Framework}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class Benchmark contains a simple set of facilities for timing the execu-\line \expndtw3 tion of some block of code and reporting the result. The central message\line \expndtw-5 is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li475\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 test: aBlock labeled: label repeated: nTimes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aBlock must be a BlockContext that contains the code to be timed; and\line label should be a string describing this code, i.e., describing the system\line behavior that is being timed. Since timings are recorded in milliseconds,\line \expndtw5 it often happens (e.g., when testing atomic system functions such as\line \expndtw4 pushing a value onto the stack or popping it off) that a single execution\line \expndtw1 of aBlock would yield a value so small that it would be dwarfed by the\line \expndtw4 roundoff error. So the method allows us to specify the number of times\line \expndtw1 the block should be executed (nTimes).\line \expndtw3 For example,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li662\fi-184\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Benchmark new\line \expndtw2 test: \lang1032 [20 \lang1033 factorial]\line \expndtw0 labeled: 'testing \lang1032 20 \lang1033 factorial'\line \expndtw-1 repeated: \lang1032 100}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-493\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-360\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 would time \lang1032 100 \lang1033 executions of the block\line \expndtw-4\lang1032 [20 \lang1033 factorial]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb90\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 and report the result, using the label 'testing \lang1032 20 \lang1033 factorial' for purposes of\line \expndtw1 identification.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1497\margrsxn1488\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb526\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1497\margrsxn1488\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw993\colsr1271\colno2\colw6660 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Reporting}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class Benchmark contains various facilities for reporting its results in a\line permanent, semi-permanent, or evanescent way.\line \expndtw1 A report consists of:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi-256\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx490 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1)\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi-256\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx490 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta )}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An \i identification line, \i0 giving the label, enclosed in square brack-\line \expndtw-1 ets.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx490 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2)\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx490 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta )}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \i total time line, \i0 giving the number of times aBlock was executed\line \expndtw3 and the total time (in seconds).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx490 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3)\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi-256\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx490 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta )}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the number of executions of aBlock is greater than one, a \i single\line \expndtw6 iteration line, \i0 giving the time (in microseconds) taken to execute\line \expndtw-3 aBlock once.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1398\margrsxn1569\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4183\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8662 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 155}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Benchmark Framework}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri25\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, if it took \lang1032 26133 \lang1033 milliseconds to run \lang1032 100 \lang1033 repetitions of \lang1032 [20\line \expndtw3\lang1033 factorial], the report would read}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2516\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 [testing \lang1032 20 \lang1033 factorial]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li130\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 100 \lang1033 repetitions timed at \lang1032 26.133 \lang1033 seconds\line \lang1032 261330.0 \lang1033 microseconds per repetition.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri4\sb367\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After each benchmark test, some feedback is printed in the\line \expndtw3 SystemTranscript. The user can choose to print either the full report as\line \expndtw8 described above or just the identification line. If she or he desires a\line \expndtw7 more permanent record, the user may also elect to print a report on a\line \expndtw4 file. For implementations that do not yet include a working file system,\line \expndtw6 the report may be printed on a Stream.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The default reporting behavior will be to print the full report in the\line \expndtw5 Transcript. To change the default, the user sends the message\line \expndtw2 setOutputParameters to the instance of class Benchmark that is going to\line \expndtw3 perform the test. In response to setOutputParameters, the instance of\line \expndtw4 Benchmark will invite the user to select among the various reporting\line \expndtw-1 options.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In order to generate a file containing reports for a \i series \i0 of bench-\line \expndtw7 marks, class Benchmark provides the message testList:. The\line \expndtw8 setOutputParameters message is sent automatically in response to\line \expndtw3 testList:. There are built-in facilities in class Benchmark for comparing\line two output files in order to chart progress over time or to compare two\line different implementations. These facilities are invoked by the message\line \expndtw-3 compareOldTimes:newTimes:outputTo:.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2239\fi0\ri0\sb396\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The actual timing is done by the method}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex116\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Timing}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 time: aBlock repeated: nTimes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri22\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This method times nTimes executions of aBlock and nTimes executions\line \expndtw2 of an empty block (one that just returns nil), and then computes and re-\line \expndtw4 turns the difference. This should report the actual time spent executing\line \expndtw2 aBlock nTimes times, and subtract out the overhead of the counting and\line \expndtw4 execution control structures. In the code for}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2718\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 time: aBlock repeated: nTimes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri29\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 some care was taken to insure that the control structures for counting\line \expndtw1 up to nTimes would not dominate the execution of aBlock. For example,\line \expndtw3 rather than using timesRepeat: for looping, we use an open coded loop.\line We also use a doubly-nested iteration loop when nTimes is large, to in-\line \expndtw4 sure that all the counting is done using Small Integer arithmetic.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1469\margrsxn1516\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx2894 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 156{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa583 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Benchmarks}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1480\margrsxn1516\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1641\colsr608\colno2\colw6663 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-306\shpright8924\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Standard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Benchmarks}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 virtual image includes two different kinds of bench-\line \expndtw9 marks, those that measure specific parts of an interpreter and those\line \expndtw5 that measure overall system performance. In order to be able to gauge\line \expndtw4 the benefit of small changes to restricted parts of the interpreter, or to\line isolate specific areas in need of improvement, there is a set of bench-\line \expndtw5 marks that test the efficiency of each of the most basic, simple opera-\line \expndtw12 tions of the interpreter. These test the basic bytecodes and the\line \expndtw3 primitives. There is also a set of more global benchmarks that test the\line \expndtw5 performance of the system at the higher-level activities invoked by a\line \expndtw1 typical Smalltalk-80 programmer, such as compiling, decompiling, using\line \expndtw3 browser facilities, and inserting, formatting, and displaying text. We call\line \expndtw2 these the \i micro-benchmarks \i0 and the \i macro-benchmarks \i0 respectively.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1469\margrsxn1523\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb364\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1469\margrsxn1523\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1238\colsr1019\colno2\colw6660 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Micro-\line \expndtw3 Benchmarks}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Most of the micro-benchmarks consist of several repetitions of the par-\line \expndtw2 ticular bytecode or primitive to be tested. We need several repetitions of\line \expndtw6 the very fast bytecodes in order to make the execution time of aBlock\line \expndtw4 be so much greater than the execution time of the empty block that the\line \expndtw9 rounded-off results will be meaningful. To keep the stack from\line \expndtw4 overflowing, and to satisfy the demands of the compiler, the repetitions\line \expndtw5 of the operation we intend to test are interspersed with pops or quick\line \expndtw8 sends of the message \lang1032 ==. \lang1033 The micro-benchmarks all end with \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \b\lang1033 nil.\line \b0\expndtw3 This makes aBlock's execution speed more directly comparable to the\line \expndtw2 execution time of an "empty" block (which just returns nil). In the de-\line \expndtw5 scriptions of the individual micro-benchmarks below, I generally give\line \expndtw-1 the percentage of bytes in the block (ignoring the \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'d4\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \b\expndtw18\lang1033 nil)\expndtw-1  \b0 that actually exe-\line \expndtw5 cute the behavior we are trying to test. I will present the standard sys-\line \expndtw3 tem benchmarks in the order in which they occur in the method for\line setStandardTests in class Benchmark class. This list is intended to serve\line \expndtw7 as a documentation reference, and the casual reader is invited to skip\line \expndtw3 or skim it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Please note that the inclusion in the benchmarks of the \lang1032 = = \lang1033 message\line or other byte codes that we really didn't want to test may greatly alter\line \expndtw2 the execution speed of the block and compromise the meaningfulness of\line \expndtw7 the results. For example, I thought that I had implemented the quick\line \expndtw2 push of a constant (like \lang1032 1) \lang1033 onto the stack about as efficiently as possible,\line and I was perplexed to learn that a machine-coded \lang1032 MC68000 \lang1033 implemen-\line \expndtw3 tation performed the testLoadQuickConstant benchmark faster than my\line \expndtw2 own microcoded implementation. After looking at the code that actually\line \expndtw4 implements the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine instruction 16r76 \lang1032 (118\line \expndtw1\lang1033 decimal), I was even more perplexed, since my implementation looked\line \expndtw2 considerably \i more \i0 efficient. Finally, I realized that most of the time re-\line \expndtw5 ported by the benchmark was going into the execution of (and return\line \expndtw6 from) the \lang1032 = = \lang1033 message which I had included just to satisfy the compil-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3712\margrsxn1519\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1926\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4788\tlul\tx6397 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 .{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-11 157}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Standard System Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb223\sa220\sl-227\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 er. (This \lang1032 MC68000 \lang1033 implementation gave better results because it uses a\line \expndtw3 delayed reference-counting scheme that greatly expedites pushes, pops,\line and simple primitive operations.)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4208\margrsxn1519\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1875\colsr850\colno2\colw3456 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testLoadlnstVar}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb1411\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testLoadTempNRef}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb1105\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testLoadTempRef}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb1274\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testLoadQuickConstant}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb932\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testLoadLiteralNRef}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb1098\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testLoadLiterallndirect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb1278\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testPopStore I nstVar}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb50\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly an in-\line stance variable of the receiver can be pushed\line \expndtw4 onto the stack. Because this variable is set to\line \expndtw3\lang1032 1, \lang1033 there is little reference-counting overhead\line on the push (although there may be more on\line \expndtw0 the other operations in the block). \lang1032 50% \lang1033 of the\line \expndtw-1 bytes in the block are 16rO, a push of the re-\line \expndtw2 ceiver's first instance variable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb180\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a vari-\line \expndtw3 able that is local to a method can be pushed\line \expndtw8 onto the stack. Setting this variable to \lang1032 1\line \expndtw3\lang1033 avoids most reference-counting overhead on\line \expndtw4 the push. \lang1032 50% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block are\line \expndtw0 16rlO, a push of the method's first local vari-\line \expndtw-3 able.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark also measures how quickly a\line \expndtw8 variable that is local to a method can be\line \expndtw3 pushed onto the stack, but because the vari-\line \expndtw1 able is set to some newly created point, the\line implementation's reference-counting mecha-\line \expndtw3 nism (if any) will be fully exercised. Again,\line \expndtw6\lang1032 50% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block are 16rlO, a\line \expndtw2 push of the receiver's first instance variable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly one of\line \expndtw-2 the "quick constants" \lang1032 (-1, 0, 1, 2, \lang1033 nil, true, false)\line \expndtw2 can be pushed onto the stack. There is little\line \expndtw0 reference-counting overhead on the push. \lang1032 50%\line \expndtw3\lang1033 of the bytes in the block are 16r76, a push of\line \expndtw2 the constant \lang1032 1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly an or-\line dinary literal (a constant generated at compile\line \expndtw4 time) can be pushed onto the stack. Because\line \expndtw2 we have used the constant \lang1032 3, \lang1033 there is little\line \expndtw0 reference-counting overhead on the push. \lang1032 50%\line \expndtw2\lang1033 of the bytes in the block are 16r21, a push of\line the method's second literal.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly the\line \expndtw5 contents of an indirectly accessed variable\line \expndtw2 (such as a class variable or a global variable)\line \expndtw3 can be pushed onto the stack. Because our\line \expndtw0 variable refers to class Point, there is little ref-\line erence-counting overhead on the push. \lang1032 50% \lang1033 of\line \expndtw3 the bytes in the block are 16r41, an indirect\line \expndtw2 push of the method's second literal.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a val-\line \expndtw4 ue can be popped off the stack and stored in\line \expndtw2 an instance variable of the receiver. Because\line this value is the Smalllnteger \lang1032 1, \lang1033 there is little\line \expndtw5 reference-counting overhead on the push or\line \expndtw3 the store. \lang1032 50% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block are\line \expndtw0 16r60, a pop of the top of the stack into the\line \expndtw2 receiver's first instance variable.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn910\margrsxn2089\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw4334\colsr1120\colno2\colw3456 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4280 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 158{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex127\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Smalltalk-80 Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testPopStoreTemp}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi0\ri0\sb1087\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 test3plus4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi0\ri0\sb760\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 test3lessThan4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi0\ri0\sb749\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 test3times4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi0\ri0\sb742\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 test3div4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi0\ri0\sb745\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testi 6bitArith}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi0\ri0\sb1274\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testLargelntArith}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb922\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testActivationReturn}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi0\ri0\sb2344\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testShortBranch}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb670\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a val-\line \expndtw0 ue can be popped off the stack and stored in a\line local variable of the method. Again, since this\line \expndtw1 value is \lang1032 1, \lang1033 there is little reference-counting\line \expndtw2 overhead. \lang1032 50% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block are\line \expndtw0 16r68, a pop of the top of the stack into the\line \expndtw1 method's first local variable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of\line \expndtw0 Smalllnteger addition. Because all values are\line \expndtw2 Smalllntegers, there is little reference-count-\line \expndtw4 ing overhead. \lang1032 25% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block\line \expndtw2 are 16rB0, a quick send of the message \lang1032 +.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of\line \expndtw-2 Smalllnteger comparison. Because all values are\line \expndtw0 Smalllntegers, there is little reference-counting\line \expndtw2 overhead. \lang1032 25% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block are\line \expndtw-1 16rB2, a quick send of the message \lang1032 <.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of\line \expndtw-1 Smalllnteger multiplication. Because all values\line \expndtw2 are Smalllntegers, there is little reference-\line \expndtw4 counting overhead. \lang1032 25% \lang1033 of the bytes in the\line \expndtw-2 block are 16rB8, a quick send of the message \lang1032 *.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of\line \expndtw0 Smalllnteger division. Because all values are\line \expndtw2 Smalllntegers, there is little reference-count-\line \expndtw4 ing overhead. \lang1032 25% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block\line \expndtw2 are 16rBD, a quick send of the message \lang1032 //.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of 16-bit\line \expndtw3 integer arithmetic. Since some implementa-\line tions use more than \lang1032 15 \lang1033 bits \i to \i0 represent\line \expndtw0 Smalllntegers (see Chapter \lang1032 8), \lang1033 the reference-\line counting overhead will depend on the repre-\line \expndtw4 sentation chosen for 16-bit integers. \lang1032 25% \lang1033 of\line \expndtw2 the bytes in the block are 16rB0, a quick send\line \expndtw3 of the message \lang1032 +.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb54\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of arith-\line \expndtw0 metic on integers greater than 2\super 16\nosupersub . The refer-\line \expndtw4 ence-counting overhead will depend on the\line \expndtw1 exact representation chosen for these integers.\line \expndtw5\lang1032 25% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block are 16rB0, a\line \expndtw2 quick send of the message \lang1032 +.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb58\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This very important benchmark uses a call on\line \expndtw5 a doubly-recursive method to measure the\line \expndtw3 speed of method activation and return. There\line \expndtw2 is little reference-counting overhead associat-\line \expndtw4 ed with knowing when to end the recursion,\line \expndtw8 but there may be a great deal in managing\line \expndtw3 the Contexts that represent the activations.\line \expndtw0 About \lang1032 12.5% \lang1033 of the bytes executed during this\line \expndtw1 benchmark are 16rE0, a 