0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a sim-\line \expndtw-3 ple BlockContext can be evaluated. \lang1032 33% \lang1033 of the\line \expndtw2 bytes in the block are 16rC9, a quick send of\line \expndtw0 the message value.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li32\fi0\ri11\sb101\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a class\line \expndtw4 can be instantiated. \lang1032 33% \lang1033 of the bytes in the\line \expndtw0 block are 16rCC, a quick send of the message\line \expndtw-10 new.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li32\fi0\ri11\sb83\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly one co-\line ordinate can be extracted from a Point. \lang1032 33% \lang1033 of\line \expndtw1 the bytes in the block are 16rCE, a quick send\line \expndtw-1 of the message x.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li40\fi0\ri0\sb79\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly the\line current context can be pushed onto the stack.\line \expndtw6\lang1032 50% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block are 16r89, a\line \expndtw2 quick push of the current context.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li43\fi0\ri0\sb76\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly the\line \expndtw6 value can be read from an unnamed (but\line \expndtw0 indexed) field of a variable-length object. \lang1032 25%\line \expndtw2\lang1033 of the bytes in the block are 16rE4, a send of\line the method's fifth literal, in this case the mes-\line \expndtw-6 sage basic At:.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi0\ri11\sb83\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a val-\line \expndtw10 ue can be stored into an unnamed (but\line \expndtw0 indexed) field of a variable-length object. \lang1032 20%\line \expndtw2\lang1033 of the bytes in the block are 16rF5, a send of\line \expndtw5 the method's sixth literal, in this case the\line \expndtw-1 message basicAt:put.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li47\fi0\ri4\sb90\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw4 perform: primitive. \lang1032 20% \lang1033 of the bytes in the\line \expndtw-1 block are 16rFl, a send of the method's second\line \expndtw0 literal, in this case the message perform:with:.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li54\fi0\ri4\sb94\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw0 String replacement primitive.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi0\ri14\sb79\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw0 Smalllnteger to Float conversion. \lang1032 33% \lang1033 of the\line \expndtw3 bytes in the block are 16rDl, a send of the\line \expndtw2 method's second literal, in this case the mes-\line \expndtw-1 sage asFloat.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri18\sb83\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw1 floating point number arithmetic. \lang1032 25% \lang1033 of the\line \expndtw3 bytes in the block are 16rB0, a quick send of\line \expndtw5 the message \lang1032 + \lang1033 which must be looked up in\line \expndtw-3 class Float.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri36\sb76\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw-1 BitBIt primitive. \lang1032 33% \lang1033 of the bytes in the block\line \expndtw3 are 16rD2, a send of the method's third liter-\line \expndtw0 al, in this case the message copyBits.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri50\sb76\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw3 primitive method that displays characters on\line \expndtw1 the screen.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1420\margrsxn1543\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li5666\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8640 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 161}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li5724\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa220 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Standard System Benchmarks}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1420\margrsxn1543\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1234\colsr1026\colno2\colw6685 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Macro-\line \expndtw3 Benchmarks}\par \column {\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-972\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx479\posnegy832\absw1966\absh-9086\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testClassOrganizer\line \expndtw-1 testPrintDefinition}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-796\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx479\posnegy832\absw1966\absh-9086\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testPrintHierarchy\line testAIICallsOn}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb32\sa0\sl-965\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx479\posnegy832\absw1966\absh-9086\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testAlllmplementors\line \expndtw2 testlnspect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-965\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx479\posnegy832\absw1966\absh-9086\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testCompiler}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb428\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx479\posnegy832\absw1966\absh-9086\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testDecompiler}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-432\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx479\posnegy832\absw1966\absh-9086\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testKeyboardLookAhead\line testKeyboardSingle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb540\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx479\posnegy832\absw1966\absh-9086\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testTextDisplay}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The macro-benchmarks provide examples of the main activities in\line \expndtw10 which a Smalltalk programmer is typically engaged. Since the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 virtual image does not contain many applications besides\line \expndtw5 the programming environment as a whole, most of the macro-bench-\line marks test system support for programming activities.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3240\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of con-\line \expndtw4 version between the textual and the structur-\line \expndtw1 al representations of a class organization. The\line \expndtw-1 example chosen is class Benchmark because its\line \expndtw1 organization contains many categories.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3233\fi0\ri7\sb79\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a class\line \expndtw3 definition, as it appears in the system brows-\line \expndtw4 er, can be generated. The example chosen is\line \expndtw0 an instance of class Compiler because it has a\line \expndtw2 moderate number of instance variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3229\fi0\ri11\sb72\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark times the printing of a por-\line \expndtw1 tion of the Smalltalk-80 class hierarchy. The\line \expndtw-1 example chosen is class InstructionStream be-\line \expndtw1 cause it has several subclasses.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3229\fi0\ri14\sb72\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly all the\line \expndtw4 methods in which a given selector is refer-\line \expndtw5 enced can be found. The example chosen is\line \expndtw-2 the selector printStringRadix: because it has a\line \expndtw1 moderate number of senders.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3226\fi0\ri22\sb68\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly all the\line \expndtw-1 implementors of a given selector can be found.\line \expndtw2 The example chosen is the selector next be-\line \expndtw14 cause it has a moderate number of\line \expndtw0 implementors.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3226\fi0\ri18\sb68\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a stan-\line \expndtw0 dard inspect window can be created. The ex-\line \expndtw-2 ample chosen is an instance of class Compiler\line \expndtw0 because it has a moderate number of instance\line variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3226\fi0\ri22\sb68\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw4 compiler on a slightly longer than normal\line \expndtw0 method, one containing \lang1032 87 \lang1033 tokens and compil-\line \expndtw-1 ing into \lang1032 73 \lang1033 bytecodes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3226\fi0\ri22\sb72\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the speed of the\line \expndtw-2 Decompiler by decompiling all the methods in\line class InputSensor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3218\fi0\ri29\sb79\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark gauges keyboard response,\line \expndtw2 simulating a typist who is able to type ahead.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3215\fi0\ri25\sb76\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures keyboard handling\line \expndtw1 by simulating a typist who waits until each\line keystroke appears on the screen before typing\line \expndtw2 the next one.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3218\fi0\ri29\sb72\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures how quickly a\line \expndtw2 paragraph can display itself. The paragraph\line \expndtw0 chosen contains \lang1032 13 \lang1033 lines with no font or em-\line phasis changes.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1598\margrsxn1462\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw4226\colsr1177\colno2\colw3445 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4226 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 162{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Smalltalk-80 Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testTextFormatting}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2671\fi0\ri0\sb1094\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 testTextEditing}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb677\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures the system facili-\line \expndtw2 ties required to display a piece of source code\line \expndtw1 in the system browser. First, the message pat-\line \expndtw2 tern is discovered and highlighted (by making\line \expndtw1 it bold). Next, the line breaks are determined\line which will allow it to be displayed between\line certain margins. Finally, the text is displayed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb54\sa0\sl-176\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 This benchmark measures text-editing speed\line \expndtw9 by repeatedly inserting characters into a\line \expndtw3 string and redisplaying the string.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1530\margrsxn1466\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1465\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1530\margrsxn1466\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1504\colsr734\colno2\colw6674 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-310\shpright8917\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Writing\line \expndtw-4 Your Own\line \expndtw-3 Benchmarks}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Suppose you want to use the general benchmark facilities to test and\line \expndtw6 record your system's performance of some tasks that are not already\line \expndtw3 tested by class Benchmark. This is quite straightforward if you are test-\line \expndtw4 ing some large system function, such as compilation, that does not re-\line quire user interaction. As explained above, you send an instance of\line \expndtw2 Benchmark the message}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li497\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 test: aBlock labeled: label repeated: nTimes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 where aBlock invokes the function whose speed you want to measure.\line But if you want to test a more atomic system facility, such as a primi-\line \expndtw3 tive specific to your implementation or a simple system facility, primi-\line \expndtw4 tive, or byte code for which I have not included a test (such as the\line \expndtw0 performance of your low-level disk code), or if you want to test your sys-\line \expndtw4 tem's behavior at a task that involves user input, I have a few words of\line \expndtw-3 advice.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 First, advice for macro-benchmarks that require user input: It should\line \expndtw5 be obvious that if the benchmark ever has to wait for input, the results\line will depend on the behavior of the user rather than that of the system.\line \expndtw2 This is unacceptable. So unless you can be sure of feeding your bench-\line \expndtw5 mark all the input you need ahead of time (by "type ahead" for exam-\line ple), you will need to figure out a more creative way to discover the\line \expndtw4 results you want. The implementors at University of California, Berke-\line \expndtw2 ley, (see Chapter \lang1032 11), \lang1033 have developed a "script" facility in which the in-\line \expndtw4 put events required to drive some application can be recorded and then\line \expndtw5 played back later. This allows a script to drive an application or a\line benchmark, and thus avoids uneven waiting.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next, advice for the designers of new micro-benchmarks: The main\line \expndtw5 problem in defining a benchmark is being sure that the benchmark ac-\line tually tests what you mean it to test. The main problem here is making\line \expndtw7 sure that that operation is not swamped by other extraneous computa-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3690\margrsxn1548\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1919\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6397 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 163}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Writing Your Own Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb263\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tion. For example, if you want to test the speed of your BitBIt primitive,\line \expndtw3 you might think of a simple way of getting a BitBIt to occur, such as}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li482\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Display reverse: (100@100 corner: \lang1032 400\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161 \'ae\hich\af0\dbch\af24\loch\f0\cchs161  400)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb277\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 which uses BitBIt to complement the bits in a rectangle on the screen.\line \expndtw2 But this would probably be a mistake, because several (costly) message\line \expndtw8 sends are required to turn this high-level description of a particular\line \expndtw1 BitBIt into a completely specified call on the primitive. The Smalltalk-80\line "spy" facilities contained in class MessageTally can help you discover\line \expndtw3 whether your proposed benchmark times primitive or higher-level exe-\line \expndtw0 cution.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To solve this problem you need to find a much lower-level way of in-\line \expndtw2 voking the desired behavior. But sometimes you have to be very careful.\line \expndtw8 For example, consider my original code for testing the speed of\line \expndtw5 BlockContext creation. Smalltalk methods often call for unevaluated\line \expndtw3 blocks of code to be passed as arguments. This happens, for example, in\line all code of the general form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li486\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 1 \lang1033 to: \lang1032 10 \lang1033 do: [:i |   array at: i put: true].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri7\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When it sees something like this, the compiler realizes that it needs to\line \expndtw5 create a BlockContext to serve as the second argument of the to:do:\line \expndtw2 message. So it generates the following sequence of byte codes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 pushThisContext        pushO (or \lang1032 1 \lang1033 or \lang1032 2, \lang1033 etc.)        sendMsg: blockCopy:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now, I was interested in testing the speed of execution of this\line \expndtw0 blockCopy: message. Since I wanted to follow my general rule of making\line \expndtw8 sure that this operation was not swamped by other computation, I\line \expndtw5 thought it would be smart to store the current context in a temporary\line \expndtw4 variable and thus be able to get it onto the stack by a simple pushTemp\line \expndtw5 instruction rather than a (at least in some implementation strategies)\line \expndtw3 potentially more complicated pushThisContext instruction. The problem\line \expndtw4 is that since the code that the benchmark repeatedly executes is itself\line passed as an unevaluated block, by the time this code is executed, my\line clever temporary variable no longer points at the current context, but\line \expndtw5 rather at one further down the stack. This resulted in my benchmark\line \expndtw3 testing an operation that would literally \i never \i0 be performed in any nor-\line \expndtw8 mal Smalltalk-80 system and that might go much slower (in certain\line \expndtw5 very highly optimized implementations) than the behavior I had meant\line \expndtw3 to test.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The micro-benchmark designer should also be careful to consider the\line \expndtw2 effect of other "filler" bytes, as discussed above.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1843\margrsxn1145\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft29\shptop-54\shpright4774\shpbottom-54\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa522 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Benchmarks}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1861\margrsxn1145\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1555\colsr688\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-306\shpright8910\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 How to Use\line \expndtw-3 Your Results}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 What can you learn from the results of your tests? The micro-bench-\line \expndtw3 marks can, of course, suggest parts of an implementation that need ad-\line \expndtw4 ditional work. They will often help identify local gross inefficiencies.\line \expndtw3 They can also serve to confirm that a simple local modification was, in\line \expndtw8 fact, an improvement. But I think it is a mistake to place a great deal\line \expndtw4 of emphasis on micro-benchmark performance. It seems that there are\line \expndtw5 only a few micro-benchmarks whose performance correlates strongly\line \expndtw1 with global (macro-benchmark) performance. Microcoded machines with\line \expndtw3 stringent control store limits would be especially ill-advised to spend\line micro-instruction space speeding up the relatively insignificant micro-\line benchmarks.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi223\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The macro-benchmarks appear to be a good measure of overall sys-\line \expndtw7 tem performance. Improvement in their performance correlates well\line \expndtw3 with user-perceived system performance. I have found them very useful\line \expndtw4 in gauging the effect of both small and large changes to my interpreter\line \expndtw5 and in tracking my progress over time. For example, columns A and \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c2\line \hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 of Table \lang1032 9.1 \lang1033 compare the Dolphin interpreter before and after a change\line to the handling of the display that had a significant effect on perfor-\line \expndtw8 mance. At other times however, testing the macro-benchmarks has\line \expndtw6 served primarily to quell my enthusiastic anticipation of the value of\line \expndtw4 some intended enhancement.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1843\margrsxn1159\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1343\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1843\margrsxn1159\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1623\colsr626\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-313\shpright8914\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 A Brief\line \expndtw2 Analysis of\line \expndtw-1 Early Results}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As this book attests, there are now several successful Smalltalk-80 im-\line \expndtw5 plementations. The standard system benchmarks have been run in each\line \expndtw6 of these implementations, and the reports are presented below. There\line \expndtw4 are several interesting lessons to be gleaned from these results, which I\line \expndtw2 will explore here.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri4\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It should be stressed that the figures we will be comparing were\line \expndtw2 achieved on machines of vastly different speeds and hardware configu-\line rations. Some of our figures are very early results. Some come from im-\line \expndtw5 plementations that were intended to teach the implementors about the\line structure of the interpreter and that placed very low priority on execu-\line \expndtw3 tion speed. Some implementations were written in high-level languages\line \expndtw5 while others were written in microcode. None of these results is final,\line \expndtw4 and none indicates the expected performance of any future products.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I include these figures because the fact that they were achieved on\line vastly different machines and in pursuit of different goals allows us to\line \expndtw3 do interesting cross-correlations. We can learn which of the micro-\line \expndtw11 benchmarks seem to be good predictors of the speed of the macro-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3645\margrsxn1586\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1944\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlhyph\tx4104\tlul\tx6412 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 165}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex122\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 A Brief Analysis of Early Results}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb187\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 benchmarks (which is what we really care about). And we can glimpse\line the results of various strategies such as reference counting versus gar-\line \expndtw0 bage collecting.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi238\ri4\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I will present the results in two tables. Table \lang1032 9.1 \lang1033 gives raw times for\line \expndtw7 each of the benchmarks, and Table \lang1032 9.2 \lang1033 gives times as a proportion of\line \expndtw8 the average (geometric mean) time across all implementations. The\line \expndtw5 time reported for each benchmark is the execution time for the entire\line benchmark (rather than for a single repetition), i.e., that given in the\line \expndtw6 report's "total time line."}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft18\shptop446\shpright6671\shpbottom446\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li173\fi0\ri0\sb540\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex122\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Table \lang1032 9.1 \lang1033 The Benchmarks, Raw Times}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft18\shptop29\shpright6671\shpbottom29\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li148\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx810\tx1454\tx2081\tx3427\tx4075\tx4777\tx6167 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex122\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 A{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }B{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }C{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 D         \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c5{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 F{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }G{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\charscalex296\expndtw120 HI{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 J}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Micro Tests\line testLoadlnst Var}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi140\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx814 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 4.47{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4 3.72      0.28      5.85      27.6        3.85      23.41      10.62      5.89       10.02\line \i\expndtw4\lang1033 testLoadTempNRef}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi140\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx814 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 4.37{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4 3.07      0.28      5.97      26.2        2.78      23.3        10.62      5.92       9.71\line \i\expndtw-3\lang1033 tes tLoad TempRef}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi144\ri198\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 6.42 4.53 0.4 5.85 31.16 2.78 26.7 13.89 7.53 11.1\line \i\expndtw0\lang1033 testLoadQuickConstant}\par \pard \plai