58 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6 Xerox (see Chapter \lang1032 7). \lang1033 Implementation written in microcode and\line \expndtw3 machine code running on a 40Mhz Xerox Dolphin.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2758\fi-356\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2758 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 B.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6 Xerox (see Chapter \lang1032 7). \lang1033 Implementation written in microcode and\line machine code running on a 44.5Mhz Xerox Dolphin with special\line \expndtw3 display alignment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2758\fi-356\ri0\sb101\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2758 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 C.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6 Xerox (see Chapter \lang1032 7). \lang1033 Implementation written in microcode and\line \expndtw3 machine code running on a Xerox Dorado.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2401\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0\tx2758 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 D.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw4 U.C. Berkeley (see Chapter \lang1032 11). \lang1033 Implementation written in C run-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3305\margrsxn1930\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1930\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6401 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 171}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 A Brief Analysis of Early Results}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li497\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ning on a DEC VAX-11/780, under 4.1BSD UNIX. Uses AED-512\line \expndtw2 over a RS-232 link for the display. Uses 32-bit Oops.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-353\ri0\sb108\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx493 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 E.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw7 Tektronix (see Chapter \lang1032 5). \lang1033 Implementation written in Pascal and\line \expndtw2 assembly language running on an 8Mhz \lang1032 MC68000.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-353\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx493 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 F.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw9 Tektronix (see Chapter \lang1032 4). \lang1033 Implementation written in assembly\line \expndtw2 language running on a lOMhz \lang1032 MC68000.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li493\fi-353\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx493 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-17\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 G.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw4 Hewlett-Packard (see Chapter \lang1032 6). \lang1033 Implementation written in high-\line \expndtw0 level   language   (C)   running   on   a   DEC   VAX-11/780   with   \lang1032 4\line \expndtw6\lang1033 megabyte main memory running 4.1BSD UNIX. Portable among\line UNIX systems. "By the book" implementation; limited optimiza-\line \expndtw1 tion.     16-bit    and     Largelnteger    arithmetic    performed    with\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk-80 code (no primitives); Float arithmetic using VAX for-\line \expndtw7 mat (not IEEE); some additional class and argument checking in\line \expndtw4 primitives;   standard   reference-count   management   with   8-bit\line \expndtw8 counts (contexts are reference counted); recursive marking gar-\line \expndtw0 bage collector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li493\fi-353\ri22\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 H. Apple (see Chapter \lang1032 10). \lang1033 Implementation written in assembly lan-\line \expndtw6 guage running on a 5Mhz \lang1032 MC68000. \lang1033 Synchronization of CPU\line \expndtw3 memory accesses and display memory accesses reduces the effec-\line \expndtw2 tive processor speed to about 4.5Mhz.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li497\fi-356\ri29\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I. Digital Equipment Corp. (see Chapter \lang1032 8). \lang1033 Implementation written\line \expndtw5 in assembly language, running on a DEC VAX-11/780 with \lang1032 4\line \expndtw0\lang1033 megabyte main memory, under VMS. Small Integer and Float prim-\line \expndtw2 itives implemented; Largelnteger primitives not implemented. All\line \expndtw4 primitives do class and argument checking. Incremental compact-\line \expndtw1 ing garbage collector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li493\fi-360\ri25\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 J. Digital Equipment Corp. (see Chapter \lang1032 8). \lang1033 Implementation written\line \expndtw2 in Bliss-32, running on a DEC VAX-11/780 with \lang1032 4 \lang1033 megabyte main\line \expndtw3 memory, under VMS. "By the book" implementation using refer-\line ence counting; moderately optimized. All arithmetic primitives\line \expndtw4 implemented. All primitives do class and argument checking.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One can see from the above data that some micro-benchmarks matter\line \expndtw3 very little to overall performance. Performance on the division and\line \expndtw1 Largelnteger benchmarks for example, does not seem to be a very good\line \expndtw2 predictor of overall performance. As might be expected, performance on\line \expndtw5 the String replace benchmark correlates strongly with performance on\line \expndtw4 the text editing macro-benchmark, but not for example, with compiler\line \expndtw1 performance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another observation is that performance on the micro-benchmarks\line \expndtw3 varies much more widely than performance on the macro-benchmarks.\line \expndtw4 While this was to be expected, since the macro-benchmarks test perfor-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1641\margrsxn1362\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx2858 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 172{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri0\sb263\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mance over a much broader range of activities than do the micro-\line \expndtw4 benchmarks (thus smoothing out peaks and valleys), I was surprised at\line \expndtw2 the magnitude of this difference between the smoothness of the macro-\line \expndtw4 benchmarks and the variations among the micro-benchmarks. (For the\line \expndtw5 Dolphin implementation, the standard deviation for the micro-bench-\line \expndtw3 mark results is \lang1032 0.86 \lang1033 while the standard deviation for the macro-bench-\line \expndtw5 marks is only \lang1032 0.087.) \lang1033 This leads me to the hypothesis that, for any\line \expndtw3 reasonably efficient interpreter, virtually all macro-level performance is\line \expndtw4 determined by a very few micro-level factors. This hypothesis is sup-\line \expndtw6 ported by the observation that while the Dorado performance on most\line \expndtw3 of the micro-benchmarks is on the order of \lang1032 15-20 \lang1033 times as fast as the\line \expndtw5 Dolphin, performance on the macro-benchmarks is only approximately\line \expndtw4\lang1032 10 \lang1033 times as fast. This appears to be due primarily to the relative speeds\line \expndtw21 of activation and return in the two implementations. The\line \expndtw5 activation/return benchmark correlates much more strongly with mac-\line \expndtw4 ro-level performance than do most other micro-benchmarks. So it ap-\line pears that general system behavior is most strongly influenced by the\line \expndtw5 speed of method activation and return. (This may be less true of ma-\line \expndtw10 chines with special stack hardware or other special hardware that\line \expndtw2 would affect Smalltalk-80 bytecode performance in a non-uniform way.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 From an analytic point of view, this hypothesis, that (within reason-\line \expndtw6 able bounds) overall system performance is most strongly determined\line \expndtw3 by the speed of method activation and return, can be supported by two\line \expndtw4 other general facts: method activation and return are by far the most\line expensive atomic operations the interpreter must perform; and a fairly\line \expndtw3 high proportion of the byte codes encountered by a running interpreter\line cause activation or return. Of course, neither of these "facts" is univer-\line \expndtw5 sally true. The String replacement and BitBIt primitives can be much\line \expndtw6 more expensive than an activation, so code that is very rich in these,\line \expndtw3 such as text editing, can be expected to perform more like the relevant\line \expndtw1 micro-benchmarks. Also, highly optimized code (such as that invoked by\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk allCallsOn:) will "open code" important message calls rather\line \expndtw5 than incurring the activation and return overhead and can therefore be\line expected to perform closer to the level predicted by the other bench-\line \expndtw1 marks.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi241\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Exceptions to the general rule that macro-behavior is best predicted\line \expndtw4 by the testActivationRetum benchmark are illustrated by the perform-\line \expndtw0 ance of implementation D on the testTextEditing and testKeyboardSingle\line \expndtw2 macro-benchmarks. Most of D's normalized micro-benchmark scores fall\line \expndtw3 in the range \lang1032 0.8 \lang1033 to \lang1032 1.6, \lang1033 but the scores for the display-intensive micro-\line \expndtw2 benchmarks testBitBLT and testTextScanning are much worse. The dis-\line \expndtw17 play configuration that D uses is much slower than other\line \expndtw5 implemenations and accounts for these low scores. While performance\line \expndtw3 on most macro-benchmarks is very near \lang1032 1.0, \lang1033 as predicted by the majori-\line \expndtw2 ty   of   the   micro-benchmarks,   the   scores   for   testTextEditing   and}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1444\margrsxn1552\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4154\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8622 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-27\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 T73}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Concluding Summary}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi0\ri14\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 testKeyboardSingle are much worse \lang1032 (3.4 \lang1033 and \lang1032 6.16) \lang1033 due to the fact that\line \expndtw5 these are display-intensive macro-benchmarks. It is also worth noting\line \expndtw10 that these particular macro-benchmarks seem to correlate strongly\line \expndtw5 with user satisfaction. While D appears to be computationally accept-\line \expndtw4 able in other respects, its users are generally not satisfied with its re-\line \expndtw-1 sponsiveness.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop1116\shpright8917\shpbottom1116\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1429\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex139\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Concluding{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw4 Discoveries and warnings have been sprinkled throughout this paper;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw37\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Summary{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 here is my chance to put them in one place.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Many of the micro-benchmarks contain operations that I had not\line \expndtw0 meant to test, but that I included simply to satisfy the demands of the\line \expndtw1 compiler or stack management discipline. These operations, although\line \expndtw0 fairly fast, may be slower than the operations being tested and this may\line \expndtw1 distort somewhat the meaningfulness of these benchmarks.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In writing your own benchmarks, you must be very careful to make\line \expndtw6 sure that the operations you are trying to time are not swamped by\line \expndtw-1 other computation. For micro-benchmarks, it is good to look at the com-\line \expndtw4 piled code to see whether you have created the test you thought you\line \expndtw-4 had.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Most of the micro-benchmarks are not very good predictors of overall\line \expndtw-1 system performance. The speed of certain specialized applications may\line \expndtw0 correlate well with some specific micro-benchmarks, such as BitBIt or\line String replacement, but it appears from our data that the efficiency of\line \expndtw2 activation and return is the overriding determinant of system perfor-\line \expndtw-4 mance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Finally, let me stress again that the data I presented in the analysis\line \expndtw0 section represent early or interim results, designed for very different\line \expndtw1 purposes, written in different level languages, and running on vastly\line \expndtw5 different hardware. None of these are final, and none indicate the\line \expndtw12 expected performance of any future systems. We thank the\line \expndtw0 implementors for their courage in allowing us to publish such prelimi-\line \expndtw2 nary measurements.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1607\margrsxn1337\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li101\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx2938 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 174{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li83\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Smalltalk-80 Benchmarks}\par \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb346\sa0{\pict\picw15811\pich7785\picwgoal8964\pichgoal4413\jpegblip
}\par\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1550\margrsxn992\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4165\shptop-328\shpright8903\shpbottom-328\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9493\shptop-2516\shpright9493\shpbottom2020\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-432\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 An MC68000-Based\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4165\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-331\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Richard Meyers\line \expndtw1 David Casseres}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4165\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1390\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Apple Computer Inc.\line \expndtw1 Cupertino, California}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1553\margrsxn1445\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1515\colsr742\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-256\shpright8914\shpbottom-256\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This paper describes some preliminary results of an MC68000-based im-\line \expndtw3 plementation of the Smalltalk-80 system. The implementation was con-\line \expndtw2 ducted as a research project by Apple Computer Inc. Our purpose in\line \expndtw4 presenting this data is to provide to other implementors some of the in-\line \expndtw2 formation we wish we had known when we first set out to implement\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1550\margrsxn1453\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1123\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1550\margrsxn1453\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw892\colsr1364\colno2\colw6649 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-263\shpright8910\shpbottom-263\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 History}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Apple Computer began its Smalltalk-80 research project in early Octo-\line ber \lang1032 1980. \lang1033 The project has been staffed by one engineer (Rick Meyers)\line \expndtw2 and, for about half of the last \lang1032 18 \lang1033 months, by a technical writer (David\line \expndtw3 Casseres). Apple has consistently viewed Smalltalk as a research effort,\line and has afforded the project the freedom from schedules and from mar-\line \expndtw4 keting considerations that allows research efforts to thrive.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our Smalltalk-80 implementation has been running since April \lang1032 1981\line \expndtw1\lang1033 on a series of experimental Motorola MC68000-based computer systems.\line \expndtw5 The Smalltalk-80 virtual machine described in this paper is a straight-\line \expndtw3 forward translation of the Xerox virtual machine specification to \lang1032 68000\line \expndtw7\lang1033 assembly language. The bytecode interpreter, object memory system,}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3821\margrsxn992\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb281\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3821\margrsxn992\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw4456\colsr1919\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Apple Computer Inc., \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 175}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1625\margrsxn1363\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3812 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 176{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 An MC68000-Based Smalltalk-80 System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri11\sb259\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 and primitive routines were written slowly and carefully by a single en-\line \expndtw4 gineer. Other engineers contributed the BitBIt (screen display), text\line \expndtw6 scanning (textual display), and floating-point arithmetic routines. To\line \expndtw1 date, only about three weeks of effort have been devoted specifically to\line \expndtw3 optimizing the virtual machine, and the only major optimization is the\line \expndtw4 message lookup cache described later in the paper.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa1184\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This paper reports on work in progress. The measurements reported\line \expndtw4 were first made in June \lang1032 1981 \lang1033 and were repeated in June and July \lang1032 1982.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1632\margrsxn1363\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw986\colsr1267\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-306\shpright8917\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Memory\line \expndtw-2 Layout}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The measurements were made on a Smalltalk-80 system configured to\line \expndtw0 use about 793K bytes of memory. Estimates of the sizes of major system\line \expndtw3 components are shown in Table \lang1032 10.1. \lang1033 The virtual machine size includes\line \expndtw2 BitBIt, text scanning, and the floating-point routines. The data does not\line \expndtw8 include the program development system used to host Smalltalk, the\line \expndtw7 file system and the device drivers, the system debugger, or the data\line \expndtw4 areas used by program analysis tools.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1625\margrsxn1363\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2250\shptop407\shpright8910\shpbottom407\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4381\fi0\ri0\sb461\sa104 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 10.1 \lang1033 Memory Layout}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3864\margrsxn2576\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw3121\colsr1627\colno2\colw720 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft11\shptop-72\shpright6671\shpbottom-72\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1292\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Area}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 virtual machine data\line \expndtw3 virtual machine code\line \expndtw1 display memory\line \expndtw-1 object table (24K objects)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li313\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 15K system-supplied objects (60K)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi302\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 9K user objects (36K)\line \expndtw2 heap storage \lang1032 (10 \lang1033 heaps, 64K/heap)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li299\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 system supplied objects (407K)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi299\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 user objects (233K)\line \b\expndtw2 Total}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Bytes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li101\fi97\ri288\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 4K\line \expndtw-7 21K\line 32K\line 96K}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb479\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 640K}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-14\shptop418\shpright425\shpbottom418\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb482\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\d