\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 1%}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1915 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2887 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 unaccounted}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri104\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 1%}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-317 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1915 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2887 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Total}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri104\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex108\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 100%}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb428\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 book and the BS source code gave us the types and\line \expndtw3 numbers of key operations for each type of bytecode. Finally, we calcu-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1571\margrsxn1403\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5184 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 192{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex129\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Berkeley Smalltalk: Who Knows Where the Time Goes?}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 lated a weighted average for the mean number of key operations per\line \expndtw-5 bytecode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Multiplying the time per operation by the number of operations per\line \expndtw-1 bytecode gave us the time spent per bytecode on each operation (except\line BitBIt). To measure individual optimizations, we also computed the time\line \expndtw5 spent by an implementation that performed all the optimizations \i but\line \i0\expndtw0 the one in question. Table \lang1032 11.1 \lang1033 summarizes effects of optimizing these\line \expndtw1 areas. We'll examine these areas individually in the following sections,\line \expndtw0 discuss other optimizations, and draw some conclusions.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft14\shptop882\shpright8935\shpbottom882\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb1195\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2282 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Reference{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw-1 Smalltalk-80    implementations,    including    BS,    reclaim    space    by}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2279 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Counting{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw0 maintaining a reference count for every object. Two reference-count op-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 erations are required when the virtual machine stores an object pointer\line \expndtw0 (Oop): one to account for the destruction of the word's former contents,\line and one to account for the creation of a new reference. Each reference-\line \expndtw2 count operation must first verify that its operand represents a refer-\line \expndtw1 ence-counted object and not a small integer. Then it must increment or\line \expndtw3 decrement the appropriate count and check for over- and underflow.\line \expndtw4 When an object's reference count diminishes to zero, all of its refer-\line \expndtw0 ences must be destroyed by recursively decrementing the counts of the\line \expndtw1 referenced objects. Only then can the virtual machine reuse the space.\line Although each reference-count operation is not expensive, the cumula-\line \expndtw2 tive time counting references threatens to throttle by-the-book imple-\line \expndtw1 mentations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We designed the Oop and OT structures to speed up reference count-\line \expndtw2 ing. We enlarged the book's reference-count variables from \lang1032 8 \lang1033 to \lang1032 32 \lang1033 bits\line \expndtw1 to obviate overflow checking. The object table was split into separate\line \expndtw2 arrays of reference counts, addresses, and flags to eliminate the multi-\line \expndtw0 plication needed to index an array whose elements are not a power of \lang1032 2\line \expndtw3\lang1033 long. The Small Integer tag bit was moved to the high order bit of the\line \expndtw4 Oop from the low order bit to avoid the right shift required to convert\line \expndtw3 an Oop to an array index.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2524\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3964 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Oops by-the-book.\line \i0\expndtw-7\lang1032 15 \expndtw35 ...\expndtw-7  1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2552\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 <OT Index \lang1032 >    0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3967 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-5\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 < \lang1033 Integer \lang1032 >        1\line \i\lang1033 Berkeley Oops\line \i0\expndtw-6\lang1032 15 14 \expndtw34 ...{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2923 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6\lang1033 <OTIndex \lang1032 >}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2520\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3064 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw4 < \lang1033 Integer \lang1032 >}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1328\margrsxn1639\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8636 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 193}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Reference Counting}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri22\sb292\sa486\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Berkeley Oop format also reduced the overhead of reference count-\line \expndtw5 ing by hastening the check to decide whether an object must be refer-\line ence counted. Since the integers all have negative Oops, and the Oops\line \expndtw3 not needing reference counting (invalid Oop, nil, true, false) have values\line \expndtw4\lang1032 0 \lang1033 through \lang1032 3; \lang1033 a single signed comparison can ferret out nonreference-\line \expndtw3 counted Oops. A typical BS count operation became \lang1032 25% \lang1033 faster\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 8.2\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'ec\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 8\line \expndtw-10\lang1033 to 6.3ju,s.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1328\margrsxn1639\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1494\colsr738\colno2\colw6710 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb259\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Reference-\line \expndtw3 Counting\line \expndtw-3 Operations Per\line \expndtw-1 Bytecode}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BS incorporated many strategies from the Xerox Dorado\super 7\nosupersub  and Dolphin\line \expndtw3 implementations\super 8\nosupersub  that reduce the number of reference-count operations.\line \expndtw5 For example, when returning the top of stack, the result's Oop must be\line \expndtw4 pushed onto the caller's stack. A straightforward implementation would\line \i\expndtw5 copy \i0 the Oop into the new context and increment its reference count.\line \expndtw3 Later, when freeing the callee's context, the implementation would dec-\line \expndtw6 rement the result's reference count. Instead, \i destructively moving \i0 the\line \expndtw7 Oop obviates these two reference-count operations. After storing the\line \expndtw8 Oop in the caller's context, BS destroys the old reference from the\line callee's context by nilling it out. Since the net number of references\line \expndtw6 does not change, the reference count stays the same. An implementa-\line \expndtw7 tion can also avoid a decrement operation when it can guarantee that\line \expndtw2 the target field contains a nonreference-counted object (e.g. nil). If the\line \expndtw10 implementation can count on the new contexts of a field to be\line \expndtw4 nonreference counted, it can avoid the increment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi245\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Perhaps the single most significant trick for optimizing reference\line \expndtw4 counting is the stack management strategy we adopted from the Dorado\line \expndtw5 implementation. A Smalltalk-80 interpreter must free a context on al-\line most every return. Since the context may contain references to objects,\line \expndtw4 a conventional implementation must sweep the entire context to free it.\line \expndtw5 The stack occupies two-thirds of a context; most of the stack is never\line \expndtw1 used. BS avoids sweeping the area above the top of the stack (TOS) by\line \expndtw3 banishing references to objects from that area. Only nil, Smalllntegers,\line \expndtw6 true, and false may reside there. Performing context freeing as part of\line \expndtw5 the return operation whenever possible is another important optimiza-\line tion. In addition to saving the overhead of invoking the recursive free-\line ing routine, many other fields in a context are sure to be nonreference\line \expndtw4 counted and are thus ignored. With these changes, recursive freeing ac-\line \expndtw3 counts for less than 2%\super 9\nosupersub  of the execution time. Moving the sender Oop\line \expndtw5 and result Oop instead of copying them saved four more count opera-\line \expndtw4 tions in the return bytecode. Tables \lang1032 11.3 \lang1033 and \lang1032 11.4 \lang1033 enumerate the refer-\line \expndtw5 ence-count operations required for a full send and return.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri40\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Be warned: explicitly altering either the stack pointer or the fields\line \expndtw2 above the top of stack (both pointless actions) can compromise the in-\line \expndtw8 tegrity of an implementation with this optimization. This hazard has\line \expndtw3 not posed a problem for BS or the Dorado implementation.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1672\margrsxn736\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5184 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 194{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex122\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Berkeley Smalltalk: Who Knows Where the Time Goes?}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3893\margrsxn2248\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft14\shptop324\shpright6667\shpbottom324\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li922\fi0\ri0\sb382\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 11.3 \lang1033 Reference-Count Operations for Full Sends}\par \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-259 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1462 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2081 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3780\posnegy230\absw0\absh4053\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 By-the-book}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3780\posnegy230\absw0\absh4053\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Bi}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-281 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1462 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2081 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li324\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3780\posnegy230\absw0\absh4053\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 1}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3780\posnegy230\absw0\absh4053\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 0}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1462 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2081 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li320\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \