of indirections per bytecode. Ta-\line \expndtw7 ble \lang1032 11.14 \lang1033 shows these results. These optimizations reduce indirection}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1537\margrsxn724\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5216 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 202{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Berkeley Smalltalk: Who Knows Where the Time Goes?}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri745\sb288\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 from a major cost of the interpreter to merely \lang1032 4% \lang1033 of the total cost.\line \expndtw3 There have been proposals to use 32-bit Oops and eliminate the object\line table. Table \lang1032 11.14 \lang1033 implies that eliminating the speed penalty of the in-\line \expndtw6 direction is a poor justification for abolishing the object table. There\line \expndtw2 may of course, be other, more compelling reasons.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa313\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-331 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2830 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx7410 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1242\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 11.14 \lang1033 Cost}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 of Object Table Indirection}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-482 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2830 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3867 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5084 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx7410 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li648\fi