4054\shptop-18\shpright5739\shpbottom-18\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft6077\shptop-4\shpright7733\shpbottom-4\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2218\fi0\ri22\sb382\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex97\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Using the equations above, the ratio S\sub xK\nosupersub /S\sub optimal\nosupersub  is computed. This ratio\line \expndtw14 expresses how close the implementation comes to the theoretical lower\line limit of zero access time for method lookups, and may be expressed as}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3438\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\tx4612\tx6905 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex97\expndtw1\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 \'ef\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\sub\lang1033 K{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\nosupersub\expndtw-17\lang1032 1 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'ef\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \sub\lang1033 nc\nosupersub  \i\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 A \sub nc{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\nosupersub\expndtw0\lang1032 1}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3233\shptop7\shpright3903\shpbottom7\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4241\shptop7\shpright5955\shpbottom7\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft6293\shptop14\shpright7819\shpbottom14\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3236\fi0\ri0\sb86\sa0\tx4248 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 ^optimal{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw0\lang1032 1 \lang1033 ~ *^ \i nc \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 (A \sub nc\nosupersub  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \sub\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\'ca\nosupersub\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 )         \i0 1 + \i\lang1033 &\sub optimal\nosupersub  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \sub\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\'ca}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi0\ri7\sb187\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Furthermore, the ratio S\sub xK\nosupersub /S\sub nc\nosupersub  gives the factor increase in execution\line \expndtw11 speed of a cache implementation compared to a non-cache implementa-\line \expndtw13 tion. This ratio can be written as}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4345\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0\tx5900 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 S\sub xK{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\nosupersub\expndtw0\lang1032 1}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4990\shptop-22\shpright6696\shpbottom-22\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4363\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw22\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 S^      l-S\sub nc\nosupersub fi(A\sub nc\nosupersub -A\sub xK\nosupersub )}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2243\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 If we consider a given cache organization, and a fixed program behav-\line \expndtw8 ior, the speed increase has the form \lang1032 1 \lang1033 /(l-KS\sub nc\nosupersub ), where \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ca\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 =/3(A\sub nc\nosupersub -A\sub xK\nosupersub ).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 One use of this formula is to determine the speed increase that a\line \expndtw15 non-cache-implementation will get by using a caching scheme. In the}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1587\margrsxn1394\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4198\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5418\tlul\tx8644 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 .{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-15 243}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 The Implementation in Berkeley Smalltalk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri22\sb288\sa1242\sl-227\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 next section we present a particular Smalltalk-80 implementation, and\line \expndtw2 on p. \lang1032 245 \lang1033 we present some measurements on its behavior. Implementors\line \expndtw5 can use these measurements as guidelines to obtain for their particular\line \expndtw3 case, approximations to the expected gains.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1587\margrsxn1401\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1908\colsr349\colno2\colw6663 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-313\shpright8928\shpbottom-313\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 The}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Implementation\line \expndtw11 in Berkeley\line \expndtw9 Smalltalk}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Currently, Smalltalk-80 virtual machines are being implemented on\line \expndtw3 general purpose computers. Smalltalk-80 implementors should consider\line \expndtw2 adding a software cache for method-lookup. As an example of the con-\line \expndtw5 siderations involved, we now present, in some detail, the implementa-\line \expndtw1 tion used in BS. BS executes on the VAX-\lang1032 11 \lang1033 family of computers, under\line \expndtw3 the UNIX operating system. The programming language used is C. The\line algorithms were coded with extreme care (sometimes checking the code\line produced by the C compiler), and macros were used whenever possible.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1595\margrsxn1408\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb644\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1595\margrsxn1408\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1591\colsr659\colno2\colw6656 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Cache Structure}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The implemented cache is of the direct-mapped variety. The underlying\line \expndtw7 data structure consists of four arrays, each with IK entries. The first\line \expndtw5 three arrays have entries \lang1032 2 \lang1033 bytes long (one VAX-\lang1032 11 \lang1033 word); the last one\line \expndtw3\lang1032 1 \lang1033 byte long. The first two arrays (CacheSel and CacheClass) contain the\line Oops of the combinations for selector and class that are cached. The re-\line maining two (CacheMethod and CachePrimitive) contain the actual in-\line \expndtw4 formation: the method Oop, and an indication of whether it is a primi-\line \expndtw3 tive or not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The organization as separate arrays allows the use of the VAX-\lang1032 11 \i\lang1033 in-\line dex \i0 mode to access all desired information. Thanks to a careful man-\line \expndtw8 agement of the available registers on the hardware, most of the time\line \expndtw1 the fastest modes can be employed. Thus, to access CacheSel[i], if i is in\line \expndtw2 register rO, we can simply use}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 movw CacheSelfrO],}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that an implementation as a field in an array of records would\line \expndtw2 produce much longer code, including shifts (or divides) to access the ap-\line \expndtw5 propriate entry in the array, plus additional code to access the field.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri4\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Recently BS has been extended to include a playback facility. With\line \expndtw3 this facility, a script of all the bytecodes executed in a session can be\line \expndtw6 obtained, and later replayed to reproduce the session. In this way we\line \expndtw4 have been able to study the effect of different cache sizes on the hit ra-\line tio. For a particular interactive session of about 2M bytecodes, using as\line a hash function a simple EX-OR the hit ratios found were:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1646\margrsxn1388\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7034 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 244{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 An Assessment of Method-Lookup Caches for Smalltalk-80 Implementations}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2257\shptop342\shpright8903\shpbottom342\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3974\fi0\ri0\sb403\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 13.2 \lang1033 Hit Ratio and Cache Size}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2250\shptop25\shpright8903\shpbottom25\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2250\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\tx3283\tx4086\tx4871\tx5666\tx6462 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 entries{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-9\lang1032 64{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-11 128{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-9 256{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-10 512{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 1024        2048       4096}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2250\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 hit ratio       \lang1032 65.3%      77.0%      86.1%      90.4%      93.1%      95.0%    95.4%}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2250\shptop50\shpright8910\shpbottom50\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi0\ri4\sb342\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This makes a cache of size IK or 2K the more adequate for most situa-\line \expndtw-1 tions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2207\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i The lookup algorithm \i0 The lookup algorithm is simple. An entry on\line \expndtw2 the cache is selected using some function of the class and selector Oops.\line \expndtw3 Then a comparison is done to check the validity of the information as-\line \expndtw7 sociated to the entry. On a hit, the required information is already\line \expndtw3 obtained. On a miss we have to go through the complete lookup search;\line \expndtw5 when the correct binding is found, the entries in the cache are updated\line with the appropriate information.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2210\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Clearly, the selection of the hashing function is important. Three\line \expndtw3 simple functions are:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2448\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2704 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2448\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2704 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 hash(class,selector) \lang1032 <- \lang1033 (class EXOR selector) AND cacheSize}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2448\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2704 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2448\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2704 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 hash(class,selector) \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 (class AND selector) AND cacheSize}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2448\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2704 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2448\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2704 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 hash(class,selector) \lang1032 <- \lang1033 (class ADD selector) AND cacheSize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2207\fi79\ri14\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As the difference in speed between an EXOR and an ADD is small, on\line \expndtw7 the order of \lang1032 .07 \lang1033 us (approximate value for the \lang1032 2 \lang1033 register versions of\line \expndtw3 these instructions on a VAX-\lang1032 11/780), 1 \lang1033 and \lang1032 3 \lang1033 have similar qualities. Al-\line \expndtw6 though AND requires two instructions on the VAX-\lang1032 11, \lang1033 it also has a\line \expndtw4 similar speed. The total access time in the case of a hit, A\sub hit\nosupersub  is reason-\line \expndtw3 ably small because of the explicit handling of registers done in C.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2214\fi234\ri4\sb11\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The playback facility of BS has allowed us to compare the different\line \expndtw5 functions. The results for the same sample as mentioned above are as\line \expndtw-7 follows:}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2200\shptop493\shpright8846\shpbottom493\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3679\fi0\ri0\sb547\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 13.3 \lang1033 Hit Ratio and Hash Function}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa270\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx878 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2663 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3541 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Function}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Hit Ratio}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\super\cf0\charscalex145\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 A\nosupersub hit}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A IK}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-295 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx878 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2663 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3541 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 1.}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li115\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 93.1%}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 4.4}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 23.6}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx878 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2663 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3541 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 2.}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li115\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 61.6%}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 4.5}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 111.82}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-194 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx878 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2663 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3541 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li238\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 3.}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li112\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 94.8%}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 4.5}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 19.08}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2192\shptop101\shpright8845\shpbottom101\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri22\sb407\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is clear that \lang1032 2 \lang1033 is a loser and its use is discouraged. The function origi-\line \expndtw3 nally used in BS was \lang1032 1; \lang1033 its performance is acceptable. The best choice is}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1551\margrsxn1423\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4183\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8644 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex124\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 245}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex124\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Measurements in BS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 3, \lang1033 the hashing function currently being used in BS (and also in the Dol-\line \expndtw-1 phin and Dorado implementations\super 4\nosupersub ).}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop871\shpright8931\shpbottom871\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1195\sa0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Measurements{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw4 We now present some measurements of the BS implementation. They}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw25\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 in BS{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 show a \lang1032 32-37% \lang1033 increase in the execution speed of the implementation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 When these measurements were made, the hashing function used\line \expndtw1 was the one referred to as \lang1032 1 \lang1033 above; otherwise the implementation is the\line \expndtw0 one presented there. Two different Smalltalk-80 programs were execut-\line \expndtw2 ed to obtain representative samples. One program was an interactive\line \expndtw1 session of editing, browsing, and short arithmetic computations com-\line \expndtw0 prising \lang1032 6.8 \lang1033 million bytecodes. The other was the Tower of Hanoi prob-\line \expndtw-2 lem, a computation intensive problem, comprising \lang1032 1.5 \lang1033 million bytecodes.\line \expndtw0 On a VAX-\lang1032 11/780, \lang1033 the constants for cache accesses for BS are}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4853\fi0\ri3179\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A\sub hlt\nosupersub  \i0\lang1032 4.4\line \i\expndtw0\lang1033 A\sub nc\nosupersub  \i0\lang1032 187\line \i\expndtw5\lang1033\fs18 A\sub miss\nosupersub    \i0\lang1032 284}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The data from the two programs and the computed results are summa-\line \expndtw0 rized in the table below.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa414\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-324 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1332\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Table}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 13.4 \lang1033 Berkeley Smalltalk Results}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-425 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Parameter}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li889\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Interactive}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Tower of Hanoi}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-223 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 \'f6\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 (hit ratio)}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1109\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.943}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri500\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.996}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-259 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 \'e2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i0\lang1033 lookup/bytecode}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1109\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.186}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri500\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.078}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-230 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 A\sub 1K\nosupersub  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'ec\'e2\'e2\'ef}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1112\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 20.4}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri504\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 5.52}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 S\sub nc\nosupersub  bytecodes/sec}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1102\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 8,750}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri508\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 17,350}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\super\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 S\nosupersub opt\sub ima\nosupersub i bytecodes/sec}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1102\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 12,570}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri508\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 23,230}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 S\sub 1K\nosupersub  bytecodes/sec}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1105\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 12,000}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri508\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 23,000}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-216 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\super\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 F\nosupersub nc}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1116\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.304}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri504\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.253}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 F\sub 1K}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1116\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.046}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri500\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.010}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-281 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs14 \'b0\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 1\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'ca\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ' \lang1033 ^optimal}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1066\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 0.955}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri457\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0.990}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-302 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1987 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4932 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6689 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex60\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 s\sub 1K\nosupersub /s\sub nc}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri1066\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 1.372}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri457\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 1.326}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri0\sb385\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The numeric values for the parameters and values were obtained using\line \expndtw2 various mechanisms. A\sub hit\nosupersub  was obtained from the VAX-\lang1032 11 \lang1033 code that\line \expndtw1 accesses the cache. The exact time used by the sequence of instructions\line \expndtw-1 is difficult to measure because of the effect of the VAX-\lang1032 11/780 \lang1033 cache;\line \expndtw1 timing the instruction loop gives an overly optimistic value since all the}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1622\margrsxn1352\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7063 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 246{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 An Assessment of Method-Lookup Caches for Smalltalk-80 Implementations}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 code ends up in the cache. A\sub nc\nosupersub  and A\sub miss\nosupersub  were obtained by profiling the\line \expndtw1 execution of the updating routines\super 5\nosupersub . The difference between the A\sub nc\nosupersub  val-\line ue and the A\sub miss\nosupersub  is the time required to update the cache. The cache is\line \expndtw4 updated every time there is a cache miss. The profile tool also gave the\line \expndtw2 number of lookups and the number of bytecodes executed, used to de-\line termine \i\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e2\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 , \i0\lang1033 and the number of misses, used to determine \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'f6\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 . \lang1033 Finally, S\sub 1K\line \nosupersub\expndtw4 was obtained from the user time it took to execute the programs. The\line remaining values were obtained from the equations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi230\ri0\sb0\sa1123\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are several important points to make. The software cache\line \expndtw1 achieves an average method access time of \lang1032 20.4 \lang1033 microseconds for the in-\line \expndtw4 teractive session. The Tower of Hanoi problem is atypical and tends to\line \expndtw6 give a best case result since the small amount of code generates few\line \expndtw4 cache misses. Nevertheless note that both hit ratios are high, \lang1032 94% \lang1033 and\line \expndtw3\lang1032 99%. \lang1033 The cache of IK entries increases execution speed by \lang1032 37.2 \lang1033 percent\line (interactive), and \lang1032 32.6 \lang1033 percent (Hanoi). Execution speed is slower than\line \expndtw0 ^optimal by only \lang1032 4.5 \lang1033 percent (interactive) and \lang1032 1.0 \lang1033 percent (Hanoi). (Note\line \expndtw4 that, since the two execution profiles are different, we cannot compare\line \expndtw3 the two executions directly.)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1626\margrsxn1356\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1447\colsr806\colno2\colw6674 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-245\shpright8938\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusions}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Fast method access is important in achieving better Smalltalk-80 per-\line \expndtw7 formance. We have presented the main parameters and relations in-\line \expndtw8 volved in the method lookup process. From these it has been shown\line that the benefits of adding a method cache depend on the relation of\line \expndtw9 the overall speed of the implementation to the speed of the cache\line \expndtw6 accesses, as well as on the more traditional considerations of hit ratio\line \expndtw3 and miss/hit access speeds.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 On an optimized VAX-11/780 implementation, the addition of a soft-\line \expndtw7 ware-supported method cache produced a nine-fold reduction of the\line \expndtw2 time required by the lookup process. This by itself, increased the overall\line \expndtw4 speed of the Berkeley Smalltalk-80 implementation by \lang1032 37%.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1622\margrsxn1366\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1188\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1622\margrsxn1366\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1569\colsr691\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-306\shpright8928\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Acknowledg-\line ments}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We heartily thank all the people on the Smalltalk island, both at\line \expndtw3 Berkeley and at Xerox Palo Alto Research Center. We especially thank\line \expndtw12 the two Daves, Dave Ungar for making BS a reality, and Dave\line \expndtw8 Patterson for encouraging us to write this paper. Without them this\line \expndtw5 work would have never existed. We also want to thank all the review-\line ers, their comments largely increased the readability of this paper.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1557\margrsxn1388\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7927\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8669 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 247}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 References}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop317\shpright8935\shpbottom317\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb637\sa0\tx2520 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 References{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\charscalex112\expndtw2\lang1032 1. \b0\lang1033 Goldberg, Adele, and Robson, David, \i Smalltalk-80: The Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2768\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 and Its Implementation, \i0 Addison-Wesley, Reading, Mass., \lang1032 1983.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-230\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-230\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Krasner, Glenn, "The Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine", \i Byte \i0 vol. \lang1032 6,\line \expndtw-1\lang1033 no. \lang1032 8, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 300-320, \lang1033 Aug. \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Lipway, J. S. "The Structural Aspects of the System/\lang1032 360 \lang1033 Model \lang1032 85\line \expndtw-1\lang1033 II: The Cache", \i IBM Systems Journal \i0 vol. \lang1032 7, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 1, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 15-21, 1968.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2502\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2502\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Deutsch, L. Peter, Private Communication, \lang1032 1982.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-259\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Graham, Susan L., Kessler, Peter B., and McKursick, Marshall K.,\line \expndtw8 "Gprof:  A Profiler Using Call Graphs",  in Proceedings of the\line \expndtw0 Sigplan Conference on Compiler Construction, June \lang1032 1982.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn16834\pghsxn11909\marglsxn1440\margrsxn3139\margtsxn1285\margbsxn360\lndscpsxn \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx0\posnegy0\absw0\absh9339\nowrap\dfrmtxtx10080\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw21615\pich16471\picwgoal12254\pichgoal9338\jpegblip
}\par{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn10829\margrsxn360\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-263\shpright364\shpbottom-263\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Int}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1370\margrsxn1979\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3809\shptop-256\shpright8554\shpbottom-256\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3823\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 LOOM\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Large Object\line \expndtw6 Oriented Memory for\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 Systems}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3838\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0\sl-277\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Ted Kaehler\line \expndtw4 Glenn Krasner}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3816\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Software Concepts Group\line \expndtw-1 Xerox Palo Alto Research Center\line Palo Alto, California}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop1195\shpright8561\shpbottom1195\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1505\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1897 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 roduction{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw0 The Smalltalk-80 virtual machine is specified as a memory-resident sys-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1890\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 tem containing up to 2\super 15\nosupersub  objects. When full, it typically occupies about\line 2M bytes of memory. Unfortunately, many machines do not have this\line \expndtw2 capacity in main memory, and many applications require, or will re-\line \expndtw0 quire, more than this capacity. To solve this space problem, one typical-\line \expndtw3 ly uses a virtual memory system in which the resident, "real" memory\line \expndtw7 is used as a cache for the larger mass storage, "virtual" memory.\line \expndtw0 LOOM, Large Object-Oriented Memory, is a virtual memory system\line designed and implemented for the Smalltalk-80 system. The most im-\line portant feature of the LOOM design is that it provides virtual addresses\line \expndtw4 that are much wider than either the word size or the memory address\line \expndtw1 size of the computer on which it runs.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1879\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 LOOM is a single-user virtual memory system that swaps objects and\line \expndtw0 operates without assistance from the programmer. Virtual memory sys-\line \expndtw2 tems may be characterized by the amount of attention that the pro-\line grammer must pay to the transfers between virtual and real memories,\line \expndtw1 by the extent to which the memory is shared among users, and by the\line \expndtw4 granularity of transfer between memory levels. Overlay mechanisms\line are an example of systems that require much programmer attention,\line \expndtw6 while all common paging systems require none\super 1\nosupersub . Databases may be}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1894\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Xerox Corporation, \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 251}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1624\margrsxn1368\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6113 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 252{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 LOOM \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Large Object-Oriented Memory for Smalltalk-80 Systems}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri22\sb259\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 viewed as the extreme in allowing sharing; the virtual memory for\line \expndtw-1 Interlisp-D\super 2\nosupersub  is one example of a single-user virtual memory. Most over-\line \expndtw2 lay systems transfer program segments, while paging systems transfer\line \expndtw-1 disk pages, and a few systems such as the OOZE virtual memory for\line \expndtw-2 Smalltalk-76\super 3\nosupersub  transfer objects.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop774\shpright8924\shpbottom774\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1094\sa0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The LOOM{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw7 We view virtual memory design as a process of trying to determine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Design{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw1 what happens most often, making it go fast, and hoping that it will con-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 tinue to be what happens most often. Our experience with previous\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk systems gave us three major assumptions on which we based\line \expndtw2 the LOOM design: programmers and users have a large appetite for\line \expndtw-1 memory, object-swapping is an efficient and effective scheme, and the\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk-80 design for handling resident objects is worth keeping.\line \expndtw0 From these assumptions and the desire to provide a large number of ob-\line jects on a machine with a narrow word width, we created the major de-\line \expndtw-3 sign decisions.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 LOOM assumes that the object is the unit of locality of reference.\line \expndtw1 It swaps individual objects between primary and secondary memo-\line ry, and allows into main memory only those objects actually need-\line ed by the interpreter. Unlike paging systems, LOOM packs objects\line \expndtw2 in main memory at maximum density.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 LOOM is designed for machines with 16-bit words. Fields of objects\line \expndtw1 in main memory are \lang1032 16 \lang1033 bits wide.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The address space of the secondary memory is large. LOOM allows\line \expndtw-5 as many as 2\super 31\nosupersub  objects.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2678 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2678\fi-198\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2678 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The interpreter accesses objects in main memory exactly as it does\line \expndtw3 in a resident Smalltalk-80 interpreter. When the necessary objects\line \expndtw5 are already in main memory, the interpreter runs as fast as it did\line \expndtw2 in the resident system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2218\fi0\ri18\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In order to allow the large number of possible objects, and yet treat the\line \expndtw2 resident objects in the same way they are treated in a non-LOOM\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk-80 implementation, we decided to create two different name\line \expndtw3 spaces. The same object is identified by names from different spaces\line \expndtw2 when it resides in different parts of the system, as shown in Fig. \lang1032 14.1.\line \expndtw1\lang1033 The identifier of an object is called an \i Oop, \i0 which stands for "object\line \expndtw0 pointer." An object in secondary storage has a 32-bit Oop (a long Oop),\line \expndtw4 and each of its fields containing a pointer to another object holds that}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2577\margrsxn1325\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6246\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7726 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 253}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6304\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The LOOM Design}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4111\margrsxn6596\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4014\shptop248\shpright5166\shpbottom248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-191\shptop846\shpright-191\shpbottom1645\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1343\shptop846\shpright1343\shpbottom1652\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5166\posnegy292\absw0\absh1944\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw2311\pich3429\picwgoal1310\pichgoal1944\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3197\posnegy695\absw3143\absh-972\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Main Memory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li202\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3197\posnegy695\absw3143\absh-972\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 16-bit object pointers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li202\fi173\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3197\posnegy695\absw3143\absh-972\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (short Oops)\line \expndtw1 16-bit values in fields of objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li94\fi-94\ri0\sb1004\sa238\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw8 Interpreter}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4024\margrsxn3316\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw2376\colsr670\colno2\colw1522 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4208\shptop-252\shpright5382\shpbottom-252\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4619\shptop-130\shpright4619\shpbottom727\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 20320}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4864\shptop-130\shpright4864\shpbottom727\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 20320}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To the interpreter, objects\line \expndtw-7 look very much like they did\line \expndtw2 in resident Smalltalk-80}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object Swapping}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2577\margrsxn5508\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb198\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2577\margrsxn5508\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1083\colsr364\colno2\colw2376 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5598\shptop-43\shpright6736\shpbottom-43\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx6736\posnegy0\absw0\absh1980\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw2247\pich3492\picwgoal1274\pichgoal1980\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4403\posnegy7\absw0\absh1973\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw2286\pich3479\picwgoal1296\pichgoal1972\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5180\posnegy425\absw2142\absh-976\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Secondary Memory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li191\fi0\ri0\sb54\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5180\posnegy425\absw2142\absh-976\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 32-bit object pointers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li191\fi191\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5180\posnegy425\absw2142\absh-976\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (long Oops)\line \expndtw0 32-bit values in fields}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2214\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 14.1}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1811\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To the large secondary\line \expndtw10 memory, main memory\line \expndtw-3 looks like a cache.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2577\margrsxn1325\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1343\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pointer as a 32-bit Oop. An object cached in main memory has a 16-bit\line \expndtw2 Oop (a short Oop) and 16-bit fields. As in the resident Smalltalk-80 im-\line \expndtw3 plementation, main memory has a resident object table \i \{ROT \i0 or some-\line times called an OT), which contains the actual main memory address of\line each resident object. An object's short Oop is an index into the ROT, so\line \expndtw4 that the object's address can be determined from its Oop with a single\line \expndtw3 addition and memory reference. When an object is brought into main\line memory from disk, it is assigned a short Oop, and those of its fields that\line \expndtw9 refer to other objects in main memory are assigned the appropriate\line \expndtw4 short Oop. Fields pointing to objects that are not resident are handled\line \expndtw3 specially, the details of which make up the crux of LOOM.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1339\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Thus, when all objects in the working set are in main memory,\line \expndtw3 LOOM behaves just like a resident Smalltalk-80 implementation\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 all\line objects have short Oops that index the ROT, providing their actual core\line \expndtw4 address. When an object in core must access one of its fields that refers\line \expndtw3 to an object that is not in core, something special must happen. LOOM\line \expndtw4 brings that object into core, assigns it a short Oop, and resumes normal\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk execution. The main memory resident space of 2\super 15\nosupersub  objects acts\line \expndtw2 as a cache for up to 2\super 31\nosupersub  objects on the disk.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1676\margrsxn1295\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6134 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex120\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 254{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa572 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex120\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 LOOM\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 Large Object-Oriented Memory for Smalltalk-80 Systems}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1676\margrsxn1306\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1789\colsr468\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-299\shpright8932\shpbottom-299\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The LOOM\line \expndtw0 Details}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop1595\shpright1905\shpbottom1595\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb1786\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Representation of\line \expndtw2 Resident Objects}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The important issues in the LOOM design implementation are:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The representation of resident objects,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The representation of objects in secondary memory,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The translation between representations, and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx446 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The identification of times when the translations must occur.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Resident objects are represented in a manner similar to their represen-\line \expndtw4 tation in a resident Smalltalk-80 system. Each object has as its name in\line \expndtw5 main memory, a short (16-bit) Oop. The Oop indexes the ROT in order\line \expndtw7 to provide the starting address of the object's body, as shown in Fig.\line \expndtw3\lang1032 14.2. \lang1033 The ROT entry also has reference-count bits, and a few other bits,\line \expndtw5 described later. The body of each object contains a word for the length\line \expndtw4 of the body, a pointer to the object's class, and the object's fields. Each\line \expndtw3 field is either a pointer to another object or a collection of "bits", in the\line \expndtw5 same manner as resident Smalltalk-80 fields. We will only deal with\line \expndtw7 pointer fields here. Each field (as well as the class pointer) that refers}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1676\margrsxn1295\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3535\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Format of Objects in Main Memory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3521\fi0\ri0\sb270\sa0\tx6635 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Resident Object Table{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }Body of an Object}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4115\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa151 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 (ROT)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3951\margrsxn1295\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols4\colno1\colw867\colsr1915\colno2\colw1047\colsr882\colno3\colw720\colsr464\colno4\colw766 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1465\shptop0\shpright5835\shpbottom2837\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw7708\pich5003\picwgoal4370\pichgoal2836\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;9;(0,0);(0,18420);(11956,18420);(11956,21600);(21600,21600);(21600,18420);(21600,18420);(21600,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb612\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Indexed by\line short Oop\line \expndtw-5 (16-bits)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1386\sa0\sl-184\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ROT entry\line \expndtw0 of an object}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Main Memory\line \expndtw2 address of an\line \expndtw1 Object Body}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb32\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af3\hich\f1\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 \u9632\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 16-bits-}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb450\sa0\sl-180\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Object\line \expndtw0 Header}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1015\sa0\sl-184\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Fields of\line \expndtw1 the Object}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2597\margrsxn5442\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb932\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2597\margrsxn5442\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1087\colsr2063\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 14.2}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 - \lang1033 16-bits-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1593\margrsxn1373\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7106\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8456 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex79\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 J?55}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri43\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The LOOM Details}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2236\fi0\ri83\sb173\sa0\sl-223\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 to another resident object contains the short Oop of that object Fields\line \expndtw4 that refer to non-resident objects (objects on secondary storage) contain\line \expndtw3 a short Oop of one of two types, a \i leaf or \i0 a \i lambda.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi227\ri61\sb4\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition to these fields, resident objects in a LOOM system have\line three extra words. Two of these words contain the long (32-bit) Oop of\line \expndtw4 that object. The third word, known as the delta word, contains a delta\line reference count and some other bits. The short Oop of an object is not\line only an index into the ROT for that object's address, but is also the re-\line \expndtw6 sult of a hash function applied to that object's long Oop. See Fig \lang1032 14.3,\line \lang1033 p. \lang1032 256. \lang1033 The algorithm for translating an object's short Oop to its long\line \expndtw-4 Oop is:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2477\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2477\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index the ROT with the short Oop to get the body address}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2477\fi0\ri0\sb94\sa0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2477\fi0\ri0\sb94\sa0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Load the long Oop from the first two words of the body}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2236\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The algorithm for translating an object's long Oop to its short Oop is:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi-259\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2732 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi-259\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2732 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Convert the long Oop into a short Oop by applying the hash func-\line \expndtw3 tion}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2473\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2732 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2473\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2732 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index the ROT with this short Oop to get a body address}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2473\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2732 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2473\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2732 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Look at the first two words of the body}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2473\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2732 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2473\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx2732 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If they match the long Oop, then the short Oop is correct}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi-259\ri0\sb90\sa367\sl-241\slmult0\tx2732 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2732\fi-259\ri0\sb90\sa367\sl-241\slmult0\tx2732 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw16\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If not, create a new short Oop from  the current one with a\line \expndtw2 reprobe function (e.g., add \lang1032 1), \lang1033 and go to step \lang1032 2}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1593\margrsxn1373\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1900\colsr349\colno2\colw6692 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Representation of\line \expndtw0 Objects in\line Secondary Memory}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Secondary memory is addressed as a linear space of 32-bit words. Ob-\line jects start with a header word that contains \lang1032 16 \lang1033 bits of length and some\line \expndtw3 status bits. Each pointer field in the object is \lang1032 32 \lang1033 bits wide. Non-pointer\line \expndtw6 fields (such as the bytes in Strings) are packed, with \lang1032 4 \lang1033 bytes in each\line \expndtw4 32-bit word. Resident Smalltalk-80 Smalllntegers are rather short to be\line \expndtw3 occupying a full word on the disk. However, since they represent legiti-\line \expndtw7 mate object pointers, their \lang1032 15 \lang1033 significant bits are stored along with a\line \expndtw3 flag value in a 32-bit pointer field on the disk. The long Oops in pointer\line \expndtw2 fields are 31-bit disk pointers, addressing as many objects as will fit into\line \expndtw0 2\super 31\nosupersub  disk words (32-bit words). Fields of objects on secondary storage al-\line \expndtw4 ways refer to objects in secondary storage and do not change when the\line \expndtw8 object to which they point is currently cached in main \i memory. \i0 As\line \expndtw10 shown in Fig. \lang1032 14.4, \lang1033 no information about primary memory is ever\line \expndtw3 stored in secondary memory. Information such as an object's short Oop,\line \expndtw5 its location in primary memory, or whether it is currently cached in\line \expndtw4 primary memory are never recorded in secondary memory.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1675\margrsxn2969\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw6379\colsr162\colno2\colw723 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6098 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex118\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 256{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex118\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 LOOM \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Large Object-Oriented Memory for Smalltalk-80 Systems}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Finding an Object's Long Oop from Its Short Oop}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2448\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\tx5242 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ROT{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 Object Body}\par \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phcol\posnegx2027\posnegy-50\absw0\absh2152\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw5308\pich3797\picwgoal3009\pichgoal2152\jpegblip
