}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx850\posnegy5897\absw0\absh-158\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs14 - 10 1 \lang1033 'hoodie: scrolling}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 15.12}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 15.13}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1555\margrsxn1433\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1458\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1555\margrsxn1433\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1105\colsr1145\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7\shptop-428\shpright8935\shpbottom-428\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Creating}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Database}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Entries}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb47\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There is also a special window used for creating each kind of database\line record. The user sends a Smalltalk message to the VersionHandler re-\line \expndtw4 questing to submit a bug report, bug fix, goodie or new system version.\line \expndtw6 In response, a new window is created that contains a form for the user\line \expndtw4 to fill in. The entry is added to the database when the user selects the\line \expndtw2 accept command.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri4\sb29\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 15.14 \lang1033 shows the window for creating a \i system change \i0 entry\line which fixes some known bug. The lower half of the window contains a\line \expndtw5 bug report browser. The user selects the titles of the bug reports which\line he has fixed. Before selecting accept, the user also fills in a title, his\line \expndtw2 name, and the name of the Smalltalk-80 code file he has already created.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1546\margrsxn1384\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li65\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4633 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 284{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li61\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Managing the Evolution of Smalltalk-80 Systems}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2333\fi238\ri0\sb252\sa490\sl-230\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The user can submit changes or goodies which are not bug fixes us-\line \expndtw3 ing a window which resembles just the top half of the bug fix window.\line \expndtw4 Bug reports are also submitted using a similar window.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1546\margrsxn4624\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1202\colsr3334\colno2\colw1202 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx-11\posnegy0\absw0\absh6048\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw15976\pich10668\picwgoal9057\pichgoal6048\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 15.14}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb32\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 15.15}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1546\margrsxn1384\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2225\fi0\ri86\sb554\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Fig. \lang1032 15.15 \lang1033 shows a new system version being created. This is done by\line \expndtw4 selecting from a menu of the \i system changes \i0 (goodies) submitted since\line the last version. A conflict detection program can then point out possi-\line \expndtw3 ble conflicts between the selected changes. (This is currently \i not \i0 done\line \expndtw2 automatically.) The ID's of any bugs which have not been fixed by the\line \expndtw4 selected changes are inherited and included in the new version. When-\line ever a new version is created, the source files involved are automatical-\line \expndtw11 ly copied to a new sub-directory on the file server to freeze and\line \expndtw4 preserve the integrity of the new Smalltalk-80 system. The new system\line \expndtw8 may be released as a new image file, or, due to the large overhead of}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2332\margrsxn1473\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3319\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7794 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-22\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 285}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Creating Database Entries}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1444\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 generating an entire system, it may be left as an \i interim release \i0 which\line \expndtw3 can be quickly generated from a previous image version each time it is\line \expndtw-3 used.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1426\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa1397\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Fig. \lang1032 15.16 \lang1033 is a diagram of a portion of the database. Bug reports are\line \expndtw4 shown in the first column with lines connecting them with entries for\line \expndtw3 corresponding fixes, if any. The second column shows the changes (in-\line \expndtw4 cluding bug fixes) which have not yet been incorporated into any ver-\line \expndtw3 sion; these make up the "goodies" application library. The third column\line shows changes entries connected by lines to the versions in which they\line have been incorporated. The rectangular boxes denote image versions,\line \expndtw4 while the large ovals are interim release versions. The links between\line \expndtw5 version entries and bug reports are not shown. When the next version,\line \i\expndtw2 V9, \i0 is created, the changes \i C21, C22, \i0 and \i C23 \i0 will no longer be consid-\line \expndtw1 ered "goodies."}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3790\margrsxn2517\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw720\colsr1130\colno2\colw936\colsr983\colno3\colw1832 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi173\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-144\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Bug\line \expndtw-8 Reports}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li166\fi-166\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-148\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Incorporated\line \expndtw-9 Changes}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb68\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 System Versions}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2332\margrsxn1473\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2376\fi0\ri0\sb40\sa25 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 "Goodies"}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2332\margrsxn1509\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1206\colsr6142\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1447\posnegy0\absw0\absh5076\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw10185\pich8953\picwgoal5774\pichgoal5076\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4874\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 15.16}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-140\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 image\line \expndtw-9 version}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1292\sa0\sl-144\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 interim\line \expndtw-8 version}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb1012\sa0\sl-137\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 image\line \expndtw-8 version}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1008\sa0\sl-140\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 next\line \expndtw-9 version}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1263\margrsxn1696\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4568 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 286{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa515 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Managing the Evolution of Smalltalk-80 Systems}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1306\margrsxn1696\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1321\colsr932\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-274\shpright8910\shpbottom-274\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusion}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb47\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The system version database has proved to be a very useful software\line \expndtw3 tool for managing and maintaining our Smalltalk-80 research system. It\line has provided us with a convenient mechanism for documenting versions\line \expndtw4 of the Smalltalk-80 system. Each change made to the system has a cor-\line \expndtw6 responding entry in the database. This provides a complete history of\line \expndtw4 the system's evolution. The most popular feature is that users may sub-\line \expndtw3 mit or browse bug reports directly from their Smalltalk-80 system. The\line \expndtw6 database also provides a software applications library which may be\line \expndtw3 easily accessed and augmented.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1263\margrsxn1696\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb266\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1263\margrsxn1696\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx745\posnegy0\absw0\absh7876\nowrap\dfrmtxtx10080\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw13855\pich13893\picwgoal7855\pichgoal7876\jpegblip
}\par{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1186\margrsxn1331\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4180\shptop-306\shpright8932\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4183\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Implementing a\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 File System\line \expndtw11 and the Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw9 System as a\line \expndtw5 Programming Tool}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 D. Jason Penney*}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4183\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1130\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tektronix, Inc.\line \expndtw0 Beaverton, Oregon}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1193\margrsxn1802\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1029\colsr1228\colno2\colw6656 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-248\shpright8917\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Abstract}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 system may be used without any file system, but a\line \expndtw3 number of its features presuppose a working file system. Work must be\line done to effect a file system on a new Smalltalk-80 implementation. This\line \expndtw4 paper describes the design and implementation of the file system for a\line \expndtw3 Tektronix Smalltalk-80 implementation and concludes with some reflec\line \expndtw4 tions on the use of Smalltalk-80 as a programming tool.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1186\margrsxn1802\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1267\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1186\margrsxn1802\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1476\colsr767\colno2\colw6678 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-248\shpright8931\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Background}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 system is designed to be host machine independent.\line \expndtw3 The system uses files, but file systems tend to rely on the idiosyncrasies\line \expndtw7 of a particular host. Thus the Smalltalk-80 virtual image can support\line \expndtw6 files for a new implementation in an abstract manner. Higher-level\line \expndtw5 methods in the system usually deal with files through FileStream, a\line \expndtw2 subclass of ReadWriteStream. Files themselves have their functionality\line \expndtw5 partially separated out into abstract classes File, FileDirectory, and\line \expndtw2 FilePage. Fig. \lang1032 16.1 \lang1033 shows the superclass relationships of these classes.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3439\margrsxn1331\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb248\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3439\margrsxn1331\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw5605\colsr814\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 *Mr. Penney is currently employed by Servio Logic Corp., Portland, Oregon.\line \expndtw1 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Tektronix, Inc. \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb166\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 287}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1632\margrsxn1352\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3924 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 288{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa227 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 File System}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2582\margrsxn2504\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1080\colsr720\colno2\colw5022 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2840\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 16.1}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li65\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Object \lang1032 ()\line \lang1033 File \lang1032 ( \lang1033 'fileDirectory' 'fileName' 'pageCache\super 1\nosupersub  'serialNumber'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li187\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 'lastPageNumber' 'binary' 'readWrite' 'error')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li119\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 TekFile \lang1032 (\lang1033 'pageCacheOffset' 'lastPageAddress' 'creationDate'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 'creationTime' 'modificationDate' 'modificationTime'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi187\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 'firstPageMap')\line Object \lang1032 ()\line \expndtw1\lang1033 FileDirectory \lang1032 ( \lang1033 'directoryName' 'closed')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li115\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 TekFileDirectory \lang1032 ( \lang1033 'nextSerialNumber' 'medium' 'freeListFile'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi184\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 'directoryFile' 'directoryStream')\line \expndtw-1 Object \lang1032 ()\line \expndtw3\lang1033 FilePage \lang1032 (\lang1033 'file' 'page' 'binary')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li119\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 TekFilePage \lang1032 ( \lang1033 'address')}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1632\margrsxn1352\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri4\sb472\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The virtual image contains the details of one implementation in the\line \expndtw-3 "concrete" subclasses of File, FileDirectory, and FilePage; to wit, AltoFile,\line \expndtw-4 AltoFileDirectory, and AltoFilePage.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri4\sb4\sa1184\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implementors could implement the primitives these classes require,\line \expndtw7 reimplement subclasses of the abstract classes, or start from scratch.\line \expndtw4 The Alto classes presuppose a particular kind of disk and disk control-\line \expndtw7 ler, as well as the existence of system utilities to manage the disk.\line \expndtw5 Starting from scratch would require reimplementing FileStream, which\line \expndtw3 is the interface class for all file manipulation in the system. We eventu-\line \expndtw1 ally chose the second approach, creating TekFile, TekFileDirectory, and\line \expndtw-2 TekFilePage.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1632\margrsxn1352\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1702\colsr554\colno2\colw6667 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-310\shpright8928\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Description of}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Chosen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Problem}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class FileStream divides files into fixed-length chunks of bytes, called a\line \expndtw2 FilePage. FilePage is intended to map directly to page-oriented media. A\line \expndtw3 FilePage is associated with a File, and has additional state such as the\line \expndtw2 page's pageNumber in the file, actual number of bytes used on the page,\line \expndtw5 and a hardware-related address.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A File contains such things as where to find its FilePages and the\line \expndtw4 String that represents the file's name. When the file is open, some of its\line state is cached in the File instance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A FileDirectory contains the necessary information to access all the\line \expndtw2 files within a given directory. It responds to some Stream messages such\line \expndtw4 as reset and next (but not atEnd). A FileDirectory can iterate over all of\line \expndtw1 its files (with do: but not reverseDo:). A FileDirectory keeps some of its\line \expndtw4 state in object memory when it is open, hence it responds to state-cach-\line \expndtw1 ing messages (open, close, and flush).}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1639\margrsxn1338\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1389\colsr878\colno2\colw6663 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb889\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Alternate}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Programming}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Approaches}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1915\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6383 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 289}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Description of Chosen Problem}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb241\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At Tektronix we did not have the luxury of an existing file system on\line \expndtw4 our Smalltalk-80 hardware. Thus it was not appropriate for us to try to\line \expndtw7 emulate the Alto file system or to make another existing file system\line \expndtw4 work on our hardware and map into the Smalltalk-80 FileStream class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since our virtual machine was originally implemented in Pascal, we\line \expndtw5 briefly considered writing the file system itself in Pascal and providing\line \expndtw7 a minimal interface in the Smalltalk-80 system. The disadvantage in\line \expndtw6 this approach is that the resulting file system would be largely opaque\line \expndtw3 to Smalltalk-80 inspectors and debuggers. Instead we chose to design,\line \expndtw6 implement, and test the file system using Smalltalk and a small num-\line \expndtw3 ber of disk primitives.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1675\margrsxn1313\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb824\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1675\margrsxn1313\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1890\colsr367\colno2\colw6663 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Design Constraints}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our primary constraint was that the file system had to be simple,\line \expndtw4 debuggable, and maintainable, so that we could use the file system in a\line \expndtw5 short amount of time.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Alto file system puts enough redundant information on file data\line \expndtw5 pages so that a "scavenger" can recover significant amounts of a disk\line after a disk crash. We felt that this should be in our own file system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the Smalltalk-80 source file system manipulates one very large\line \expndtw5 file, it is necessary to be able to find the address of a specified page\line \expndtw4 number in the file with relatively little effort. In other words, the file\line \expndtw5 system should have reasonable performance for random access as well\line \expndtw2 as sequential access.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1657\margrsxn1331\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb857\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1657\margrsxn1331\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1699\colsr551\colno2\colw6670 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Design Approach}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Abstract class FilePage allows a chunk of data bytes to be sandwiched\line \expndtw7 between a non-data header and trailer of a specified (possibly zero)\line length. We use a header in TekFilePage to describe the data bytes on\line the page. Since our disk driver does address checking and data\line \expndtw3 checksumming, we did not include these in the page header. The page\line \expndtw6 header does however, have redundant information (file serialNumber,\line \expndtw5 file pageNumber, previous page's address, next page's address) as well\line \expndtw4 as necessary state (size of data in bytes). Fig. \lang1032 16.2 \lang1033 shows the organiza-\line \expndtw1 tion of a TekFilePage.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi245\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instances of a concrete subclass of File are suppose to "open" them-\line \expndtw7 selves when issued the message findLastPageNumber. Since the last\line \expndtw6 page number (and the address of the last page) are quite useful for\line \expndtw7 appending a new page to the end of a file and truncating a page from\line \expndtw2 the end of an existing file, our concrete instances of TekFile have\line \expndtw5 lastPageNumber and lastPageAddress as additional information for an\line \expndtw-1 open file.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1521\margrsxn1482\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3895 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex119\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 290{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex119\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 File System}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2261\shptop263\shpright4162\shpbottom263\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2387\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 backPointer}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2254\shptop4\shpright4162\shpbottom4\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2380\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa50 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 thisPageNumber}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3908\margrsxn4578\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw925\colsr1073\colno2\colw1425 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-133\shptop-40\shpright1775\shpbottom-40\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-133\shptop-493\shpright-133\shpbottom1941\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1760\shptop-486\shpright1760\shpbottom1940\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 nextPointer}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-133\shptop11\shpright1775\shpbottom11\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 serialNumber}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb104\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 \\ header \lang1032 (24 \lang1033 bytes)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1521\margrsxn1482\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2254\shptop4\shpright4162\shpbottom4\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2383\fi0\ri0\sb54\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 size}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2254\shptop7\shpright4162\shpbottom7\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2387\fi0\ri0\sb54\sa767 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 (unused)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2396\margrsxn6763\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1090\colsr418\colno2\colw1242 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1379\shptop-763\shpright3287\shpbottom-763\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb76\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 16.2}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 data \lang1032 (1000 \lang1033 bytes)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1524\margrsxn1489\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb202\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1524\margrsxn1489\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1760\colsr497\colno2\colw6638 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Smalltalk-80\line System's Role\line \expndtw9 in Initial\line \expndtw3 Design Effort}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our first descriptions of file system design presupposed using Pascal,\line \expndtw4 Pascal will handle certain types of data structures (such as 32-bit ma-\line chine integers) more gracefully than the Smalltalk-80 system will. Pas-\line cal encourages fixed-length data typing. Sometimes this is acceptable,\line \expndtw3 since FilePage pages are fixed length. On the other hand, a file name is\line merely a String of arbitrary length, which is not convenient at all with\line \expndtw4 Pascal data structures. For this and other reasons, we chose to use Pas-\line \expndtw3 cal for only the disk primitives.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1528\margrsxn1482\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb320\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1528\margrsxn1482\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw2012\colsr230\colno2\colw6656 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Directory Design}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li918\fi0\ri0\sb5198\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 16.3}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since we were free to design the directory in any way, we chose to im-\line \expndtw5 plement it as a sequential file in the format shown in Fig. \lang1032 16.3. \lang1033 This\line \expndtw7 format allows the directory to be human readable as well as readable\line \expndtw3 by Smalltalk-80 classes. Many Smalltalk-80 classes support a self-de-\line scription facility through the selectors readFrom: and storeOn:. These\line \expndtw5 selectors do their work on an accompanying Stream in a human-read-\line \expndtw4 able as well as Smalltalk-80-readable form. Since a FileDirectory is\line \expndtw2 expected to deliver up a sequence of files in a Stream-like fashion, a\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 directory can be naturally envisioned as a FileStream that\line \expndtw2 has a sequence of file definitions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ('Directory',1,1,12,2567462400,41166,2567462400,41166,11)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CFreeList',0,1188,1260,2567462400,41166,2567462400,41166,1)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ('Smalltalk80.sources',2,1242,1259,2567462400,41242,2567462400,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi403\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 60378,13)\line \expndtw3\lang1033 Items for each file are in order:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 fileName, a String}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 serialNumber, an Integer}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 lastPageNumber, an Integer}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 firstPageMap, an Integer}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creationDate, seconds since \lang1032 1 \lang1033 Jan \lang1032 1901}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creationTime, seconds since midnight}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 modificationDate, seconds since \lang1032 1 \lang1033 Jan \lang1032 1901}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 modificationTime, seconds since midnight}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  9.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx612 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 lastPageAddress, an integer}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1649\margrsxn1333\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6041\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8636 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 291}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6098\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa248 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Description of Chosen Problem}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1649\margrsxn1336\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1450\colsr796\colno2\colw6678 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Design Details}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our hardware provides random access to a variable number of "sectors"\line \expndtw4\lang1032 1024 \lang1033 bytes long. There is exactly one TekFileDirectory for a medium.\line \expndtw5 The exact number of sectors available for each medium is available\line through a primitive.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each "medium" may have bad sectors\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 i.e., ones that the device\line \expndtw4 drivers will not handle without raising error conditions. One sector at a\line \expndtw1 fixed address is presupposed to be good. This sector with its prespecified\line \expndtw6 address is called a "leader"; it provides information about the medium\line \expndtw2 as a whole.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each sector on a medium (including the leader) is treated as a\line \expndtw3 TekFilePage. A TekFilePage has a 24-byte "header" and \lang1032 1000 \lang1033 bytes of\line \expndtw6 data. "Data" refers to bytes that are handled by Stream messages next\line \expndtw0 and nextPut:.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The header on a TekFilePage contains a back pointer to the previous\line \expndtw2 TekFilePage within a file (or a nil-address if none), a pointer to the next\line \expndtw5 TekFilePage within a file (or nil), the serial number of the page's file,\line \expndtw7 the ordinal page number of this page in its file (i.e., \lang1032 "1" \lang1033 for the first\line \expndtw4 page in a file), and the number of data bytes that are actually in use on\line \expndtw5 this page (the size). All pages in a file except the last one are guaran-\line \expndtw3 teed to be full.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The header information in a TekFilePage is, strictly speaking, suffi-\line \expndtw6 cient to completely recover files on a crashed disk. Exceptions to this\line \expndtw4 are lost sectors containing file data and some information in the direc-\line \expndtw5 tory file such as creation/modification date and the String used for the\line \expndtw6 file name. On the other hand this is not enough information to allow\line \expndtw5 rapid nonhomogeneous access to a file, such as is required by the\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk-80 browser. To accomplish this an extra data structure is\line written with each file in the system: pageMap pages. Fig. \lang1032 16.4 \lang1033 shows\line \expndtw4 the organization of secondary structures on a meduim.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 leader}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2577\margrsxn1981\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb184\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2577\margrsxn1981\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1090\colsr4399\colno2\colw1861 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1339\shptop-130\shpright2124\shpbottom-130\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1339\shptop-130\shpright1339\shpbottom950\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2110\shptop-122\shpright2110\shpbottom958\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2268\shptop2621\shpright2268\shpbottom3355\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1476\shptop2628\shpright1476\shpbottom3348\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2131\posnegy0\absw0\absh3363\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw5740\pich5930\picwgoal3254\pichgoal3362\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3359\posnegy544\absw0\absh-259\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pageMap for Directory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1494\posnegy2333\absw0\absh-259\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 data pages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb3139\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 16.4}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1120\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 <-> \lang1033 (other pageMaps)}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1578\margrsxn1399\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3542 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 292{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw78 __}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 File System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The directory entry for a file specifies the address for the first pageMap\line \expndtw-1 for that file. A file always has at least one pageMap page. A pageMap is\line \expndtw2 forward- and reverse-linked with other pageMaps. Its serialNumber is\line the same as the one used for the FreeList file, so that a disk scavenger\line \expndtw1 will not confuse it with data. The pageNumber field of a pageMap page\line \expndtw0 is not used in practice. A pageMap contains \lang1032 250 \lang1033 4-byte addresses in its\line \expndtw3 data section, each address corresponding to the appropriate page in a\line file. For instance, if one wished to read the 300th page in a file, one\line \expndtw0 would read the first pageMap for the file, use the forward-link in that\line \expndtw1 pageMap to read another pageMap, and read the 50th address in that\line pageMap to determine the correct address for the 300th page in the file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 As files are created on a given media, they must be given unique\line \expndtw1 serialNumbers. The next available serialNumber is written on the lead-\line \expndtw-1 er page, using Integer storeOn:. The leader page's forward-link points to\line \expndtw1 the first pageMap page of the file which is the Directory file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The first two file definitions in a directory file are special: The first is\line \expndtw0 the directory file itself (named "Directory") and the second is a relative-\line \expndtw1 ly large file that occupies all the unused pages in the system (named\line "FreeList"). Both of these files are bona fide in the sense that they have\line \expndtw-1 a serialNumber and a complete set of pageMaps. The serialNumber of\line \expndtw-2 FreeList is zero, which signifies non-data. For the sake of consistency,\line \expndtw2 FreeList's "data" pages are fully linked just like all other files in the\line \expndtw-4 system.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop1022\shpright8932\shpbottom1022\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1440\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Implementation      \b0 There is a bootstrap problem involved with implementing a file system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Approach{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw8 The source code for the new classes must be typed in by hand. The}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 physical media must be formatted with a Directory and a FreeList.\line \expndtw1 Once the file system is up and running, the very large file that contains\line \expndtw3 the commented sources for the system must be converted to reside on\line \expndtw0 the new format. Finally, one must recompile the file system methods,\line \expndtw1 adding comments and regenerating variable names.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi230\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Typing in the new classes was irritating but not difficult on our imple-\line \expndtw0 mentation. If one's interpreter runs at \lang1032 3500 \lang1033 bytecodes per second, key-\line board echo requires three to five seconds. Our virtual image had a built-\line \expndtw-1 in performance problem that additionally delayed keyboard echo. Since\line \expndtw0 there is no source code system, all the new and untested methods lack\line \expndtw1 argument variable names, temporary variable names, and comments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi241\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 None of the file system could be tested until all necessary methods\line \expndtw5 were typed in and initialization code written to properly initialize a\line \expndtw0 new media. The debugging itself will be described later.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1695\margrsxn1289\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4151\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8615 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 293}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementation Approach}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi241\ri18\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Converting the source code to the file system format turned out to be\line \expndtw4 easy but time consuming. Our system already has software for saving\line \expndtw5 and restoring memory images on our disk media. We split the source\line \expndtw13 file into manageable chunks, converted the ASCII text to a\line \expndtw-1 downloadable image (as if the text were executable processor code), and\line \expndtw2 saved the resulting memory-images on floppies using the existing non-\line \expndtw0 file system format. A small bit of code in a workspace was necessary to\line \expndtw2 read bytes off of a bogus TekFilePage onto a legitimate FileStream, and\line \expndtw0 a very large amount of time was necessary for the conversion (about \lang1032 11\line \expndtw-4\lang1033 hours).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Before   implementing   the   file   system,   we   were   familiar   with   the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex123\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Managing{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex112\expndtw1 Smalltalk-80 user interface, but we had only superficial experience with}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2254 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex123\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Windows{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex112\expndtw6 it- Each of us had his favorite snapshot with his windows placed just}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 the way he liked it, but our interaction with the system was casual. We\line \expndtw2 typically would execute selected examples to show to visitors or com-\line \expndtw1 pose short methods to answer specific questions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi241\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 When we started the file system project, our placement of windows\line \expndtw7 underwent metamorphosis. The old arrangements changed as new\line \expndtw6 things came to light. For instance, the only kind of browser that we\line \expndtw0 ever had at first was a full five-pane System Browser. These are useful\line \expndtw4 because of their full generality. Unfortunately five panes take up a lot\line \expndtw-1 of room on our \lang1032 512 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'f7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  512 \lang1033 display, which in turn means that less of each\line \expndtw2 method ends up visible in the code pane. Since all of the code that we\line \expndtw0 were entering belonged in a single class category, "Files-Tektronix," we\line quickly collapsed the System Browser and kept a Class Category Brows-\line \expndtw1 er instead, which has proportionately more room in the code part of the\line \expndtw-3 browser.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Our typical display had a System Category Browser and a Workspace\line to do development work in. A System Transcript, a collapsed System\line \expndtw-1 Brower, and a collapsed System Workspace were kept off to the side. At\line \expndtw2 first we did not overlap the Category Browser and the Workspace. After\line a little bit of use we reframed these two windows so that they did over-\line \expndtw0 lap substantially. Since we typically moved between the two windows,\line \expndtw2 making changes in one window and testing the changes in the other, it\line was not necessary to see all of both windows at the same time. This was\line a significantly better use of display space on our system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The Browser worked pretty much as advertised. We added our classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Using the Browser       \i0 without any difficulty, but when we finished, we discovered that we had}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 misspelled an instance variable name. Several methods later in that\line \expndtw2 class, we decided to change the class definition. Much to our surprise,\line \expndtw3 when we changed the spelling of the instance variable, we did not get\line \expndtw2 any syntax error messages while the class was being recompiled. If we\line \expndtw4 had attempted to change the order of the instance variables, the actual}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1485\margrsxn1485\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4777 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 294{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 File System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri29\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 roles of the instance variables would have reversed. This is because the\line \expndtw3 decompiler used the new instance variable names rather than the old.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The method category pane of the browser, which provides categories\line \expndtw-1 of methods within a given class, was originally a nuisance. We chose to\line \expndtw4 ignore it initially. An odd thing was that as long as we added methods\line \expndtw-1 to a new class, the selection in this pane read "no messages" but if we\line \expndtw1 switched to another class and back, the selection would then change to\line \expndtw-1 "As yet unclassified".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In general the text editor was a joy to use: all basic editing operations}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li50\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2286 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex123\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Using the Editor{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw3 conform nicely with user intuition. Some features are not intuitive, but}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2290\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 neither are they difficult to learn. For example, using control-F for\line \expndtw1 "ifFalse:" or control-[ for inserting \lang1032 "[]".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We used a full-ASCII keyboard on our implementation, which caused\line \expndtw-1 a special set of problems using the editor. Some of us were initially per-\line plexed about how to type the back-arrow assignment symbol which is\line \expndtw6 pervasive in Smalltalk-80 code: it turns out that this maps into the\line \expndtw1 ASCII underscore character. A more serious problem was presented by\line \expndtw2 the unencoded keyboard interface. Xerox uses an unencoded keyboard\line \expndtw4 for their own Smalltalk-80 systems. An unencoded keyboard reports\line \expndtw-2 only key-up and key-down activity on the keyboard. An unencoded key-\line \expndtw2 board is closer functionally to a piano than a typewriter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Whereas an unencoded typewriter keyboard provides greater flexibil-\line \expndtw-1 ity than a conventional encoded-ASCII keyboard, they are less common.\line Our Smalltalk-80 hardware uses a readily available encoded-ASCII key-\line board. The way Xerox has written InputSensor and InputState gave us a\line \expndtw1 little grief. We were unable to force a user interrupt with the control-C\line \expndtw2 character simply because no one at Xerox thought it would be possible\line \expndtw1 for a keyboard to generate a control-C character without causing a con-\line \expndtw6 trol-key event. Thus when one types a control-C on an untuned\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk-80 system with an encoded keyboard, one gets a garbage\line \expndtw5 character instead of the expected Notifier. The unencoded keyboard\line \expndtw0 also manifests itself as a problem with such things as control-0 through\line \expndtw4 control-9 (used for changing fonts), and control-\lang1032 [ \lang1033 (which is different\line \expndtw-1 from ASCII ESCAPE). Although the encoded keyboard was suitable for\line use with the Smalltalk-80 system, the control-C problem caused us grief\line \expndtw0 when we were debugging the file system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The debugger has done more to spoil us than any other single feature}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex123\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Using the{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw3 in the user interface. All aspects are fully integrated. It was never nec-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Debugger{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0 essary for us to go to machine-level debuggers or anything else in order}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 for us to fully debug the file system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi234\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The interpreter simulator, invoked whenever one selects "step" or\line "send", runs about \lang1032 1000 \lang1033 times slower than the machine interpreter. On\line \expndtw5 our system this meant that the simulator ran at an effective \lang1032 3 \lang1033 or \lang1032 4\line \expndtw3\lang1033 bytecodes per second. On at least one occasion we accidentally started}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1699\margrsxn1286\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4165\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8626 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 295}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Typical Programming Problems}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa1213\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the simulator only to determine afterward that we would have to wait\line \expndtw3 longer than the MTBF of our hardware to allow the simulation to com-\line \expndtw-1 plete.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1699\margrsxn1289\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1670\colsr569\colno2\colw6681 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-317\shpright8921\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Typical}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Programming}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Problems}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop1004\shpright1174\shpbottom1004\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1156\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Syntax Errors}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 During the course of "accepting" methods into the system and testing\line \expndtw2 our resulting code, we ran into a series of problems familiar to every\line \expndtw4 programmer. In our estimation, this is the part of the Smalltalk-80 sys-\line \expndtw6 tem that must distinguish itself: from a productivity standpoint, the\line \expndtw4 amount of time that it takes to effect an application and the resulting\line reliability are paramount indices into a workbench's success.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Syntax errors on any system have a fairly limited number of causes, in-\line cluding user unfamiliarity and "cockpit errors". Those of us accustomed\line \expndtw5 to higher level languages such as Pascal or Modula-II are familiar with\line \expndtw4 a definite syntax phase when entering new program text. Such strongly\line \expndtw7 typed languages attempt to limit certain kinds of errors by making a\line \expndtw4 large number of static checks on the program text. The Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw2 compiler makes relatively few static checks. It does little or no semantic\line checking. Abnormal conditions are left to the objects involved during\line \expndtw3 execution to report. This is all right in principle, but there are some se-\line \expndtw6 mantic checks that the compiler does not do which could theoretically\line \expndtw-1 be done.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi245\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One syntax error that occurred early on is worth mentioning. When\line \expndtw3 one writes a Smalltalk-80 conditional expression, it will look something\line \expndtw0 like}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li842\fi-594\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 3 \lang1033 frob ifTrue: \expndtw25 [T1]\line \expndtw5 ifFalse: \lang1032 [12].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 However, if one wishes to code a whileFalse:, one might be tempted to\line \expndtw2 type}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li479\fi-234\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 3 \lang1033 frob \lang1032 " \lang1033 creates syntax error"\line \expndtw0 whileFalse: \lang1032 [4 \lang1033 frob].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri32\sb241\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This latter construct gives a syntax error because the compiler requires\line the receiver of a whileFalse: to be a literal block. Thus the correct syn-\line \expndtw3 tax for a whileFalse: using the previous example would be}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi245\ri0\sb40\sa0\sl-482\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 [3 \lang1033 frob] whileFalse: \lang1032 [4 \lang1033 frob].\line \expndtw3 Once the novice user has discovered this, he may be tempted to write}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1506\margrsxn1478\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx3910 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 296{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa223 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 File System}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1506\margrsxn1478\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1742\colsr504\colno2\colw6678 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2394\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Off-by-One Errors}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft22\shptop1253\shpright1916\shpbottom1253\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb1408\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Learning Curve}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li490\fi-234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 [3 \lang1033 frob] \lang1032 " \lang1033 gets by compiler but doesn't work \lang1032 "\line \expndtw-7\lang1033 ifTrue: \expndtw6 [T1]\line \expndtw-2 ifFalse: [T \lang1032 2].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri29\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The compiler will accept this without complaint because it makes no re-\line \expndtw4 quirements on the receiver of an ifTrue:ifFalse:. However, when this\line \expndtw5 method is executed the response is a mustBeBoolean notifier, because\line \expndtw3 the receiver is a block instead of a boolean.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb245\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Programming languages that allow indexing data structures open\line \expndtw2 themselves up to off-by-one errors. Smalltalk-80 is such a language. The\line \expndtw4 first item in a Smalltalk-80 indexable collection is indexed by one in-\line \expndtw1 stead of zero. Some of us have grown accustomed to programming with\line \expndtw5 adjustable-offset indexes, which resulted in three off-by-one errors in\line \expndtw6 our initial attempts.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li32\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As with any problem, there is a "learning curve" involved with ap-\line proaching a novel situation. In implementing the file system, we tra-\line versed learning curves involved with file systems, disk hardware, and\line \expndtw3 the Smalltalk-80 system itself.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi230\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Familiarization with the abstract and concrete implementations of\line \expndtw6 File took a significant amount of the time necessary to implement the\line \expndtw5 file system. After that, there was a certain amount of raw familiariza-\line \expndtw6 tion that had to take place. For instance, is the correct selector to ap-\line \expndtw7 pend an object to an OrderedCollection addLast: or lastAdd:? (The\line \expndtw2 former is correct). Also, several times we discovered that we had made\line inefficient use of existing methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi238\ri7\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Once we discovered that although we could add and update files\line \expndtw3 properly, we could not delete them from the directory. Closer examina-\line tion revealed that close-ing a FileStream does not necessarily entail\line shorten-ing a FileStream. In other words, there were indeed occasions\line \expndtw2 where hidden functionality of the supplied building blocks caused prob-\line \expndtw-3 lems.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi234\ri11\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After we had corrected most of the rudimentary syntax and design\line \expndtw5 errors, a peculiar snag came to light. The system returns self from a\line \expndtw3 message in absence of some particular result that is expected. This is\line \expndtw2 reminiscent of the difference between procedures and functions in more\line \expndtw4 traditional languages. Unfortunately, unlike functions, a Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw2 method can rely on "self" being returned from a message. This caused a\line \expndtw5 problem for us when the documentation for the abstract file classes\line \expndtw4 specified no particular result for TekFile read:. It turns out that this se-\line \expndtw3 lector is supposed to return the TekFilePage that it reads, not self. The\line \expndtw11 error that resulted from this misunderstanding was a doesNot-\line \expndtw2 Understand: message quite removed in time and execution from the ac-\line \expndtw5 tual source of the error.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1645\margrsxn1354\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1371\colsr886\colno2\colw6652 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb896\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Design Errors}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1897\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6358 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 297}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Summary /Conclusions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After the first several hours of debugging, the remaining errors in the\line system were all in our design. There were such things as Disk (a\line \expndtw5 TekFileDirectory) telling itself to close in the midst of closing itself.\line \expndtw3 There were some problems involving managing the pageMaps, particu-\line larly on larger files.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi238\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since problems in this category go directly back to the definition and\line solution of the actual problem to be solved, these problems must reflect\line \expndtw7 the programmer/analyst more than the system itself. The impressive\line \expndtw5 part of the Smalltalk-80 system's performance in this regard is that the\line turnaround from clerical, syntactic, and learning curve errors back to\line \expndtw3 design errors occurred quickly, even though the system itself was slow.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1641\margrsxn1358\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1440\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1641\margrsxn1358\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1436\colsr814\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-317\shpright8917\shpbottom-317\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Summary/\line \expndtw-3 Conclusions}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Design and implementation of concrete subclasses for the file system\line \expndtw6 took \lang1032 43 \lang1033 hours over the space of two and a half weeks. A large part of\line \expndtw3 this time reflects the speed of our implementation as opposed to pro-\line \expndtw5 grammer time.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At the end of this time all of the higher file-related functions in the\line \expndtw2 system worked perfectly. Most debugging problems were due to our in-\line \expndtw4 complete understanding of the Smalltalk-80 system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One measure of success of a workbench is the quality and applicabili-\line \expndtw3 ty of the tools that it offers. Our experience with workbenches in gener-\line \expndtw5 al is that if a tool indeed exists for the problem at hand, its interface is\line \expndtw4 poorly documented and its behavior erratic if not outright destructive.\line \expndtw5 This is the first workbench we have ever used in which we are willing\line \expndtw4\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 albeit reluctantly\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 to use new and unfamiliar tools that are already\line available. The integration of tools in the system is so complete that the\line apparent reliability of higher level system components (such as brows-\line \expndtw3 ers and debuggers) is enhanced.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Like any large software system, the Smalltalk-80 system has its\line \expndtw7 share of bugs. By and large, bugs tend to remain isolated, although a\line \expndtw3 bug in a widely used component will have significant repercussions\line \expndtw5 throughout the system. To this extent the full integration of the system\line \expndtw3 is a double-edged sword: although compactness and ultimate reliability\line \expndtw6 are aided, one is also able to make changes that blow up the system.\line Then again, the system uses snapshots and transaction files to help a\line \expndtw5 user regain his work in the event of a system crash.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi230\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our final judgment is that yes, the Smalltalk-80 system is a good\line \expndtw2 programming tool. It would be simplistic to cite a single reason for our\line \expndtw3 decision, but one of the salient strengths of the system is its high reli-\line \expndtw4 ability and useability.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn10519\margrsxn360\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-245\shpright1126\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Abstract}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2797\margrsxn2412\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1958\shptop-295\shpright6710\shpbottom-295\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1958\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Implementing a\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 System\line \expndtw7 on the Intel \lang1032 432:\line \lang1033 A Feasibility Study}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1944\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0\sl-274\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs32 Guy Almes\line \expndtw2 Alan Borning\line \expndtw-4 Eli Messinger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1955\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Department of Computer Science\line \expndtw3 University of Washington\line \expndtw4 Seattle, Washington}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-1116\shptop1109\shpright6682\shpbottom1109\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri22\sb1418\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 During autumn \lang1032 1981, \lang1033 the authors carried out a feasibility study on the\line implementation of the Smalltalk-80 language on the Intel iAPX \lang1032 432.\line \expndtw4\lang1033 This report presents the conclusions of that study, together with sup-\line \expndtw5 porting technical material.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Briefly, a Smalltalk implementation on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 would result in sev-\line \expndtw5 eral important advantages over Smalltalk on conventional computers.\line \expndtw4 These advantages include support for multilingual systems and for par-\line \expndtw3 allelism, including parallel garbage collection. There are also however,\line \expndtw4 some corresponding disadvantages that could prevent a Smalltalk im-\line \expndtw5 plementation on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 from being competitive with implementations\line \expndtw4 on conventional computers. These disadvantages include large storage\line overhead per object, very heavy loading of the parallel garbage collec-\line \expndtw1 tor, and the possibility of insufficient microcode space. These difficulties\line \expndtw3 can probably be surmounted; some approaches to dealing with them are\line \expndtw-2 discussed below.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb497\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Guy Almes, Alan Borning, and Eli Messinger, \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 299}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1573\margrsxn1405\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4964 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 300{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa522 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1602\margrsxn1405\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1515\colsr745\colno2\colw6642 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-245\shpright8907\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This report describes an effort to study the feasibility of a Smalltalk-80\line implementation on the Intel \lang1032 432. \lang1033 The main body of the report is divided\line \expndtw6 into three parts:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li234\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx436 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li234\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx436 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The potential benefits of a Smalltalk-432 system.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li234\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx436 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li234\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx436 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The principal threats to the feasibility of Smalltalk-432.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li234\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx436 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li234\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-353\slmult0\tx436 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A sketch of an implementation of Smalltalk-432.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb209\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A final section presents some conclusions and recommendations for fur-\line \expndtw5 ther study.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1580\margrsxn1419\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1242\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1580\margrsxn1419\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1476\colsr763\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-248\shpright8914\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Background}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri4\sb68\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We assume that readers of this report are acquainted with the\line \expndtw8 Smalltalk-80 system. However, not all readers will know about the\line \expndtw1 Intel \lang1032 432 \lang1033 processor, so in this section we give a brief description of the\line \expndtw7 features of the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 that are particularly relevant to the task at hand.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi238\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Intel iAPX \lang1032 432 \lang1033 is a 32-bit microprocessor. It is designed to be a\line \expndtw3 processor for Ada; however, as we shall see, with suitable modifications\line \expndtw2 it may be an effective Smalltalk engine as well. Physically, the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 con-\line \expndtw0 sists of a two-chip \i general data processor \i0 (GDP) and a single-chip \i inter-\line \expndtw6 face processor \i0 (IP). Standard I/O functions are handled separately by\line one or more attached processors, which will typically be Intel 8086s.\line \expndtw1 The basic clock rate is \lang1032 8 \lang1033 MHz. The \lang1032 432 \lang1033 chips include microcode in read-\line \expndtw2 only memory; there is no user-modifiable microcode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi234\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \lang1032 432 \lang1033 supports an object-oriented environment (although the\line \expndtw2 meaning of "object" is not the same as in Smalltalk). A \lang1032 432 \lang1033 object is a\line \expndtw4 contiguous segment of memory, up to 64K bytes in length, containing\line \expndtw2 data and addresses. There can be up to 2\super 24\nosupersub  segments in a system. Each\line \expndtw9 segment has a type, which determines the operations that can be\line \expndtw2 performed on it. Addresses for these segments are called access descrip-\line \expndtw4 tors, and are protected addresses very similar to capabilities on systems\line \expndtw1 such as Hydra\super 1\nosupersub\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'b7\super\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 2\nosupersub\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'b7\super\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 3\nosupersub . Two fields totaling \lang1032 24 \lang1033 bits contain the unique identi-\line \expndtw6 fier for the object, \lang1032 1 \lang1033 bit indicates whether the \lang1032 32 \lang1033 bits in fact constitute\line \expndtw5 a valid access descriptor, and each of the remaining \lang1032 7 \lang1033 bits indicates\line \expndtw6 whether the owner of the access descriptor has a given right for the\line \expndtw8 segment in question. These rights include "read rights" (can parts of\line \expndtw5 the segment be examined?), "write rights" (can the segment be modi-\line \expndtw2 fied?), and "delete rights" (can the access descriptor be deleted or over-\line \expndtw8 written?). Every access to a segment is checked automatically to see}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1630\margrsxn1343\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4162\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8629 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 301}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Approaches to Running Smalltalk on the \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri7\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 that the possessor of the access descriptor has the required rights, and\line \expndtw4 that the access is within the bounds of the segment? The hardware and\line microcode makes a distinction between data and access descriptors, so\line \expndtw3 that it is not possible to inadvertently treat \lang1032 32 \lang1033 bits of data as an access\line \expndtw2 descriptor or vice versa.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi241\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \lang1032 432 \lang1033 is designed for multiprocessing. Many 432s can be connected\line \expndtw6 to a common memory, the maximum being determined by electrical\line \expndtw3 rather than logical characteristics. Within the common memory, \i process\line \expndtw2 objects \i0 represent tasks requiring servicing, and \i processor objects \i0 are the\line \expndtw3 representatives of the physical processors. This representation makes it\line \expndtw2 easy to add new processors without the need for making software modi-\line \expndtw-1 fications.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interprocess communication is handled by a \i port \i0 mechanism. A given\line \expndtw3 process can send an access descriptor for a message segment to a port\line \expndtw4 object, which can be received by any process with an access descriptor\line \expndtw5 for that port.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Objects can be allocated from a global heap or on a local stack. Par-\line \expndtw2 allel garbage collection for heap-allocated objects is supported. Using\line \expndtw4 Dijkstra's parallel garbage collection algorithm, objects are marked as\line \expndtw3 "white" (possibly inaccessible), "black" (accessible and traversed), or\line \expndtw4 "gray" (accessible but not traversed). The hardware supports parallel\line garbage collection by marking each white object as gray when a pointer\line \expndtw2 to it is copied. A software garbage collector, running as a \lang1032 432 \lang1033 process in\line \expndtw5 parallel with other processes, handles the rest of the work.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa940\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Papers on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 architecture have recently appeared in the litera-\line \expndtw4 ture. These include papers describing the port system\super 4\nosupersub , the structured\line \expndtw3 address space\super 5\nosupersub , and the structure of the operating system\super 6\nosupersub .}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1630\margrsxn1358\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1749\colsr504\colno2\colw6667 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-306\shpright8925\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Approaches to\line \expndtw1 Running\line Smalltalk on\line \expndtw-3 the \lang1032 432}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are a number of possible ways to run Smalltalk on the \lang1032 432. \lang1033 The\line best approach is probably to make a Smalltalk object be the same as a\line \expndtw3\lang1032 432 \lang1033 object (in general), and to modify the microcode of the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 to allow\line \expndtw4 it to execute Smalltalk bytecodes directly. A system that used this chip\line \expndtw9 would include both Smalltalk processors (STPs) and ordinary Ada\line \expndtw-6 GDPs.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the remainder of this section, we describe some of the alternatives\line \expndtw4 and indicate why the above approach was selected.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It would be very convenient to run Smalltalk on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 without\line \expndtw5 modification to the chip. One way of doing this would be to allocate a\line \expndtw6 set of 64K-byte \lang1032 432 \lang1033 data segments to hold Smalltalk objects, and to\line \expndtw7 handle Smalltalk object allocation and deallocation independently of\line \expndtw6 the facilities provided by the \lang1032 432. \lang1033 This may in fact be the best way to\line run Smalltalk on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 as it exists. However, if this is the case, then}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1588\margrsxn1397\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4975 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 302{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 is just being used as a rather slow conventional processor, with\line \expndtw4 no use being made of its special properties. If this is the best that can\line \expndtw1 be done, it would be better to select another processor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi245\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A more attractive alternative, still using an unmodified \lang1032 432, \lang1033 would\line \expndtw-2 be to identify Smalltalk objects and \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects. There are two possibili-\line \expndtw2 ties for storing Smalltalk code: writing an interpreter for the present\line \expndtw1 bytecode set, or changing the Smalltalk compiler to emit mixed native\line \expndtw4\lang1032 432 \lang1033 code and subroutine calls. We suspect that a software interpreter\line \expndtw-1 for the present bytecode set would be quite slow. Nevertheless, such an\line \expndtw1 interpreter should be developed for at least two reasons:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\tx2711 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\tx2711 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 It might turn out to be acceptably fast.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2711\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2711 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2711\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2711 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Even if it is too slow, it will yield valuable experience that will in-\line \expndtw2 form the eventual Smalltalk processor design. Specifically, it will\line \expndtw-1 show how well the Smalltalk object to \lang1032 432 \lang1033 object mapping works.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri4\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Once this interpreter is built, its designers will be able to judge the ex-\line \expndtw5 tent to which such an implementation is competitive with other\line \expndtw-1 Smalltalk-80 systems.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Another alternative would be to mix native \lang1032 432 \lang1033 code and subroutine\line calls. A severe disadvantage of doing this is that there will be an enor-\line \expndtw-1 mous expansion in code size, probably by \lang1032 5 \lang1033 to \lang1032 10 \lang1033 times. This expansion\line \expndtw-2 would be acceptable only if done for those methods most frequently exe-\line \expndtw0 cuted. If a small set of very frequently executed methods can be identi-\line \expndtw1 fied, either statically or dynamically, then this set might be a candidate\line \expndtw-1 for such expansion.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 We are thus led to consider producing an additional \lang1032 432 \lang1033 processor\line \expndtw6 type. In addition to the present Ada GDP and IP processors, there\line \expndtw-2 would be a new Smalltalk processor (STP) designed as a modification of\line \expndtw1 the present Ada GDP processor. Our approach is to try to modify only\line \expndtw0 one of the two chips, by making changes to the microcode stored in its\line \expndtw1 ROM and in its PLAs. Again, we make Smalltalk objects and \lang1032 432 \lang1033 ob-\line \expndtw3 jects be the same, since not doing so would lose whatever advantages\line \expndtw0 the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 has over a conventional chip. The advantage of this approach is\line \expndtw6 that it is faster than other approaches; a disadvantage is that it does\line \expndtw0 not allow its implementors to change the interpreter or bytecode format\line without replacing the processor chips.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop767\shpright8914\shpbottom767\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1084\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2257 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex131\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Benefits Of{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw3 The implementation of Smalltalk on a \lang1032 432 \lang1033 would yield many benefits.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex138\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Smalltalk-432{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex113\expndtw4 Some of these benefits improve the performance of the system, while}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 others make a Smalltalk system on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 qualitatively better than}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2257\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Smalltalk systems on conventional machines.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1611\margrsxn1377\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6527\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8608 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 303}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6584\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa230 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex137\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Benefits of Smalltalk-432}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1611\margrsxn1377\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1796\colsr461\colno2\colw6663 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Support for Large\line \expndtw9 Numbers of\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk Objects}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop1613\shpright1912\shpbottom1613\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb1796\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Support for\line \expndtw1 Communication\line \expndtw4 with non-\line \expndtw10 Smalltalk\line \expndtw-2 Programs}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop1843\shpright1905\shpbottom1843\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2027\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Support for\line Parallelism}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One of the most important benefits of implementing Smalltalk on the\line \expndtw2\lang1032 432 \lang1033 would be its ability to handle systems of more than \lang1032 32,000 \lang1033 objects.\line \expndtw3 Most existing Smalltalk implementations use 16-bit words for pointers,\line and one bit is effectively lost in implementing Small Integers. It should\line \expndtw4 also be pointed out that a \lang1032 432 \lang1033 implementation would share this benefit\line with any other implementation with a large address space; an imple-\line \expndtw3 mentation on the Digital Equipment Corp. VAX, for example, can sup-\line port similarly large numbers of objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi241\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the Smalltalk system moreover, is well suited for large ad-\line \expndtw3 vanced applications, this benefit could be crucial.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The design of the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 system seems to lend itself particularly well to\line \expndtw7 the idea of a multi-lingual system. With Smalltalk and, for example,\line \expndtw2 Ada processes agreeing on the \lang1032 "432 \lang1033 protocol," communication between\line \expndtw3 them can be done.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Smalltalk processor and a general Ada processor would have the\line \expndtw2 same notion of what \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects and messages are. Thus processes run-\line \expndtw4 ning concurrently in different languages would be able to share memo-\line \expndtw5 ry, and send messages to each other through \lang1032 432 \lang1033 ports. No existing\line Smalltalk implementations support such a multi-lingual system. The\line \expndtw3 Dolphin and Dorado systems at Xerox PARC, for example, run either\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk or Mesa, but never both within the same processor. This is\line \expndtw4 due in part to conflicting technical requirements of Mesa and Smalltalk\line \expndtw5 runtime environments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb130\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One of the primary advantages of a 432-based Smalltalk system would\line \expndtw3 be support for parallelism. As described in the introduction, the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 en-\line \expndtw5 vironment includes process and processor objects. Instances of the\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk class Process can be mapped in a straightforward way onto\line \expndtw9 the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 process objects, so that in a system with multiple STPs,\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk processes could be executed in parallel.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri22\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are a number of relatively simple ways of exploiting this par-\line \expndtw0 allelism:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi-202\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx418 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi-202\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx418 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When filing in a class definition, each method is compiled sepa-\line \expndtw13 rately. It would be easy to make each of these compilations a\line \expndtw4 separate process, thus speeding up filing in considerably. The only\line \expndtw6 synchronization needed would be a lock on the method dictionary\line \expndtw4 when a new method was being inserted.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi-202\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx418 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi-202\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx418 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are a number of messages to dictionaries for doing searches\line \expndtw6 for example, to find all classes that implement a method for some\line \expndtw4 selector, to find all methods that invoke a given selector, to find all\line references to a symbol, and so forth. All these searches could prof-\line \expndtw3 itably employ parallelism.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx418 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx418 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Parallelism could also be used in graphics. When displaying a\line \expndtw4 paned window, or updating several windows, each pane or window}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1585\margrsxn1388\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4961 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 304{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2722\fi0\ri4\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 could have a separate process to display it. When displaying an\line \expndtw3 elaborate image, the display method could divide the work among\line several processes. In general some synchronization between the\line \expndtw1 subprocesses would be necessary, but in many cases (e.g., when the\line \expndtw6 subimages were nonintersecting, or when they were or'd together\line \expndtw2 to form the entire image) the subprocesses could proceed asynchro-\line \expndtw-2 nously.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri7\sb342\sa353\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are other applications for which parallelism would be valuable,\line \expndtw7 but its use would require more sophisticated synchronization tech-\line \expndtw3 niques. In simulations for example, it is sometimes useful to have one\line \expndtw4 process perform the simulation, and a separate viewing process to dis-\line play snapshots of the simulation's state.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1611\margrsxn1392\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1620\colsr637\colno2\colw6649 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb259\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Support for\line \expndtw1 Virtual Memory}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because a virtual memory scheme is in the design of the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 system, it\line will automatically accrue as a benefit to the Smalltalk system. This will\line \expndtw4 be especially important for applications with large numbers of objects.\line \expndtw2 The quality of the iMax virtual memory mechanism will be very impor-\line \expndtw6 tant for the quality of the Smalltalk implementation.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1607\margrsxn1392\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb360\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1607\margrsxn1392\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1710\colsr540\colno2\colw6660 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Support for\line \expndtw-1 Parallel Garbage\line \expndtw-3 Collection}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Similarly the parallel garbage collector, which is to be part of the stan-\line \expndtw4 dard \lang1032 432 \lang1033 system, will also benefit the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 Smalltalk implementation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because a storage manager will not have to be written anew for the\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk virtual machine, the task of implementation will be simpli-\line fied. Also, because the garbage collector\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 as opposed to reference\line \expndtw10 counting techniques\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 will be able to collect circular structures,\line \expndtw7 Smalltalk users will not have to break loops explicitly to deallocate\line \expndtw4 them. (An anomaly of the Smalltalk object representation called "soft\line fields" requires modification to the garbage collector. The soft field\line \expndtw3 technique is used to add a pseudo field to instances of some class. Soft\line \expndtw5 fields are implemented through the use of a global dictionary whose\line \expndtw9 entries contain a pointer to an object, paired with that object's soft\line \expndtw6 field. The problem then is that an object whose sole reference is from\line \expndtw3 the soft field dictionary is in fact garbage. Given the large overhead per\line object already present, one might prefer to add a real field to Object to\line \expndtw6 point to a list of backpointers. This would remove this special case in\line \expndtw3 the garbage collector, at the cost of \lang1032 4 \lang1033 more bytes per object.)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1607\margrsxn1388\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb364\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1607\margrsxn1388\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1310\colsr925\colno2\colw6678 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb266\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Support for\line \expndtw-1 Object Filing}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A scheme is planned for iMax that would allow \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects to be filed\line away in an archival format, onto external memory\super 7\nosupersub . Thus Smalltalk ob-\line \expndtw6 jects could be stored and later retrieved from secondary memory after\line \expndtw4 an arbitrary amount of time. This scheme would allow Smalltalk pro-\line \expndtw3 grammers a more flexible facility than the saving of entire work-spaces.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1561\margrsxn1435\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6534\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8600 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 305}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6595\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa569 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Threats to Smalltalk-432}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1561\margrsxn1438\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1616\colsr641\colno2\colw6652 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-310\shpright8910\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Threats to\line \expndtw-6 Smalltalk-432}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk-80 implementation on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 seems to be feasible, and\line due to the advantages cited above would result in a qualitatively better\line \expndtw5 facility. There are several potential problems however, with the map-\line \expndtw6 ping of Smalltalk onto the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 which could make the performance of\line \expndtw3 the resulting implementation unacceptable. At this point we do not be-\line \expndtw4 lieve that any of these threats is fatal, but they do serve to focus our at-\line \expndtw6 tention on the real issues in the rest of the study.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1564\margrsxn1435\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb360\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1564\margrsxn1435\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1760\colsr472\colno2\colw6678 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Storage Overhead}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Release Three of the 432's architecture incurs an average storage over-\line \expndtw2 head of approximately \lang1032 24 \lang1033 bytes per object. This breaks down as \lang1032 16 \lang1033 bytes\line \expndtw4 for the object descriptor, and a minimum of \lang1032 8 \lang1033 bytes for the "memory\line \expndtw9 image." On the other hand, in the model implementation of the\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 system, there is only an 8-byte overhead per object; simi-\line \expndtw6 larly, the LOOM implementation has \lang1032 14 \lang1033 bytes of overhead per object\line \expndtw0 (see Chapter \lang1032 14).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Statistics show that the initial Xerox Smalltalk virtual memory con-\line \expndtw2 tains approximately 20K objects (including 4K method objects), with a\line \expndtw6 mean size of \lang1032 20 \lang1033 bytes. However, since the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 implementation would\line \expndtw2 use 32-bit object pointers (as opposed to 16-bit pointers on the Xerox\line systems), the average object size would increase. At one extreme, where\line \expndtw5 no Smalltalk object contains a pointer to another object (e.g., strings),\line the average size would remain at \lang1032 20 \lang1033 bytes; at the other extreme, where\line \expndtw6 all Smalltalk objects are composed solely of pointers to other objects,\line \expndtw3 the average size would double to \lang1032 40 \lang1033 bytes. Clearly the true average lies\line \expndtw4 somewhere in between. For the basic Smalltalk system, Ballard (see\line \expndtw5 Chapter \lang1032 8) \lang1033 found that its size increased by \lang1032 50% \lang1033 when going from \lang1032 16 \lang1033 to\line \expndtw6\lang1032 32 \lang1033 bit pointers, implying that the average object size increased to \lang1032 30\line \expndtw4\lang1033 bytes. For a big application program with more data than code, the av-\line erage number of bytes per object might be somewhat larger.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Regardless of the average object size, the overhead remains \lang1032 24 \lang1033 bytes\line \expndtw1 per object. Thus a system of 20K objects would incur approximately \lang1032 0.5\line \lang1033 Mbytes of overhead for about \lang1032 0.6 \lang1033 Mbytes of objects. (Assuming here an\line \expndtw2 average object size of \lang1032 30 \lang1033 bytes.) Also note that the 20K objects figure is\line \expndtw8 only for the Smalltalk s^tem itself. A reasonably large application,\line \expndtw5 which takes advantage of the 432's ability to support more than 32K\line \expndtw3 objects, might use upwards of 100K objects, thus making the overhead\line \expndtw1 about \lang1032 2.5 \lang1033 Mbytes for \lang1032 3.0 \lang1033 Mbytes of objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It is thus clear that the Smalltalk-80 system on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 is practical\line \expndtw7 only with a virtual memory system; otherwise the number of objects\line \expndtw2 that could be used would be so restricted that one would lose all the ad-\line \expndtw3 vantages of moving from 16-bit to 32-bit pointers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi230\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Also, even with virtual memory, the large per object storage over-\line \expndtw10 head will increase the amount of real memory required to support a}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1571\margrsxn1410\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4950 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 306{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 given working set of Smalltalk objects. This increases both the cost of a\line \expndtw2 given hardware configuration and the amount of disk traffic due to\line \expndtw3 swapping. The user should certainly not be forced into a constrained\line \expndtw-2 style of programming where she or he becomes wary of using too many\line \expndtw-1 objects; this would be very destructive of good Smalltalk programming\line \expndtw1 style. Any way that is found to reduce the storage overhead of \lang1032 432 \lang1033 ob-\line \expndtw0 ject descriptors or memory images would certainly benefit Smalltalk.\line \expndtw-2 However, even if the \lang1032 24 \lang1033 bytes/object overhead is regarded as a fixed pa-\line \expndtw5 rameter of the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 there are other things that can be done to reduce\line \expndtw2 the total overhead in a Smalltalk system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 One technique would be to represent some Smalltalk objects in other\line \expndtw-1 ways than as full-fledged \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects, for example, by embedding their\line \expndtw4 representation in a pointer rather than storing it separately as a \lang1032 432\line \expndtw2\lang1033 object. Small integers are a prime candidate for such a representation,\line \expndtw0 but other objects could be so represented as well. This topic is discussed\line \expndtw-4 on p. \lang1032 316 \lang1033 below.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Experience with the Xerox Smalltalk system has shown the value of}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2268 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex128\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Insufficient{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw6 implementing a good portion of the Smalltalk virtual machine in the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2264 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Microcode Space{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw6 host system's micro-architecture. At a minimum, the bytecode inter-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 preter, plus some critical primitives, must be implemented in microcode\line if reasonable performance is to be achieved on the \lang1032 432.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Clearly the size of the 432's microcode store is much smaller than\line \expndtw0 any of the Xerox systems. For example, the Xerox Alto has a 32-bit mi-\line croinstruction, in comparison to the 432's 16-bit word. Further, the Alto\line \expndtw13 has IK of ROM and 3K of RAM available to the Smalltalk\line \expndtw0 implementor; the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 has much less. In speaking of the microcode re-\line quirements of the Smalltalk-80 system, Glenn Krasner\super 8\nosupersub  says:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2491\fi0\ri14\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 For the systems that we have implemented at Xerox, the\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk-80 Virtual Image consists of about 300K bytes of objects.\line \expndtw2 Our typical implementation of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine is \lang1032 6\line \expndtw-3\lang1033 to 12K bytes of assembly code, or 2K microcode instructions plus 10K\line \expndtw-1 bytes of assembly code. Of this, about \lang1032 40% \lang1033 is in the storage manager,\line \expndtw3\lang1032 20% \lang1033 in the interpreter, and \lang1032 40% \lang1033 in the primitive subroutines.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi0\ri14\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The \lang1032 432 \lang1033 has a total of 4K microwords, of which a certain amount will\line \expndtw-1 be left devoted to \lang1032 432 \lang1033 system primitives (e.g., ports and object tables). A\line \expndtw8 rough guess would estimate this at 2K, thus leaving 2K for the\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk implementor. It is difficult to evaluate the extent to which\line \expndtw2 the PLAs and special-purpose data paths of the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 microengine will\line \expndtw0 offset the smaller amount of microcode space.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi234\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 There are several functions which should be implemented within the\line \expndtw2 microcode of the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 in order to achieve reasonable performance. We\line list them here in decreasing order of importance:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4242\margrsxn1432\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1454\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5922 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 307}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Threats to Smalltalk-432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb187\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The standard object table functions\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 necessary. In order for\line \expndtw10 Smalltalk programs and Ada programs to be able to share the\line \expndtw4 same memory space, it is necessary that both have the same notion\line \expndtw2 of object table and access descriptor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The standard port functions\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 necessary. In order for Smalltalk\line programs and Ada programs to be able to engage in interprocess\line \expndtw5 communication, it is necessary that both have the same notion of\line \expndtw6 port and carrier.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The standard process and processor functions\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 necessary. In order\line \expndtw4 for Smalltalk processes to be dispatched in a uniform manner, it is\line \expndtw6 necessary that Smalltalk processes share the same structure and\line \expndtw4 dispatching functions as Ada processes. (Note that these three re-\line \expndtw6 quirements, taken together, require that the Smalltalk processor\line \expndtw4 support the standard \lang1032 432 \lang1033 Carrier, Communication Segment, De-\line \expndtw2 scriptor Control, Object Table, Port, Process, Processor, Refinement\line \expndtw4 Control, and Type Definition Objects. It is not however, necessary\line to support the standard \lang1032 432 \lang1033 Context, Domain, Instruction, or Stor-\line \expndtw0 age Resource Objects.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The bytecode interpreter\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 necessary. The bytecode set of the\line Smalltalk machine is a relatively simple set of instructions. Most\line of them are simple push, store, and jump instructions. The others\line \expndtw5 are more complex instructions for sending a message to an object\line and returning from such a send; these functions are similar both in\line \expndtw7 nature and complexity to the call-context and return instructions\line \expndtw5 on the standard GDP.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb137\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk primitives\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 varying. The Smalltalk bytecodes do\line \expndtw4 not for example, include add or compare instructions per se. Rath-\line er these functions are handled by invoking special methods, called\line primitive methods, known to the Smalltalk virtual machine imple-\line mentation. These methods are primitive for either of two reasons:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li274\fi-256\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx274 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li274\fi-256\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx274 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Intrinsic. Some primitives would be impossible or at least very\line \expndtw6 hard,  to  express  as  ordinary  methods  coded  with  ordinary\line \expndtw5 bytecodes. Examples are the Process Resume and BitBIt meth-\line ods for process synchronization and icon manipulation, respec-\line \expndtw3 tively. Most of these primitives could be supported by sending a\line \expndtw10 request message to an Ada program executing on a standard\line \expndtw4 GDP, then receiving a reply from it.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li274\fi-256\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx274 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li274\fi-256\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx274 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Performance. Some primitives are invoked so frequently that\line the system would be slower if they were handled by the stan-\line dard message-sending scheme. Examples are the methods for\line \expndtw9 stream operations next, nextPut:, and atEnd. (Note that these}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1604\margrsxn1366\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4964 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 308{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li3017\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 two motivations for making a method primitive are not mutual-\line \expndtw3 ly exclusive. Consider, for example, the at: and at:put: methods\line for indexing into objects. They would be hard to implement as\line \expndtw4 ordinary methods both for intrinsic and performance reasons.\line Those methods that are primitive only for intrinsic reasons can\line often be implemented by auxiliary processes running either on\line Ada GDPs or on attached processors. Those that are primitive\line \expndtw5 only for mild performance reasons might best be implemented\line by means of standard Smalltalk methods or by auxiliary pro-\line \expndtw4 cesses as above. Many however, will best be implemented di-\line \expndtw3 rectly in the microcode. Various techniques for implementing\line \expndtw2 primitives other than directly in the microcode will be discussed\line \expndtw-2 on p. \lang1032 317 \lang1033 below.)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-198\ri0\sb263\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-198\ri0\sb263\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object creation\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 optional. We may decide to have actual allocation\line \expndtw9 of segments from Storage Resource objects be performed by an\line \expndtw3 auxiliary Ada process. It should be put into microcode only if there\line \expndtw5 is plenty of microcode to spare. Allocation of Smalltalk objects is\line \expndtw4 discussed in more detail in the implementation sketch.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-198\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2725\fi-198\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2725 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent198\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Large  integer and  floating  point\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 optional.  The  primitives  for\line \expndtw6 large integers and floating point numbers may also be handled by\line \expndtw3 an auxiliary Ada process. Since the floating-point microcode in the\line \expndtw7 standard \lang1032 432 \lang1033 uses considerable space, there would be substantial\line \expndtw6 benefit in performing floating-point arithmetic using an auxiliary\line \expndtw0 Ada process.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri14\sb266\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Thus just what can be fit into microcode is bound to be a central issue.\line \expndtw6 However, a partial solution can be effected by implementing some of\line \expndtw3 the Smalltalk primitives "off board." Here, low-overhead linkage to\line primitives will be very important. Possible implementation techniques\line \expndtw2 include using an attached processor (AP), an Ada co-process, or inter-\line \expndtw3 processor communication (IPC). We would expect that some combina-\line \expndtw2 tion of these techniques would be used in an eventual system. These\line \expndtw5 techniques are discussed in more detail in the implementation sketch.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb497\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A final major threat is the overloading of the garbage collector, since}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Overloading of the       \i0 Smalltalk will put a much heavier load on it than does Ada. In the \lang1032 432\line \i\expndtw4\lang1033 Garbage Collector        \i0 implementation of Ada, contexts and local variables for procedure calls}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 are not stored in the global heap, and so don't need to be reclaimed by\line \expndtw2 the parallel garbage collector. However, in the book implementation of\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk, all objects are allocated from a global heap.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the garbage collector can keep up, then all is well. However, if\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk processes spend a significant amount of time blocked, waiting}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3776\margrsxn1462\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1901\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6372 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 309}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Threats to Smalltalk-432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 for the garbage collector to free up some storage, or if the garbage col-\line \expndtw2 lector consumes a large amount of processor or memory resources, then\line \expndtw3 performance will be improved if ways can be found to reduce the num-\line ber of objects that need to be garbage collected. This could be done ei-\line \expndtw9 ther by reducing the number of real \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects created in the first\line \expndtw4 place, or by reclaiming some objects by means other than the parallel\line \expndtw1 garbage collector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi234\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One place to look is contexts. In general, contexts must be allocated\line \expndtw6 from the heap, since it is possible, for example, to obtain a pointer to\line \expndtw9 the current context and store it into a global variable. However, in\line \expndtw4 most cases, contexts will be allocated and deallocated in a stack-like\line \expndtw3 fashion, and probably advantage could be taken of this fact. (The pres-\line \expndtw6 ence of block contexts, used for many control structures, complicates\line \expndtw5 the situation. Each block context includes a pointer to its "home con-\line \expndtw7 text", which will be some method context. When used to implement\line \expndtw5 such standard control structures as iterations through the elements of a\line collection, the block context is only passed up the stack, and the meth-\line \expndtw3 od contexts still obey a stack discipline. However, there is no guarantee\line \expndtw8 that a pointer to the block context won't be bound to an instance or\line \expndtw1 global variable. Hence the presence of block contexts makes it more dif-\line \expndtw5 ficult to keep track of all the pointers into the stack.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi234\ri4\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another place to look is short-lived objects in general, that is objects\line \expndtw7 that are created, bound to temporary variables, and used in the course\line \expndtw5 of executing a method, but which are not returned or bound to an in-\line \expndtw3 stance or global variable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For Ada, the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 uses "level numbers" to help with this situation.\line \expndtw3 Each context object, along with that context's local variables/objects,\line \expndtw5 contains a level number that is one greater than that of the calling con-\line text. Upon termination of a context (i.e., a return instruction), the run-\line \expndtw4 time stack is popped by deallocating all objects whose level number\line \expndtw5 matches that of the terminating context. We devoted some effort to\line studying whether modifications to the level-number scheme could be\line \expndtw4 used with Smalltalk, for example, just using level numbers for method\line \expndtw5 and block contexts. Unfortunately, none of the techniques we devised\line \expndtw3 had satisfactory characteristics. Generally, these techniques suffered ei-\line ther from too much complexity, too little applicability, or too great an\line \expndtw5 overhead. We believe however, that reasonable solutions to this prob-\line \expndtw3 lem can be found with additional work. For example, in Ballard's VAX\line implementation, which uses a garbage collector together with a limited\line \expndtw4 reference count scheme, only about \i\lang1032 5% \i0\lang1033 of the contexts needed to be\line \expndtw1 garbage collected (see Chapter \lang1032 8).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri14\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This is an area that will require further investigation if in fact the\line \expndtw10 garbage collector threat materializes. Intelligent responses to this\line \expndtw4 threat will be based on detailed experience with running Smalltalk sys-\line \expndtw11 tems (for example, what is the rate of creation of method contexts,}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1656\margrsxn1318\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 310}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop29\shpright4954\shpbottom29\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri18\sb216\sa1102\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 block contexts, and other objects, and how frequently do they exhibit\line stack-like behavior?) with the iMax Garbage Collector, and a detailed\line \expndtw4 review of current garbage collection literature. (See for example, the\line \expndtw2 October \lang1032 1981 \lang1033 issue of \i Computing Surveys. \i0 This area is very active, part-\line \expndtw4 ly due to work on various kinds of Lisp machines.) If this area is pur-\line \expndtw6 sued, benefits will accrue not only to Smalltalk programmers, but also\line \expndtw4 to Ada programmers who desire to use the heap heavily.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1677\margrsxn1318\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1908\colsr328\colno2\colw6678 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7\shptop-299\shpright8913\shpbottom-299\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Implementation\line \expndtw1 Sketch}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7\shptop1246\shpright1901\shpbottom1246\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb1429\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Representation of\line Smalltalk Objects}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop3125\shpright1894\shpbottom3125\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb3312\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 System Types for\line \expndtw10 Smalltalk}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This section presents approaches to several aspects of Smalltalk imple-\line mentation. Areas discussed include the representation of Smalltalk ob-\line \expndtw2 jects, special system types for Smalltalk, allocation of Smalltalk objects,\line ways of reducing storage overhead, execution of primitive methods,\line \expndtw6 communication between Smalltalk and Ada programs, and interfacing\line \expndtw3 to the display.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk objects would be represented directly as 432-objects, each\line \expndtw5 with an object descriptor and a memory image. The object descriptor\line \expndtw4 would include the object's length and its system type, if any. The few\line \expndtw2 system types needed specifically for Smalltalk will be presented in a fol-\line lowing section. The Smalltalk notion of class would be mapped directly\line \expndtw5 onto the Release Three \lang1032 432 \lang1033 extended type notion. Thus Smalltalk ob-\line \expndtw4 jects that are not of some system type would contain an access descrip-\line \expndtw10 tor to a Type Definition Object, which would take the form of a\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk Class object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With the exception of Smalltalk methods, all Smalltalk objects are\line \expndtw3 either pure data objects, e.g., strings, or pure access objects, e.g., dictio-\line \expndtw4 naries. In the case of pure data objects, they may be byte-addressed or\line \expndtw9 (16-bit) word-addressed, depending on the characteristics of the\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk class. In the case of pure access objects, they will use the re-\line \expndtw2 lease three notion of embedded values (see p. \lang1032 316).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk system makes heavy use of several system types. Ordi-\line nary Ada GDPs need only be able to access these types as generic ob-\line \expndtw3 jects, i.e., be able to access their fields, but not their peculiar operations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'d0\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Class \i0 The first type is Class. Each object of this type describes\line \expndtw3 some Smalltalk Class, and includes the following kinds of information:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\tx443 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\tx443 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 How to create a new object of this Class, and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li443\fi-202\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx443 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li443\fi-202\ri0\sb104\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx443 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Methods to be executed when a message is sent to an instance of\line \expndtw1 this Class.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2510\margrsxn1381\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3269\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx7729 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 311}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementation Sketch}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1336\fi0\ri18\sb245\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The format of this type is shown in Fig. \lang1032 17.1. \lang1033 The first pointer leads to\line another Class object, which is the Superclass of this Class. The second\line leads to a Message Dictionary, which contains the Methods for all mes-\line sages defined directly on this Class. The third pointer is actually an em-\line \expndtw2 bedded value, which includes:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1570\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\tx1771 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1570\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\tx1771 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The number of fixed fields in an instance of this Class,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1771\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1771 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1771\fi-202\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1771 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whether the instance's representation contains pointers or numer-\line \expndtw2 ic data,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1570\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx1771 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1570\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx1771 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If data, whether it is organized as bytes or words, and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1570\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa1800\tx1771 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1570\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa1800\tx1771 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whether the object is indexable.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2510\margrsxn2562\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1080\colsr227\colno2\colw720\colsr2884\colno3\colw1926 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2038\shptop-1364\shpright4428\shpbottom-1364\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1307\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 17.1}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri202\sb0\sa0\sl-475\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 AD \lang1032 2:\line \expndtw-10\lang1033 AD \b\lang1032 1:\line \b0\expndtw-8\lang1033 ADO:}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-475\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Creation Parameters\line \expndtw3 Message Dictionary\line Superclass}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2510\margrsxn1381\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1310\fi0\ri54\sb238\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Message Dictionary \i0 Just as in the model implementation, we re-\line \expndtw3 quire a Message Dictionary to be an array of pointers to Symbols with a\line \expndtw5 parallel array of pointers to Methods. This Message Dictionary is used\line \expndtw2 by the microcode which implements Message Send bytecodes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1303\fi0\ri54\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Method \i0 Another system-defined type is the Method, an analogue\line \expndtw4 of the Ada Instruction Segment. This type is the only Smalltalk class to\line contain both data and pointers. The format of these objects is shown in\line \expndtw3 Fig. \lang1032 17.2. \lang1033 The byte-organized data, shown below the thick line, contain\line the bytecodes for the Method. (A nice property of the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 implementa-\line \expndtw7 tion is that the initial instruction address is always zero!) All but the\line \expndtw5 first pointers are literals, i.e., pointers to constants or global variable\line \expndtw3 objects. The first pointer is the Method Header, packed within an em-\line \expndtw4 bedded value. The representation for the Header is:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1548\fi0\ri0\sb184\sa0\tx1750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1548\fi0\ri0\sb184\sa0\tx1750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Temporary Frame Size: \lang1032 5 \lang1033 bits}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1548\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx1750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1548\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx1750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Argument Count: \lang1032 5 \lang1033 bits}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1339\margrsxn1617\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx961\tlul\tx4957 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 312{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex126\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0