lbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2879 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3592 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-446\slmult0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn10\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs62 \'d5}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 0}\cell {\par \cell }\pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1447 \clvmgf\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2167 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2879 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3592 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell {\par \cell }\pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-252 \clvertalt\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1447 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2167 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2879 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3592 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell {\par \cell }\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-950\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Stack\line Temporaries}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-950\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Arguments}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-475\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Receiver (Self)\line \expndtw1 Method\line \expndtw26 IP&SP\line \expndtw3 Sender}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1910\margrsxn1950\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1375\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The fields in a Method Context are as follows:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-202\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-202\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pointer to the Sender, i.e., the Context that issued the Send in-\line \expndtw8 struction that caused this Context to be created. On the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 this\line \expndtw3 pointer would lack DeleteRights.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-202\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Instruction Pointer and Stack Pointer embedded in one pseu-\line \expndtw11 do-access descriptor.  The IP is a byte offset into the Method's\line \expndtw9 bytecode part. The SP is a pointer offset into the Context itself,\line \expndtw4 and indicates the top of the stack region of the Method Context.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-202\ri0\sb108\sa101\sl-241\slmult0\tx1804 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-202\ri0\sb108\sa101\sl-241\slmult0\tx1804 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pointer to the Method of which this Context is an invocation. This\line \expndtw3 pointer would also lack DeleteRights on the \lang1032 432.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3512\margrsxn1975\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw3182\colsr378\colno2\colw2862 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li198\fi-198\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'95\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161   \lang1033 Pointer to the receiving object\line \expndtw3 lack DeleteRights on the \lang1032 432.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Self). This pointer would also}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1910\margrsxn1950\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1786\fi-194\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1786 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1786\fi-194\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1786 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent194\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next come zero or more arguments. These are the values pushed\line \expndtw6 on the stack by the sender prior to issuing the Send bytecode. The\line \expndtw3 number of these is specified by the Method Header, Fig. \lang1032 17.2.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1786\fi-194\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1786 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1786\fi-194\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1786 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent194\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next come zero or more Temporaries, or local variables. The num-\line \expndtw3 ber of these is also specified by the Method Header, Fig. \lang1032 17.2.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1786\fi-194\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1786 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1786\fi-194\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1786 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent194\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Finally, there is room for the expression stack for this context.\line \expndtw5 This stack is initially empty, so the initial value of the SP must be\line determined from the size of the Argument and Temporary frames.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1465\margrsxn1501\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4961 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 314{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex142\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2290\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa3607\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Block Context system type is very much like the Method Context,\line \expndtw6 and is used to implement control structures other than ordinary Mes-\line \expndtw4 sage Sending. Like the Method Context and all other Smalltalk objects,\line \expndtw1 it is logically allocated from a heap. The format of the Block Context is\line \expndtw0 shown in Fig. \lang1032 17.4.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2405\margrsxn2099\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1090\colsr205\colno2\colw720\colsr3510\colno3\colw1879 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2056\shptop-2804\shpright4677\shpbottom-2804\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2048\shptop-1854\shpright4683\shpbottom-1854\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2056\shptop-904\shpright4684\shpbottom-904\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1728\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure \lang1032 17.4}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri209\sb0\sa0\sl-475\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 AD \lang1032 3:\line \expndtw-10\lang1033 AD \lang1032 2:\line \expndtw-11\lang1033 AD \b\lang1032 1:\line \b0\expndtw-10\lang1033 ADO:}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb176\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Home Meth Context}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Initial IP \lang1032 & \lang1033 Arg\line \expndtw1 Comments}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw26\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 IP&SP}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Caller}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1465\margrsxn1501\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9425\shptop2941\shpright9425\shpbottom3251\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2297\fi0\ri0\sb565\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Three fields of the Block Context differ from those in the Method Con-\line \expndtw0 text:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-202\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-202\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The caller field of the Block Context points to the (Block or Meth-\line \expndtw11 od) Context that called this Block Context. Note that this may\line \expndtw4 differ from the Context that created the Block Context.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An embedded value storing the initial IP and argument count re-\line \expndtw4 places the Method pointer of the Method Context. These values al-\line \expndtw7 low the interpreter to initialize the Block Context for a new call.\line \expndtw3 (The Method can be found via the Home Method Context pointer.)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2729\fi-202\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2729 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf25{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Home Method Context points to the Method Context that cre-\line \expndtw11 ated the Block Context. It is used for locating the receiver and\line \expndtw3 temporaries of the Method.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1156\margrsxn1832\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1263\colsr1001\colno2\colw6656 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb882\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Allocation/\line \expndtw-1 Deallocation\line of Objects}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1901\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6365 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 315}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implementation Sketch}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The allocation and deallocation of Smalltalk objects poses several diffi-\line \expndtw-1 culties:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li436\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk objects are allocated from global heaps. Their\line \expndtw3 deallocation cannot therefore, be performed simply because some\line \expndtw7 method has executed a return instruction. As mentioned earlier,\line \expndtw5 this threatens to overload the parallel garbage collector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li436\fi0\ri4\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Microcode space on the processor chip is at a premium. It would\line \expndtw3 conserve microcode sp ace if object allocation, including mainte-\line \expndtw1 nance of Storage Resource Objects and Object Tables, could be re-\line moved from the microcode.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1156\margrsxn1832\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2246\fi0\ri14\sb295\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One consequence of the first point is that any implementation of\line \expndtw5 Smalltalk on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 (or any other machine) must pay serious attention\line to efficient garbage collection of Smalltalk objects. This topic is dis-\line \expndtw1 cussed fully on p. \lang1032 308.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The allocation of objects, on the other hand, is easier and gives us an\line \expndtw2 opportunity both to speed allocation and to save microcode space. We\line \expndtw4 propose that an Ada auxiliary process be assigned to maintain a data\line \expndtw5 structure that contains pointers to well-formed, unallocated segments.\line \expndtw4 This data structure would take the form of an access segment, with one\line \expndtw1 access descriptor for each of several commonly used sizes of segments.\line \expndtw4 (Since most Smalltalk objects are small, and since object images in the\line \expndtw5\lang1032 432 \lang1033 come in chunks of \lang1032 8 \lang1033 bytes, the sizes \lang1032 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48, 56, 64\line \expndtw2\lang1033 would take care of the vast majority of cases.) Each access descriptor in\line \expndtw3 this structure would be the head of a singly-linked list of unallocated\line \expndtw6 segments of a certain size. If the Smalltalk processor wants to "create"\line \expndtw4 a new Smalltalk object, it need only pull a segment of the right size off\line \expndtw3 the appropriate list. No carving of storage from heap SROs or allocation\line \expndtw2 of entries from object tables is needed. Also, the auxiliary process could\line \expndtw5 ensure that the segments it places in the list are all zeroed out. The\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk processor would only have to adjust the object descriptor to\line \expndtw9 indicate the proper actual size and type, and would have to put the\line \expndtw3 class access descriptor in the object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2225\fi230\ri22\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This way of allocating objects should in fact be faster than doing the\line \expndtw4 actual manipulation of SROs and object tables required of the Ada \lang1032 432.\line \expndtw8\lang1033 In addition to entries in the list for each of the most common sizes,\line \expndtw4 there should also be a port for use in making requests for odd sizes.\line (Handling this case by having a request-reply protocol via ports would\line \expndtw5 not be very fast, but it is general and simple and would only be used\line \expndtw7 rarely.) Another set of lists should be maintained specifically for the\line \expndtw2 two sizes of method contexts; here there would not even be any need for\line \expndtw7 setting the class/type in the object. One elegant possibility is to use\line \expndtw6 ports as the heads of the linked lists\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 pulling a segment off a list then}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1735\margrsxn1231\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4961 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 316{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 becomes the ordinary Receive Port primitive, synchronized access to the\line \expndtw4 list is taken care of, and the auxiliary process could use surrogate sends\line to find out when certain sizes of segments are being used up. Further-\line \expndtw3 more, this auxiliary process could coordinate closely with the parallel\line \expndtw9 garbage collector to effectively recycle objects, rather than going\line \expndtw4 through the overhead deallocating objects by putting their storage back\line into an SRO and deallocating their object table entries.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0\sl-248\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As discussed on p. \lang1032 305, \lang1033 one of the threats to a \lang1032 432 \lang1033 Smalltalk implemen-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-248\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reducing Storage         \i0 tation is excessive storage overhead per object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-248\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Overhead}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Embedded Values \i0 One technique for reducing this overhead would\line \expndtw9 be to represent some Smalltalk objects in other ways than as full-\line \expndtw2 fledged \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects, for example, by embedding their representation in a\line \expndtw5 pointer rather than storing it separately as a \lang1032 432 \lang1033 object. Note that any\line object stored this way must be of an immutable class, since one isn't\line \expndtw3 sharing pointers to a mutable piece of storage. (The model implementa-\line tion uses this technique to represent small integers. Instead of small in-\line \expndtw5 tegers being represented as an object pointer to an integer object, the\line \expndtw4 value of the integer is encoded directly into the object pointer.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 On the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 if the "valid bit" of an access descriptor is turned off,\line \expndtw3 that access descriptor is not regarded as referencing any \lang1032 432 \lang1033 object, and\line \expndtw5 the remaining \lang1032 31 \lang1033 bits can be used to store the representation of some\line \expndtw2 object. Since we would have more bits available than in 16-bit imple-\line \expndtw3 mentations, more things than just small integers can be encoded. A rea-\line \expndtw1 sonable set of objects to be encoded in the access descriptor is as follows.\line \expndtw5 (We assume that bits \lang1032 29-30 \lang1033 are used to disambiguate the different sorts\line \expndtw4 of objects so encoded, so that bits \lang1032 0-28 \lang1033 are available for storing data. If\line \expndtw6 it were important, another bit could be squeezed out for representing\line \expndtw2 some of the classes by a more clever encoding.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2257\fi0\ri0\sb284\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4518 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 small integers{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw1 These are integers between -2\super 28\nosupersub  and 2\super 28\nosupersub -l. Note}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4518\fi0\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 that this is much larger than the maximum\line \expndtw9 small integer in the 16-bit implementations,\line \expndtw5 and should mean that large integers would be\line \expndtw3 used even less frequently.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2408\fi-158\ri0\sb54\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4511 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the constants \b0 true,         There would in fact be only a trivial space sav-\line \expndtw-1 false, \b and \b0 nil{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw6 ing due to encoding these constants as embed-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4507\fi0\ri36\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ded values, since there is only one instance of\line \expndtw4 each. However, this will make life much sim-\line \expndtw5 pler for the garbage collector, since it will be\line \expndtw3 able to ignore these oft-encountered values.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2236\fi0\ri0\sb54\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx4496 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 characters{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw6 There are exactly \lang1032 256 \lang1033 distinct instances of this}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4507\fi0\ri43\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 class, representing the ASCII characters. As\line with the above constants, there would only be}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1447\margrsxn1495\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4518\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8680 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 317}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementation Sketch}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4579\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a trivial space saving due to this encoding; the\line \expndtw2 garbage collector would be the primary benefi-\line \expndtw-1 ciary.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2304\fi0\ri7\sb331\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the current version of the Smalltalk-80 system, points are mutable\line \expndtw5 objects. However, it would take a relatively small amount of work to\line make them immutable. If this were done, small points would be anoth-\line \expndtw8 er good candidate for representation as embedded values. (Making\line \expndtw3 points be immutable would also eliminate some annoying sorts of bugs\line \expndtw6 arising from accidental sharing.) The \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'f7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 and y values of a small point\line \expndtw1 could be stored in 2's complement in the low-order \lang1032 28 \lang1033 bits of the access\line \expndtw4 descriptor, \lang1032 14 \lang1033 bits each. This is sufficient to hold points arising in near-\line \expndtw3 ly all graphics applications, save perhaps the generation of very high\line resolution hardcopy images. There would also be a class LargePoint to\line \expndtw5 hold points with floating point values or large integer values, in analo-\line \expndtw1 gy with the Smalltalk classes LargePositivelnteger and LargeNegative-\line \expndtw-5 Integer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2300\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Floating point numbers are another possible candidate for this repre-\line \expndtw3 sentation. They are stored in 32-bit fields on the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 so if one is willing\line \expndtw4 to give up \lang1032 2 \lang1033 bits of precision, they could be represented as embedded\line \expndtw2 values. However, as described on p. \lang1032 306, \lang1033 it is likely that floating point\line \expndtw4 arithmetic will be handled by an auxiliarly Ada process running on an-\line \expndtw5 other chip, rather than on the Smalltalk chip. In this case, it would be\line \expndtw4 better to let floating point numbers be ordinary \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects, making it\line easier to send them to the Ada process.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri11\sb320\sa302\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Other Techniques for Reducing Storage Overhead \i0 Another\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 more\line \expndtw1 problematic\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 possibility for reducing the number of objects is to merge\line \expndtw5 some collections of objects into a single object. This sort of merging\line \expndtw3 should be used in only a few critical places, if at all; certainly the aver-\line \expndtw4 age programmer shouldn't need to think about it. As an example of this\line \expndtw6 technique, rather than maintaining each method object as a separate\line \expndtw5 segment, all the methods for a given class could be stored in a single\line \expndtw2 object, with \lang1032 432 \lang1033 refinements being used to refer to individual methods.\line \expndtw5 There are about \lang1032 4000 \lang1033 methods in the Smalltalk virtual image, so this\line \expndtw1 would reduce the number of objects by close to \lang1032 4000, \lang1033 since most classes\line \expndtw6 have many methods. Making this modification might also improve\line swapping behavior, but would have the disadvantage that all the code\line \expndtw4 for a given class would need to be in main memory at one time, rather\line \expndtw5 than just the methods being used.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1447\margrsxn1531\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1486\colsr781\colno2\colw6663 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Techniques for}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Executing}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Primitives}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As discussed on p. \lang1032 306, \lang1033 several time-critical primitives will be directly\line \expndtw5 executed on the processor chip. Other primitives are best executed off\line \expndtw2 the chip. This section discusses several different ways of executing these\line primitives.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1458\margrsxn1512\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9522\shptop7888\shpright9522\shpbottom12302\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx4961 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 318{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-80 System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 \'d0\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i\lang1033 Execution of Primitives on an Attached Processor \i0 One alternative\line \expndtw3 is to send requests for primitives to a port served by an attached proces-\line \expndtw5 sor. In the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 this is the natural technique to use for i/o primitives. It\line may also be important for non-i/o primitives handled better by an at-\line \expndtw2 tached processor than by another \lang1032 432 \lang1033 GDP. If BitBIt is not implemented\line \expndtw5 on the Smalltalk processor, it may be an example of this.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Execution of Primitives by an Ada Process \i0 Here we devote an Ada\line process to be a Smalltalk primitive server. Then, using inter-process\line \expndtw4 communication (i.e., ports), the Smalltalk system sends requests to the\line Ada co-process to execute primitives. Again, this is a natural technique\line \expndtw1 to use on the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 and if it works well, is the method of choice for most\line \expndtw4 non-i/o primitives handled off-board. The speed, and therefore the val-\line \expndtw2 ue, of this idea will hinge on both the execution speed of Ada programs,\line \expndtw5 and the speed of inter-process communications. Preliminary statistics\line \expndtw2 give an overhead of approximately \lang1032 300 \lang1033 microseconds for such inter-pro-\line \expndtw1 cess communications.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One advantage of this technique is that the primitives will be coded\line \expndtw4 in Ada; this should simplify the implementation. Also, with this tech-\line \expndtw5 nique, the Ada co-process is not locked onto a particular GDP as it is\line \expndtw3 with the inter-processor communication technique discussed below.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that in both of these first two techniques, a message is sent to a\line \expndtw5 port, and a reply is received on another port. This means that the\line \expndtw3 implementors of Smalltalk can postpone or change the decision as to\line \expndtw6 whether a given primitive is implemented by an attached processor or\line \expndtw4 by an Ada process, and that no change to the microcode would be re-\line \expndtw2 quired. This flexibility would be achieved by having an access segment,\line \expndtw4 indexed by the primitive index, that mapped a primitive to a port. This\line would make the first two techniques indistinguishable at the microcode\line \expndtw-3 level.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri11\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Q \i Execution of Primitives by a Dedicated GDP \i0 This technique de-\line \expndtw3 votes one GDP exclusively to the Ada primitive handler. Thus when\line Smalltalk needs an off-board primitive executed, it sends a wake-up sig-\line \expndtw4 nal to the GDP and then sends its request. When the GDP is finished\line \expndtw6 handling the request, it sends itself a stop processor signal. Thus the\line \expndtw5 GDP is either working for the Smalltalk system, or it is blocked, wait-\line \expndtw6 ing for a wake-up signal from Smalltalk. The Intel \lang1032 432 \lang1033 Architecture\line Group suggested implementing this approach via the Lock instruction\line \expndtw5 on the standard GDP.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The advantage here is that we make use of the faster inter-processor\line \expndtw4 communications facilities of the \lang1032 432. \lang1033 The disadvantage is that the sys-\line \expndtw7 tem becomes less general, since the Ada process must be resident on\line \expndtw4 the GDP whenever the Smalltalk processor is active. This alternative is\line \expndtw5 attractive only if the improved overhead is worth the reduced generali-\line \expndtw3 ty and dedicated processor.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1146\margrsxn1820\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6743\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8662 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 319}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6800\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa349 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex143\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementation Sketch}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1150\margrsxn1824\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1897\colsr360\colno2\colw6678 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Communication\line \expndtw5 between Smalltalk\line \expndtw2 and Ada Programs}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As described in the introduction, one of the potential benefits of a \lang1032 432\line \expndtw6\lang1033 implementation of Smalltalk would be support for multi-lingual sys-\line \expndtw3 tems. In this section we outline how communication between Smalltalk\line \expndtw5 and Ada programs might take place.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Within the Smalltalk image, there would be "local representatives"\line \expndtw6 of Ada tasks with which the Smalltalk program could communicate.\line \expndtw1 These would be full-fledged Smalltalk objects to which one would send\line \expndtw4 messages in the usual way. Internally, these representatives would have\line \expndtw3 an access descriptor for a \lang1032 432 \lang1033 port. The Smalltalk invocation messages\line \expndtw7 would thus run methods that would in turn send \lang1032 432 \lang1033 messages to the\line \expndtw5 Ada task. There should be a Smalltalk class AdaTask that holds this\line \expndtw4 general sort of information, with subclasses of AdaTask used to repre-\line \expndtw6 sent particular Ada tasks.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk and Ada will have different ideas about how data is to be\line \expndtw5 represented. For example, Smalltalk integers will be represented either\line \expndtw6 as embedded values or as large integers; Ada integers will be stored\line \expndtw5 simply as 32-bit quantities. To convert between these representations,\line \expndtw2 the class AdaTask should have a set of conversion messages that accept\line \expndtw9 certain Smalltalk objects as arguments and encode them on a byte\line \expndtw7 stream for eventual transmission to the Ada task. For example, the\line \expndtw6 message encodelnteger would take a (Smalltalk) integer argument and\line \expndtw5 a stream of bytes, and put out the \lang1032 4 \lang1033 bytes representing the integer as a\line \expndtw1 32-bit quantity. An analogous set of messages would be used to convert\line \expndtw5 values returned by the Ada task back to Smalltalk objects.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1146\margrsxn1827\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb349\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1146\margrsxn1827\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1144\colsr1105\colno2\colw6685 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Interface to\line \expndtw1 the Display}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A critical factor in the performance of a Smalltalk system, particularly\line the user's perception of its performance, is the speed of the graphics.\line \expndtw3 There are a number of plausible ways in which the display can be con-\line \expndtw4 nected to the processor, as discussed below. In regard to the production\line \expndtw7 of a new version of the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 the relevant question is whether BitBIt\line \expndtw5 should be supported on the chip; other decisions regarding the display\line \expndtw8 can be made independently. We conclude that to ensure flexibility,\line \expndtw2 BitBIt should indeed be supported on the \lang1032 432, \lang1033 unless there is a severe\line \expndtw3 shortage of microcode space. While the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 hardware doesn't have any\line \expndtw1 special facilities to support BitBIt, its performance would still be quite\line \expndtw4 adequate if it were implemented in microcode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One way of connecting the bitmap display to the processor, used in\line \expndtw4 the Xerox machines, is to make the memory for the bitmap display be\line \expndtw3 simply part of the machine's main memory. This approach gives maxi-\line \expndtw4 mum flexibility. An unfortunate problem is that the maximum size of a\line \expndtw3\lang1032 432 \lang1033 object is just short of what would be needed to represent the bitmap\line \expndtw1 as a single \lang1032 432 \lang1033 object. The maximum size of a \lang1032 432 \lang1033 object is 64K bytes. A\line \expndtw3\lang1032 600x800 \lang1033 display would just fit, but a more generous size \lang1032 (1000x1000)\line \expndtw7\lang1033 would not; such a limitation shouldn't be built into the system. With\line \expndtw5 some additional software complexity, the problem can be overcome by}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1758\margrsxn1194\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 320}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementing a Smalltalk-8O System on the Intel \lang1032 432}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi0\ri22\sb173\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mapping the display memory onto several \lang1032 432 \lang1033 objects. The \lang1032 432 \lang1033 would\line \expndtw2 write some data into these objects, which would be reflected on the dis-\line \expndtw4 play. This approach clearly requires that BitBIt be implemented on the\line \expndtw-9\lang1032 432.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi241\ri29\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another approach is to have the display memory be separate from\line the machine's main memory, and to send messages to a separate dis-\line \expndtw3 play processor to make changes to it. For this approach to have accept-\line \expndtw4 able performance, there should be some high-level requests that can be\line \expndtw2 made of the display processor, for example, to display a paragraph or to\line \expndtw3 replicate a bit pattern along a given path. For flexibility and complete-\line \expndtw0 ness, low-level BitBIt requests should be supported as well. There would\line \expndtw4 need to be facilities for swapping fonts and the like into display memo-\line \expndtw8 ry. Even with this approach, there may well be occasions on which\line \expndtw1 BitBIt on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 itself would be valuable. For example, the programmer\line \expndtw9 might create an image in main memory\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 not displayed at all\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 and\line \expndtw4 only later show this image on the display.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri32\sb7\sa1026\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Thus for maximum flexibility, we recommend that BitBIt be included\line \expndtw4 in the primitives supported by the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 microcode, unless there is a se-\line \expndtw2 vere shortage of microcode space.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1758\margrsxn1255\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1429\colsr817\colno2\colw6649 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-245\shpright8907\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusions}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our study of Smalltalk and the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 leads us to conclude that the imple-\line \expndtw4 mentation we have described here is feasible. We have pointed to what\line \expndtw6 we believe to be the three chief technical threats, but we believe them\line to be surmountable. The potential qualitative advantages of Smalltalk\line \expndtw5 on the \lang1032 432 \lang1033 present real motivations to attempt its implementation.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1805\margrsxn1194\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb997\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1805\margrsxn1194\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1296\colsr1177\colno2\colw6436 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-252\shpright8910\shpbottom-252\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 References}\par \column \pard\plain\fs2\par{\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi-259\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx259 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi-259\ri0\sb58\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx259 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Wulf, William \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 Cohen, Ellis; Corwin, William, Jones, Anita,\line \expndtw4 Levin, Roy, Pierson, Charles, and Pollack, Frederick, "Hydra: The\line \expndtw5 Kernel  of  a  Multiprocessor Operating System",   \i Comm.   of the\line \expndtw4 Assoc. for Computing Machinery \i0 vol. \lang1032 17, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 6, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 337-345, \lang1033 June\line \expndtw-9\lang1032 1974.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx259 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx259 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Wulf, William \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 Levin, Roy, and Pierson, Charles, "Overview of\line \expndtw4 the Hydra Operating System Development", in \i Proceedings of the\line \expndtw6 Fifth Symposium on Operating Principles, \i0 Assoc. for Computing\line \expndtw1 Machinery, pp. \lang1032 122-131, \lang1033 Austin, TX, Nov. \lang1032 1975.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx259 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi-259\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx259 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Wulf,   William   \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 .,   \lang1033 Levin,   Roy,   and   Harbison,   Samuel   P.,\line Hydra/C.mmp: An Experimental Computer System, McGraw-Hill,\line \expndtw-1 New York, \lang1032 1980.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4022\margrsxn1431\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1685\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6152 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-24\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 321}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 References}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx263 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx263 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Cox, George W., Corwin, William M., Lai, Konrad K., and Pollack,\line \expndtw8 Fred J., "A Unified Model and Implementation for Interprocess\line \expndtw7 Communication in a Multiprocessor Environment", Presented at\line \expndtw9 the Eighth ACM Symposium on Operating Systems Principles,\line \expndtw-1\lang1032 1981; \lang1033 (to be published).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx263 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx263 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pollack, Fred J., Cox, George W., Hammerstram, Dan W., Kahn,\line \expndtw5 Kevin C, Lai, Konrad K., and Rattner, Justin R., "Supporting Ada\line \expndtw10 Memory Management in the iAPX-432", in \i Proceedings of the\line \expndtw4 Symposium on Architectural Support for Programming Languages\line \expndtw2 and Operating Systems, \i0 Assoc. for Comp. Machinery, pp. \lang1032 117-131,\line \expndtw7\lang1033 March \lang1032 1982; \lang1033 (also distributed as \i SigArch Computer Architecture\line \expndtw1 News \i0 vol. \lang1032 10, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 2, \lang1033 and as \i SigPlan Notices \i0 vol. \lang1032 17, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 4).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx263 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx263 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Kahn, Kevin C, Corwin, William M., Dennis, T. Don, D'Hooge,\line Herman, Hubka, David E., Hutchins, Linda \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 Montague, John\line \expndtw4 T., and Pollack, Fred J., "iMAX: A Multiprocessor Operating Sys-\line \expndtw5 tem for an Object-Based Computer", in \i Proceedings of the Eighth\line \expndtw1 Symposium on Operating Systems Principles, \i0 Assoc. for Comp. Ma-\line chinery, pp. \lang1032 127-136, \lang1033 Dec. \lang1032 1981; \lang1033 (also distributed as \i SigOps Review\line \i0\expndtw-3 vol. \lang1032 15, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 5).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx263 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx263 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pollack, Fred J., Kahn, Kevin C, and Wilkinson, Roy M., "The\line \expndtw8 iMAX-432 Object Filing System", in \i Proceedings of the Eighth\line \expndtw1 Symposium on Operating Systems Principles, \i0 Assoc. for Comp. Ma-\line \expndtw-1 chinery, pp. \lang1032 137-147, \lang1033 Dec. \lang1032 1981; \lang1033 (also \i SigOps Review \i0 vol. \lang1032 15, \lang1033 no. \lang1032 5).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx263 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi-263\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx263 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart4\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Krasner, Glenn, "The Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine", \i Byte \i0 vol. \lang1032 6,\line \expndtw0\lang1033 no. \lang1032 8, \lang1033 pp. \lang1032 300-320, \lang1033 Aug. \lang1032 1981.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn10710\margrsxn360\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop-263\shpright817\shpbottom-263\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-824\shptop-277\shpright-824\shpbottom3870\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Abstra}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft18\shptop2552\shpright839\shpbottom2552\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb2812\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Introdi}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2998\margrsxn2222\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1940\shptop-302\shpright6692\shpbottom-302\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1944\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Preferred Classes: A\line \expndtw9 Proposal for Faster\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 Execution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1940\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Robert Hagmann}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1937\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Computer Science Division}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1940\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Department of Electrical Engineering and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1937\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Computer Sciences}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1937\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 University of California, Berkeley}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-1415\shptop882\shpright6692\shpbottom882\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb1192\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A straightforward implementation of a Smalltalk-80 interpreter has\line \expndtw5 two main bottlenecks: memory management and message send/return\line \expndtw4 overhead. In addition, since Smalltalk-80 is a typeless language, it is\line \expndtw5 harder to compile than to interpret. This proposal addresses both the\line send/return bottleneck and the difficulty of compilation by introducing\line \expndtw6 an optional limited typing mechanism for the Smalltalk-80 language.\line \expndtw5 The typing mechanism does not change the Smalltalk-80 semantics in\line \expndtw3 any way. Its sole purpose is to allow for a more efficient execution.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-1444\shptop889\shpright6685\shpbottom889\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb1202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This proposal is for a Smalltalk-80 implementation strategy\super 1\nosupersub  that is dif-\line \expndtw5 ferent from conventional interpreters. Where existing implementations\line \expndtw4 typically try to gain performance through caching and special casing of\line \expndtw3 high probability cases (see Chapter \lang1032 11), \lang1033 this proposal explores the gains\line made possible by using compiler technology. This strategy has not been\line \expndtw6 implemented, nor have all of the performance data needed to validate\line the effectiveness of this proposal been collected. This paper presents\line \expndtw2 only one way to apply compiler technology to Smalltalk-80 implementa-}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Robert Hagmann \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 323}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1654\margrsxn1327\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5962 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 324{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Preferred Classes: A Proposal for Faster Smalltalk-80 Execution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri7\sb277\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 tions. The author hopes that by illustrating one technique that other\line researchers will be motivated to explore alternate strategies.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Briefly, the idea is to "fix" the class of arguments and variables to\line \expndtw0 selected Smalltalk-80 methods by explicit declaration. By making the\line \expndtw-1 "fixed" classes be only hints, the semantics would not change. Several\line \expndtw0 benefits could then occur. First, the target methods of some sends could\line \expndtw1 be identified at compile time. Second, translation to machine code in-\line \expndtw2 stead of bytecodes would be made easier since the class of all objects\line \expndtw-2 used in some methods would be known at compile time. Finally, some\line methods could be compiled in-line.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The proposals in this paper have some similarity to those of Borning\line and Ingalls\super 2\nosupersub . Where their proposal deals with compile time type check-\line ing, this proposal addresses the issue of performance.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop904\shpright8921\shpbottom904\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1220\sa0\tx2261 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Assumptions{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\charscalex114\expndtw3 There are three assumptions that are necessary for this technique to be}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri40\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 efficient in speeding up Smalltalk-80 execution.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2747\fi-256\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-227\slmult0\tx2747 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2747\fi-256\ri0\sb252\sa0\sl-227\slmult0\tx2747 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The overhead associated with the send and return bytecodes is\line \expndtw-3 high.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2747\fi-256\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2747 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2747\fi-256\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2747 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A Smalltalk-80 application spends a large fraction of its time at or\line \expndtw4 near the leaves of the message-send tree: that is, in methods that\line \expndtw2 send no other messages other than to primitive methods.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2747\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2747 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2747\fi-256\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2747 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A significant portion of methods executed dynamically do not ex-\line ploit the polymorphic nature of the Smalltalk-80 language: that is,\line \expndtw2 the classes of the arguments and variables used by the method re-\line \expndtw1 main nearly constant over some relatively long time period.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2243\fi0\ri22\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The assumption that message sends and returns are a bottleneck was\line \expndtw-1 confirmed by measuring several implementations. The percentage of ex-\line \expndtw3 ecution time attributable to send/return, excluding memory manage-\line \expndtw0 ment, for the Dorado and Dolphin are \lang1032 38% \lang1033 and 34%\super 3\nosupersub . For Berkeley\line Smalltalk, the same statistic is about 30%\super 4\nosupersub . Since memory management\line \expndtw2 during message send/return is also a bottleneck for the Dorado and\line \expndtw-1 Dolphin\super 5\nosupersub , for these implementations about half of the execution time is\line \expndtw1 directly attributable to message-send and return bytecodes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2239\fi234\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex114\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The second assumption has also been verified. In measurements of a\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk-80 interpreter\super 6\nosupersub , it was found that \lang1032 55% \lang1033 of all non-primitive\line \expndtw2 message sends go to methods that do no further non-primitive sends.\line \expndtw3 That is, \lang1032 55% \lang1033 of all methods executed dynamically are at the leaves of\line \expndtw2 the message send tree. A second interesting result is that the leaf nodes}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1521\margrsxn1443\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4194\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8658 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 325}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Technique}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi0\ri11\sb252\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tend to be extremely short: \lang1032 70% \lang1033 of the leaf nodes executed \lang1032 5 \lang1033 or fewer\line \expndtw5 bytecodes before returning. The conclusion is that most methods exe-\line cute at the leaves of the message-send tree and they tend to be small.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The final assumption is that a significant portion of the methods do\line \expndtw3 not exploit the polymorphic nature of the Smalltalk-80 language. In\line particular, a large fraction of methods are executed with "fixed" classes\line \expndtw5 for the arguments and variables. That is, for many methods, the classes\line of the arguments and variables nearly always are the same from call to\line \expndtw3 call. No direct evidence has been collected to verify this assumption.\line \expndtw4 However, one measurement has been made that makes this seem plau-\line \expndtw3 sible. As measured dynamically, for a given send bytecode in a given\line \expndtw5 method, the probability is \lang1032 95% \lang1033 that the receiver is of the same class as\line the last time this particular bytecode was executed\super 7\nosupersub . This indicates a\line \expndtw4 strong lack of polymorphism for a large part of the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi238\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Of course, this measurement does not directly validate the third as-\line \expndtw3 sumption. Even though the class of the receiver was often the same, the\line \expndtw6 classes of the arguments and variables (if any) were not measured.\line \expndtw4 However, it is unlikely that the \lang1032 5% \lang1033 of variability in receiver is distrib-\line \expndtw3 uted evenly over the system. Probably, many sends almost always have\line \expndtw6 the same receiver as the last time. It is also likely that similar results\line \expndtw5 hold for arguments and variables in methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2275\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For this final assumption to be true, there must be dynamically a\line \expndtw7 significant number of methods that are almost always presented with\line \expndtw10 the same classes for the arguments and variables as the last time.\line \expndtw2 While the number of methods for which this holds is unknown, it is cer-\line \expndtw8 tainly true for some methods. For example, some graphics methods\line \expndtw3 most certainly expect only to be passed an instance of class Point.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop889\shpright8946\shpbottom889\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1199\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Technique        \b0 The proposal is to augment the Smalltalk-80 language with an optional}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 declaration of the class of arguments, instance variables and class vari-\line \expndtw6 ables. In addition, some methods would have optional declarations of\line \expndtw4 the class of the value returned from the method and/or the class of the\line \expndtw1 receiver. These declarations would define the \i preferred class \i0 of the ar-\line \expndtw5 gument, variable, return value, or receiver. This preferred class is the\line \expndtw4 class expected to match the actual class used in the method in the vast\line \expndtw5 majority of activations of the method. The receiver and arguments are\line \expndtw4 not forced to match: if at run time the actual class did not match the\line \expndtw3 preferred class, then the method would be executed by a conventional\line \expndtw7 interpreter. If however, the preferred classes match the actual classes\line \expndtw3 for all arguments and variables, then a more efficient method of execu-\line \expndtw2 tion could be performed.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1143\margrsxn1834\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5958 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 326{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Preferred Classes: A Proposal for Faster Smalltalk-80 Execution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi238\ri4\sb266\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At least two other techniques for introducing the notion of types to\line \expndtw2 Smalltalk languages have been documented. One\super 8\nosupersub  uses type inferencing,\line \expndtw3 and the other\super 9\nosupersub  uses type declarations. This proposal is somewhat differ-\line \expndtw4 ent from either of these.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2282\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For the purpose of this paper, the use of pool dictionaries or the\line \expndtw3 Smalltalk-80 dictionary in a method would make it ineligible for this\line \expndtw4 technique. The problems involved in incorporating these dictionaries\line appear not to be insurmountable, but they confuse the concept being\line presented here.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2279\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the system would always interpret bytecodes when the actual\line \expndtw4 and preferred classes did not match, the Smalltalk-80 semantics would\line \expndtw2 not change. The only effect of the preferred classes would be that some\line \expndtw3 methods would run more efficiently.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi234\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The idea behind all of this is that the upper portions (near the root)\line \expndtw2 of the message-send tree are likely to be polymorphic. However, to per-\line \expndtw5 form some low level function, only a small locus of methods is used.\line \expndtw4 Many sends and much looping occur inside of this locus. If the bound-\line \expndtw6 ary of this locus can be identified and the classes passing over this\line \expndtw2 boundary checked, then the class of objects inside the locus might be\line predicted. This would lead to faster execution of this small part of the\line \expndtw7 computation. The rule of thumb in conventional programming lan-\line \expndtw2 guages is that \lang1032 10% \lang1033 of the code accounts for \lang1032 90% \lang1033 of the execution. Typi-\line cally most of this \lang1032 90% \lang1033 is found in inner loops. If this conjecture is also\line \expndtw3 accepted for the Smalltalk-80 language, then this technique could effect\line the vast majority of execution.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There is a danger in this approach: by having two execution strate-\line \expndtw6 gies for methods, the more efficient one with preferred classes might\line \expndtw7 tend to encourage programmers not to exploit the polymorphic nature\line \expndtw8 of the language. Programmers might tend to use the more restrictive\line \expndtw10 but more efficient style of programming rather than the fuller and\line \expndtw3 more powerful nature of the Smalltalk-80 language. If however, the ad-\line \expndtw1 dition of preferred classes to a collection of methods is viewed as an op-\line \expndtw4 timization step performed by the applications programmer after initial\line \expndtw5 system test, then the polymorphic nature of the Smalltalk-80 language\line \expndtw1 will be effectively preserved.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-11\shptop655\shpright8917\shpbottom655\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb968\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implementation      \b0 The implementation technique is to compile methods to machine code}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 as much as possible. For methods where the preferred classes are de-\line \expndtw3 clared, two types of compiled methods are produced: the existing com-\line \expndtw4 piled method and a new machine code compiled method. It is assumed\line \expndtw8 that a conventional interpreter is available to execute methods where}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3454\margrsxn1759\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1948\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6412 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 327}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Implementation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li40\fi0\ri11\sb259\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 there are no preferred classes and for use when the preferred classes do\line \expndtw5 not match the actual classes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li32\fi245\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For methods where the preferred classes of all arguments, instance\line variables, and class variables are declared, a variant of the message dic-\line \expndtw2 tionary will be used. This will be set up at compile time. The message\line \expndtw3 dictionary entry will be flagged to indicate machine code is to be exe-\line \expndtw4 cuted. The machine code for the method will be divided into two sec-\line \expndtw2 tions. The first section, called the \i prologue, \i0 will validate the class of the\line \expndtw4 arguments and variables. To do so, only a few instructions are executed\line for each argument and variable. Variables will only be checked if they\line \expndtw8 are used in the method (unused instance and class variables need not\line \expndtw4 be checked). If the method explicitly uses self, the class of the receiver\line \expndtw2 will also be checked. If any of these tests fail, the method will be run by\line \expndtw8 the standard interpreter. If all these tests succeed, the second part of\line \expndtw2 the machine code, called the \i body, \i0 will be executed. The body is the ma-\line chine code program to perform the method. The body would be all that\line would be executed from other machine code methods when the classes\line \expndtw3 matched. That is, there will really be two entries to the machine code: a\line \expndtw6 checked (prologue) and an unchecked (body) entry. The first is used\line \expndtw4 when the classes of the arguments and variables cannot be predicted at\line \expndtw3 compile time. The second will be used when this prediction is possible.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi238\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 More optimizations could occur during compilation. If the class of the\line \expndtw9 receiver of a send can be predicted at compile time and the target\line \expndtw6 method is short, then it could be compiled in-line. If the target method\line \expndtw7 is not short and the classes match, the send could be bound to the\line \expndtw4 unchecked entry. Finally, if the target method can be predicted, a hard\line \expndtw8 pointer to this method can be used instead of a lookup at execution\line \expndtw4 time. Primitives are prime candidates for these optimizations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Additional information must be kept to allow the debugger to oper-\line ate as usual. In case of an error, it must be possible to map from ma-\line \expndtw3 chine code and expanded in-line machine code to a more conventional\line \expndtw4 representation. This could be done with an auxiliary table for each ma-\line chine code method that would set up the correspondence of program\line \expndtw1 counter values between the two types of methods. Code would be gener-\line \expndtw5 ated such that at each potential error point, the execution would clean-\line \expndtw3 ly map into the conventional representation. Dependency lists could be\line \expndtw5 kept to detect what methods need to be recompiled after another meth-\line \expndtw-5 od is changed\super 10\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri29\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Sometimes during the execution of a method in machine code, the\line \expndtw4 class of the value returned by some message might not match the pre-\line \expndtw2 ferred class. In this case, the machine code form of the execution would\line \expndtw3 also need to be converted to interpreter form (i.e., compute the effective\line \expndtw6 program counter for the interpreter). The interpreter would then start\line \expndtw3 executing as usual.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1198\margrsxn1808\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5915 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 328{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Preferred Classes: A Proposal for Faster Smalltalk-80 Execution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi241\ri0\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, suppose the \lang1032 + \lang1033 primitive when called with a receiver\line \expndtw2 and argument of class Smalllnteger, returns either a Smalllnteger or\line Largelnteger. The normal case is for a Smalllnteger to be returned. If a\line \expndtw1 Large Integer is to be returned (possibly detected by testing the overflow\line \expndtw5 bit in machine code), the system would invoke full fault recovery to\line \expndtw4 convert the executing machine code form to the normal interpreter for-\line mat. Once this is done, the interpreter would continue to execute the\line method. It is hoped that this type of fault is a low probability event, so\line that it would not add significant overhead.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By insuring the class of the receiver, arguments, and variables at\line \expndtw4 method entry, the class of all objects relevant to the computation at the\line \expndtw3 start of the method are known at compile time. By checking the class of\line \expndtw2 all objects returned by message sends where needed, the class of all ob-\line \expndtw4 jects would be known at compile time for the whole method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2232\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The checking of the preferred class at method entry need not be too\line \expndtw6 time consuming. For class variables, checking could occur when they\line \expndtw5 are changed. If the setting did not match the preferred class, then the\line \expndtw1 object table could be flagged to not execute the machine code for those\line \expndtw3 methods that depend on this class variable. Conversely, the setting of a\line class variable to the preferred class would re-enable the execution of\line \expndtw5 machine code. By doing the checking of class variables when they are\line \expndtw2 set, normal method execution would do no class variable checking. This\line \expndtw4 would be faster since it is presumed that variables are read more often\line than they are written. The instance variables would have to be checked,\line \expndtw3 but this could also be reduced to checking a flag in the object if the set-\line \expndtw5 ting of the instance variables by the standard interpreter also caused a\line check of the preferred class. Note that changing an instance or class\line \expndtw3 variable might involve converting existing suspended methods from the\line \expndtw8 machine to the bytecode form of execution. Finally, the arguments\line \expndtw1 would have to be checked. Since dynamically, most methods have few\line \expndtw3 arguments (an average of \lang1032 1.3 \lang1033 has been reported\super 11\nosupersub ), this would take only\line \expndtw6 a few instructions. The number of machine instructions to check the\line \expndtw8 classes might be about six. This would make the checking be about\line \expndtw4 three times faster than executing a single bytecode in a conventional\line \expndtw8 interpreter. If the tests succeeded in the clear majority of the cases,\line \expndtw4 then this overhead would be acceptable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2221\fi241\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The final question is that of execution efficiency. Will anything be\line gained or lost by the use of this technique? Certainly there will be add-\line \expndtw2 ed complexity during compilation, debugging, and error handling. Addi-\line \expndtw8 tional space will be consumed with machine instructions, mappings\line \expndtw3 from bytecodes to machine instructions, and dependency lists. Addition-\line al overhead will be incurred when sending messages to methods with\line \expndtw5 preferred classes when the actual classes do not match the preferred\line classes. The interpreter must also do extra work when setting instance\line \expndtw4 and class variables that have preferred classes. But what savings can be}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1236\margrsxn1719\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4201\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8669 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 329}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex136\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Acknowledgments}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri11\sb238\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 obtained from running part of the system in machine code instead of\line \expndtw4 using a standard bytecode interpreter? If we assume:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2779\fi-256\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-230\slmult0\tx2779 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2779\fi-256\ri0\sb238\sa0\sl-230\slmult0\tx2779 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Executing machine instructions is five times faster than interpre-\line \expndtw4 tation,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2524\fi0\ri0\sb108\sa0\tx2779 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2524\fi0\ri0\sb108\sa0\tx2779 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Half of all executed bytecodes are compiled to machine mode, and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2779\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2779 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2779\fi-256\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2779 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Half of all messages sends are eliminated by compilation in-line or\line \expndtw4 direct calls without method lookup,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2286\fi0\ri7\sb238\sa1123\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 then the savings would be that half the execution would run five times\line \expndtw5 as fast, while the other half would run at nearly the same speed. This\line \expndtw4 means that a savings of about \lang1032 40% \lang1033 could be obtained. Of course this\line \expndtw5 number is approximate and depends on the selection of the preferred\line \expndtw4 classes to be nearly correct. However, this shows that there is potential-\line ly a large gain possible by using techniques like the one proposed here.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1254\margrsxn1727\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1440\colsr817\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop-248\shpright8935\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Conclusions}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A technique to augment a conventional Smalltalk-80 interpreter has\line \expndtw5 been proposed. This technique uses the concept of a preferred class to\line \expndtw4 gain efficiency in those methods that do not use the polymorphic nature\line \expndtw3 of the Smalltalk-80 language. This technique allows for more efficient\line \expndtw2 compilation to machine code. Since the receiver class can sometimes be\line \expndtw6 identified at compile time, either the method lookup can be done at\line \expndtw4 compile time, or the method can be expanded in-line.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With the assumptions stated in the body of this paper, it seems possi-\line \expndtw6 ble that some form of optimizing compiler techniques can be used to\line \expndtw5 gain efficiency. Although only one technique was presented here (ex-\line \expndtw3 plicit declaration), many variations on this theme are possible. Type de-\line \expndtw4 duction, where the type is inferred from dynamic usage, seems equally\line \expndtw2 viable but requires more compiler sophistication\super 12\nosupersub .}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1236\margrsxn1734\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1181\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1236\margrsxn1734\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1558\colsr706\colno2\colw6674 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-302\shpright8946\shpbottom-302\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Acknowledg-\line \expndtw-2 ments}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Professor David Patterson provided overall motivation, help, and guid-\line \expndtw4 ance for this work. Hewlett-Packard allowed Berkeley to run an early\line \expndtw13 version of their interpreter that was helpful in understanding\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 implementations and allowed certain statistics to be ac-\line \expndtw8 quired. Xerox granted Berkeley access to documentation as well as\line \expndtw9 many personal discussions. In particular, L. Peter Deutsch was most}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1297\margrsxn1680\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft9662\shptop7679\shpright9662\shpbottom9544\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx5936 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 330{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex135\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Preferred Classes: A Proposal for Faster Smalltalk-80 Execution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2257\fi0\ri18\sb256\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 helpful in providing statistics and discussing alternate implementation\line \expndtw0 strategies. L. Peter Deutsch, Adele Goldberg, Glenn Krasner, and D. Ja-\line son Penny also served as reviewers for this paper. Their comments were\line \expndtw2 most helpful and greatly improved the quality of this paper.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop868\shpright8928\shpbottom868\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1188\sa0\tx2516 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 References{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\charscalex112\expndtw1\lang1032 1. \b0\lang1033 Goldberg, Adele, and Robson, David, \i Smalltalk-80: The Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2765\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 and Its Implementation, \i0 Addison-Wesley, Reading, Mass., \lang1032 1983.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Borning, Alan H., and Ingalls, Daniel \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 . \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161 \'c7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 "A Type Declaration\line \expndtw1 and Inference System for Smalltalk"; Ninth Symposium on Princi-\line \expndtw2 ples of Programming Languages, pp. \lang1032 133-141, \lang1033 Albuquerque, NM,\line \expndtw-12\lang1032 1982.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb112\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Deutsch,  L.   Peter,   Berkeley  Computer   Systems  Seminar,   Fall\line \expndtw-12\lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Ungar, David M., Private Communication, \lang1032 1982.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 3.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-13\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Hagmann, Robert, "Some Smalltalk Performance Measurements\line \expndtw4 Emphasizing Compiler  Performance  and/or Context Windows",\line \expndtw1 Unpublished Class  Paper  for  CS292R,  Computer  Science  Div.,\line \expndtw-1 Dept. of E.E.C.S., Univ. of California, Berkeley, Fall \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  7.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Deutsch, L. Peter, Private Communication, \lang1032 1982.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  8.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi-259\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Suzuki, Nori, "Inferring Types in Smalltalk", Eighth Symposium\line \expndtw-2 on     Principles    of    Programming    Languages,     pp.     \lang1032 187-199,\line \lang1033 Williamsburg, VA, \lang1032 1981.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2743 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  9.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2484\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2743 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 2.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\par{\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-346\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  10.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2736\fi-346\ri0\sb119\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent346\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Mitchell, James \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'c1\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 ., \lang1033 "The Design and Construction of Flexible and\line \expndtw1 Efficient Interactive Programming Systems", Garland, N.Y., \lang1032 1979;\line \expndtw3\lang1033 (A Monograph of Mitchell's \lang1032 1970 \lang1033 thesis at Carnegie-Mellon Uni-\line \expndtw-3 versity).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2390\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  11.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2390\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent346\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-17\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 7.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2390\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2736 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  12.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2390\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\tx2736 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart10\pnindent346\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex112\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 See reference \lang1032 10.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1184\margrsxn1336\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4180\shptop-310\shpright8932\shpbottom-310\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs44 Low-Overhead Storage\line \expndtw10 Reclamation in the\line \expndtw6 Smalltalk-80\line \expndtw11 Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb288\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs32 Scott B. Baden}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Computer Science Division}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Department of Electrical Engineering and}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4172\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Computer Sciences}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4176\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1109\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 University of California, Berkeley}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1184\margrsxn1796\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1033\colsr1228\colno2\colw6667 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-256\shpright8931\shpbottom-256\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Abstract}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb61\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Measurements of the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine indicate that \lang1032 20%\line \expndtw3\lang1033 to \lang1032 30% \lang1033 of the time is spent managing storage. Following the work of\line \expndtw0 Deutsch, Bobrow, and Snyder\super 1\nosupersub '\super 2\nosupersub -\super 3\nosupersub -\super 4\nosupersub , we introduce a strategy that reduces\line \expndtw3 the overhead of storage reclamation by more than \lang1032 80%. \lang1033 We also discuss\line \expndtw8 the design of simple hardware to support this strategy, and compare\line \expndtw2 our approach to one using only software. We conclude by suggesting di-\line \expndtw5 rections for future research.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1192\margrsxn1793\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1141\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1192\margrsxn1793\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1530\colsr734\colno2\colw6660 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop-248\shpright8924\shpbottom-248\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Introduction}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb68\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Last fall, Smalltalk came to Berkeley. Under the direction of Professor\line \expndtw6 David Patterson, students in the Computer Science department ported\line \expndtw3 the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine (SVM), generously provided by\line Hewlett-Packard Laboratories, to a research VAX-1\lang1032 1/780, \lang1033 and analyzed\line \expndtw-1 several aspects of its performance\super 5\nosupersub\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'b7\super\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 67\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi234\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As a result of these studies, we discovered that a large percentage of\line \expndtw1 SVM execution time was spent managing storage\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161 20% \lang1033 to 30%\super 8\nosupersub -\super 9\nosupersub . Most}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3463\margrsxn1336\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb234\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3463\margrsxn1336\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw3974\colsr2416\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copyright \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'a9\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \lang1033 Scott B. Baden \lang1032 1982. \lang1033 All rights reserved.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 331}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1636\margrsxn1345\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6808 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 332{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Low-Overhead Storage Reclamation in the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri4\sb313\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 of this overhead is due to management of activation contexts. According\line \expndtw2 to our statistics, context objects account for \lang1032 82% \lang1033 of all object allocations\line \expndtw6 and deallocations, and references from the evaluation stack and from\line \expndtw4 local variables (both of which are contained by contexts) cause \lang1032 91% \lang1033 of\line \expndtw3 all reference-count operations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi252\ri0\sb4\sa1123\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These findings are encouraging: if the SVM could treat context ob-\line \expndtw3 jects as special objects, then it would save considerable time managing\line them. Consistent with this reasoning we show how to reduce storage\line reclamation overhead by a factor of five.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1636\margrsxn1349\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1544\colsr702\colno2\colw6678 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-245\shpright8928\shpbottom-245\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Assumptions}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop515\shpright1901\shpbottom515\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb670\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 SVM Changes}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop5047\shpright1894\shpbottom5047\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb5202\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Hardware}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb65\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our assumptions deal with changes to the SVM, both to its specification\line \expndtw5 and implementation, and to the processor that executes it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An invariant of the SVM specification states that "the reference count\line of an object must equal the number of references to that object"\super 10\nosupersub . In\line \expndtw5 our implementation we will relax this invariant; the reference count of\line \expndtw3 an object will usually not include the references made from the \i\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'e7\hich\af1\dbch\af25\loch\f1\cchs161  \i0\lang1033 most\line recent context activations. We call those contexts whose fields are not\line \expndtw0 reference counted \i volatile \i0 contexts\super 11\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Owing to the presence of volatile contexts, an object might not be\line \expndtw4 free when its reference count reaches zero. This condition prevents the\line \expndtw3 SVM from reclaiming storage incrementally. Instead it reclaims storage\line \expndtw2 periodically, accounting for all the volatile references before freeing any\line \expndtw7 storage (we must ensure that the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine never\line \expndtw4 runs out of storage between reclamation phases).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition to relaxing the reference-count invariant we also relax\line \expndtw3 one other: that "all objects must be assigned an Oop"\super 12\nosupersub . Usually, the\line \expndtw1 proposed SVM does not assign Oops to method contexts nor does it allo-\line \expndtw8 cate space in the object memory for them. Instead, the system stores\line \expndtw4 the contexts in FIFO order from a fixed region of physical memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi216\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Occasionally, nonlinearities in the context nesting sequence or other\line \expndtw8 exceptions will arise, causing the system to momentarily enforce\line \expndtw3 previously relaxed invariants. Later, we will show that these conditions\line \expndtw4 arise infrequently enough so that they do not degrade performance sig-\line \expndtw2 nificantly.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Although volatile contexts can be stored in main memory, they are\line \expndtw4 used like registers in a conventional CPU, so we provide a small regis-\line \expndtw2 ter cache, called the \i context cache.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi234\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Two registers, the \i Top Window Pointer \i0 and the \i Bottom Window\line \expndtw4 Pointer, \i0 mark the physical memory bounds of the volatile contexts. The}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1465\margrsxn1473\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4208\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx8680 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 333}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Assumptions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2272\fi0\ri40\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 system uses these pointers, as in RISC-I\super 13\nosupersub , to resolve references to con-\line \expndtw2 texts (e.g., is the context volatile\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 in the registers\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 or not?). All the\line \expndtw3 contexts between the two markers are volatile, while all the contexts\line \expndtw0 below the Bottom Window Pointer are \i stable \i0 (i.e., their fields are refer-\line \expndtw1 ence countable). In our implementation, the storePointer operation will\line \expndtw0 not do any reference counting if the destination field is volatile. To sim-\line \expndtw2 plify the cache design we assume that all contexts are \lang1032 32 \lang1033 words deep.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2268\fi238\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 To speed up storage reclamation, we provide a special memory, called\line \expndtw0 the Zero Count Table (ZCT), that indicates all the objects with a zero\line \expndtw4 reference count. The ZCT has 32K entries and is \lang1032 1 \lang1033 bit wide (the depth\line \expndtw2 of the ZCT will always equal the number of possible Oops in the sys-\line \expndtw0 tem). It is capable of operating in both random access and content asso-\line ciative modes. The system accesses the ZCT over its memory data and\line \expndtw1 address busses, using a special request line to distinguish the ZCT from\line the object memory. The ZCT will behave like a Content Associative\line \expndtw0 Memory (CAM) when the system searches it for free objects\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 this be-\line \expndtw1 havior speeds up the search time considerably (compare with queues\line \expndtw-5 elsewhere\super 14\nosupersub\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'b7\super\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 1516\nosupersub ). At all other times it behaves like a RAM. When an ob-\line \expndtw1 ject's reference count reaches zero the CPU sends the object's Oop over\line \expndtw3 the memory address bus and tells the ZCT to mark the appropriate en-\line \expndtw2 try (owing to possible volatile references to the object, it might not be\line \expndtw-7 free).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The CPU suspends normal execution during the storage reclamation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx2275 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex125\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Reclamation{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex113\expndtw4 phase. First, it accounts for the volatile references\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 a process we call}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri36\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 stabilization\super 11\nosupersub . \i0 To stabilize a register, the CPU increments the reference\line \expndtw1 count of its contents (a re/7 operation). During the stabilization phase, a\line reference count may get incremented from zero to one\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 we call such a\line \expndtw-2 zero reference count a \i spurious zero reference count. \i0 To prevent the\line \expndtw4 Smalltalk-80 virtual machine from freeing an object that had a spuri-\line \expndtw0 ous zero count, the CPU clears the ZCT entry on a zero to one reference\line \expndtw2 count transition.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi238\ri43\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 After stabilizing the registers, the CPU frees any object marked in\line \expndtw6 the ZCT. During the reclamation phase further storage may become\line \expndtw1 free and so new ZCT entries will be set. When reclamation finishes, the\line system \i volatilizes \i0 the registers by decrementing the reference count of\line \expndtw2 their contents (we call a reference-count decrement a \i refD).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2254\fi234\ri43\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The difference between our scheme and that of its predecessors lies\line \expndtw2 in the structure of the ZCT (Others used a queue.). Our implementation\line \expndtw1 of the ZCT is preferable to a queue for two reasons:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2502\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2502\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The table will not overflow.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-248\ri0\sb169\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2750\fi-248\ri0\sb169\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx2750 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent248\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex113\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The search time will depend on the number of free objects, and\line \expndtw3 not on the number of possible objects (i.e., there is no need to ex-\line \expndtw0 amine spurious zero counts).}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1692\margrsxn1282\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6786 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 334{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa310 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex134\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Low-Overhead Storage Reclamation in the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1710\margrsxn1286\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw936\colsr1310\colno2\colw6667 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb266\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Overhead}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are three potential causes of overhead in a volatilizing system:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Window underflows and overflows.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Periodic stabilizations and revolatilizations.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx497 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Special case treatment of volatile contexts.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the depth of the stack is bounded, some sends will cause a win-\line \expndtw1 dow overflow and some returns will cause a window underflow. The sys-\line \expndtw4 tem must stabilize the bottom window in the cache on an overflow and\line volatilize the top window in memory on an underflow. For an \lang1032 8 \lang1033 deep\line context cache, we found that only \lang1032 3% \lang1033 of all message sends and returns\line caused an overflow or an underflow; these conditions result in negligi-\line \expndtw-5 ble overhead\super 18\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi238\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are certain cases where improper treatment of volatile con-\line \expndtw5 texts could cause the system to fail: non-linearities in the context nest-\line \expndtw3 ing sequence, caused by blocks (e.g., a message may return a block as a\line \expndtw4 result), and sending messages to contexts (e.g., sending a thisContext\line \expndtw6 message). We assume that these activities have a negligible cost. We\line \expndtw3 have observed that only \lang1032 6% \lang1033 of all activations are due to blocks\super 19\nosupersub , and it\line \expndtw5 is well known that messages to contexts happen much less frequently\line \expndtw2 than method activations and messages to other types of objects\super 20\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Although we have provided a fixed region of memory devoted to con-\line \expndtw8 texts, this does not mean that we have imposed a hardwired limit on\line \expndtw6 the maximum context nesting depth. When the system overflows the\line \expndtw4 fixed region, it migrates least-recently used contexts into the object\line \expndtw5 memory\super 21\nosupersub . We believe that this exceptional case can be ignored\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 we\line \expndtw4 have observed a maximum context nesting depth of only \lang1032 40 \lang1033 contexts in\line \expndtw1 an execution trace of 559K bytecodes\super 22\nosupersub . Assuming that the sample is\line \expndtw4 representative, it would be reasonable to allocate a fixed region of, say,\line \expndtw1\lang1032 64 \lang1033 contexts.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1692\margrsxn1282\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb1418\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1692\margrsxn1282\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1717\colsr547\colno2\colw6670 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-306\shpright8939\shpbottom-306\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Experiments\line \expndtw0 and Their\line \expndtw-2 Interpretation}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop526\shpright1904\shpbottom526\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb684\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 General Method}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Experimental evidence shows that our scheme improves storage recla-\line \expndtw7 mation overhead by at least \lang1032 80%. \lang1033 First we will discuss our general\line \expndtw6 method for gathering statistics; next, the experiments; and finally, our\line \expndtw-1 conclusions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb234\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We monitored a session involving the execution of system code and appli-\line \expndtw4 cation code\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 browsing, compiling, and execution of simple messages\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\line \hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 a total of 559K bytecodes were executed. To collect a more representa-}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1510\margrsxn1463\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1616\colsr630\colno2\colw6688 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb5940\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Experiment}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop4111\shpright1757\shpbottom4111\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4259\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Figures of Merit}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1933\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6408 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex145\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 335}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex145\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Experiments and Their Interpretation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri25\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tive sample we started the measurements after system initialization\line \expndtw1 had completed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi234\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The H-P code was written exactly as specified in the Smalltalk speci-\line \expndtw0 fication\super 23\nosupersub , hence it was highly modular and easy to change. We modified\line \expndtw2 the code by inserting non-invasive calls to special auditing routines. Ap-\line \expndtw4 propriate Oops, method headers, and other data were written onto disk.\line \expndtw1 Owing to its size \lang1032 (17 \lang1033 megabytes), the audit file was copied onto magnetic\line \expndtw5 tape. A context cache simulator was written and ran directly from the\line magnetic tape audit files. Complete documentation for the audit tape\line \expndtw2 format appears in our previous work\super 24\nosupersub .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Several activities were audited:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Bytecode Execution}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference Counting}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Deallocations and Allocations}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Method Lookups}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Context Activations and Returns}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx504 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent263\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Primitive Successes and Failures}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb187\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We measured the effects of volatilization in a non-volatilizing system.\line \expndtw2 There are two experimental variables: stabilization period (in bytecodes)\line \expndtw5 and context stack depth (in 32-word windows). First we introduce a set\line \expndtw6 of criteria for assessing the validity of our approach. Then we present\line \expndtw5 the numbers to support our claims.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi234\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We had to adjust our figures to account for four optimizations not\line present in the Smalltalk-80 specification, that reduce reference-count-\line \expndtw-1 ing activity by 50%\super 252627\nosupersub . These optimizations include: nilling the top of\line \expndtw3 stack on a pop, not reference counting explicit use of distinguished val-\line \expndtw2 ues (nil, true, false) in the push and return bytecodes, and not reference\line counting when moving Oops (e.g., return top of stack).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi241\ri25\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since we did not have access to a volatilizing Smalltalk-80 system,\line we could not measure certain fine-grained activities such as spurious\line \expndtw5 zero counts, or overflowed queues. Hence, we could not quantify our\line \expndtw4 choice of ZCT implementation over that in the literature. However, we\line can justify our choice from an analytic standpoint since it allows us to\line \expndtw5 place an upper bound on the cost of scanning for free objects. Resolu-\line \expndtw4 tion of this issue is a topic for future research.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We evaluate our results by reporting the net savings in:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\tx508 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\tx508 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent260\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference Counting,}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx508 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx508 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent260\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Allocation Activities, and}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1668\margrsxn1320\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6793 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw-14\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 336{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex132\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Low-Overhead Storage Reclamation in the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 3. \lang1033 Deallocation Activities.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2250\fi0\ri18\sb374\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The savings in \lang1032 2 \lang1033 and \lang1032 3 \lang1033 equal the number of allocated and deallocated\line \expndtw1 method contexts. We do not include block contexts owing to the difficul-\line ties with handling blocks (see p. \lang1032 332). \lang1033 This omission will not affect our\line \expndtw2 results significantly because we observed that only \lang1032 7% \lang1033 of all contexts\line \i\expndtw6 allocated \i0 are block contexts (in contrast to \lang1032 6% \lang1033 of all \i activated \i0 con-\line \expndtw2 texts). The savings in \lang1032 1 \lang1033 equals the number of reference counts of cached\line \expndtw3 method context fields (e.g., in active and deactivated contexts, also in\line \expndtw4 initialized, but inactive, contexts) minus a small overhead.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2246\fi238\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltaik-80 system has three types of reference-count opera-\line \expndtw4 tions, listed here in order of increasing time complexity:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb450\sa0\sl-245\slmult0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2761\fi-256\ri0\sb450\sa0\sl-245\slmult0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent256\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference-count requests that cannot be satisfied (the object can-\line \expndtw2 not be reference counted).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb101\sa0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference-count increment (re/7).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2761 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2506\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx2761 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent255\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reference-count decrement \i (refD).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2261\fi0\ri4\sb338\sa497\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 1 \lang1033 is decided by a simple check of the object table. \lang1032 2 \lang1033 or \lang1032 3 \lang1033 occur\line \expndtw2 depending on the outcome of the check in \lang1032 1, \lang1033 and \lang1032 3 \lang1033 is accompanied by a\line \expndtw3 check for zero, since the object might be free.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1668\margrsxn1320\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1389\colsr871\colno2\colw6660 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb263\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Experimental\line \expndtw-2 Results\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 The\line \expndtw0 Effects of\line \expndtw-4 Volatilization}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We simplify the analysis of reference-count savings by ignoring refer-\line \expndtw1 ences from block contexts (they account for only \lang1032 5% \lang1033 of all references\super 28\nosupersub )\line \expndtw3 and by assuming a minimum cache depth of two windows. This latter\line simplification forces most references contained by method contexts to\line always be in the cache:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1668\margrsxn1320\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2754 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2754 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The home context.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2754 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2754 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The sender (the caller context for blocks) context.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2754 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2495\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-356\slmult0\tx2754 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent259\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A newly created context.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li2264\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Only two of these three contexts need be cached at one time since the\line \expndtw3 SVM disposes of the sender context when activating a new one. To de-\line \expndtw8 termine the savings owing to volatilization we maintained separate\line \expndtw5 tallies for reference-count operations of volatile fields (as mentioned in\line \expndtw2\lang1032 1 \lang1033 through \lang1032 3 \lang1033 above) and nonvolatile fields. The tallies were broken down\line \expndtw8 further into refl's and refD's. Table \lang1032 19.1 \lang1033 summarizes these data\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f25\cchs161\lang1032 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0\lang1033 it\line \expndtw2 shows that volatilization of contexts reduces reference counting by \lang1032 91%.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3789\margrsxn1427\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3136\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tlul\tx6408 \f2\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw-21\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 337}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex144\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Experiments and Their Interpretation}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft18\shptop299\shpright6671\shpbottom299\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1440\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 19.1 \lang1033 Savings Owing to Volatilization}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft18\shptop54\shpright6606\shpbottom54\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li590\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\tx2264\tx4226 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Object{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 refi's        refD's{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 Totals}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa173\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-180 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1800 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2866 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3831 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4753 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Volatile Contexts}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 492890}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri151\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 847979}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 1340869}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-252 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1800 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2866 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3831 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4753 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Other Objects}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 65747}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri155\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 66789}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 132536}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-202 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1800 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2866 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3831 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4753 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Savings \b0\lang1032 (%)}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 88}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri148\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 93}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 91}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft18\shptop79\shpright6671\shpbottom79\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Two events reduce these savings:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li266\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\tx518 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li266\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\tx518 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Window underflows and overflows.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li266\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx518 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li266\fi0\ri0\sb97\sa0\tx518 \f2\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent252\pnf25{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Periodic stabilization.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri18\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To measure these reductions we simulate a register cache. The simula-\line \expndtw6 tor stacks the active contexts (in memory and in the registers) and\line \expndtw14 maintains the bounds of the cached contexts to keep track of\line \expndtw1 underflows and overflows.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi238\ri18\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 On an overflow the SVM writes out part of the bottom window to\line memory: the stack, header, and temporaries. When done writing it also\line reference counts these fields. Owing to linear context nesting the SVM\line \expndtw5 can infer the sender from the top of stack pointer so the context header\line \expndtw3 is shortened to four fields.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi241\ri22\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 On an underflow the SVM restores the top window in memory into\line \expndtw10 the registers and then it refD's the cached fields. The cost of an\line \expndtw4 underflow is the same as an overflow. Table \lang1032 19.2 \lang1033 shows that for an \lang1032 8\line \lang1033 window cache, the additional reference-count operations caused by\line \expndtw5 underflows and overflows offsets the gains (of \lang1032 91%) \lang1033 by no more than\line \expndtw-4\lang1032 2%.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft25\shptop511\shpright6678\shpbottom511\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1328\fi0\ri0\sb558\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 19.2 \lang1033 Cost of Overflows and Underflows}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft25\shptop18\shpright6678\shpbottom18\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\tx2488 \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Activity{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 Cache Depth \lang1032 (# \lang1033 of Windows)}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa180\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-173 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1894 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3132 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4673 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri238\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 4}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 8}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li263\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 16}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1894 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3132 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4673 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 % \i0\lang1033 overflows}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri248\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 19}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 3.0}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 0.0}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1894 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3132 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4673 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 % \lang1033 underflows}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri248\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 20}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 3.0}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 0.0}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-223 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1894 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3132 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4673 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs20 % \i0\lang1033 degradation}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri252\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 6}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 2.0}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f25\cchs161\lang1032\fs18 1.4}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft11\shptop72\shpright6671\shpbottom72\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri32\sb374\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 During reclamation the Smalltalk-80 virtual machine accounts for all\line \i\expndtw2 reference-countable \i0 cached references, so it does not reference count the\line \expndtw5 ip and sp fields of volatile contexts. When done it revolatilizes these\line fields. Table \lang1032 19.3 \lang1033 shows that this overhead degrades the savings (by\line \expndtw4 causing extra reference-count operations) by less than \lang1032 1%.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1623\margrsxn1365\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f24\cchs161\lang1032\fs16 338}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f2\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Low-Overhead Storage Reclamation in the Smalltalk-80 Virtual Machine}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2246\shptop302\shpright8899\shpbottom302\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri281\sb349\sa299 \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Table \lang1032 19.3 \lang1033 Losses Owing to Periodic Stabilization and Volatilization}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3870\margrsxn1689\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw4399\colsr842\colno2\colw1108 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-238\shpright6653\shpbottom-238\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-194 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1188 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2009 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2801 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4399 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Cache Depth}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-281 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1188 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2801 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4399 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li230\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f2\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex